window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("hebrews", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"Hebrews","chapters":13,"bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Jesus Is Better — So Hold Fast","paragraphs":["Picture a community of believers who once burned with faith. When they first believed, they suffered for it: they were publicly insulted, some were imprisoned, and their property was seized. They took it joyfully. “For you both had compassion on me in my chains, and joyfully accepted the plundering of your possessions, knowing that you have for yourselves a better possession and an enduring one in the heavens” (Hebrews 10:34). But years have passed. The pressure has not let up, the Lord has not yet returned, and some are growing tired. Their ears have become dull. Some have stopped meeting with the church. And many are being pulled back toward the familiar world of the old covenant — the temple, the priests, the sacrifices — which looked so solid and safe.","Into that situation comes the letter to the Hebrews. Its writer calls it a “word of exhortation” (Hebrews 13:22): a sermon written down. It does not open with a greeting but with a fanfare: “God, having in the past spoken to the fathers through the prophets at many times and in various ways” (Hebrews 1:1), “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son” (Hebrews 1:2). From there it shows, step by step, that Jesus is greater than everything the readers were tempted to go back to: greater than the angels, than Moses, than Joshua, than Aaron and the whole priesthood, than the old covenant, the tabernacle and its sacrifices.","The key word is ‘better’. Jesus is “so much better than the angels” (Hebrews 1:4). He is “the collateral of a better covenant” (Hebrews 7:22) and “the mediator of a better covenant, which on better promises has been given as law” (Hebrews 8:6). Believers have a better hope, better sacrifices, a better possession, a better country and a better resurrection. Everything in the old covenant was a shadow; Christ is the substance. To go back would be to swap the reality for the picture.","And the key command is ‘hold fast’. Woven through the teaching are five solemn warnings and many encouragements. “let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering; for he who promised is faithful” (Hebrews 10:23). Hebrews is written for anyone who is weary, discouraged or tempted to drift. It shows you Jesus — your great High Priest, who understands your weakness and lives forever to intercede for you — and calls you to run your race with endurance, “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith” (Hebrews 12:2)."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"Titus","intro":"The Holy Spirit placed Hebrews after Paul's letters. (Philemon, the short personal letter that stands between Titus and Hebrews, will be added to this study once its text has been re-fetched.) Titus ended by pointing ahead to Hebrews, and the connections are rich.","points":[{"title":"From ‘Our Great God and Savior’ to ‘The Radiance of His Glory’","body":"Titus called Jesus “our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13). Hebrews opens by showing what that means: “His Son is the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power” (Hebrews 1:3). The Son is God's final word, the heir of all things, the one through whom the worlds were made, worshipped by angels and addressed by the Father as God (1:8). No book of the Bible sets out the greatness of Christ more fully."},{"title":"From ‘Purify for Himself a People’ to ‘Purified Us of Our Sins’","body":"Titus said that Christ gave Himself to “purify for himself a people for his own possession, zealous for good works” (Titus 2:14). Hebrews explains how He did it: “when he had by himself purified us of our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high” (Hebrews 1:3). He is both the High Priest and the sacrifice. He entered the true holy place “through his own blood” and obtained “eternal redemption” (Hebrews 9:12)."},{"title":"From a Letter by Paul to a Sermon by an Unknown Preacher","body":"Titus opened with Paul's name. Hebrews names no author. Early Christians suggested Paul, Barnabas, Apollos or Luke, but the letter itself does not say, and its writer says he received the gospel from those who heard the Lord (2:3), which Paul insisted he did not. The honest answer is that we do not know who wrote it. What we do know is that it was written by a gifted teacher who knew Timothy (“Know that our brother Timothy has been freed” (Hebrews 13:23)) and that the church received it as Scripture. Its authority rests on its message, not on its author's name."},{"title":"From ‘Zealous for Good Works’ to ‘Provoke One Another to Love and Good Works’","body":"Titus ended with a call for believers to be “careful to maintain good works” (Titus 3:8). Hebrews carries this into the life of a struggling church: “Let us consider how to provoke one another to love and good works” (Hebrews 10:24), not forsaking meeting together. And it closes with practical commands about love, hospitality, marriage, money and leaders (Hebrews 13). The doctrine of Christ's greatness always leads to the practice of love."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"Jesus Is Better — So Hold Fast","anchorRef":"Hebrews 1:1–3; 4:14–16; 10:19–25; 12:1–2","paragraphs":["The whole letter alternates between teaching and exhortation. The teaching shows that Jesus is better. He is better than the angels, because He is the Son (chapters 1–2). He is better than Moses, because Moses was a servant in God's house and Jesus is the Son over it (chapter 3). He gives a better rest than Joshua could give (chapters 3–4). He is a better High Priest than Aaron, a priest forever in the order of Melchizedek (chapters 5–7). He mediates a better covenant, serves in a better sanctuary, and offers a better sacrifice — Himself, once for all (chapters 8–10).","The exhortation follows from the teaching: so hold fast. “Having then a great high priest, who has passed through the heavens, Jesus, the Son of God, let us hold tightly to our confession” (Hebrews 4:14). Because He was tempted in every way as we are, yet without sin, “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need” (Hebrews 4:16). The great ‘therefore’ of chapter 10 calls believers to draw near, hold fast and encourage one another.","The warnings are serious. Hebrews says that those who neglect “so great a salvation” (Hebrews 2:3) will not escape; that an evil, unbelieving heart can lead to “falling away from the living God” (Hebrews 3:12); and that there is no other sacrifice for those who deliberately reject Christ. This study will not soften those passages. But they are written to people the writer is “persuaded of better things” about (6:9). The warnings are a loving shout to people near a cliff edge.","And the goal is endurance with eyes on Jesus. Chapter 11 parades the heroes of faith who lived by promises they had not yet received. Chapter 12 turns from them to Him: “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith, who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross” (Hebrews 12:2). Faith is not a leap into the dark but a steady fixing of the eyes on the One who has already run the race and finished it."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 13 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"God Has Spoken His Final Word in His Son","body":"God spoke in many ways through the prophets, but in these last days He has spoken by His Son. There is no greater revelation to wait for. Everything God wants you to know about Himself, His salvation and His purposes is found in Jesus. To hear Him is to hear God; to neglect Him is to neglect the greatest salvation."},{"title":"You Have a Great High Priest Who Understands","body":"“For we don’t have a high priest who can’t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin” (Hebrews 4:15). Jesus knows weakness, pain and temptation from the inside. He lives forever to intercede for you, so you can come to God's throne boldly, not to be condemned but to receive mercy and grace in your time of need."},{"title":"Faith Endures by Looking to Jesus","body":"“Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, proof of things not seen” (Hebrews 11:1). The heroes of faith lived for a better country they never saw on earth. Faith keeps going when the promise is delayed and the road is hard, because it is fixed on God's faithfulness and on Jesus, who endured the cross for the joy set before Him."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Where Are You Drifting?","body":"Hebrews warns against drifting away — not a sudden fall, but a slow slide caused by inattention. Honestly consider: is your love for Christ as warm as it was? Are you meeting with other believers, listening to God's word, praying? Name any area where you have been drifting, and ask God to draw you back as you read."},{"title":"What Are You Tempted to Go Back To?","body":"The first readers were tempted to go back to a religion of ritual that felt safer than following Jesus through suffering. What ‘old ways’ attract you — a comfortable religion without cost, a past life, a reliance on your own performance? As you read, let the writer show you that Jesus is better than all of them."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, You have spoken in many ways through the prophets, and in these last days You have spoken to me by Your Son. Open my ears to hear Him. Let me see Jesus as He is: the heir of all things, the radiance of Your glory, the exact imprint of Your nature, who upholds all things by His powerful word and who purified me from my sins. Show me that He is better than every angel, every leader, every ritual and every comfort I might be tempted to go back to. Keep me from drifting away and from a hard, unbelieving heart. Thank You for my great High Priest, who understands my weakness and was tempted in every way as I am, yet without sin. Give me boldness to draw near to Your throne of grace and receive mercy and help in my time of need. Make me hold fast to my confession without wavering, because You who promised are faithful. Give me endurance to run the race You have set before me, looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of my faith. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"God Has Spoken in His Son","themeTitle":"“The Radiance of His Glory”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"God, having in the past spoken to the fathers through the prophets at many times and in various ways,","understanding":"Hebrews begins without a greeting, like a sermon starting with its biggest claim. πολυμερῶς καὶ πολυτρόπως πάλαι ὁ θεὸς λαλήσας τοῖς πατράσιν ἐν τοῖς προφήταις, “God, having in the past spoken to the fathers through the prophets at many times and in various ways.” πολυμερῶς, in many portions — a piece here, a piece there; πολυτρόπως, in many manners — visions, dreams, laws, poems, history. πάλαι, long ago. God has always been a speaking God; but before Christ His revelation came in fragments.","words":[{"strongs":"G4181","hebrew":"πολυμερῶς","translit":"polymerōs","english":"at many times"},{"strongs":"G4187","hebrew":"πολυτρόπως","translit":"polytrópōs","english":"in various ways"},{"strongs":"G3819","hebrew":"πάλαι","translit":"pálai","english":"in the past"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoken"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"}]},{"v":2,"text":"has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son, whom he appointed heir of all things, through whom also he made the worlds.","understanding":"ἐπ᾽ ἐσχάτου τῶν ἡμερῶν τούτων ἐλάλησεν ἡμῖν ἐν υἱῷ, “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son.” ἡμέρα from the time family: the ‘last days’ began with Christ. ἐν υἱῷ is literally ‘in Son’ — no article: God has spoken in one who is Son by nature. This is the final word, not one more fragment. ὃν ἔθηκεν κληρονόμον πάντων, “whom he appointed heir of all things.” δι᾽ οὗ καὶ ἐποίησεν τοὺς αἰῶνας, “through whom also he made the worlds” — αἰών, from the world and time families: the ages, the whole created order in its unfolding history. The Son is the beginning (Creator) and the end (heir) of everything.","words":[{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoken"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G2818","hebrew":"κληρονόμος","translit":"klēronómos","english":"heir"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"worlds"}]},{"v":3,"text":"His Son is the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power, who, when he had by himself purified us of our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high;","understanding":"Seven statements about the Son begin here. ὃς ὢν ἀπαύγασμα τῆς δόξης, “His Son is the radiance of his glory” — not a reflection, like a mirror, but the outshining, like light streaming from the sun. καὶ χαρακτὴρ τῆς ὑποστάσεως αὐτοῦ, “the very image of his substance” — χαρακτήρ from the image family: the exact imprint a seal leaves in wax, line for line (εἰκών in Colossians 1:15 is ‘image’ as portrait; χαρακτήρ is exact impression). φέρων τε τὰ πάντα τῷ ῥήματι τῆς δυνάμεως αὐτοῦ, “and upholding all things by the word of his power” — ῥῆμα from the word family: the spoken utterance, not the λόγος, the message; δύναμις from the power family. He carries the universe forward by His command. καθαρισμὸν τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ποιησάμενος ἐκάθισεν, “when he had by himself purified us of our sins, sat down” — the Creator became the cleanser, and then sat: the work was finished (10:11–12).","words":[{"strongs":"G541","hebrew":"ἀπαύγασμα","translit":"apaúgasma","english":"radiance"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5481","hebrew":"χαρακτήρ","translit":"charaktḗr","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G5287","hebrew":"ὑπόστασις","translit":"hypóstasis","english":"substance"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"upholding"},{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2512","hebrew":"καθαρισμός","translit":"katharismós","english":"purified"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G2523","hebrew":"καθίζω","translit":"kathízō","english":"sat down"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G3172","hebrew":"μεγαλωσύνη","translit":"megalōsýnē","english":"Majesty"},{"strongs":"G5308","hebrew":"ὑψηλός","translit":"hypsēlós","english":"on high"}]},{"v":4,"text":"having become so much better than the angels, as he has inherited a more excellent name than they have.","understanding":"τοσούτῳ κρείττων γενόμενος τῶν ἀγγέλων, “having become so much better than the angels” — κρείττων, ‘better’, is the key word of Hebrews, used thirteen times. ‘Having become’ refers to His exaltation after His saving work: the eternal Son, having taken human nature and finished redemption, was enthroned as the God-Man. ὅσῳ διαφορώτερον παρ᾽ αὐτοὺς κεκληρονόμηκεν ὄνομα, “as he has inherited a more excellent name than they have” — the name ‘Son’, which the next verses prove.","words":[{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherited"},{"strongs":"G1313","hebrew":"διάφορος","translit":"diáphoros","english":"more excellent"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For to which of the angels did he say at any time, “You are my Son. Today I have become your father?” and again, “I will be to him a Father, and he will be to me a Son?”","understanding":"Now seven Old Testament quotations prove the Son's superiority. The first two: “You are my Son. Today I have become your father” — from Psalm 2, a royal psalm. σήμερον, ‘today’, a time-family word Hebrews will use often (3:7–4:7). Paul applied this psalm to the resurrection (Acts 13:33): the eternal Son was publicly installed as King. The second, “I will be to him a Father, and he will be to me a Son,” comes from God's promise to David about his royal descendant (2 Samuel 7). No angel was ever addressed like this.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"Today"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"become your father"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":6,"text":"When he again brings in the firstborn into the world he says, “Let all the angels of God worship him.”","understanding":"ὅταν δὲ πάλιν εἰσαγάγῃ τὸν πρωτότοκον εἰς τὴν οἰκουμένην, “When he again brings in the firstborn into the world” — πρωτότοκος, firstborn: the title of rank and inheritance, not of being created first. οἰκουμένη from the world family: the inhabited earth (compare αἰῶνας, ‘worlds’, in v. 2). Then: “Let all the angels of God worship him.” This line is from the Greek Old Testament of the Song of Moses (Deuteronomy 32), which the writer uses throughout; it is also found in a Hebrew manuscript from the Dead Sea Scrolls, though not in the traditional Hebrew text. The point is plain: angels worship the Son, so He is not an angel.","words":[{"strongs":"G4416","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","english":"firstborn"},{"strongs":"G3625","hebrew":"οἰκουμένη","translit":"oikouménē","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Of the angels he says, “Who makes his angels winds, and his servants a flame of fire.”","understanding":"“Who makes his angels winds, and his servants a flame of fire” — from Psalm 104. λειτουργούς, ‘servants’, from the servant family: those who carry out public, priestly service. Angels are powerful, but they are made, and they serve. God shapes them like wind and fire to do His will. The contrast in the next verse is ‘but of the Son’.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"winds"},{"strongs":"G3011","hebrew":"λειτουργός","translit":"leitourgós","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G5395","hebrew":"φλόξ","translit":"phlóx","english":"flame"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But of the Son he says, “Your throne, O God, is forever and ever. The scepter of uprightness is the scepter of your Kingdom.","understanding":"πρὸς δὲ τὸν υἱόν, ‘But of the Son’: “Your throne, O God, is forever and ever.” From Psalm 45, a royal wedding psalm. The Son is addressed as God — ὁ θεός. εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα τοῦ αἰῶνος, ‘to the age of the age’: αἰών from the time family. “The scepter of uprightness is the scepter of your Kingdom.” Angels serve; the Son reigns, and His reign is straight and just.","words":[{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"scepter"},{"strongs":"G2118","hebrew":"εὐθύτης","translit":"euthýtēs","english":"uprightness"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"}]},{"v":9,"text":"You have loved righteousness, and hated iniquity; therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness above your fellows.”","understanding":"ἠγάπησας δικαιοσύνην καὶ ἐμίσησας ἀνομίαν, “You have loved righteousness, and hated iniquity” — ἀγαπάω from the love family, and ἀνομία from the sin family: lawlessness, rebellion against God's law. The King's love is not neutral; it loves what is right and hates what destroys. διὰ τοῦτο ἔχρισέν σε ὁ θεὸς ὁ θεός σου, “therefore God, your God, has anointed you” — χρίω, the root of Χριστός, ‘Christ’, the Anointed One. ἔλαιον ἀγαλλιάσεως παρὰ τοὺς μετόχους σου, “with the oil of gladness above your fellows” — μέτοχοι, partners: believers are His companions (3:14).","words":[{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hated"},{"strongs":"G458","hebrew":"ἀνομία","translit":"anomía","english":"iniquity"},{"strongs":"G5548","hebrew":"χρίω","translit":"chríō","english":"anointed"},{"strongs":"G1637","hebrew":"ἔλαιον","translit":"élaion","english":"oil"},{"strongs":"G20","hebrew":"ἀγαλλίασις","translit":"agallíasis","english":"gladness"},{"strongs":"G3353","hebrew":"μέτοχος","translit":"métochos","english":"fellows"}]},{"v":10,"text":"And, “You, Lord, in the beginning, laid the foundation of the earth. The heavens are the works of your hands.","understanding":"Another quotation, from Psalm 102 — words addressed to the LORD, now applied to the Son: “You, Lord, in the beginning, laid the foundation of the earth.” κατ᾽ ἀρχάς — ἀρχή here in its first sense, ‘beginning’ (the same word that elsewhere means ‘rule’ in the power family). “The heavens are the works of your hands” — ἔργον from the work family. The Son is not part of creation; He made it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"beginning"},{"strongs":"G2311","hebrew":"θεμελιόω","translit":"themelióō","english":"laid the foundation"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"}]},{"v":11,"text":"They will perish, but you continue. They all will grow old like a garment does.","understanding":"αὐτοὶ ἀπολοῦνται, σὺ δὲ διαμένεις, “They will perish, but you continue” — διαμένω, to remain right through. καὶ πάντες ὡς ἱμάτιον παλαιωθήσονται, “They all will grow old like a garment does.” The universe, for all its age and vastness, is wearing out like clothing. Its Maker is not.","words":[{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perish"},{"strongs":"G1265","hebrew":"διαμένω","translit":"diaménō","english":"continue"},{"strongs":"G3822","hebrew":"παλαιόω","translit":"palaióō","english":"grow old"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garment"}]},{"v":12,"text":"You will roll them up like a mantle, and they will be changed; but you are the same. Your years will not fail.”","understanding":"“You will roll them up like a mantle, and they will be changed” — the Son will fold up the old creation like a cloak and replace it with new (compare the ‘new heaven and a new earth’). σὺ δὲ ὁ αὐτὸς εἶ, “but you are the same.” καὶ τὰ ἔτη σου οὐκ ἐκλείψουσιν, “Your years will not fail.” The letter will end with the same truth: ‘Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today, and forever’ (13:8).","words":[{"strongs":"G4018","hebrew":"περιβόλαιον","translit":"peribólaion","english":"mantle"},{"strongs":"G1667","hebrew":"ἑλίσσω","translit":"helíssō","english":"roll"},{"strongs":"G236","hebrew":"ἀλλάσσω","translit":"allássō","english":"changed"},{"strongs":"G846","hebrew":"αὐτός","translit":"autós","english":"same"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"},{"strongs":"G1587","hebrew":"ἐκλείπω","translit":"ekleípō","english":"fail"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But which of the angels has he told at any time, “Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet?”","understanding":"The seventh quotation, from Psalm 110, the Old Testament verse most quoted in the New: “Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet?” κάθου, ‘sit’: the posture of finished work and royal authority. Jesus Himself used this psalm to show that the Messiah is David's Lord. God never said this to any angel.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"Sit"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemies"},{"strongs":"G5286","hebrew":"ὑποπόδιον","translit":"hypopódion","english":"footstool"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Aren’t they all serving spirits, sent out to do service for the sake of those who will inherit salvation?","understanding":"οὐχὶ πάντες εἰσὶν λειτουργικὰ πνεύματα, “Aren’t they all serving spirits” — λειτουργικός, related to λειτουργός in verse 7: spirits in priestly service. εἰς διακονίαν ἀποστελλόμενα, “sent out to do service” — διακονία, practical service, from the servant family's διάκονος. διὰ τοὺς μέλλοντας κληρονομεῖν σωτηρίαν, “for the sake of those who will inherit salvation.” The Son sits enthroned; the angels are sent to serve — and they serve not only Him, but those He saves.","words":[{"strongs":"G3010","hebrew":"λειτουργικός","translit":"leitourgikós","english":"serving"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent out"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"service"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherit"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Fragments and the Full Picture","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture an ancient mosaic being uncovered piece by piece. One archaeologist finds a fragment of a face, another a piece of a crown, another part of a hand holding a sceptre. For centuries scholars study the fragments, guessing at the whole. Then, one day, the entire mosaic is lifted from the earth and set upright in the light — and every fragment finds its place in a single, glorious figure.","meaning":"“God, having in the past spoken to the fathers through the prophets at many times and in various ways” (v. 1), “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son” (v. 2). The prophets gave true but partial pieces. In Jesus, the full picture stands in the light. Every promise, sacrifice and prophecy finds its place in Him. Ask the Spirit to help you read all of Scripture with Jesus at the centre."},{"title":"The Sun and Its Light","verseRef":"v. 3","image":"Picture the sun rising over a dark landscape. You cannot look directly at the sun, but its light streams across the hills, warming the earth, revealing colours and shapes, giving life. The light is not separate from the sun; it is the sun's own glory reaching you. Then picture a royal seal pressed into warm wax: when it lifts, every line of the king's emblem is there, exact and complete.","meaning":"“His Son is the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power” (v. 3). Jesus is God's glory reaching us, and the exact imprint of God's being. If you want to know what God is like, look at Jesus. And the One who holds all things together by His word stooped to purify you of your sins."},{"title":"He Sat Down","verseRef":"v. 3, 13","image":"Picture the temple courts in Jerusalem. Priests move constantly: carrying animals, sprinkling blood, washing, burning, standing day after day at their duties. There are no chairs in the holy place; their work is never finished. Then picture a throne high above, and on it One who has completed His work, seated in peace and authority, waiting for His enemies to be made His footstool.","meaning":"“when he had by himself purified us of our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high” (v. 3). God said to Him, “Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet” (v. 13). His sitting means the work of cleansing is finished. You do not need to add to it. Rest in what He has done, and trust Him to complete His victory."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"God's Final Word: the Son (v. 1-4)","body":"God spoke through the prophets in many partial ways, but now has spoken in His Son, the heir of all things and Creator of the worlds, the radiance of God's glory and exact imprint of His being, who sustains all things by His word, made purification for sins and sat down at God's right hand, having become far better than the angels."},{"label":"Seven Proofs That the Son Is Greater Than Angels (v. 5-14)","body":"A chain of Old Testament texts shows that God called the Son His Son, commanded angels to worship Him, made angels servants but addressed the Son as God on an eternal throne, declared Him the unchanging Creator who will outlast the universe, and invited Him to sit at His right hand; angels, by contrast, are serving spirits sent for the sake of those who will inherit salvation."}],"sections":[{"heading":"God Has Spoken His Final Word","paragraphs":["Hebrews opens with one of the most majestic sentences in Scripture. “God, having in the past spoken to the fathers through the prophets at many times and in various ways” (v. 1), “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son” (v. 2). The God of the Bible is a God who speaks. Through Moses, the psalms, the prophets and the history of Israel, He revealed Himself truly, but in fragments and in many different ways. Now He has spoken in His Son — not one more prophet, but the One to whom all the prophets pointed.","John's Gospel says the same: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God” (John 1:1), and “No one has seen God at any time. The one and only Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has declared him” (John 1:18). Jesus told Philip, “He who has seen me has seen the Father” (John 14:9). God's final word is not a book added to the prophets but a Person who fulfils them.","This has huge implications for the first readers and for us. If God has spoken His final and fullest word in His Son, then there is nothing better to go back to and nothing greater to wait for. To turn from Jesus to the old ways, or to new revelations, is to turn from the clearest light to dimmer ones. The rest of Hebrews will press this home: “how will we escape if we neglect so great a salvation” (Hebrews 2:3)."]},{"heading":"Seven Glories of the Son","paragraphs":["Verses 2–3 give seven statements about the Son. He is the heir of all things; the One through whom the worlds were made; the radiance of God's glory; the exact imprint of His being; the One who upholds all things by His word; the One who made purification for sins; and the One who sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high. Paul says similarly that “all things have been created through him, and for him” (Colossians 1:16), and that “He is before all things, and in him all things are held together” (Colossians 1:17).","Two images describe His relationship to the Father. ‘Radiance’ (ἀπαύγασμα) is light streaming out from its source: the Son is not a reflection of God's glory but God's glory shining out. ‘Very image’ (χαρακτήρ) is the exact imprint of a seal. The image-family words help: εἰκών, the ‘image’ of Colossians 1:15, is a true portrait; χαρακτήρ is an exact impression. Together they say that the Son is fully God and perfectly reveals God. And He sustains everything “by the word of his power” — ῥῆμα, His spoken command.","Most wonderful is that this Creator and Sustainer also became the Saviour: “when he had by himself purified us of our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high” (v. 3). The One who holds galaxies in place stooped to wash away human sin. ‘By himself’ means He needed no help; ‘sat down’ means the work was complete. Old covenant priests stood daily because their work was never done (10:11). Christ sat down because His work was finished."]},{"heading":"Better Than the Angels","paragraphs":["Why compare Christ with angels? Jewish tradition taught that angels were involved in giving the law at Sinai. Stephen said, “You received the law as it was ordained by angels, and didn’t keep it!” (Acts 7:53), and Paul wrote that the law “was ordained through angels by the hand of a mediator” (Galatians 3:19). If the readers revered the law and its angelic mediators, the writer shows that the Son is far greater. Some may also have been tempted to treat angels as objects of worship, as Paul warned the Colossians.","The writer proves the Son's superiority from Israel's own Scriptures, with seven quotations. God called Him ‘Son’ (Psalm 2; 2 Samuel 7). God commanded angels to worship Him. Angels are servants like wind and fire, but to the Son God says, “Your throne, O God, is forever and ever” (v. 8). The Son created heaven and earth and will outlast them: they will wear out like clothing, “but you are the same. Your years will not fail” (v. 12). And only the Son is invited to sit at God's right hand (Psalm 110).","The chapter ends with a beautiful twist: angels are “serving spirits, sent out to do service for the sake of those who will inherit salvation” (v. 14). The mighty beings who worship Christ are sent to help His people. When an angel freed Peter from prison, Peter said, “Now I truly know that the Lord has sent out his angel and delivered me” (Acts 12:11). The greatness of the Son does not distance Him from us; it guarantees the help of heaven for those He saves."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Son: Creator, Image, Sustainer and Exalted King","paragraphs":["The New Testament repeatedly presents the Son as Creator. “All things were made through him. Without him was not anything made that has been made” (John 1:3). Paul says that “by him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, things visible and things invisible” (Colossians 1:16). And the Son reveals the Father fully: “The Word became flesh, and lived among us. We saw his glory, such glory as of the one and only Son of the Father, full of grace and truth” (John 1:14). He is “Christ, who is the image of God” (2 Corinthians 4:4).","Psalm 110:1, quoted in verse 13, is the Old Testament verse most often used in the New. Jesus quoted it to show that the Messiah is David's Lord (Matthew 22:44). Peter quoted it at Pentecost, insisting “For David didn’t ascend into the heavens” (Acts 2:34), so the psalm must speak of Christ; and Paul applied its promise: “For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet” (1 Corinthians 15:25). Psalm 2:7, quoted in verse 5, Paul connected with the resurrection (Acts 13:33).","The worship of the Son by angels appears in heaven itself. John heard “a voice of many angels around the throne” (Revelation 5:11), saying with a loud voice, “Worthy is the Lamb who has been killed to receive the power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honor, glory, and blessing!” (Revelation 5:12). Yet when John fell down to worship an angel, the angel refused: “I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers, the prophets, and with those who keep the words of this book. Worship God” (Revelation 22:9). Angels worship; the Son is worshipped. That is the difference Hebrews 1 proclaims."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Radiance / outshining","hebrew":"ἀπαύγασμα","translit":"apaúgasma","strongs":"G541","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “an off-flash, i.e. effulgence.” It appears only here in the New Testament. Some take it as ‘reflection’, but the stronger reading is ‘radiance’: the light that streams out from a source and shares its nature. As sunlight is the sun reaching us, so the Son is God's glory reaching the world. You cannot have the sun without its light, and there was never a time when the Father was without the Son."},{"english":"Exact imprint / very image","hebrew":"χαρακτήρ","translit":"charaktḗr","strongs":"G5481","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “a graver (the tool or the person), i.e. (by implication) engraving ((\"character\"), the figure stamped, i.e. an exact copy or (figuratively) representation).” It is the root of our word ‘character’. It was used of the image stamped on a coin or pressed by a seal. Here only in the New Testament, it says that the Son bears the exact impression of God's very being. In the image family, it stands beside εἰκών (portrait) and μορφή (essential form)."},{"english":"Substance / essential being / assurance","hebrew":"ὑπόστασις","translit":"hypóstasis","strongs":"G5287","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “a setting under (support), i.e. (figuratively) concretely, essence, or abstractly, assurance (objectively or subjectively).” Literally ‘that which stands under’, the underlying reality of something. In 1:3 it is God's very being; in 3:14 and 11:1 the same word means ‘confidence’ and ‘assurance’ — faith as the solid ground under what we hope for. The Son is the exact imprint of God's reality, and faith rests on that reality."},{"english":"Better / superior","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","strongs":"G2909","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “stronger, i.e. (figuratively) better, i.e. nobler.” It is the signature word of Hebrews, used thirteen times: a better name, a better hope, a better covenant, better promises, better sacrifices, a better possession, a better country, a better resurrection, and blood that speaks better than Abel's. The argument of the whole letter is that Christ and what He brings are better than everything before Him."},{"english":"Firstborn","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","strongs":"G4416","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “first-born (usually as noun, literally or figuratively).” In Israel the firstborn son received the family's leadership and a double inheritance. God called Israel His firstborn, and promised to make David's heir His firstborn, highest of the kings of the earth. Applied to Christ, it is a title of supremacy and inheritance, not of being created first: He is the Creator of all things (v. 10). The church is ‘the assembly of the firstborn’ in Him (12:23)."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"As Hebrews opens, the Holy Spirit lifts your eyes to the greatness of the Son.","items":[{"title":"Listen to the Son","body":"God has spoken His fullest word in Jesus. The Spirit wants to tune your ears to Him above every other voice — your fears, your culture, your own opinions, even good teachers. Ask Him to make Jesus' words and life the lens through which you read all of Scripture and see all of life."},{"title":"See the Glory in His Face","body":"Jesus is the radiance of God's glory and the exact imprint of His nature. If you want to know what God is like, look at Jesus: His compassion, His truth, His holiness, His sacrifice. Ask the Spirit to replace any distorted picture of God in your heart with the true one revealed in Christ."},{"title":"Rest in a Finished Work","body":"The Son sat down because He had made purification for sins. The Spirit wants you to stop striving to cleanse yourself and to rest in what Christ has done. Bring Him any sin you still carry as if it were unforgiven, and receive the cleansing He accomplished once for all."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Listening Check","body":"How much of your week is spent listening to Jesus through His word, compared with other voices? Choose one way to give His voice more room this week (vv. 1–2)."},{"title":"The Worship Check","body":"Is your worship centred on Christ, or on experiences, leaders, or even spiritual things that are not Him? Let verse 6 call you to worship the Son above all."},{"title":"The Security Check","body":"What are you relying on that is wearing out like a garment — health, wealth, reputation, relationships (vv. 11–12)? Write down how Jesus, who ‘remains the same’, is a better foundation."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Seven Glories Meditation","body":"For seven days, meditate on one of the seven statements about the Son in verses 2–3 each day: heir of all things, Creator, radiance of glory, exact imprint, sustainer by His word, purifier of sins, seated at God's right hand. Read the verse slowly, write down what that truth means for you, and turn it into worship before you start your day."},{"title":"Thank God for Unseen Help","body":"Verse 14 says angels are sent to serve those who will inherit salvation. This week, look back over your life and write down times when you were protected, guided or helped in ways you could not explain. Thank God for His care through seen and unseen means, and worship the Son who rules over all."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, You spoke in many ways through the prophets, and now You have spoken to me in Your Son. Thank You that Jesus is Your final and fullest word. Lord Jesus, You are the heir of all things and the Creator of the worlds. You are the radiance of the Father's glory and the exact imprint of His being. You uphold all things by Your powerful word. And You, by Yourself, purified me of my sins and then sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high. I worship You with all the angels of God. Your throne, O God, is forever and ever, and Your sceptre is a sceptre of righteousness. You loved righteousness and hated lawlessness. The heavens and earth will wear out like a garment, but You remain the same, and Your years will never end. Help me listen to You above every other voice. Let me rest in Your finished work. Thank You for sending Your angels to serve those who inherit salvation. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Baptism of Jesus: ‘This Is My Beloved Son’","ref":"Matthew 3:13-17","relatedVerses":"v. 5","summary":"When Jesus came to John the Baptist to be baptized in the Jordan, John at first refused, saying he needed to be baptized by Jesus. Jesus insisted, to fulfil all righteousness. As He came up out of the water, the heavens opened, the Spirit of God descended on Him like a dove, and a voice out of the heavens said, “This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased” (Matthew 3:17). The Father publicly declared what Hebrews 1:5 proclaims from Psalm 2: Jesus is the Son. No angel was ever addressed this way."},{"title":"The Angels Worship at Jesus' Birth","ref":"Luke 2:8-14","relatedVerses":"v. 6","summary":"On the night Jesus was born, shepherds were watching their flocks near Bethlehem. An angel of the Lord appeared, and the glory of the Lord shone around them. The angel announced good news of great joy: a Saviour, Christ the Lord, had been born in the city of David. “Suddenly, there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly army praising God, and saying” (Luke 2:13), ‘Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace.’ When God brought His firstborn into the world, the angels did exactly what Hebrews 1:6 says: they worshipped."},{"title":"An Angel Frees Peter From Prison","ref":"Acts 12:1-17","relatedVerses":"v. 14","summary":"King Herod killed James and arrested Peter, intending to bring him to trial after the Passover. The church prayed earnestly. The night before the trial, Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with chains. “And behold, an angel of the Lord stood by him, and a light shone in the cell” (Acts 12:7). The angel woke Peter, and his chains fell from his hands. The angel led him past the guards and through the iron gate, which opened by itself. Peter said, “Now I truly know that the Lord has sent out his angel and delivered me out of the hand of Herod” (Acts 12:11). Angels are serving spirits sent to help those who will inherit salvation."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"So Great a Salvation, Such a Brother","themeTitle":"“Not Ashamed to Call Them Brothers”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Therefore we ought to pay greater attention to the things that were heard, lest perhaps we drift away.","understanding":"The first of five warnings in Hebrews. διὰ τοῦτο δεῖ περισσοτέρως προσέχειν ἡμᾶς τοῖς ἀκουσθεῖσιν, “Therefore we ought to pay greater attention to the things that were heard” — προσέχω, to hold the mind toward, like a ship holding toward harbour. μήποτε παραρυῶμεν, “lest perhaps we drift away” — παραρρέω, to flow past: a boat carried past its harbour by the current, or a ring slipping off a finger. The danger is not a dramatic rebellion but a slow, unnoticed drift.","words":[{"strongs":"G4337","hebrew":"προσέχω","translit":"proséchō","english":"pay greater attention"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3379","hebrew":"μήποτε","translit":"mḗpote","english":"lest perhaps"},{"strongs":"G3901","hebrew":"παραῤῥυέω","translit":"pararrhyéō","english":"drift away"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For if the word spoken through angels proved steadfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just recompense;","understanding":"εἰ γὰρ ὁ δι᾽ ἀγγέλων λαληθεὶς λόγος ἐγένετο βέβαιος, “For if the word spoken through angels proved steadfast” — the law given at Sinai, which Stephen said was ‘ordained by angels’. λόγος from the word family: the message as a whole (compare ῥῆμα, the spoken command, in 1:3). Then two members of the sin family: πᾶσα παράβασις, “every transgression” — stepping over a known line; καὶ παρακοή, “and disobedience” — literally ‘mis-hearing’, refusing to listen. It connects directly to verse 1: the danger of not paying attention to what was heard. Both ἔλαβεν ἔνδικον μισθαποδοσίαν, “received a just recompense.”","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"steadfast"},{"strongs":"G3847","hebrew":"παράβασις","translit":"parábasis","english":"transgression"},{"strongs":"G3876","hebrew":"παρακοή","translit":"parakoḗ","english":"disobedience"},{"strongs":"G1738","hebrew":"ἔνδικος","translit":"éndikos","english":"just"},{"strongs":"G3405","hebrew":"μισθαποδοσία","translit":"misthapodosía","english":"recompense"}]},{"v":3,"text":"how will we escape if we neglect so great a salvation—which at the first having been spoken through the Lord, was confirmed to us by those who heard;","understanding":"πῶς ἡμεῖς ἐκφευξόμεθα τηλικαύτης ἀμελήσαντες σωτηρίας, “how will we escape if we neglect so great a salvation” — ἀμελέω, to be careless about, not bother with. Not ‘reject’ but ‘neglect’: you do not have to attack the gospel to lose it; you only have to ignore it. This salvation ἀρχὴν λαβοῦσα λαλεῖσθαι διὰ τοῦ κυρίου, “at the first having been spoken through the Lord” — Jesus Himself preached it. ὑπὸ τῶν ἀκουσάντων εἰς ἡμᾶς ἐβεβαιώθη, “was confirmed to us by those who heard” — the writer and readers received it second-hand from eyewitnesses.","words":[{"strongs":"G1628","hebrew":"ἐκφεύγω","translit":"ekpheúgō","english":"escape"},{"strongs":"G272","hebrew":"ἀμελέω","translit":"ameléō","english":"neglect"},{"strongs":"G5082","hebrew":"τηλικοῦτος","translit":"tēlikoûtos","english":"so great"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G950","hebrew":"βεβαιόω","translit":"bebaióō","english":"confirmed"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"}]},{"v":4,"text":"God also testifying with them, both by signs and wonders, by various works of power, and by gifts of the Holy Spirit, according to his own will?","understanding":"συνεπιμαρτυροῦντος τοῦ θεοῦ, “God also testifying with them” — adding His own witness. Three members of the signs family in one verse: σημείοις τε καὶ τέρασιν καὶ ποικίλαις δυνάμεσιν, “both by signs and wonders, by various works of power.” σημεῖον points beyond itself; τέρας astonishes; δύναμις (here plural, ‘powers’) is the power at work. Then: πνεύματος ἁγίου μερισμοῖς, “and by gifts of the Holy Spirit” — literally ‘distributions’, portions given out — κατὰ τὴν αὐτοῦ θέλησιν, “according to his own will.” The gospel came with God's own signature.","words":[{"strongs":"G4901","hebrew":"συνεπιμαρτυρέω","translit":"synepimartyréō","english":"testifying"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"signs"},{"strongs":"G5059","hebrew":"τέρας","translit":"téras","english":"wonders"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"works of power"},{"strongs":"G3311","hebrew":"μερισμός","translit":"merismós","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2308","hebrew":"θέλησις","translit":"thélēsis","english":"will"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For he didn’t subject the world to come, of which we speak, to angels.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ ἀγγέλοις ὑπέταξεν τὴν οἰκουμένην τὴν μέλλουσαν, “For he didn’t subject the world to come, of which we speak, to angels.” οἰκουμένη from the world family: the inhabited earth, here the coming renewed world over which Christ and His people will reign. Angels served the old order; the world to come belongs to the Son and to humanity in Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subject"},{"strongs":"G3625","hebrew":"οἰκουμένη","translit":"oikouménē","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But one has somewhere testified, saying, “What is man, that you think of him? Or the son of man, that you care for him?","understanding":"διεμαρτύρατο δέ πού τις, “But one has somewhere testified” — the writer is not vague because he does not know the source, but because the human author does not matter; it is God's word. The quotation is Psalm 8: “What is man, that you think of him? Or the son of man, that you care for him?” ἐπισκέπτομαι, to visit, to look after. In the psalm, ‘son of man’ means a human being; but it is also Jesus' favourite name for Himself, and the writer will apply the psalm to Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G1263","hebrew":"διαμαρτύρομαι","translit":"diamartýromai","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G3403","hebrew":"μιμνήσκω","translit":"mimnḗskō","english":"think of"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G1980","hebrew":"ἐπισκέπτομαι","translit":"episképtomai","english":"care for"}]},{"v":7,"text":"You made him a little lower than the angels. You crowned him with glory and honor.","understanding":"“You made him a little lower than the angels. You crowned him with glory and honor.” βραχύ τι can mean ‘a little’ in rank or ‘for a little while’ in time. Psalm 8 means humanity's place in creation; the writer will apply it to Jesus, who was made lower than the angels ‘for a little while’ in His incarnation and death. δόξα and τιμή: the original dignity God gave humanity.","words":[{"strongs":"G1642","hebrew":"ἐλαττόω","translit":"elattóō","english":"lower"},{"strongs":"G1024","hebrew":"βραχύς","translit":"brachýs","english":"a little"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4737","hebrew":"στεφανόω","translit":"stephanóō","english":"crowned"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"}]},{"v":8,"text":"You have put all things in subjection under his feet.” For in that he subjected all things to him, he left nothing that is not subject to him. But now we don’t see all things subjected to him, yet.","understanding":"“You have put all things in subjection under his feet.” God's plan was that humanity would rule creation. οὐδὲν ἀφῆκεν αὐτῷ ἀνυπότακτον, “he left nothing that is not subject to him.” But reality looks different: νῦν δὲ οὔπω ὁρῶμεν αὐτῷ τὰ πάντα ὑποτεταγμένα, “But now we don’t see all things subjected to him, yet.” ὁράω from the see family: to perceive, take in. Look around: humanity does not rule creation; sin, death and chaos do. Hold this word beside the next verse.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"left"},{"strongs":"G506","hebrew":"ἀνυπότακτος","translit":"anypótaktos","english":"not subject"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But we see him who has been made a little lower than the angels, Jesus, because of the suffering of death crowned with glory and honor, that by the grace of God he should taste of death for everyone.","understanding":"τὸν δὲ βραχύ τι παρ᾽ ἀγγέλους ἠλαττωμένον βλέπομεν Ἰησοῦν, “But we see him who has been made a little lower than the angels, Jesus.” A different see-family word: βλέπω, to look at, fix the eyes on. We do not yet ‘see’ (ὁράω) everything subject to humanity, but we do ‘see’ (βλέπω) Jesus — the one true Man who fulfils Psalm 8. διὰ τὸ πάθημα τοῦ θανάτου δόξῃ καὶ τιμῇ ἐστεφανωμένον, “because of the suffering of death crowned with glory and honor.” ὅπως χάριτι θεοῦ ὑπὲρ παντὸς γεύσηται θανάτου, “that by the grace of God he should taste of death for everyone” — γεύομαι, to taste, to experience fully. (A few manuscripts read ‘apart from God’ instead of ‘by the grace of God’; nearly all read ‘grace’.)","words":[{"strongs":"G1642","hebrew":"ἐλαττόω","translit":"elattóō","english":"lower"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"suffering"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G1089","hebrew":"γεύομαι","translit":"geúomai","english":"taste"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For it became him, for whom are all things, and through whom are all things, in bringing many children to glory, to make the author of their salvation perfect through sufferings.","understanding":"ἔπρεπεν γὰρ αὐτῷ, “For it became him” — it was fitting, worthy of God's character. δι᾽ ὃν τὰ πάντα καὶ δι᾽ οὗ τὰ πάντα, “for whom are all things, and through whom are all things.” πολλοὺς υἱοὺς εἰς δόξαν ἀγαγόντα, “in bringing many children to glory.” τὸν ἀρχηγὸν τῆς σωτηρίας αὐτῶν διὰ παθημάτων τελειῶσαι, “to make the author of their salvation perfect through sufferings.” ἀρχηγός is a pioneer, the leader who blazes the trail. τελειόω from the new perfect family: not moral improvement — He was never imperfect — but being brought to the goal, fully qualified as Saviour by going through suffering.","words":[{"strongs":"G4241","hebrew":"πρέπω","translit":"prépō","english":"became"},{"strongs":"G71","hebrew":"ἄγω","translit":"ágō","english":"bringing"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G747","hebrew":"ἀρχηγός","translit":"archēgós","english":"author"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For both he who sanctifies and those who are sanctified are all from one, for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brothers,","understanding":"ὅ τε γὰρ ἁγιάζων καὶ οἱ ἁγιαζόμενοι ἐξ ἑνὸς πάντες, “For both he who sanctifies and those who are sanctified are all from one” — one Father, one family, one humanity. ἁγιάζω, to set apart as holy. δι᾽ ἣν αἰτίαν οὐκ ἐπαισχύνεται ἀδελφοὺς αὐτοὺς καλεῖν, “for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brothers.” The Son to whom angels bow is not embarrassed to be family with us.","words":[{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctifies"},{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G1870","hebrew":"ἐπαισχύνομαι","translit":"epaischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"}]},{"v":12,"text":"saying, “I will declare your name to my brothers. Among of the congregation I will sing your praise.”","understanding":"The proof comes from Psalm 22, the psalm Jesus quoted on the cross: “I will declare your name to my brothers. Among of the congregation I will sing your praise.” The psalm moves from suffering to praise; after His death the risen Christ calls His people ‘my brothers’ and leads their worship. ἐκκλησία, ‘congregation’, is the word for ‘church’.","words":[{"strongs":"G518","hebrew":"ἀπαγγέλλω","translit":"apangéllō","english":"declare"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"congregation"},{"strongs":"G5214","hebrew":"ὑμνέω","translit":"hymnéō","english":"sing your praise"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Again, “I will put my trust in him.” Again, “Behold, here I am with the children whom God has given me.”","understanding":"Two more quotations, from Isaiah 8: “I will put my trust in him.” The Son lives the life of trust in the Father that every child of God must live. And: “Behold, here I am with the children whom God has given me.” Jesus is the elder brother standing with the family the Father gave Him — a theme He echoed: ‘those whom you have given me’.","words":[{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"put my trust"},{"strongs":"G2400","hebrew":"ἰδού","translit":"idoú","english":"Behold"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Since then the children have shared in flesh and blood, he also himself in the same way partook of the same, that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil,","understanding":"ἐπεὶ οὖν τὰ παιδία κεκοινώνηκεν αἵματος καὶ σαρκός, “Since then the children have shared in flesh and blood,” καὶ αὐτὸς παραπλησίως μετέσχεν τῶν αὐτῶν, “he also himself in the same way partook of the same.” Why? ἵνα διὰ τοῦ θανάτου καταργήσῃ τὸν τὸ κράτος ἔχοντα τοῦ θανάτου, “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil.” κράτος from the power family: grip, hold, dominion — not ἐξουσία, rightful authority. The devil's hold over humanity was through fear of death and the guilt that makes death terrible. καταργέω, to make idle, render powerless. Jesus defeated death by dying.","words":[{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2841","hebrew":"κοινωνέω","translit":"koinōnéō","english":"shared"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G3348","hebrew":"μετέχω","translit":"metéchō","english":"partook"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"bring to nothing"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"}]},{"v":15,"text":"and might deliver all of them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage.","understanding":"καὶ ἀπαλλάξῃ τούτους, “and might deliver all of them” — release, set free. ὅσοι φόβῳ θανάτου διὰ παντὸς τοῦ ζῆν ἔνοχοι ἦσαν δουλείας, “who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage” — δουλεία, slavery, related to δοῦλος in the servant family. Fear of death enslaves, because it makes people grasp, hide and compromise. Christ frees His people from that fear.","words":[{"strongs":"G525","hebrew":"ἀπαλλάσσω","translit":"apallássō","english":"deliver"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lifetime"},{"strongs":"G1777","hebrew":"ἔνοχος","translit":"énochos","english":"subject"},{"strongs":"G1397","hebrew":"δουλεία","translit":"douleía","english":"bondage"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For most certainly, he doesn’t give help to angels, but he gives help to the offspring of Abraham.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ δήπου ἀγγέλων ἐπιλαμβάνεται, “For most certainly, he doesn’t give help to angels” — ἐπιλαμβάνομαι, to take hold of, grasp by the hand to rescue. ἀλλὰ σπέρματος Ἀβραὰμ ἐπιλαμβάνεται, “but he gives help to the offspring of Abraham” — the people of faith. He reached down to grasp human beings, not angels.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G1949","hebrew":"ἐπιλαμβάνομαι","translit":"epilambánomai","english":"give help"},{"strongs":"G4690","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","english":"offspring"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Therefore he was obligated in all things to be made like his brothers, that he might become a merciful and faithful high priest in things pertaining to God, to make atonement for the sins of the people.","understanding":"ὅθεν ὤφειλεν κατὰ πάντα τοῖς ἀδελφοῖς ὁμοιωθῆναι, “Therefore he was obligated in all things to be made like his brothers.” ἵνα ἐλεήμων γένηται καὶ πιστὸς ἀρχιερεύς, “that he might become a merciful and faithful high priest” — the first mention of the great theme of Hebrews: Jesus our High Priest. Merciful toward us, faithful toward God. εἰς τὸ ἱλάσκεσθαι τὰς ἁμαρτίας τοῦ λαοῦ, “to make atonement for the sins of the people” — ἱλάσκομαι, to make propitiation: to deal with sin so that God's righteous anger is turned away.","words":[{"strongs":"G3784","hebrew":"ὀφείλω","translit":"opheílō","english":"obligated"},{"strongs":"G3666","hebrew":"ὁμοιόω","translit":"homoióō","english":"made like"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G1655","hebrew":"ἐλεήμων","translit":"eleḗmōn","english":"merciful"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G2433","hebrew":"ἱλάσκομαι","translit":"hiláskomai","english":"make atonement"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For in that he himself has suffered being tempted, he is able to help those who are tempted.","understanding":"ἐν ᾧ γὰρ πέπονθεν αὐτὸς πειρασθείς, “For in that he himself has suffered being tempted” — πειράζω, to test, to tempt. His temptations were real and they hurt. δύναται τοῖς πειραζομένοις βοηθῆσαι, “he is able to help those who are tempted” — βοηθέω, to run to the cry of someone in danger. He knows from the inside what you are facing, and He comes when you call.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tempted"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G997","hebrew":"βοηθέω","translit":"boēthéō","english":"help"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Drifting Boat","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a small boat tied loosely at a harbour wall. Its owner falls asleep in the warm sun. The rope slips, and the tide begins to draw the boat away — so slowly that there is no jolt, no sound. When he wakes, the harbour is a speck on the horizon and the current is carrying him toward the rocks. He never chose to leave; he simply stopped paying attention.","meaning":"“Therefore we ought to pay greater attention to the things that were heard, lest perhaps we drift away” (v. 1). “how will we escape if we neglect so great a salvation” (v. 3). Drift is rarely dramatic. It happens through neglect: skipping prayer, ignoring Scripture, drifting from fellowship. Ask the Spirit to wake you if you are drifting, and to help you tie your life firmly to Christ."},{"title":"We Don't See — But We See Jesus","verseRef":"v. 8-9","image":"Picture a battlefield after a long war. Smoke still hangs in the air; some enemy outposts still fire; the land is scarred. A soldier looks around and sees no sign that the war is won. Then a messenger points to the hill above the valley, where the victorious King stands, his banner raised, crowned with glory. The fighting is not yet over, but the outcome is certain because of who stands on that hill.","meaning":"“But now we don’t see all things subjected to him, yet” (v. 8). “But we see him who has been made a little lower than the angels, Jesus, because of the suffering of death crowned with glory and honor” (v. 9). The world still looks broken, and you may not see God's purposes fulfilled in your circumstances. But you can see Jesus, crowned after His suffering. Fix your eyes on Him."},{"title":"The Elder Brother in the Family Photograph","verseRef":"v. 11-13","image":"Picture a large family gathered for a photograph: children of every age, some well dressed, some scruffy, some with scars and bandages. In the middle stands the eldest brother — famous, honoured, admired by everyone in the city. He could stand apart. Instead he puts his arms around the youngest and the most embarrassing, smiles broadly and says to the photographer, ‘These are my brothers and sisters.’","meaning":"“For both he who sanctifies and those who are sanctified are all from one, for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brothers” (v. 11). “Behold, here I am with the children whom God has given me” (v. 13). The Son whom angels worship is not ashamed of you. He became human to be part of your family and to bring you into His. Receive His welcome without shame."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The First Warning: Do Not Drift (v. 1-4)","body":"Because the Son is so great, we must pay closer attention to the gospel so that we do not drift away; if the law delivered by angels was binding and every violation punished, we will not escape if we neglect so great a salvation, first spoken by the Lord, confirmed by those who heard Him, and attested by God through signs, wonders, miracles and gifts of the Spirit."},{"label":"Jesus, the True Man of Psalm 8 (v. 5-9)","body":"God did not subject the coming world to angels but to humanity, as Psalm 8 says; we do not yet see everything subject to humanity, but we see Jesus, made lower than the angels for a little while and now crowned with glory and honour because He suffered death, tasting death for everyone by the grace of God."},{"label":"The Pioneer Who Became Our Brother (v. 10-18)","body":"It was fitting for God to perfect the pioneer of salvation through suffering; He and those He sanctifies share one family, and He is not ashamed to call them brothers; He shared flesh and blood to destroy the devil, who held the power of death, and to free those enslaved by fear of death; He was made like His brothers in every way to become a merciful and faithful high priest who makes atonement for sins and helps those who are tempted."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Do Not Drift Away","paragraphs":["Chapter 2 opens with the first of five warning passages in Hebrews. “Therefore we ought to pay greater attention to the things that were heard, lest perhaps we drift away” (v. 1). The ‘therefore’ looks back to chapter 1: because the Son is so great, His message demands our full attention. The word for ‘drift’ pictures a boat slipping past the harbour, carried by the current. Drifting requires no effort; it only requires inattention.","The argument is from lesser to greater. The law given through angels at Sinai was binding: “every transgression and disobedience received a just recompense” (v. 2). Two sin-family words appear together: transgression, stepping over the line; and disobedience, refusing to hear. If ignoring the law brought judgement, “how will we escape if we neglect so great a salvation” (v. 3)? The message of the Son is greater than the message through angels, so neglecting it is more dangerous.","This salvation has the strongest possible credentials. Jesus Himself spoke it. Eyewitnesses confirmed it. And God added His own testimony “both by signs and wonders, by various works of power, and by gifts of the Holy Spirit, according to his own will” (v. 4). All three members of the signs family appear here together. Luke records the same in Acts, where the Lord “testified to the word of his grace, granting signs and wonders to be done by their hands” (Acts 14:3). The gospel is not a rumour; it came with God's own signature."]},{"heading":"We See Jesus","paragraphs":["The writer turns to Psalm 8, which celebrates humanity's dignity: “What is man, that you think of him?” (v. 6). God made human beings a little lower than the angels and crowned them with glory, putting everything under their feet. That was the original design: humanity ruling creation for God. But the writer is honest: “But now we don’t see all things subjected to him, yet” (v. 8). Sin, suffering and death show that humanity has lost its crown.","Then comes the turning point: “But we see him who has been made a little lower than the angels, Jesus, because of the suffering of death crowned with glory and honor, that by the grace of God he should taste of death for everyone” (v. 9). Two see-family words make the point: we do not yet ‘see’ (ὁράω) the world restored, but we do ‘see’ (βλέπω) Jesus. He is the true Man of Psalm 8, who went down lower than the angels, tasted death on our behalf, and now wears the crown humanity lost.","This is how believers live in a broken world: not by pretending everything is fine, but by fixing their eyes on Jesus. What God intended for humanity has already been achieved in Him, and He will share it with His people. As Paul wrote, Christ “abolished death, and brought life and immortality to light through the Good News” (2 Timothy 1:10)."]},{"heading":"The Pioneer Who Became Our Brother and Priest","paragraphs":["Why did the Son have to suffer? “For it became him, for whom are all things, and through whom are all things, in bringing many children to glory, to make the author of their salvation perfect through sufferings” (v. 10). The pioneer goes ahead and opens the way. ‘Perfect’ here belongs to the perfect family: it does not mean Jesus was once flawed, but that through suffering He was fully qualified as Saviour. And He joins Himself to us completely: “he is not ashamed to call them brothers” (v. 11). After the resurrection He told the women, “Go tell my brothers” (Matthew 28:10).","He shared our flesh and blood “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil” (v. 14), “and might deliver all of them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage” (v. 15). The devil's ‘power’ is κράτος, a grip or hold, not rightful authority. By dying, Jesus broke that grip. John says, “To this end the Son of God was revealed: that he might destroy the works of the devil” (1 John 3:8). Fear of death no longer needs to rule us.","Finally, Jesus “was obligated in all things to be made like his brothers, that he might become a merciful and faithful high priest in things pertaining to God, to make atonement for the sins of the people” (v. 17). Here the great theme of Hebrews first appears. Because He fully shared our humanity, He can represent us before God. And because He was tempted and suffered, “he is able to help those who are tempted” (v. 18). Your High Priest is not distant; He knows your struggle from the inside and runs to help when you call."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Victory Over Death and the Atoning Sacrifice","paragraphs":["Hebrews 2:14–15 announces the defeat of death that Paul celebrates: “Death is swallowed up in victory” (1 Corinthians 15:54). “Death, where is your sting? Hades, where is your victory?” (1 Corinthians 15:55). “But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ” (1 Corinthians 15:57). The risen Christ says, “I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever. Amen. I have the keys of Death and of Hades” (Revelation 1:18).","The ‘atonement’ of 2:17 (ἱλάσκομαι) belongs to a word group used across the New Testament for Christ's sacrifice that turns away God's wrath and removes sin. God sent Christ “to be an atoning sacrifice, through faith in his blood” (Romans 3:25). John writes, “And he is the atoning sacrifice for our sins, and not for ours only, but also for the whole world” (1 John 2:2), and “In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins” (1 John 4:10).","Jesus' brotherhood with His people runs through the Gospels and letters. God “predestined to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brothers” (Romans 8:29). The risen Lord told Mary, “go to my brothers” (John 20:17). And His experience of temptation was real: “Then Jesus was led up by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil” (Matthew 4:1). In Gethsemane, “Being in agony he prayed more earnestly” (Luke 22:44). He knows what it is to be tested."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Drift away / flow past","hebrew":"παραῤῥυέω","translit":"pararrhyéō","strongs":"G3901","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “to flow by, i.e. (figuratively) carelessly pass (miss).” It was used of a ship drifting past a harbour, of a ring slipping off a finger, and of something slipping out of the memory. It appears only here in the New Testament. The danger it describes is gradual, unnoticed and caused by inattention rather than by deliberate rebellion. The cure is in the same verse: pay closer attention."},{"english":"Neglect / be careless of","hebrew":"ἀμελέω","translit":"ameléō","strongs":"G272","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “to be careless of.” It is used in Jesus' parable of the guests who ‘made light of’ the wedding invitation and went off to their farms and businesses. They did not attack the king; they simply did not care enough to come. Hebrews warns that the same indifference toward so great a salvation leaves no way of escape."},{"english":"Author / pioneer / leader","hebrew":"ἀρχηγός","translit":"archēgós","strongs":"G747","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “a chief leader.” It describes someone who goes first and opens the way for others: a founder of a city, a leader of a column, a trailblazer. Peter calls Jesus ‘the Prince of life’ with this word. In Hebrews 2:10 and 12:2, Jesus is the pioneer who has gone ahead of His people through suffering and death into glory, and who leads them along the same road."},{"english":"Bring to nothing / render powerless","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","strongs":"G2673","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “to be (render) entirely idle (useless), literally or figuratively.” It means to put something out of action rather than to annihilate it. The devil still exists, but his grip — the power of death through guilt and fear — has been broken for those who belong to Christ. Paul uses the same word for death itself being ‘abolished’."},{"english":"Make atonement / propitiate","hebrew":"ἱλάσκομαι","translit":"hiláskomai","strongs":"G2433","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “to conciliate, i.e. (transitively) to atone for (sin), or (intransitively) be propitious.” The tax collector in Jesus' parable prayed with this word: ‘God, be merciful to me, a sinner.’ In Hebrews 2:17 Jesus, as High Priest, deals with sin in a way that satisfies God's justice and opens the way to His mercy. The related nouns describe Christ as the ‘atoning sacrifice’ in Romans 3:25 and 1 John 2:2."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit speaks to you about attention, hope in a broken world, and the brotherhood of Christ.","items":[{"title":"Pay Attention to What You Have Heard","body":"The Spirit is not condemning you, but gently warning against drift. Is there an area where you have stopped listening — to Scripture, to His conviction, to the counsel of believers? Ask Him to restore your attentiveness and to anchor you again to the gospel."},{"title":"Look at Jesus When You Cannot See the Answer","body":"There may be things in your life that do not yet look subject to Christ: illness, injustice, unanswered prayer. The Spirit does not ask you to pretend. He asks you to see Jesus, crowned after suffering. Fix your eyes on Him and let His victory shape your hope."},{"title":"Freedom From the Fear of Death","body":"Christ shared your humanity to break the grip of death and free you from lifelong fear. The Spirit wants to replace that fear with confidence. Bring Him your fears about death, loss or the unknown, and let Him remind you that your elder Brother has gone through death and holds its keys."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Drift Check","body":"Compare your spiritual habits now with a year ago. Where have you drifted? Name one practice to restore this week (vv. 1–3)."},{"title":"The Fear Check","body":"What fears control your decisions — of death, loss, failure, people? Write them down and set verses 14–15 beside them."},{"title":"The Temptation Check","body":"What temptation do you face most often? Next time it comes, pray immediately, ‘Lord Jesus, You were tempted too — help me,’ trusting verse 18."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Anchor Points","body":"Drift is prevented by anchors. Choose three daily anchors for the next month: a fixed time in Scripture, a moment of prayer at midday, and a short evening review. Also commit to one weekly anchor: gathering with believers. Write them on a card and keep it where you will see it each morning."},{"title":"Brother-Language Prayer","body":"Jesus is not ashamed to call you His brother or sister. For a week, begin your prayers by thanking Him specifically for this: ‘Lord Jesus, thank You that You are not ashamed of me.’ Then ask yourself whether there is anyone in your church you have been ashamed of or kept at a distance, and take a step to treat them as family."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, I do not want to drift away. Help me pay close attention to the gospel I have heard. Forgive me for every way I have neglected so great a salvation. Thank You that this message was spoken by You, confirmed by those who heard, and attested by God with signs, wonders, works of power and gifts of the Holy Spirit. When I look at the world and do not yet see all things subject to You, help me to see You, crowned with glory and honour because You suffered death and tasted death for everyone by the grace of God. Thank You for becoming the pioneer of my salvation, made perfect through sufferings. Thank You that You are not ashamed to call me Your brother. You shared flesh and blood to destroy the devil's grip on death and to free me from the fear of death. You are my merciful and faithful High Priest, who made atonement for my sins. Because You were tempted, You are able to help me when I am tempted. Help me now. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Jesus Tempted in the Wilderness","ref":"Matthew 4:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 17-18","summary":"After His baptism, “Jesus was led up by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil” (Matthew 4:1). After forty days of fasting He was hungry, and the devil tempted Him to turn stones into bread, to throw Himself from the temple, and to worship Satan in exchange for the kingdoms of the world. Each time Jesus answered from Scripture and refused. The devil left Him ‘until another time’. Hebrews 2:18 says that because Jesus suffered when tempted, He is able to help those who are tempted. He did not resist from a distance; He fought the battle in the same weakness of flesh that we have."},{"title":"Gethsemane: Sharing Our Fear and Sorrow","ref":"Matthew 26:36-46; Luke 22:39-46","relatedVerses":"v. 14-18","summary":"On the night before He died, Jesus went with His disciples to Gethsemane. He told them, “My soul is exceedingly sorrowful, even to death. Stay here, and watch” (Mark 14:34). He fell on His face and prayed, “My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass away from me; nevertheless, not what I desire, but what you desire” (Matthew 26:39). Luke writes, “Being in agony he prayed more earnestly” (Luke 22:44). Here we see Jesus fully sharing our flesh and blood, facing death with real anguish, yet trusting the Father. He knows the fear of death from the inside, which is why He can free us from it."},{"title":"The Risen Lord Calls Them ‘My Brothers’","ref":"John 20:11-18; Matthew 28:8-10","relatedVerses":"v. 11-12","summary":"On the first Easter morning, Mary Magdalene stood weeping at the empty tomb. Jesus appeared to her and called her by name. Then He gave her a message: “go to my brothers” (John 20:17), and tell them He was ascending to His Father and theirs, His God and theirs. The same morning He told the women, “Go tell my brothers that they should go into Galilee, and there they will see me” (Matthew 28:10). The disciples had abandoned Him, yet the risen Lord called them brothers. Hebrews 2:11 explains why: He is not ashamed to call them brothers."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Greater Than Moses: Today, Do Not Harden Your Hearts","themeTitle":"“Today If You Will Hear His Voice”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Therefore, holy brothers, partakers of a heavenly calling, consider the Apostle and High Priest of our confession, Jesus;","understanding":"ὅθεν, ἀδελφοὶ ἅγιοι, “Therefore, holy brothers” — the family language of 2:11 continues. κλήσεως ἐπουρανίου μέτοχοι, “partakers of a heavenly calling” — sharers in a call that comes from heaven and leads there. κατανοήσατε, “consider” — look closely, fix your mind on. τὸν ἀπόστολον καὶ ἀρχιερέα τῆς ὁμολογίας ἡμῶν Ἰησοῦν, “the Apostle and High Priest of our confession, Jesus” — the only place Jesus is called ‘apostle’: the one sent from God to us. As High Priest He represents us to God. Moses and Aaron held these two roles separately; Jesus holds both.","words":[{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G3353","hebrew":"μέτοχος","translit":"métochos","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G2821","hebrew":"κλῆσις","translit":"klēsis","english":"calling"},{"strongs":"G2657","hebrew":"κατανοέω","translit":"katanoéō","english":"consider"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"Apostle"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"High Priest"},{"strongs":"G3671","hebrew":"ὁμολογία","translit":"homología","english":"confession"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"}]},{"v":2,"text":"who was faithful to him who appointed him, as also was Moses in all his house.","understanding":"πιστὸν ὄντα τῷ ποιήσαντι αὐτόν, “who was faithful to him who appointed him” — ποιέω, made or appointed. ὡς καὶ Μωϋσῆς ἐν ὅλῳ τῷ οἴκῳ αὐτοῦ, “as also was Moses in all his house” — echoing God's words that Moses was ‘faithful in all my house’ (Numbers 12). The writer honours Moses before showing that Jesus is greater. οἶκος, ‘house’, means household: God's people.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For he has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, because he who built the house has more honor than the house.","understanding":"πλείονος γὰρ οὗτος δόξης παρὰ Μωϋσῆν ἠξίωται, “For he has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses.” Why? καθ᾽ ὅσον πλείονα τιμὴν ἔχει τοῦ οἴκου ὁ κατασκευάσας αὐτόν, “because he who built the house has more honor than the house.” Moses was part of the house; Jesus built it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4119","hebrew":"πλείων","translit":"pleíōn","english":"more"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G515","hebrew":"ἀξιόω","translit":"axióō","english":"counted worthy"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2680","hebrew":"κατασκευάζω","translit":"kataskeuázō","english":"built"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For every house is built by someone; but he who built all things is God.","understanding":"πᾶς γὰρ οἶκος κατασκευάζεται ὑπό τινος, “For every house is built by someone;” ὁ δὲ πάντα κατασκευάσας θεός, “but he who built all things is God.” A quiet but bold claim: if Jesus built the house (v. 3), and God built all things, then Jesus does the work of God (as 1:2 and 1:10 already said).","words":[{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2680","hebrew":"κατασκευάζω","translit":"kataskeuázō","english":"built"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Moses indeed was faithful in all his house as a servant, for a testimony of those things which were afterward to be spoken,","understanding":"καὶ Μωϋσῆς μὲν πιστὸς ἐν ὅλῳ τῷ οἴκῳ αὐτοῦ ὡς θεράπων, “Moses indeed was faithful in all his house as a servant.” θεράπων from the servant family: not δοῦλος (slave) or διάκονος, but an honoured attendant, a trusted steward — the very word the Greek Old Testament uses for Moses. His role was εἰς μαρτύριον τῶν λαληθησομένων, “for a testimony of those things which were afterward to be spoken” — Moses pointed forward to Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2324","hebrew":"θεράπων","translit":"therápōn","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoken"}]},{"v":6,"text":"but Christ is faithful as a Son over his house; whose house we are, if we hold fast our confidence and the glorying of our hope firm to the end.","understanding":"Χριστὸς δὲ ὡς υἱὸς ἐπὶ τὸν οἶκον αὐτοῦ, “but Christ is faithful as a Son over his house.” Servant in the house versus Son over the house. οὗ οἶκός ἐσμεν ἡμεῖς, “whose house we are” — the household is not a building but the people of God. Then a condition: ἐὰν τὴν παρρησίαν καὶ τὸ καύχημα τῆς ἐλπίδος μέχρι τέλους βεβαίαν κατάσχωμεν, “if we hold fast our confidence and the glorying of our hope firm to the end.” κατέχω from the hold family: keep a firm grip. Perseverance does not earn our place; it shows that we truly belong.","words":[{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2722","hebrew":"κατέχω","translit":"katéchō","english":"hold fast"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"confidence"},{"strongs":"G2745","hebrew":"καύχημα","translit":"kaúchēma","english":"glorying"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"firm"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Therefore, even as the Holy Spirit says, “Today if you will hear his voice,","understanding":"διό, καθὼς λέγει τὸ πνεῦμα τὸ ἅγιον, “Therefore, even as the Holy Spirit says” — present tense: the Spirit still speaks through the psalm written long ago. The quotation is Psalm 95: “Today if you will hear his voice.” σήμερον from the time family: the word this whole section turns on (3:7–4:11). The day of opportunity is now.","words":[{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"Today"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"}]},{"v":8,"text":"don’t harden your hearts, as in the rebellion, like as in the day of the trial in the wilderness,","understanding":"μὴ σκληρύνητε τὰς καρδίας ὑμῶν, “don’t harden your hearts” — σκληρύνω, to make stiff and stubborn (the root of ‘sclerosis’). ὡς ἐν τῷ παραπικρασμῷ, “as in the rebellion” — literally ‘the embitterment’, the Greek translation of the place name Meribah. κατὰ τὴν ἡμέραν τοῦ πειρασμοῦ ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ, “like as in the day of the trial in the wilderness” — the Greek for Massah. At those places Israel quarrelled with God over water (Exodus 17).","words":[{"strongs":"G4645","hebrew":"σκληρύνω","translit":"sklērýnō","english":"harden"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G3894","hebrew":"παραπικρασμός","translit":"parapikrasmós","english":"rebellion"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"trial"},{"strongs":"G2048","hebrew":"ἔρημος","translit":"érēmos","english":"wilderness"}]},{"v":9,"text":"where your fathers tested me by proving me, and saw my deeds for forty years.","understanding":"οὗ ἐπείρασαν οἱ πατέρες ὑμῶν ἐν δοκιμασίᾳ, “where your fathers tested me by proving me” — they put God on trial. καὶ εἶδον τὰ ἔργα μου τεσσεράκοντα ἔτη, “and saw my deeds for forty years.” ἔργον from the work family: manna every morning, water from the rock, the pillar of cloud and fire. They saw everything — and still did not trust. Seeing miracles does not create faith in a hardened heart.","words":[{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tested"},{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"proving"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Therefore I was displeased with that generation, and said, ‘They always err in their heart, but they didn’t know my ways;’","understanding":"διὸ προσώχθισα τῇ γενεᾷ ἐκείνῃ, “Therefore I was displeased with that generation” — deeply grieved and offended. καὶ εἶπον, ‘Ἀεὶ πλανῶνται τῇ καρδίᾳ,’ “and said, ‘They always err in their heart’” — πλανάω, to wander off course. αὐτοὶ δὲ οὐκ ἔγνωσαν τὰς ὁδούς μου, “but they didn’t know my ways” — γινώσκω from the know family: they saw His works but never came to know Him personally.","words":[{"strongs":"G4360","hebrew":"προσοχθίζω","translit":"prosochthízō","english":"displeased"},{"strongs":"G1074","hebrew":"γενεά","translit":"geneá","english":"generation"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"err"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"ways"}]},{"v":11,"text":"as I swore in my wrath, ‘They will not enter into my rest.’”","understanding":"ὡς ὤμοσα ἐν τῇ ὀργῇ μου, “as I swore in my wrath,” ‘Εἰ εἰσελεύσονται εἰς τὴν κατάπαυσίν μου,’ “‘They will not enter into my rest.’” κατάπαυσις from the new rest family: a settling down to stay. For Israel it meant the promised land of Canaan. The writer will show that God's rest is deeper than Canaan (4:8–10).","words":[{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swore"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Beware, brothers, lest perhaps there be in any one of you an evil heart of unbelief, in falling away from the living God;","understanding":"βλέπετε, ἀδελφοί, “Beware, brothers” — βλέπω, to look, here ‘watch out’. μήποτε ἔσται ἔν τινι ὑμῶν καρδία πονηρὰ ἀπιστίας, “lest perhaps there be in any one of you an evil heart of unbelief” — πονηρός from the evil family: actively wicked. Unbelief is not a neutral intellectual problem but a heart turning against God. ἐν τῷ ἀποστῆναι ἀπὸ θεοῦ ζῶντος, “in falling away from the living God” — ἀφίστημι, to stand away from, the root of ‘apostasy’. It is the second warning of the letter.","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"Beware"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G570","hebrew":"ἀπιστία","translit":"apistía","english":"unbelief"},{"strongs":"G868","hebrew":"ἀφίστημι","translit":"aphístēmi","english":"falling away"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":13,"text":"but exhort one another day by day, so long as it is called “today”; lest any one of you be hardened by the deceitfulness of sin.","understanding":"ἀλλὰ παρακαλεῖτε ἑαυτοὺς καθ᾽ ἑκάστην ἡμέραν, “but exhort one another day by day” — the remedy for a hardening heart is daily mutual encouragement. ἄχρις οὗ τὸ ‘σήμερον’ καλεῖται, “so long as it is called ‘today’” — while there is still time. ἵνα μὴ σκληρυνθῇ τις ἐξ ὑμῶν ἀπάτῃ τῆς ἁμαρτίας, “lest any one of you be hardened by the deceitfulness of sin.” ἁμαρτία from the sin family: sin does not announce itself; it deceives, promising what it cannot give, until the heart is stiff.","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"today"},{"strongs":"G4645","hebrew":"σκληρύνω","translit":"sklērýnō","english":"hardened"},{"strongs":"G539","hebrew":"ἀπάτη","translit":"apátē","english":"deceitfulness"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For we have become partakers of Christ, if we hold fast the beginning of our confidence firm to the end:","understanding":"μέτοχοι γὰρ τοῦ Χριστοῦ γεγόναμεν, “For we have become partakers of Christ” — sharers in Him, His companions (the word of 1:9 and 3:1). ἐάνπερ τὴν ἀρχὴν τῆς ὑποστάσεως μέχρι τέλους βεβαίαν κατάσχωμεν, “if we hold fast the beginning of our confidence firm to the end.” κατέχω again from the hold family. ὑπόστασις, ‘confidence’, is the same word as ‘substance’ in 1:3 and ‘assurance’ in 11:1: the solid ground under our feet. Holding to the end is the evidence that we really have become partakers.","words":[{"strongs":"G3353","hebrew":"μέτοχος","translit":"métochos","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"beginning"},{"strongs":"G5287","hebrew":"ὑπόστασις","translit":"hypóstasis","english":"confidence"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"firm"},{"strongs":"G2722","hebrew":"κατέχω","translit":"katéchō","english":"hold fast"}]},{"v":15,"text":"while it is said, “Today if you will hear his voice, don’t harden your hearts, as in the rebellion.”","understanding":"ἐν τῷ λέγεσθαι, “while it is said” — the writer repeats Psalm 95 to press it home: “Today if you will hear his voice, don’t harden your hearts, as in the rebellion.” Every time the gospel is heard, it is ‘today’ for the hearer.","words":[{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"Today"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4645","hebrew":"σκληρύνω","translit":"sklērýnō","english":"harden"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G3894","hebrew":"παραπικρασμός","translit":"parapikrasmós","english":"rebellion"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For who, when they heard, rebelled? No, didn’t all those who came out of Egypt by Moses?","understanding":"Three searching questions follow. τίνες γὰρ ἀκούσαντες παρεπίκραναν; “For who, when they heard, rebelled?” — παραπικραίνω, embittered God. ἀλλ᾽ οὐ πάντες οἱ ἐξελθόντες ἐξ Αἰγύπτου διὰ Μωϋσέως; “No, didn’t all those who came out of Egypt by Moses?” The shocking answer: the very people who had been rescued. Being redeemed from Egypt did not guarantee entering Canaan.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3893","hebrew":"παραπικραίνω","translit":"parapikraínō","english":"rebelled"},{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"came out"},{"strongs":"G125","hebrew":"Αἴγυπτος","translit":"Aígyptos","english":"Egypt"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"}]},{"v":17,"text":"With whom was he displeased forty years? Wasn’t it with those who sinned, whose bodies fell in the wilderness?","understanding":"τίσιν δὲ προσώχθισεν τεσσεράκοντα ἔτη; “With whom was he displeased forty years?” οὐχὶ τοῖς ἁμαρτήσασιν, “Wasn’t it with those who sinned” — ἁμαρτάνω, the sin family's verb: they missed the mark. ὧν τὰ κῶλα ἔπεσεν ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ, “whose bodies fell in the wilderness?” κῶλον, a limb, a corpse: a whole generation buried in the desert.","words":[{"strongs":"G4360","hebrew":"προσοχθίζω","translit":"prosochthízō","english":"displeased"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinned"},{"strongs":"G2966","hebrew":"κῶλον","translit":"kōlon","english":"bodies"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G2048","hebrew":"ἔρημος","translit":"érēmos","english":"wilderness"}]},{"v":18,"text":"To whom did he swear that they wouldn’t enter into his rest, but to those who were disobedient?","understanding":"τίσιν δὲ ὤμοσεν μὴ εἰσελεύσεσθαι εἰς τὴν κατάπαυσιν αὐτοῦ, “To whom did he swear that they wouldn’t enter into his rest,” εἰ μὴ τοῖς ἀπειθήσασιν; “but to those who were disobedient?” ἀπειθέω, to refuse to be persuaded. κατάπαυσις from the rest family. Notice how verse 18 says ‘disobedient’ and verse 19 says ‘unbelief’: the two are one. Unbelief shows itself as disobedience.","words":[{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swear"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"disobedient"}]},{"v":19,"text":"We see that they were not able to enter in because of unbelief.","understanding":"καὶ βλέπομεν ὅτι οὐκ ἠδυνήθησαν εἰσελθεῖν δι᾽ ἀπιστίαν, “We see that they were not able to enter in because of unbelief.” βλέπω, from the see family: we see it plainly in the record. The issue was not the strength of the Canaanites or the size of the walls, but ἀπιστία, unbelief. They could not enter because they would not trust.","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G570","hebrew":"ἀπιστία","translit":"apistía","english":"unbelief"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Servant and the Son","verseRef":"v. 1-6","image":"Picture a great household in the ancient world. The chief steward is a man of total integrity: he manages the accounts, cares for the servants, keeps every room in order. Everyone respects him. Then the master's son arrives — the heir, the one who will own everything, who in fact designed and built the house itself. The steward bows. He has served faithfully, but the house belongs to the son, and the steward's whole task was to keep it ready for him.","meaning":"“Moses indeed was faithful in all his house as a servant, for a testimony of those things which were afterward to be spoken” (v. 5), “but Christ is faithful as a Son over his house; whose house we are” (v. 6). Moses was the greatest servant of the old covenant, and he pointed to Christ. Jesus is the Son and Builder. You belong to His house. Ask the Spirit to deepen your loyalty to the Son over the house."},{"title":"Graves in the Desert","verseRef":"v. 16-19","image":"Picture a long line of travellers leaving Egypt, singing on the far shore of the Red Sea, their former slave-masters drowned behind them. Then picture forty years later: the same desert, but now dotted with thousands of graves. The people who sang of deliverance never reached the land they were promised. Their children, standing at the edge of Canaan, look back at the graves and remember why.","meaning":"“For who, when they heard, rebelled? No, didn’t all those who came out of Egypt by Moses?” (v. 16). “We see that they were not able to enter in because of unbelief” (v. 19). They experienced great deliverance and saw great miracles, yet unbelief kept them out. The warning is not meant to make you anxious but watchful. Keep trusting the God who brought you out."},{"title":"Today","verseRef":"v. 7-8, 13-15","image":"Picture a calendar on the wall with every page torn off except one. On that remaining page is written a single word: TODAY. Yesterday's opportunities are gone; tomorrow's are not promised. Each morning, the page is the same. And on it, in quiet handwriting, is an invitation: ‘If you will hear his voice…’","meaning":"“Today if you will hear his voice, don’t harden your hearts” (v. 15). “but exhort one another day by day, so long as it is called ‘today’” (v. 13). The time to respond to God is always now. Hardness grows when we postpone obedience. Ask the Spirit what He is saying to you today, and respond while your heart is soft."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Jesus Greater Than Moses (v. 1-6)","body":"Believers are called to consider Jesus, the Apostle and High Priest of their confession, who was faithful like Moses but worthy of greater glory, as the builder is greater than the house; Moses was faithful in God's house as a servant, Christ as a Son over it, and believers are His house if they hold fast their confidence and hope to the end."},{"label":"The Warning of Psalm 95 (v. 7-11)","body":"The Holy Spirit warns through Psalm 95: today, if you hear His voice, do not harden your hearts as Israel did in the wilderness, where they tested God for forty years despite seeing His works, so that He swore they would not enter His rest."},{"label":"Do Not Fall Away Through Unbelief (v. 12-19)","body":"Believers must beware of an evil, unbelieving heart that turns away from the living God, encourage one another daily while it is called today, and hold their original confidence firm to the end; the wilderness generation, though rescued from Egypt, rebelled, sinned and disobeyed, and could not enter God's rest because of unbelief."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Greater Than Moses","paragraphs":["After showing Jesus greater than the angels, the writer compares Him with Moses, the most revered figure in Israel's history. He does not belittle Moses. Both were faithful: Jesus “was faithful to him who appointed him, as also was Moses in all his house” (v. 2). But Jesus is “counted worthy of more glory than Moses, because he who built the house has more honor than the house” (v. 3). Moses was part of God's household; Jesus built it. As John says, “For the law was given through Moses. Grace and truth were realized through Jesus Christ” (John 1:17).","The key difference is between servant and Son. Moses served faithfully “as a servant” (v. 5) — θεράπων, an honoured attendant — and his whole ministry was “for a testimony of those things which were afterward to be spoken” (v. 5). He pointed forward. “but Christ is faithful as a Son over his house” (v. 6). For readers tempted to go back to the religion of Moses, the point is clear: why return to the servant when you belong to the Son?","And believers are the house: “whose house we are, if we hold fast our confidence and the glorying of our hope firm to the end” (v. 6). The ‘if’ is not a threat to the secure but a test of the genuine. Those who truly belong to Christ's household show it by holding on. The hold-family word κατέχω pictures a firm grip that does not let go."]},{"heading":"Today, Do Not Harden Your Hearts","paragraphs":["Now the writer quotes Psalm 95, introducing it with “even as the Holy Spirit says” (v. 7) — not ‘said’. Scripture is the Spirit's living voice now. The psalm recalls Israel's rebellion in the wilderness, at the places called Meribah (‘quarrelling’) and Massah (‘testing’), where the people demanded water and accused God of abandoning them. They “saw my deeds for forty years” (v. 9), yet their hearts went astray. God's verdict: “They will not enter into my rest” (v. 11).","Paul draws the same lesson in 1 Corinthians: “Now these things were our examples, to the intent we should not lust after evil things, as they also lusted” (1 Corinthians 10:6), and “Now all these things happened to them by way of example, and they were written for our admonition” (1 Corinthians 10:11). He warns, “Therefore let him who thinks he stands be careful that he doesn’t fall” (1 Corinthians 10:12). Israel's history is a mirror held up to the church.","The heart of the warning is ‘today’. The opportunity to respond to God's voice is always in the present. Hardness is not usually a single decision but a process: each time we refuse God's voice, our hearts grow a little stiffer. That is why the psalm says, “don’t harden your hearts” (v. 8) — it is something we do to ourselves."]},{"heading":"The Cure for Hard Hearts: Daily Encouragement","paragraphs":["The second warning of the letter follows: “Beware, brothers, lest perhaps there be in any one of you an evil heart of unbelief, in falling away from the living God” (v. 12). Unbelief is not merely intellectual doubt; it is a heart turning away from God. The word ‘falling away’ is the root of our ‘apostasy’. The writer is concerned for ‘any one of you’ — each individual in the community matters.","The remedy is community: “but exhort one another day by day, so long as it is called ‘today’; lest any one of you be hardened by the deceitfulness of sin” (v. 13). Sin deceives; it does not show its true face. We need others to see what we cannot see in ourselves and to call us back. Paul urged the Thessalonians, “Therefore exhort one another, and build each other up” (1 Thessalonians 5:11). Isolated believers are vulnerable; encouraged believers endure.","The chapter ends with three questions that expose the danger. Who rebelled? The people rescued from Egypt. With whom was God angry? Those who sinned. Whom did He bar from His rest? Those who disobeyed. “We see that they were not able to enter in because of unbelief” (v. 19). Unbelief and disobedience are two sides of one coin. The good news is that the same verses offer the remedy: hear His voice today, keep your heart soft, encourage one another, and hold fast to the end."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Wilderness Lessons for the Church","paragraphs":["Stephen summarised Israel's wilderness story before the council: Moses “led them out, having worked wonders and signs in Egypt, in the Red Sea, and in the wilderness for forty years” (Acts 7:36), yet “our fathers wouldn’t be obedient, but rejected him, and turned back in their hearts to Egypt” (Acts 7:39). Paul says that though all passed through the sea, “with most of them, God was not well pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness” (1 Corinthians 10:5).","Yet the warnings come with promises. “No temptation has taken you except what is common to man. God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted above what you are able, but will with the temptation also make the way of escape, that you may be able to endure it” (1 Corinthians 10:13). Hebrews itself later calls believers to encourage one another: “not forsaking our own assembling together, as the custom of some is, but exhorting one another” (Hebrews 10:25).","The link between unbelief and disobedience appears in John's Gospel: “One who believes in the Son has eternal life, but one who disobeys the Son won’t see life, but the wrath of God remains on him” (John 3:36). Believing and obeying are inseparable. And the call to be doers, not just hearers, echoes James: “But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves” (James 1:22)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Consider / observe fully","hebrew":"κατανοέω","translit":"katanoéō","strongs":"G2657","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “to observe fully.” It means to look closely and think carefully, to fix your attention. Jesus used it when He told His disciples to ‘consider the ravens’ and ‘consider the lilies’. Hebrews calls believers to consider Jesus (3:1), and later to consider one another (10:24). The cure for drift and hardness is a long, attentive look at Christ."},{"english":"Servant / honoured attendant","hebrew":"θεράπων","translit":"therápōn","strongs":"G2324","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “a menial attendant (as if cherishing).” It appears only here in the New Testament. In the Greek Old Testament it is used of Moses as God's trusted servant. It is a word of honour, not contempt: a servant close to the master. Yet even the most honoured servant is not the Son. This study tags it in the servant family beside δοῦλος and διάκονος."},{"english":"Harden / make stubborn","hebrew":"σκληρύνω","translit":"sklērýnō","strongs":"G4645","verses":"v. 8, 13, 15","insight":"Strong's: “to indurate, i.e. (figuratively) render stubborn.” It is used of Pharaoh's heart and of people in Ephesus who ‘were hardened and disobedient’ to Paul's preaching. The related noun gives medicine the word ‘sclerosis’, a hardening of tissue. Spiritual hardness develops gradually as warnings are ignored. The psalm puts the responsibility on us: do not harden your hearts."},{"english":"Deceitfulness / delusion","hebrew":"ἀπάτη","translit":"apátē","strongs":"G539","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “delusion.” Jesus spoke of ‘the deceitfulness of riches’ choking the word. Sin deceives by promising pleasure, freedom or fulfilment, while hiding its cost. Hebrews says the result is a hardened heart. That is why believers need daily encouragement from others: we are poor judges of our own deception."},{"english":"Unbelief / faithlessness","hebrew":"ἀπιστία","translit":"apistía","strongs":"G570","verses":"v. 12, 19","insight":"Strong's: “faithlessness, i.e. (negatively) disbelief (lack of Christian faith), or (positively) unfaithfulness (disobedience).” Hebrews connects it closely with disobedience (3:18–19). Unbelief is not simply a lack of information; it is a refusal to trust and obey the God who has spoken. It kept the wilderness generation out of Canaan, and the writer urges his readers not to repeat their mistake."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Holy Spirit speaks through this chapter about who Jesus is, about the danger of a hard heart, and about the gift of community.","items":[{"title":"Consider Jesus","body":"The Spirit invites you to take a long, careful look at Jesus — the Apostle sent from God to you and the High Priest who represents you to God. Many struggles begin when our eyes drift from Him. Set aside time to consider Him today, and let the sight of His faithfulness strengthen yours."},{"title":"What Is He Saying Today?","body":"The Spirit still speaks. Is there something He has been saying that you have postponed or resisted — a step of obedience, a confession, a reconciliation? Do not let your heart grow stiff through delay. Respond today, while your heart is soft."},{"title":"You Need Your Brothers and Sisters","body":"Sin is deceitful, and you cannot always see your own hardening. The Spirit has placed you in a community so that others can encourage and warn you, and so that you can do the same for them. Ask Him whom you should encourage today, and whom you should invite to speak honestly into your life."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Hearing Check","body":"Has God been speaking to you about something you have not yet obeyed? Write it down and decide on one step of obedience today (vv. 7–8, 15)."},{"title":"The Encouragement Check","body":"When did you last encourage another believer to keep going in faith? Who encourages you? Identify one gap and fill it this week (v. 13)."},{"title":"The Deception Check","body":"Is there a sin you have been excusing or minimising? Ask a trusted believer to pray with you about it, because sin deceives (v. 13)."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Daily ‘Today’ Encouragement","body":"For the next thirty days, send one message of genuine spiritual encouragement each day to a different believer: a verse, a prayer, a note of thanks for their faithfulness. Keep a list of names. You are obeying verse 13 and helping protect others from hardening."},{"title":"Consider Jesus Journal","body":"Each evening this week, write one sentence about something you noticed about Jesus in Scripture that day — His faithfulness, His authority, His compassion. At the end of the week, read the list and thank Him. Let ‘considering Jesus’ become a habit that steadies your heart."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, I fix my eyes on You, the Apostle and High Priest of my confession. You were faithful to the Father who appointed You, and You are worthy of more glory than Moses, because You built the house. Thank You that I belong to Your household. Help me hold fast my confidence and the hope I boast in, firm to the end. Holy Spirit, You are speaking today. Give me ears to hear Your voice, and keep me from hardening my heart as Israel did in the wilderness. Guard me from an evil heart of unbelief that turns away from the living God. Expose the deceitfulness of sin in my life. Give me brothers and sisters who will encourage me daily, and make me an encourager of others while it is still called today. I do not want to be like those who saw Your works but did not know Your ways. Teach me to trust You and to obey You, so that I may enter into Your rest. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Water From the Rock at Massah and Meribah","ref":"Exodus 17:1-7","relatedVerses":"v. 8-9","summary":"Not long after crossing the Red Sea, the Israelites camped at Rephidim, where there was no water. Instead of asking God for help, they quarrelled with Moses and accused him of bringing them out of Egypt to kill them with thirst. Moses cried to the LORD, who told him to strike the rock at Horeb, and water flowed out for the people. Moses named the place Massah (‘testing’) and Meribah (‘quarrelling’), because the Israelites tested the LORD, asking, ‘Is the LORD among us or not?’ Psalm 95, quoted in Hebrews 3, recalls this moment as the pattern of a hardened heart: seeing God's works, yet doubting His goodness."},{"title":"Refusing to Enter Canaan","ref":"Numbers 13-14","relatedVerses":"v. 16-19","summary":"When Israel reached the edge of Canaan, Moses sent twelve spies. They returned with fruit from a land flowing with milk and honey, but ten of them said the people were too strong and the cities too fortified. Only Joshua and Caleb urged the people to trust God. The people wept, grumbled, wanted to choose a leader to take them back to Egypt, and even talked of stoning Joshua and Caleb. God declared that this generation would wander in the wilderness for forty years until they died, and only Joshua, Caleb and the children would enter. Hebrews 3:19 sums it up: they could not enter because of unbelief."},{"title":"Moses, Faithful in God's House","ref":"Numbers 12:1-8","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 5","summary":"Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses, asking whether the LORD had spoken only through him. God called the three of them to the tent of meeting and declared that while He spoke to prophets in visions and dreams, with Moses He spoke face to face, because Moses was ‘faithful in all my house’. Hebrews 3 quotes this description and honours Moses for it. Yet it shows that Jesus is greater: Moses was faithful as a servant in God's house; Jesus is faithful as the Son over it."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"The Rest That Remains and the Throne of Grace","themeTitle":"“There Remains a Sabbath Rest”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Let us fear therefore, lest perhaps anyone of you should seem to have come short of a promise of entering into his rest.","understanding":"φοβηθῶμεν οὖν, “Let us fear therefore” — a healthy, sober fear, not panic. μήποτε καταλειπομένης ἐπαγγελίας εἰσελθεῖν εἰς τὴν κατάπαυσιν αὐτοῦ, “lest perhaps... a promise of entering into his rest” is left behind unclaimed. δοκῇ τις ἐξ ὑμῶν ὑστερηκέναι, “anyone of you should seem to have come short” — ὑστερέω, to arrive too late, to fall behind. κατάπαυσις from the rest family. The promise still stands; the danger is failing to enter.","words":[{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entering"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G5302","hebrew":"ὑστερέω","translit":"hysteréō","english":"come short"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For indeed we have had good news preached to us, even as they also did, but the word they heard didn’t profit them, because it wasn’t mixed with faith by those who heard.","understanding":"καὶ γάρ ἐσμεν εὐηγγελισμένοι καθάπερ κἀκεῖνοι, “For indeed we have had good news preached to us, even as they also did” — Israel heard good news of a promised land; we hear good news of Christ. ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ὠφέλησεν ὁ λόγος τῆς ἀκοῆς ἐκείνους, “but the word they heard didn’t profit them” — λόγος from the word family. μὴ συγκεκερασμένος τῇ πίστει τοῖς ἀκούσασιν, “because it wasn’t mixed with faith by those who heard” — like food that is not digested. (Many manuscripts read it slightly differently: ‘because they were not united by faith with those who listened’, meaning Joshua and Caleb. Either way, the message did them no good without faith.)","words":[{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"good news preached"},{"strongs":"G5623","hebrew":"ὠφελέω","translit":"ōpheléō","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G189","hebrew":"ἀκοή","translit":"akoḗ","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G4786","hebrew":"συγκεράννυμι","translit":"synkeránnymi","english":"mixed"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For we who have believed do enter into that rest, even as he has said, “As I swore in my wrath, they will not enter into my rest”; although the works were finished from the foundation of the world.","understanding":"εἰσερχόμεθα γὰρ εἰς τὴν κατάπαυσιν οἱ πιστεύσαντες, “For we who have believed do enter into that rest” — present tense: believers are entering it now. The rest is received by faith, not by effort. The writer quotes Psalm 95 again, “As I swore in my wrath, they will not enter into my rest” — and adds: καίτοι τῶν ἔργων ἀπὸ καταβολῆς κόσμου γενηθέντων, “although the works were finished from the foundation of the world.” κόσμος from the world family. God's rest has been available since creation was complete; it is not a new invention.","words":[{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2602","hebrew":"καταβολή","translit":"katabolḗ","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For he has said this somewhere about the seventh day, “God rested on the seventh day from all his works”;","understanding":"εἴρηκεν γάρ που περὶ τῆς ἑβδόμης οὕτως, “For he has said this somewhere about the seventh day” — the creation account (Genesis 2). “God rested on the seventh day from all his works.” κατέπαυσεν, from καταπαύω in the rest family. God's rest is not tiredness but the satisfaction of a finished work. That is the rest He invites His people into.","words":[{"strongs":"G1442","hebrew":"ἕβδομος","translit":"hébdomos","english":"seventh"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2664","hebrew":"καταπαύω","translit":"katapaúō","english":"rested"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and in this place again, “They will not enter into my rest.”","understanding":"καὶ ἐν τούτῳ πάλιν, “and in this place again” — Psalm 95: “They will not enter into my rest.” By setting Genesis 2 beside Psalm 95, the writer shows that the ‘rest’ God swore about is His own rest — the rest of the seventh day — not merely the land of Canaan.","words":[{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Seeing therefore it remains that some should enter into it, and they to whom the good news was preached before failed to enter in because of disobedience,","understanding":"ἐπεὶ οὖν ἀπολείπεται τινὰς εἰσελθεῖν εἰς αὐτήν, “Seeing therefore it remains that some should enter into it” — God's purpose to bring people into His rest has not been cancelled. καὶ οἱ πρότερον εὐαγγελισθέντες οὐκ εἰσῆλθον δι᾽ ἀπείθειαν, “and they to whom the good news was preached before failed to enter in because of disobedience” — ἀπείθεια, stubborn refusal to be persuaded. If they did not enter, the invitation passes to others.","words":[{"strongs":"G620","hebrew":"ἀπολείπω","translit":"apoleípō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"good news was preached"},{"strongs":"G543","hebrew":"ἀπείθεια","translit":"apeítheia","english":"disobedience"}]},{"v":7,"text":"he again defines a certain day, today, saying through David so long a time afterward (just as has been said), “Today if you will hear his voice, don’t harden your hearts.”","understanding":"πάλιν τινὰ ὁρίζει ἡμέραν, ‘σήμερον’, “he again defines a certain day, today” — ὁρίζω, to mark out (the root of ‘horizon’). ἐν Δαυὶδ λέγων μετὰ τοσοῦτον χρόνον, “saying through David so long a time afterward” — χρόνος from the time family: centuries after Joshua. If Israel had already entered God's rest under Joshua, why would God, through David, still be inviting people ‘today’? The quotation once more: “Today if you will hear his voice, don’t harden your hearts.” σήμερον and χρόνος side by side: a long span of time, and the one day that matters.","words":[{"strongs":"G3724","hebrew":"ὁρίζω","translit":"horízō","english":"defines"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"today"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4645","hebrew":"σκληρύνω","translit":"sklērýnō","english":"harden"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For if Joshua had given them rest, he would not have spoken afterward of another day.","understanding":"εἰ γὰρ αὐτοὺς Ἰησοῦς κατέπαυσεν, “For if Joshua had given them rest” — in Greek, ‘Joshua’ is Ἰησοῦς, the same name as Jesus. The first Ἰησοῦς brought Israel into Canaan, but that was not the final rest. καταπαύω from the rest family. οὐκ ἂν περὶ ἄλλης ἐλάλει μετὰ ταῦτα ἡμέρας, “he would not have spoken afterward of another day” — ἄλλος from the another family: another day of the same kind, a further ‘today’.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Joshua"},{"strongs":"G2664","hebrew":"καταπαύω","translit":"katapaúō","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoken"}]},{"v":9,"text":"There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God.","understanding":"ἄρα ἀπολείπεται σαββατισμὸς τῷ λαῷ τοῦ θεοῦ, “There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God.” A new word appears, found nowhere else in the Bible: σαββατισμός, a Sabbath-keeping. Three members of the rest family meet in verses 8–10: καταπαύω (Joshua's rest), σαββατισμός (the Sabbath rest that remains), and κατάπαυσις (God's own rest). Canaan was a picture; the weekly Sabbath was a picture; the reality is God's own rest, which believers begin to enjoy now through Christ and will enjoy fully in the age to come.","words":[{"strongs":"G620","hebrew":"ἀπολείπω","translit":"apoleípō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G4520","hebrew":"σαββατισμός","translit":"sabbatismós","english":"Sabbath rest"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For he who has entered into his rest has himself also rested from his works, as God did from his.","understanding":"ὁ γὰρ εἰσελθὼν εἰς τὴν κατάπαυσιν αὐτοῦ, “For he who has entered into his rest” — κατάπαυσις. καὶ αὐτὸς κατέπαυσεν ἀπὸ τῶν ἔργων αὐτοῦ ὥσπερ ἀπὸ τῶν ἰδίων ὁ θεός, “has himself also rested from his works, as God did from his.” ἔργον from the work family. Entering God's rest means ceasing to rely on our own works to make us right with God, and resting in His finished work, as God rested when creation was complete.","words":[{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entered"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G2664","hebrew":"καταπαύω","translit":"katapaúō","english":"rested"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Let us therefore give diligence to enter into that rest, lest anyone fall after the same example of disobedience.","understanding":"σπουδάσωμεν οὖν εἰσελθεῖν εἰς ἐκείνην τὴν κατάπαυσιν, “Let us therefore give diligence to enter into that rest” — a deliberate paradox: make every effort to enter rest. The effort is not working to earn it, but taking seriously the call to trust. ἵνα μὴ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ τις ὑποδείγματι πέσῃ τῆς ἀπειθείας, “lest anyone fall after the same example of disobedience” — ὑπόδειγμα, a pattern to avoid.","words":[{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"give diligence"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2663","hebrew":"κατάπαυσις","translit":"katápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fall"},{"strongs":"G5262","hebrew":"ὑπόδειγμα","translit":"hypódeigma","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G543","hebrew":"ἀπείθεια","translit":"apeítheia","english":"disobedience"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For the word of God is living and active, and sharper than any two-edged sword, piercing even to the dividing of soul and spirit, of both joints and marrow, and is able to discern the thoughts and intentions of the heart.","understanding":"ζῶν γὰρ ὁ λόγος τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ ἐνεργής, “For the word of God is living and active.” λόγος from the word family: God's message as a whole, especially the Scripture just quoted (contrast ῥῆμα, the spoken command, in 1:3). ἐνεργής from the power family (ἐνέργεια): operative, at work. καὶ τομώτερος ὑπὲρ πᾶσαν μάχαιραν δίστομον, “and sharper than any two-edged sword,” διϊκνούμενος ἄχρι μερισμοῦ ψυχῆς καὶ πνεύματος, “piercing even to the dividing of soul and spirit” — ψυχή from the life family. ἁρμῶν τε καὶ μυελῶν, “of both joints and marrow.” καὶ κριτικὸς ἐνθυμήσεων καὶ ἐννοιῶν καρδίας, “and is able to discern the thoughts and intentions of the heart” — κριτικός, a judge (our ‘critic’). The word that invites us into rest also exposes what is hiding in us.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G1756","hebrew":"ἐνεργής","translit":"energḗs","english":"active"},{"strongs":"G5114","hebrew":"τομώτερος","translit":"tomṓteros","english":"sharper"},{"strongs":"G1366","hebrew":"δίστομος","translit":"dístomos","english":"two-edged"},{"strongs":"G3162","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G1338","hebrew":"διϊκνέομαι","translit":"diïknéomai","english":"piercing"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G719","hebrew":"ἁρμός","translit":"harmós","english":"joints"},{"strongs":"G3452","hebrew":"μυελός","translit":"myelós","english":"marrow"},{"strongs":"G2924","hebrew":"κριτικός","translit":"kritikós","english":"able to discern"},{"strongs":"G1761","hebrew":"ἐνθύμησις","translit":"enthýmēsis","english":"thoughts"},{"strongs":"G1771","hebrew":"ἔννοια","translit":"énnoia","english":"intentions"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"}]},{"v":13,"text":"There is no creature that is hidden from his sight, but all things are naked and laid open before the eyes of him to whom we must give an account.","understanding":"καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν κτίσις ἀφανὴς ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ, “There is no creature that is hidden from his sight.” πάντα δὲ γυμνὰ καὶ τετραχηλισμένα τοῖς ὀφθαλμοῖς αὐτοῦ, “but all things are naked and laid open before the eyes of him” — τραχηλίζω, to bend back the neck, to lay bare the throat. πρὸς ὃν ἡμῖν ὁ λόγος, “to whom we must give an account.” A wordplay: λόγος again. The λόγος of God (v. 12) searches us, and we must give our λόγος, our account, to Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"creature"},{"strongs":"G852","hebrew":"ἀφανής","translit":"aphanḗs","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G1131","hebrew":"γυμνός","translit":"gymnós","english":"naked"},{"strongs":"G5136","hebrew":"τραχηλίζω","translit":"trachēlízō","english":"laid open"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"account"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Having then a great high priest, who has passed through the heavens, Jesus, the Son of God, let us hold tightly to our confession.","understanding":"ἔχοντες οὖν ἀρχιερέα μέγαν, “Having then a great high priest” — the theme announced in 2:17 and 3:1 now begins in earnest. διεληλυθότα τοὺς οὐρανούς, “who has passed through the heavens” — as the high priest passed through the courts into the Most Holy Place, Jesus has passed through the heavens into God's presence. Ἰησοῦν τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ θεοῦ, “Jesus, the Son of God” — His human name and divine title together. κρατῶμεν τῆς ὁμολογίας, “let us hold tightly to our confession” — κρατέω from the hold family: grip with strength (from κράτος), a firmer word than κατέχω in 3:6, 14.","words":[{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G1330","hebrew":"διέρχομαι","translit":"diérchomai","english":"passed through"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"hold tightly"},{"strongs":"G3671","hebrew":"ὁμολογία","translit":"homología","english":"confession"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For we don’t have a high priest who can’t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ ἔχομεν ἀρχιερέα μὴ δυνάμενον συμπαθῆσαι ταῖς ἀσθενείαις ἡμῶν, “For we don’t have a high priest who can’t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities” — συμπαθέω, to suffer with, to feel with (our ‘sympathy’). ἀσθένεια, weakness of every kind. πεπειρασμένον δὲ κατὰ πάντα καθ᾽ ὁμοιότητα χωρὶς ἁμαρτίας, “but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin.” His temptations were real; His sinlessness means He felt their full force without giving way.","words":[{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"can"},{"strongs":"G4834","hebrew":"συμπαθέω","translit":"sympathéō","english":"touched with the feeling"},{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"infirmities"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tempted"},{"strongs":"G3665","hebrew":"ὁμοιότης","translit":"homoiótēs","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"without"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need.","understanding":"προσερχώμεθα οὖν μετὰ παρρησίας τῷ θρόνῳ τῆς χάριτος, “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace.” προσέρχομαι, a priestly word for approaching God; παρρησία, freedom of speech, confidence. The throne of the universe has become a throne of grace. ἵνα λάβωμεν ἔλεος καὶ χάριν εὕρωμεν, “that we may receive mercy, and may find grace” — mercy for our failures, grace for our needs. εἰς εὔκαιρον βοήθειαν, “for help in time of need” — εὔκαιρος from the time family: well-timed help, arriving at exactly the right moment.","words":[{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"draw near"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"find"},{"strongs":"G2121","hebrew":"εὔκαιρος","translit":"eúkairos","english":"time of need"},{"strongs":"G996","hebrew":"βοήθεια","translit":"boḗtheia","english":"help"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Seventh Day","verseRef":"v. 3-10","image":"Picture a master builder on the evening his great house is finished. Every beam is in place, every window glazed, every garden planted. He does not collapse in exhaustion; he sits on the porch in the cool of the evening and looks at what he has made, satisfied. Then he turns to the weary labourers who have been trying for years to build houses of their own on sand, and he says, ‘Come and sit with me. It is finished. Rest in what I have made.’","meaning":"“God rested on the seventh day from all his works” (v. 4). “There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God” (v. 9). “For he who has entered into his rest has himself also rested from his works, as God did from his” (v. 10). God's rest is the satisfaction of a finished work. In Christ, you are invited to stop striving to earn God's favour and to rest in what Christ has done. Ask the Spirit where you are still trying to build your own house."},{"title":"The Surgeon's Blade","verseRef":"v. 12-13","image":"Picture an operating theatre. A patient lies under bright lights. The surgeon's blade is sharper than any soldier's sword, but it is not wielded to kill. With great skill it cuts through layer after layer — skin, muscle, the joint, down to the marrow — to reach the hidden disease that is slowly killing the patient. Nothing is hidden from the surgeon's eyes. The cutting hurts, but it is the only way to healing.","meaning":"“For the word of God is living and active, and sharper than any two-edged sword, piercing even to the dividing of soul and spirit, of both joints and marrow, and is able to discern the thoughts and intentions of the heart” (v. 12). God's word exposes what we hide, even from ourselves. It is the surgeon's blade, not the executioner's. Let it search you, because the One who uses it is the same High Priest who sympathises with your weakness."},{"title":"The Throne That Became a Mercy Seat","verseRef":"v. 14-16","image":"Picture a great king's throne room. Ordinary people stand trembling at the door; to approach uninvited could mean death. Then picture the king's own son walking out, taking a poor, frightened subject by the hand, and leading him all the way up to the throne. The son says, ‘Father, this one is with me.’ The king smiles and reaches out his sceptre. The throne of judgement has become, for this one, a throne of grace.","meaning":"“Having then a great high priest, who has passed through the heavens, Jesus, the Son of God, let us hold tightly to our confession” (v. 14). “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need” (v. 16). Your High Priest understands your weakness and has opened the way. Come boldly. The help you receive will be well-timed."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Promise of Rest Still Stands (v. 1-5)","body":"Since the promise of entering God's rest remains, believers must take care not to fall short; Israel heard good news but it did not benefit them because it was not combined with faith, while those who believe do enter that rest — God's own rest since creation was completed, of which Genesis and Psalm 95 speak."},{"label":"Today: The Sabbath Rest That Remains (v. 6-11)","body":"Because those who first heard the good news did not enter through disobedience, God again set a day — ‘today’ — through David long after Joshua, showing that Joshua did not give the final rest; a Sabbath rest remains for God's people, in which they cease from their own works as God did from His, so believers must make every effort to enter it."},{"label":"The Living Word and the Great High Priest (v. 12-16)","body":"God's word is living, active and sharper than any sword, exposing the thoughts and intentions of the heart, and every creature is laid bare before Him to whom we must give account; but we have a great High Priest, Jesus the Son of God, who sympathises with our weaknesses and was tempted in every way yet without sin, so we can hold fast our confession and approach the throne of grace with confidence for mercy and timely help."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Promise Still Open","paragraphs":["Chapter 4 continues the argument from Psalm 95. Israel failed to enter Canaan through unbelief, but God's promise of rest has not expired: “Let us fear therefore, lest perhaps anyone of you should seem to have come short of a promise of entering into his rest” (v. 1). The problem was never with the message. Israel heard good news, “but the word they heard didn’t profit them, because it wasn’t mixed with faith by those who heard” (v. 2). Hearing without believing does no good, like food that is never digested.","Then comes the encouragement: “For we who have believed do enter into that rest” (v. 3). Believers are entering it now. The rest is not something we work up but something we enter by faith. And what is this rest? The writer links Psalm 95 with the creation story: “God rested on the seventh day from all his works” (v. 4). God's ‘rest’ in the psalm is His own Sabbath rest, the rest of finished creation, which He wants to share with His people.","Canaan was a real gift, but it was only a picture of something greater. Jesus taught the same: “Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest” (Matthew 11:28). His invitation is not to a piece of land, but to Himself."]},{"heading":"The Sabbath Rest That Remains","paragraphs":["The writer's argument is careful. If Joshua had given Israel the final rest, God would not, centuries later, have spoken through David of ‘today’ as the time to enter (vv. 7–8). There is a striking detail here: in Greek, ‘Joshua’ is Ἰησοῦς, the same name as ‘Jesus’. The first Joshua brought Israel into the land; the greater Joshua brings God's people into God's own rest. So, “There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God” (v. 9).","Three rest-family words come together in verses 8–10. καταπαύω is the rest Joshua gave, but it was incomplete. σαββατισμός, used only here in the Bible, is the Sabbath-keeping that remains. κατάπαυσις is God's own rest, into which believers enter. “For he who has entered into his rest has himself also rested from his works, as God did from his” (v. 10). Paul says the Sabbath was “a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s” (Colossians 2:17). The weekly Sabbath pointed to the rest of trusting in God's finished work.","Yet entering rest requires effort of a certain kind: “Let us therefore give diligence to enter into that rest, lest anyone fall after the same example of disobedience” (v. 11). The diligence is not working to earn salvation, but taking God's word seriously, refusing to drift, and continuing to trust. The danger the writer sees is not trying too hard, but casually assuming we are safe while our hearts harden."]},{"heading":"The Sword and the Sympathiser","paragraphs":["Why must we take care? Because God's word is not a dead letter: “For the word of God is living and active, and sharper than any two-edged sword” (v. 12). It cuts through every excuse, “piercing even to the dividing of soul and spirit, of both joints and marrow” (v. 12). Paul calls it “the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God” (Ephesians 6:17). And “There is no creature that is hidden from his sight, but all things are naked and laid open before the eyes of him to whom we must give an account” (v. 13). The λόγος of God examines us, and we must give our λόγος, our account, to Him.","That could be terrifying. But the next verses bring the gospel: “Having then a great high priest, who has passed through the heavens, Jesus, the Son of God, let us hold tightly to our confession” (v. 14). The One before whom we are laid bare is also the One who represents us. “For we don’t have a high priest who can’t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin” (v. 15). He does not look at our weakness coldly; He feels it with us.","So the chapter ends with an invitation: “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need” (v. 16). The throne of the universe is, for believers, a throne of grace. Paul says that in Christ “we have boldness and access in confidence through our faith in him” (Ephesians 3:12). Come for mercy when you have failed; come for grace when you are weak. His help is εὔκαιρος — exactly on time."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Rest in Christ, the Sword of the Spirit and Our Advocate","paragraphs":["Jesus offered the rest that Hebrews 4 describes: “Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest” (Matthew 11:28). “Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart; and you will find rest for your souls” (Matthew 11:29). On the cross He cried, “It is finished” (John 19:30). And John heard a voice from heaven say of those who die in the Lord, “that they may rest from their labors; for their works follow with them” (Revelation 14:13).","The word as a sword appears in John's vision of the risen Christ: “Out of his mouth proceeded a sharp two-edged sword” (Revelation 1:16). Simeon told Mary, “Yes, a sword will pierce through your own soul, that the thoughts of many hearts may be revealed” (Luke 2:35). God “will judge the secrets of men, according to my Good News, by Jesus Christ” (Romans 2:16). And “we must all be revealed before the judgment seat of Christ” (2 Corinthians 5:10).","But the Judge is also our Advocate. “It is Christ who died, yes rather, who was raised from the dead, who is at the right hand of God, who also makes intercession for us” (Romans 8:34). “If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father, Jesus Christ, the righteous” (1 John 2:1). That is why we can draw near with boldness."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Sabbath rest / sabbath-keeping","hebrew":"σαββατισμός","translit":"sabbatismós","strongs":"G4520","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “a \"sabbatism\", i.e. (figuratively) the repose of Christianity (as a type of heaven).” It appears only here in the whole Bible, and may have been coined by the writer. It is formed from σάββατον, Sabbath. It describes not merely ceasing from work but celebrating a finished work, as God did on the seventh day. Believers begin to enjoy it now by trusting Christ, and will enjoy it fully in the age to come. This study tags it in the new rest family."},{"english":"Active / operative","hebrew":"ἐνεργής","translit":"energḗs","strongs":"G1756","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “active, operative.” It belongs with ἐνέργεια (energy) and ἐνεργέω (to work) in the power family. Paul uses it of a ‘great and effective door’ opened for his ministry. God's word is not inert information; it is energy at work, accomplishing what God sends it to do — creating, convicting, healing, transforming."},{"english":"Able to discern / judge","hebrew":"κριτικός","translit":"kritikós","strongs":"G2924","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “decisive (\"critical\"), i.e. discriminative.” It is the source of our words ‘critic’ and ‘critical’. Only here in the New Testament, it describes God's word as a judge that sifts the thoughts and intentions of the heart. We usually think we judge Scripture; Hebrews says Scripture judges us, distinguishing between what is true and false in our motives."},{"english":"Laid open / laid bare","hebrew":"τραχηλίζω","translit":"trachēlízō","strongs":"G5136","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “to seize by the throat or neck, i.e. to expose the gullet of a victim for killing (generally, to lay bare).” It may picture a sacrificial animal with its neck bared, or a wrestler gripping an opponent so that his face is exposed. Before God nothing is hidden. Yet the next verse brings comfort: the One who sees everything is also our sympathetic High Priest."},{"english":"Sympathise / feel with","hebrew":"συμπαθέω","translit":"sympathéō","strongs":"G4834","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “to feel \"sympathy\" with, i.e. (by implication) to commiserate.” It is formed from σύν, with, and πάσχω, to suffer: to suffer alongside. The writer uses it again of believers who ‘had compassion’ on prisoners (10:34). Jesus is not a detached observer of our weakness. He has felt the full force of temptation and suffering, and He feels with us now."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"Through this chapter the Holy Spirit invites you into rest, searches your heart with His word, and draws you to the throne of grace.","items":[{"title":"Stop Striving, Start Resting","body":"The Spirit sees the ways you are still trying to earn God's acceptance — by performance, religious effort or anxious self-improvement. He invites you to rest from your own works as God rested from His, trusting the finished work of Christ. Ask Him to show you where you are striving, and lay it down today."},{"title":"Let the Word Search You","body":"God's word is living and active. The Spirit wants to use it to expose thoughts and motives you may not see. This is not to shame you but to heal you. As you read Scripture this week, pray, ‘Search me, Lord,’ and respond honestly to what He shows you."},{"title":"Come Boldly for Timely Help","body":"Whatever weakness, temptation or need you face, your High Priest understands. The Spirit is urging you to come to the throne of grace — not timidly, not after you have cleaned yourself up, but boldly, now. Bring your specific need to Him and expect help at the right time."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Rest Check","body":"Where are you trying to earn God's approval by your own efforts? Write it down, and next to it write verse 10. Thank God for Christ's finished work."},{"title":"The Word Check","body":"How have you responded the last few times Scripture exposed something in your heart? Did you listen or explain it away (vv. 12–13)?"},{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"Do you approach God timidly, only after you feel ‘good enough’? Practise coming to Him with boldness this week, especially when you have failed (v. 16)."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Weekly Rest With God","body":"Set aside a regular period each week — part of a day if possible — to rest deliberately: stop your work, worship with God's people, read Scripture, enjoy God's creation and remember Christ's finished work. Use it as a weekly picture of the greater rest He offers. Notice how resting reshapes your trust in Him."},{"title":"Throne-of-Grace Prayer List","body":"Make two short lists: ‘Mercy’ (places where you need forgiveness) and ‘Grace’ (places where you need strength or help). Each day for two weeks, bring both lists boldly to God, thanking Jesus that He sympathises with your weakness. Record any timely help you receive."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You that the promise of entering Your rest still stands. Keep me from falling short through unbelief. Let the good news I hear be mixed with faith. I believe in Jesus, and I enter Your rest. I stop trying to earn Your acceptance by my works, and I rest in Christ's finished work, as You rested on the seventh day. Thank You that a Sabbath rest remains for Your people. Your word is living and active, sharper than any two-edged sword. Let it pierce my heart, divide soul and spirit, and expose every thought and intention that is not of You. Nothing is hidden from Your sight, and I must give account to You. But I have a great High Priest, Jesus, Your Son, who has passed through the heavens. I hold tightly to my confession. Thank You that He sympathises with my weaknesses, having been tempted in every way as I am, yet without sin. I come boldly to Your throne of grace now, to receive mercy and to find grace for help in my time of need. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"God Rests on the Seventh Day","ref":"Genesis 2:1-3","relatedVerses":"v. 3-4, 9-10","summary":"After six days of creating the heavens and the earth and everything in them, God finished His work. On the seventh day He rested from all the work He had done. He blessed the seventh day and made it holy, because on it He rested from all His work of creation. God's rest was not exhaustion but completion and delight. Hebrews 4 says that this rest of God has been open since the foundation of the world, and that God invites His people to share it: “There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God” (v. 9)."},{"title":"Joshua Leads Israel Into Canaan","ref":"Joshua 1-4; 21:43-45","relatedVerses":"v. 8","summary":"After Moses died, Joshua led the next generation across the Jordan into the promised land. The waters stopped as the priests carrying the ark stepped in, and the people crossed on dry ground. In the years that followed, Joshua led them in conquering the land, and the book of Joshua records that the LORD gave them rest on every side. Yet Hebrews 4:8 points out that this was not the final rest, because centuries later David still spoke of ‘today’. Joshua's name in Greek is Ἰησοῦς — Jesus. The first Joshua gave a land; the second gives God's own rest."},{"title":"Jesus Invites the Weary to Rest","ref":"Matthew 11:25-30","relatedVerses":"v. 1-3, 9-11","summary":"Jesus thanked the Father for revealing truth to little children rather than to the proud, and declared that no one knows the Father except the Son and those to whom the Son reveals Him. Then He turned to the crowds, many weighed down by religious rules and the burdens of life: “Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest” (Matthew 11:28). “Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart; and you will find rest for your souls” (Matthew 11:29). The rest promised in Hebrews 4 is found in the person of Jesus Himself."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"A Priest Called by God, a People Called to Grow Up","themeTitle":"“He Learned Obedience by the Things Which He Suffered”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"For every high priest, being taken from among men, is appointed for men in things pertaining to God, that he may offer both gifts and sacrifices for sins.","understanding":"Now the writer explains what a high priest is. πᾶς γὰρ ἀρχιερεὺς ἐξ ἀνθρώπων λαμβανόμενος, “For every high priest, being taken from among men” — he must be human, one of the people. ὑπὲρ ἀνθρώπων καθίσταται τὰ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, “is appointed for men in things pertaining to God” — he stands between them and God. ἵνα προσφέρῃ δῶρά τε καὶ θυσίας ὑπὲρ ἁμαρτιῶν, “that he may offer both gifts and sacrifices for sins.” Three members of the new offering family: προσφέρω, to bring near and offer; δῶρον, a gift; θυσία, a slain sacrifice.","words":[{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"taken"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G2525","hebrew":"καθίστημι","translit":"kathístēmi","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offer"},{"strongs":"G1435","hebrew":"δῶρον","translit":"dōron","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":2,"text":"The high priest can deal gently with those who are ignorant and going astray, because he himself is also surrounded with weakness.","understanding":"μετριοπαθεῖν δυνάμενος, “The high priest can deal gently” — μετριοπαθέω, to keep one's feelings in proportion: neither harsh anger nor soft indulgence. τοῖς ἀγνοοῦσιν καὶ πλανωμένοις, “with those who are ignorant and going astray” — sins of weakness and wandering, not defiant rebellion. ἐπεὶ καὶ αὐτὸς περίκειται ἀσθένειαν, “because he himself is also surrounded with weakness” — weakness wrapped around him like a garment.","words":[{"strongs":"G3356","hebrew":"μετριοπαθέω","translit":"metriopathéō","english":"deal gently"},{"strongs":"G50","hebrew":"ἀγνοέω","translit":"agnoéō","english":"ignorant"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"going astray"},{"strongs":"G4029","hebrew":"περίκειμαι","translit":"períkeimai","english":"surrounded"},{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"weakness"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Because of this, he must offer sacrifices for sins for the people, as well as for himself.","understanding":"καὶ δι᾽ αὐτὴν ὀφείλει, “Because of this, he must” — the human high priest had a problem: he was a sinner too. καθὼς περὶ τοῦ λαοῦ, οὕτως καὶ περὶ αὑτοῦ προσφέρειν περὶ ἁμαρτιῶν, “offer sacrifices for sins for the people, as well as for himself.” On the Day of Atonement, Aaron first offered a bull for his own sin, then a goat for the people (Leviticus 16). Jesus did not need to (7:27).","words":[{"strongs":"G3784","hebrew":"ὀφείλω","translit":"opheílō","english":"must"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offer"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Nobody takes this honor on himself, but he is called by God, just like Aaron was.","understanding":"καὶ οὐχ ἑαυτῷ τις λαμβάνει τὴν τιμήν, “Nobody takes this honor on himself,” ἀλλὰ καλούμενος ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ, καθώσπερ καὶ Ἀαρών, “but he is called by God, just like Aaron was.” Those who grabbed priestly power for themselves — Korah's rebels, King Uzziah burning incense — were judged. The priesthood is a calling, not a career.","words":[{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"takes"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2","hebrew":"Ἀαρών","translit":"Aarṓn","english":"Aaron"}]},{"v":5,"text":"So also Christ didn’t glorify himself to be made a high priest, but it was he who said to him, “You are my Son. Today I have become your father.”","understanding":"οὕτως καὶ ὁ Χριστὸς οὐχ ἑαυτὸν ἐδόξασεν γενηθῆναι ἀρχιερέα, “So also Christ didn’t glorify himself to be made a high priest.” ἀλλ᾽ ὁ λαλήσας πρὸς αὐτόν, “but it was he who said to him” — the Father appointed Him — and the writer quotes Psalm 2 again, as in 1:5: “You are my Son. Today I have become your father.” σήμερον from the time family. The One appointed priest is first of all the Son.","words":[{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorify"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"Today"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"become your father"}]},{"v":6,"text":"As he says also in another place, “You are a priest forever, after the order of Melchizedek.”","understanding":"καθὼς καὶ ἐν ἑτέρῳ λέγει, “As he says also in another place” — Psalm 110, the same psalm as 1:13. “You are a priest forever, after the order of Melchizedek.” εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα from the time family: unending. Melchizedek was the mysterious king-priest of Salem who blessed Abraham (Genesis 14). Jesus is a priest not by descent from Aaron but by God's oath, in a different and greater order. Chapter 7 will explain.","words":[{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"}]},{"v":7,"text":"He, in the days of his flesh, having offered up prayers and petitions with strong crying and tears to him who was able to save him from death, and having been heard for his godly fear,","understanding":"ὃς ἐν ταῖς ἡμέραις τῆς σαρκὸς αὐτοῦ, “He, in the days of his flesh” — His earthly life of weakness. δεήσεις τε καὶ ἱκετηρίας, “having offered up prayers and petitions” — two pray-family words: δέησις, a request born of need, and ἱκετηρία, the plea of a suppliant (originally the olive branch held out by someone begging for mercy). μετὰ κραυγῆς ἰσχυρᾶς καὶ δακρύων, “with strong crying and tears” — Gethsemane. πρὸς τὸν δυνάμενον σῴζειν αὐτὸν ἐκ θανάτου, “to him who was able to save him from death.” καὶ εἰσακουσθεὶς ἀπὸ τῆς εὐλαβείας, “and having been heard for his godly fear” — εὐλάβεια, added to the godliness family: reverent awe. He was heard: not spared from dying, but saved out of death by resurrection.","words":[{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G1162","hebrew":"δέησις","translit":"déēsis","english":"prayers"},{"strongs":"G2428","hebrew":"ἱκετηρία","translit":"hiketēría","english":"petitions"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered up"},{"strongs":"G2906","hebrew":"κραυγή","translit":"kraugḗ","english":"crying"},{"strongs":"G1144","hebrew":"δάκρυ","translit":"dákry","english":"tears"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G1522","hebrew":"εἰσακούω","translit":"eisakoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2124","hebrew":"εὐλάβεια","translit":"eulábeia","english":"godly fear"}]},{"v":8,"text":"though he was a Son, yet learned obedience by the things which he suffered.","understanding":"καίπερ ὢν υἱός, “though he was a Son” — though He was God's eternal Son. ἔμαθεν ἀφ᾽ ὧν ἔπαθεν τὴν ὑπακοήν, “yet learned obedience by the things which he suffered.” A Greek wordplay: ἔμαθεν, ‘he learned’; ἔπαθεν, ‘he suffered’. He was never disobedient; but as a man He learned by experience what obedience costs, step by step, all the way to the cross.","words":[{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learned"},{"strongs":"G5218","hebrew":"ὑπακοή","translit":"hypakoḗ","english":"obedience"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Having been made perfect, he became to all of those who obey him the author of eternal salvation,","understanding":"καὶ τελειωθείς, “Having been made perfect” — τελειόω from the perfect family: brought to the goal, fully qualified as Saviour through His suffering and obedience (as in 2:10). ἐγένετο πᾶσιν τοῖς ὑπακούουσιν αὐτῷ αἴτιος σωτηρίας αἰωνίου, “he became to all of those who obey him the author of eternal salvation.” ‘Author’ here is αἴτιος, the source or cause — a different word from ἀρχηγός, the pioneer, in 2:10 (both are in the new author family). He who obeyed (v. 8) saves those who obey Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"made perfect"},{"strongs":"G5219","hebrew":"ὑπακούω","translit":"hypakoúō","english":"obey"},{"strongs":"G159","hebrew":"αἴτιος","translit":"aítios","english":"author"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"}]},{"v":10,"text":"named by God a high priest after the order of Melchizedek.","understanding":"προσαγορευθεὶς ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ ἀρχιερεὺς κατὰ τὴν τάξιν Μελχισέδεκ, “named by God a high priest after the order of Melchizedek.” προσαγορεύω, to address formally, to hail by a title. The Father Himself publicly gave Him the title.","words":[{"strongs":"G4316","hebrew":"προσαγορεύω","translit":"prosagoreúō","english":"named"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"}]},{"v":11,"text":"About him we have many words to say, and hard to interpret, seeing you have become dull of hearing.","understanding":"The writer pauses before explaining Melchizedek. περὶ οὗ πολὺς ἡμῖν ὁ λόγος καὶ δυσερμήνευτος λέγειν, “About him we have many words to say, and hard to interpret” — λόγος from the word family. The difficulty is not in the subject but in the hearers: ἐπεὶ νωθροὶ γεγόνατε ταῖς ἀκοαῖς, “seeing you have become dull of hearing.” νωθρός means sluggish, lazy; ‘have become’ shows they were not always like this. This begins the third warning passage.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G1421","hebrew":"δυσερμήνευτος","translit":"dysermḗneutos","english":"hard to interpret"},{"strongs":"G3576","hebrew":"νωθρός","translit":"nōthrós","english":"dull"},{"strongs":"G189","hebrew":"ἀκοή","translit":"akoḗ","english":"hearing"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For although by this time you should be teachers, you again need to have someone teach you the rudiments of the first principles of the revelations of God. You have come to need milk, and not solid food.","understanding":"καὶ γὰρ ὀφείλοντες εἶναι διδάσκαλοι διὰ τὸν χρόνον, “For although by this time you should be teachers” — χρόνος from the time family: given how long they had believed. πάλιν χρείαν ἔχετε τοῦ διδάσκειν ὑμᾶς τινὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα τῆς ἀρχῆς τῶν λογίων τοῦ θεοῦ, “you again need to have someone teach you the rudiments of the first principles of the revelations of God” — στοιχεῖα, the ABCs; λόγια, God's oracles. γάλακτος, ‘milk’, καὶ οὐ στερεᾶς τροφῆς, ‘and not solid food’.","words":[{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teachers"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G4747","hebrew":"στοιχεῖον","translit":"stoicheîon","english":"rudiments"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"first principles"},{"strongs":"G3051","hebrew":"λόγιον","translit":"lógion","english":"revelations"},{"strongs":"G1051","hebrew":"γάλα","translit":"gála","english":"milk"},{"strongs":"G4731","hebrew":"στερεός","translit":"stereós","english":"solid"},{"strongs":"G5160","hebrew":"τροφή","translit":"trophḗ","english":"food"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For everyone who lives on milk is not experienced in the word of righteousness, for he is a baby.","understanding":"πᾶς γὰρ ὁ μετέχων γάλακτος ἄπειρος λόγου δικαιοσύνης, “For everyone who lives on milk is not experienced in the word of righteousness” — ἄπειρος, without experience, untested. The ‘word of righteousness’ may mean teaching about righteousness, or the skill to live rightly. νήπιος γάρ ἐστιν, “for he is a baby.” There is nothing wrong with being a baby — at first. The tragedy is remaining one.","words":[{"strongs":"G3348","hebrew":"μετέχω","translit":"metéchō","english":"lives on"},{"strongs":"G1051","hebrew":"γάλα","translit":"gála","english":"milk"},{"strongs":"G552","hebrew":"ἄπειρος","translit":"ápeiros","english":"not experienced"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G3516","hebrew":"νήπιος","translit":"nḗpios","english":"baby"}]},{"v":14,"text":"But solid food is for those who are full grown, who by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern good and evil.","understanding":"τελείων δέ ἐστιν ἡ στερεὰ τροφή, “But solid food is for those who are full grown” — τέλειος from the perfect family: mature, having reached the goal. Note the pattern: Christ was ‘made perfect’ (τελειόω, v. 9); believers are to be ‘full grown’ (τέλειος); and the next verse calls them on to ‘perfection’ (τελειότης, 6:1). τῶν διὰ τὴν ἕξιν τὰ αἰσθητήρια γεγυμνασμένα ἐχόντων, “who by reason of use have their senses exercised” — γυμνάζω, trained like an athlete (our ‘gymnasium’), by habit. πρὸς διάκρισιν καλοῦ τε καὶ κακοῦ, “to discern good and evil” — καλός from the good family and κακός from the evil family. Maturity is the trained ability to tell them apart.","words":[{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"full grown"},{"strongs":"G4731","hebrew":"στερεός","translit":"stereós","english":"solid"},{"strongs":"G5160","hebrew":"τροφή","translit":"trophḗ","english":"food"},{"strongs":"G1838","hebrew":"ἕξις","translit":"héxis","english":"use"},{"strongs":"G145","hebrew":"αἰσθητήριον","translit":"aisthētḗrion","english":"senses"},{"strongs":"G1128","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","english":"exercised"},{"strongs":"G1253","hebrew":"διάκρισις","translit":"diákrisis","english":"discern"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Garden at Night","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture an olive grove on a hillside outside Jerusalem at night. The city sleeps below. A few men lie asleep under the trees. A short distance away, one Man kneels, then falls on His face on the ground. His shoulders shake; His voice rises in loud cries; His tears fall on the dirt. He is pleading with His Father. And then, through the tears, comes the quiet surrender: ‘Not what I desire, but what you desire.’","meaning":"“He, in the days of his flesh, having offered up prayers and petitions with strong crying and tears to him who was able to save him from death, and having been heard for his godly fear” (v. 7), “though he was a Son, yet learned obedience by the things which he suffered” (v. 8). Your High Priest knows what it is to pray in agony and to obey at great cost. He was heard — not by escaping death, but by being raised from it. Bring your tears to Him."},{"title":"Milk and Solid Food","verseRef":"v. 12-14","image":"Picture a nursery with a baby in a high chair, happily drinking milk. That is beautiful. Then picture the same room years later: a grown man, tall and strong, sitting in the same high chair, still drinking from a bottle, unable to chew a meal, never having learned to feed himself. What was lovely at the beginning has become a tragedy.","meaning":"“For although by this time you should be teachers, you again need to have someone teach you the rudiments of the first principles of the revelations of God. You have come to need milk, and not solid food” (v. 12). Spiritual infancy is normal at first, but it is not meant to last. Growth comes by practice: “who by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern good and evil” (v. 14). Ask the Spirit where you need to move from milk to solid food."},{"title":"The Trained Palate","verseRef":"v. 14","image":"Picture an expert wine taster or a master chef. A beginner can only tell whether something is sweet or sour. The expert can identify each ingredient, notice the faintest note of spoilage, and tell at once whether a dish is safe and good. That skill did not come in a day; it came from thousands of tastings, careful attention and practice.","meaning":"“But solid food is for those who are full grown, who by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern good and evil” (v. 14). Spiritual discernment is a trained skill. It grows as you feed on God's word and practise obeying it, until you can sense quickly what is good and what is harmful. Ask the Spirit to train your senses through daily use."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"What a High Priest Must Be (v. 1-4)","body":"Every high priest is taken from among people to represent them before God and offer gifts and sacrifices for sins; he can deal gently with the ignorant and wayward because he too is weak, must offer for his own sins as well as the people's, and does not take the honour for himself but is called by God, as Aaron was."},{"label":"Christ, the Called and Perfected Priest (v. 5-10)","body":"Christ did not glorify Himself but was appointed by the One who called Him Son and declared Him a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek; in His earthly life He offered prayers and pleas with loud cries and tears to the One able to save Him from death, was heard because of His reverence, learned obedience through suffering, and, having been perfected, became the source of eternal salvation for all who obey Him."},{"label":"The Third Warning: Grow Up (v. 11-14)","body":"There is much to say about Melchizedek, but it is hard to explain because the readers have become sluggish in hearing; though they ought to be teachers by now, they need milk again instead of solid food, which belongs to the mature whose senses are trained by practice to distinguish good from evil."}],"sections":[{"heading":"What Makes a High Priest","paragraphs":["Chapter 5 lays out the qualifications of a high priest. He must be human: “For every high priest, being taken from among men, is appointed for men in things pertaining to God, that he may offer both gifts and sacrifices for sins” (v. 1). He must be compassionate: he “can deal gently with those who are ignorant and going astray, because he himself is also surrounded with weakness” (v. 2). And he must be appointed: “Nobody takes this honor on himself, but he is called by God, just like Aaron was” (v. 4).","Jesus meets every qualification. He is truly human, having shared our flesh and blood (2:14). He can deal gently with the weak, because He knows weakness from the inside (4:15). And He was appointed by God: “So also Christ didn’t glorify himself to be made a high priest” (v. 5). The Father who called Him Son also declared, “You are a priest forever, after the order of Melchizedek” (v. 6).","But Jesus surpasses the Aaronic priests at one crucial point. They had to offer sacrifices “for the people, as well as for himself” (v. 3), because they were sinners. Jesus never needed to. He was “tempted like we are, yet without sin” (4:15). He is a High Priest who fully understands our weakness and yet has none of our guilt."]},{"heading":"Loud Cries and Tears","paragraphs":["Verses 7–8 give one of the most moving pictures of Jesus in Scripture: “He, in the days of his flesh, having offered up prayers and petitions with strong crying and tears to him who was able to save him from death, and having been heard for his godly fear” (v. 7). This is Gethsemane, where Jesus prayed, “Abba, Father, all things are possible to you. Please remove this cup from me. However, not what I desire, but what you desire” (Mark 14:36). Luke adds that “An angel from heaven appeared to him, strengthening him” (Luke 22:43).","Was He heard? He still went to the cross. But He was heard in the deepest way: God did not spare Him from death but saved Him out of death by raising Him. Peter preached that God “raised up, having freed him from the agony of death, because it was not possible that he should be held by it” (Acts 2:24). And the reason He was heard was His εὐλάβεια, His reverent submission — a word this study adds to the godliness family.","“though he was a Son, yet learned obedience by the things which he suffered” (v. 8). Jesus was never disobedient. But as a man He learned by experience the cost of obedience, all the way to death. Paul says He became “obedient to death, yes, the death of the cross” (Philippians 2:8). “Having been made perfect, he became to all of those who obey him the author of eternal salvation” (v. 9). His perfect obedience made Him the source of salvation for all who trust and obey Him."]},{"heading":"From Milk to Solid Food","paragraphs":["The writer wants to explain the deep truth of Christ's Melchizedek priesthood, but he stops short: “About him we have many words to say, and hard to interpret, seeing you have become dull of hearing” (v. 11). The problem is not the subject but the listeners. They have ‘become’ dull: they were not always this way. This is the third warning passage of the letter. Drift (2:1), hardness (3:12) and now dullness are steps on the same downward path.","Their spiritual growth has stalled: “For although by this time you should be teachers, you again need to have someone teach you the rudiments of the first principles of the revelations of God. You have come to need milk, and not solid food” (v. 12). Paul had to tell the Corinthians the same: “I fed you with milk, not with meat; for you weren’t yet ready” (1 Corinthians 3:2). Milk is good for babies; Peter tells new believers to “long for the pure milk of the Word, that with it you may grow” (1 Peter 2:2). But the goal is to grow.","Maturity is defined practically: “But solid food is for those who are full grown, who by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern good and evil” (v. 14). The perfect-family word τέλειος describes believers who have reached the goal of growth. The way there is practice — γυμνάζω, training like an athlete. Paul's goal for the church was “to a full grown man, to the measure of the stature of the fullness of Christ” (Ephesians 4:13), so “that we may no longer be children, tossed back and forth and carried about with every wind of doctrine” (Ephesians 4:14)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Obedient Son and the Growing Church","paragraphs":["Jesus' obedience is central to the gospel. He said, “My food is to do the will of him who sent me, and to accomplish his work” (John 4:34), and “I always do the things that are pleasing to him” (John 8:29). Paul contrasts Adam and Christ: “For as through the one man’s disobedience many were made sinners, even so through the obedience of the one, many will be made righteous” (Romans 5:19). Facing the cross, Jesus said, “Now my soul is troubled. What shall I say? ‘Father, save me from this time?’ But for this cause I came to this time” (John 12:27).","His appointment as priest by God's word is repeated in Hebrews: “You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek” (Hebrews 7:17), and the oath “appoints a Son forever who has been perfected” (Hebrews 7:28). He is the “one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 2:5). And His suffering leaves an example: “Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow his steps” (1 Peter 2:21).","The call to grow appears throughout the New Testament. “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 3:18). Paul longed for believers to “attain to the unity of the faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, to a full grown man” (Ephesians 4:13). Growth is not optional; it is the normal life of those who feed on God's word and practise it."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Deal gently / be moderate in feeling","hebrew":"μετριοπαθέω","translit":"metriopathéō","strongs":"G3356","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “to be moderate in passion, i.e. gentle (to treat indulgently).” It appears only here in the New Testament. Greek philosophers used it for the middle path between cold indifference and uncontrolled emotion. A good priest neither explodes in anger at the wayward nor excuses their sin; he bears with them patiently, because he knows his own weakness. Jesus does this perfectly."},{"english":"Petition / suppliant's plea","hebrew":"ἱκετηρία","translit":"hiketēría","strongs":"G2428","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “intreaty.” It comes from ἱκέτης, a suppliant, and originally referred to the olive branch wrapped in wool that a suppliant held out when begging for mercy. It appears only here in the New Testament, describing Jesus' prayers in Gethsemane. This study adds it to the pray family beside δέησις (a request born of need). Jesus prayed as a suppliant, in agony, yet in perfect trust."},{"english":"Godly fear / reverence","hebrew":"εὐλάβεια","translit":"eulábeia","strongs":"G2124","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “properly, caution, i.e. (religiously) reverence (piety); by implication, dread (concretely).” It describes careful, reverent awe toward God. Jesus was heard because of it (5:7), and believers are to serve God ‘with reverence and awe’ (12:28). English ‘godly’ hides both εὐσεβής-words and this one, so this study adds it to the godliness family."},{"english":"Author / source / cause","hebrew":"αἴτιος","translit":"aítios","strongs":"G159","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “causative, i.e. (concretely) a causer.” It means the one from whom something comes. In Hebrews 5:9 the WEB renders it ‘author’, but it is a different word from ἀρχηγός, also rendered ‘author’ in 2:10 and 12:2. ἀρχηγός is the pioneer who goes first; αἴτιος is the source of salvation. This study tags both in the new author family."},{"english":"Exercise / train","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","strongs":"G1128","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “to practise naked (in the games), i.e. train (figuratively).” Greek athletes trained unclothed in the γυμνάσιον, the root of our ‘gymnasium’. Paul tells Timothy to ‘exercise yourself toward godliness’ with this word. Spiritual discernment is not instant; it is built through repeated practice, like an athlete's strength. Hebrews uses it again of discipline that trains us (12:11)."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you a High Priest who prayed with tears, and calls you to grow up in Him.","items":[{"title":"He Prayed With Tears","body":"Your High Priest knows what it is to plead with God in anguish. The Spirit invites you to bring Him your loud cries and tears without shame. He was heard because of His reverent submission; ask the Spirit to give you the same trust that says, ‘Not my will, but yours.’"},{"title":"Obedience Is Learned","body":"Jesus learned obedience through suffering. The Spirit may be teaching you obedience through hardship too. Rather than resent it, ask Him what He is teaching you, and how this season can deepen your trust and your willingness to follow."},{"title":"Time to Grow","body":"The Spirit is calling you beyond spiritual infancy. Where have you settled for milk — the basics, others' teaching, emotional experiences — when you could be feeding on solid food and training your discernment? Ask Him to stir an appetite for deeper truth and to give you opportunities to practise it."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Gentleness Check","body":"How do you respond to people who are ignorant or straying? Do you lean toward harshness or toward excusing sin? Ask God for the balanced gentleness of verse 2."},{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"When did you last pray with real earnestness about something? Bring one deep burden to God this week with honesty and reverence (v. 7)."},{"title":"The Growth Check","body":"Compared with a few years ago, are you growing in understanding and discernment, or still needing the basics (vv. 12–14)? Write down one step toward solid food."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Solid-Food Reading Plan","body":"Choose one meaty book of the Bible (perhaps Hebrews itself) and study it slowly over the next two months with a good study aid. Read a chapter each week several times, write a summary, list questions, and discuss them with a friend. Aim to be able to explain its main teaching to someone else — moving toward being a teacher (v. 12)."},{"title":"Discernment Training","body":"Each day for a month, choose one decision, message or piece of media you encounter and ask: ‘What is good here, and what is harmful?’ Test it against Scripture. Write a short note. Over time your senses will be trained by practice to discern good and evil (v. 14)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are my great High Priest, taken from among humanity and appointed by the Father. Thank You that You deal gently with me when I am ignorant and wandering, because You understand weakness. You did not glorify Yourself; the Father called You His Son and named You a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek. In the days of Your flesh You prayed with loud cries and tears, and You were heard because of Your reverent submission. Though You were the Son, You learned obedience through what You suffered. Thank You that You were made perfect and became the source of eternal salvation for all who obey You. Help me to obey You. Forgive me for becoming dull of hearing. Stir in me a hunger for solid food. Train my senses by practice to discern good and evil. Help me grow until I can teach others what You have taught me. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Aaron Called to Be High Priest","ref":"Exodus 28:1; Numbers 16-17","relatedVerses":"v. 1-4","summary":"God told Moses to bring his brother Aaron and Aaron's sons near from among the Israelites to serve as priests. Later, Korah and a group of leaders challenged Aaron's right to the priesthood, claiming that the whole community was holy. God judged the rebels, and then confirmed Aaron's calling by causing his staff, among the staffs of all the tribes, to sprout, blossom and produce almonds overnight. Hebrews 5:4 recalls this: ‘Nobody takes this honor on himself, but he is called by God, just like Aaron was.’ Jesus, too, did not take the priesthood for Himself; the Father appointed Him."},{"title":"Jesus in Gethsemane","ref":"Matthew 26:36-46; Luke 22:39-46","relatedVerses":"v. 7-8","summary":"After the Last Supper, Jesus went with His disciples to the Mount of Olives. Taking Peter, James and John further, He began to be deeply distressed. He prayed, “Father, if you are willing, remove this cup from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but yours, be done” (Luke 22:42). An angel came to strengthen Him, and in His agony He prayed even more earnestly. Three times He returned to find the disciples sleeping. Then He rose to face His betrayer. Hebrews 5:7 describes this night: prayers and petitions with loud cries and tears to the One who could save Him from death."},{"title":"Melchizedek Blesses Abraham","ref":"Genesis 14:17-20","relatedVerses":"v. 6, 10","summary":"After Abraham rescued his nephew Lot by defeating four kings, he was met by Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of God Most High. Melchizedek brought out bread and wine and blessed Abraham by God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth. Abraham gave him a tenth of everything. Centuries later, Psalm 110 promised a king who would be ‘a priest forever, after the order of Melchizedek’. Hebrews 5–7 shows that Jesus is that priest: not from Aaron's line, but greater, a king-priest appointed by God's oath."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":6,"chapterTitle":"Press On: The Solemn Warning and the Sure Anchor","themeTitle":"“This Hope We Have as an Anchor of the Soul”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Therefore leaving the teaching of the first principles of Christ, let us press on to perfection—not laying again a foundation of repentance from dead works, of faith toward God,","understanding":"διὸ ἀφέντες τὸν τῆς ἀρχῆς τοῦ Χριστοῦ λόγον, “Therefore leaving the teaching of the first principles of Christ” — not abandoning it, but building on it, as a builder leaves the foundation to raise the walls. λόγος from the word family. ἐπὶ τὴν τελειότητα φερώμεθα, “let us press on to perfection” — τελειότης from the perfect family: maturity, the third word of that family in a few verses (5:9, 5:14). φέρω, ‘be carried along’: let God carry us forward. Then six foundation stones in three pairs. The first pair: μετανοίας ἀπὸ νεκρῶν ἔργων, “a foundation of repentance from dead works” — ἔργον from the work family: works that cannot give life, whether sinful deeds or religious rituals done to earn favour; καὶ πίστεως ἐπὶ θεόν, “of faith toward God.” Turning from and turning to.","words":[{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"leaving"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"first principles"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G5047","hebrew":"τελειότης","translit":"teleiótēs","english":"perfection"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"press on"},{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G3341","hebrew":"μετάνοια","translit":"metánoia","english":"repentance"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":2,"text":"of the teaching of baptisms, of laying on of hands, of resurrection of the dead, and of eternal judgment.","understanding":"The second pair, practices: βαπτισμῶν διδαχῆς, “of the teaching of baptisms” — plural: probably the difference between Jewish ritual washings, John's baptism and Christian baptism; ἐπιθέσεώς τε χειρῶν, “of laying on of hands” — for blessing, commissioning and receiving the Spirit. The third pair, the future: ἀναστάσεως νεκρῶν, “of resurrection of the dead” — ἀνάστασις from the rise family; καὶ κρίματος αἰωνίου, “and of eternal judgment.” These are basic truths every believer should already know.","words":[{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G909","hebrew":"βαπτισμός","translit":"baptismós","english":"baptisms"},{"strongs":"G1936","hebrew":"ἐπίθεσις","translit":"epíthesis","english":"laying on"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"}]},{"v":3,"text":"This will we do, if God permits.","understanding":"καὶ τοῦτο ποιήσομεν, ἐάνπερ ἐπιτρέπῃ ὁ θεός, “This will we do, if God permits.” Growth depends on God's enabling. The phrase also prepares for the solemn warning that follows: there are some for whom God will not permit renewal.","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2010","hebrew":"ἐπιτρέπω","translit":"epitrépō","english":"permits"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For concerning those who were once enlightened and tasted of the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Spirit,","understanding":"The fourth warning, the most severe so far. ἀδύνατον γάρ, “For... it is impossible” — the sentence begins with the word ‘impossible’ for emphasis. Then a description of people who had experienced a great deal: τοὺς ἅπαξ φωτισθέντας, “those who were once enlightened” — φωτίζω, to give light: they understood the gospel. γευσαμένους τε τῆς δωρεᾶς τῆς ἐπουρανίου, “and tasted of the heavenly gift” — γεύομαι, to taste, to experience (the word used of Jesus tasting death in 2:9). καὶ μετόχους γενηθέντας πνεύματος ἁγίου, “and were made partakers of the Holy Spirit” — sharers in the Spirit's work in the community.","words":[{"strongs":"G102","hebrew":"ἀδύνατος","translit":"adýnatos","english":"impossible"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"enlightened"},{"strongs":"G1089","hebrew":"γεύομαι","translit":"geúomai","english":"tasted"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G1431","hebrew":"δωρεά","translit":"dōreá","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G3353","hebrew":"μέτοχος","translit":"métochos","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and tasted the good word of God, and the powers of the age to come,","understanding":"καὶ καλὸν γευσαμένους θεοῦ ῥῆμα, “and tasted the good word of God” — καλός from the good family; ῥῆμα from the word family: the spoken word of God, His promises proclaimed. δυνάμεις τε μέλλοντος αἰῶνος, “and the powers of the age to come” — δύναμις from the power family (plural: miracles) and αἰών from the time family: they had seen the power of the coming age breaking into the present through signs and healings.","words":[{"strongs":"G1089","hebrew":"γεύομαι","translit":"geúomai","english":"tasted"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"powers"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"age"}]},{"v":6,"text":"and then fell away, it is impossible to renew them again to repentance; seeing they crucify the Son of God for themselves again, and put him to open shame.","understanding":"καὶ παραπεσόντας, “and then fell away” — παραπίπτω, to fall aside: not stumbling in sin, but turning away from Christ entirely. πάλιν ἀνακαινίζειν εἰς μετάνοιαν, “it is impossible to renew them again to repentance.” Why? ἀνασταυροῦντας ἑαυτοῖς τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ παραδειγματίζοντας, “seeing they crucify the Son of God for themselves again, and put him to open shame” — by rejecting Him after knowing Him, they side with those who crucified Him. Sincere Christians read this passage differently: some believe true believers can lose their salvation; others that these people experienced much of the Spirit's work without being truly born again (compare v. 9, ‘things that accompany salvation’); others that it is a real warning God uses to keep His people. What the text plainly says is that deliberate, settled rejection of Christ after knowing Him leaves no other way back, and that is why the writer warns so urgently.","words":[{"strongs":"G3895","hebrew":"παραπίπτω","translit":"parapíptō","english":"fell away"},{"strongs":"G340","hebrew":"ἀνακαινίζω","translit":"anakainízō","english":"renew"},{"strongs":"G3341","hebrew":"μετάνοια","translit":"metánoia","english":"repentance"},{"strongs":"G388","hebrew":"ἀνασταυρόω","translit":"anastauróō","english":"crucify"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3856","hebrew":"παραδειγματίζω","translit":"paradeigmatízō","english":"put him to open shame"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For the land which has drunk the rain that comes often on it, and produces a crop suitable for them for whose sake it is also tilled, receives blessing from God;","understanding":"An illustration from farming. γῆ γὰρ ἡ πιοῦσα τὸν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς ἐρχόμενον πολλάκις ὑετόν, “For the land which has drunk the rain that comes often on it” — God's blessings falling again and again. καὶ τίκτουσα βοτάνην εὔθετον ἐκείνοις δι᾽ οὓς καὶ γεωργεῖται, “and produces a crop suitable for them for whose sake it is also tilled,” μεταλαμβάνει εὐλογίας ἀπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ, “receives blessing from God.” Same rain, different results: fruit shows the nature of the soil.","words":[{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"land"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drunk"},{"strongs":"G5205","hebrew":"ὑετός","translit":"hyetós","english":"rain"},{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"produces"},{"strongs":"G1008","hebrew":"βοτάνη","translit":"botánē","english":"crop"},{"strongs":"G2111","hebrew":"εὔθετος","translit":"eúthetos","english":"suitable"},{"strongs":"G1092","hebrew":"γεωργός","translit":"geōrgós","english":"tilled"},{"strongs":"G3335","hebrew":"μεταλαμβάνω","translit":"metalambánō","english":"receives"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"}]},{"v":8,"text":"but if it bears thorns and thistles, it is rejected and near being cursed, whose end is to be burned.","understanding":"ἐκφέρουσα δὲ ἀκάνθας καὶ τριβόλους, “but if it bears thorns and thistles” — the curse-words of Genesis 3. ἀδόκιμος, “it is rejected” — failed the test (as in 1 Timothy and Titus). καὶ κατάρας ἐγγύς, “and near being cursed,” ἧς τὸ τέλος εἰς καῦσιν, “whose end is to be burned.” Farmers burned off weed-choked fields. Jesus used the same picture of fruitless branches.","words":[{"strongs":"G1627","hebrew":"ἐκφέρω","translit":"ekphérō","english":"bears"},{"strongs":"G173","hebrew":"ἄκανθα","translit":"ákantha","english":"thorns"},{"strongs":"G5146","hebrew":"τρίβολος","translit":"tríbolos","english":"thistles"},{"strongs":"G96","hebrew":"ἀδόκιμος","translit":"adókimos","english":"rejected"},{"strongs":"G1451","hebrew":"ἐγγύς","translit":"engýs","english":"near"},{"strongs":"G2671","hebrew":"κατάρα","translit":"katára","english":"cursed"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G2740","hebrew":"καῦσις","translit":"kaûsis","english":"burned"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But, beloved, we are persuaded of better things for you, and things that accompany salvation, even though we speak like this.","understanding":"Now the tone changes completely. πεπείσμεθα δὲ περὶ ὑμῶν, ἀγαπητοί, “But, beloved, we are persuaded of better things for you” — the only time the writer calls them ‘beloved’, right after the sternest warning. κρείττων, ‘better’, the letter's key word. καὶ ἐχόμενα σωτηρίας, “and things that accompany salvation” — things that hold on to salvation, that belong with it. εἰ καὶ οὕτως λαλοῦμεν, “even though we speak like this.” The warning is for their protection, not their condemnation.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"persuaded"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"accompany"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For God is not unrighteous, so as to forget your work and the labor of love which you showed toward his name, in that you served the saints, and still do serve them.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ ἄδικος ὁ θεὸς ἐπιλαθέσθαι, “For God is not unrighteous, so as to forget” — God's justice is pictured as remembering, not forgetting. τοῦ ἔργου ὑμῶν καὶ τοῦ κόπου τῆς ἀγάπης, “your work and the labor of love” — two members of the work family side by side: ἔργον, the work done, and κόπος, the wearying toil it cost (as in 1 Thessalonians 1:3). ἧς ἐνεδείξασθε εἰς τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ, “which you showed toward his name,” διακονήσαντες τοῖς ἁγίοις καὶ διακονοῦντες, “in that you served the saints, and still do serve them.” Their ongoing love for fellow believers was evidence of real faith — fruit, not thorns.","words":[{"strongs":"G94","hebrew":"ἄδικος","translit":"ádikos","english":"unrighteous"},{"strongs":"G1950","hebrew":"ἐπιλανθάνομαι","translit":"epilanthánomai","english":"forget"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G2873","hebrew":"κόπος","translit":"kópos","english":"labor"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1731","hebrew":"ἐνδείκνυμι","translit":"endeíknymi","english":"showed"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"served"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":11,"text":"We desire that each one of you may show the same diligence to the fullness of hope even to the end,","understanding":"ἐπιθυμοῦμεν δὲ ἕκαστον ὑμῶν, “We desire that each one of you” — personal care for each. τὴν αὐτὴν ἐνδείκνυσθαι σπουδὴν πρὸς τὴν πληροφορίαν τῆς ἐλπίδος ἄχρι τέλους, “may show the same diligence to the fullness of hope even to the end” — πληροφορία, full assurance. The same energy they put into serving should go into holding on to hope until the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G1937","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμέω","translit":"epithyméō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G1538","hebrew":"ἕκαστος","translit":"hékastos","english":"each one"},{"strongs":"G4710","hebrew":"σπουδή","translit":"spoudḗ","english":"diligence"},{"strongs":"G4136","hebrew":"πληροφορία","translit":"plērophoría","english":"fullness"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"}]},{"v":12,"text":"that you won’t be sluggish, but imitators of those who through faith and perseverance inherited the promises.","understanding":"ἵνα μὴ νωθροὶ γένησθε, “that you won’t be sluggish” — νωθρός, the same word as ‘dull’ in 5:11, framing the warning section. μιμηταὶ δὲ τῶν διὰ πίστεως καὶ μακροθυμίας κληρονομούντων τὰς ἐπαγγελίας, “but imitators of those who through faith and perseverance inherited the promises” — μακροθυμία, long-tempered patience. The cure for spiritual sluggishness is to follow the examples of faithful, patient believers — a theme chapter 11 will develop.","words":[{"strongs":"G3576","hebrew":"νωθρός","translit":"nōthrós","english":"sluggish"},{"strongs":"G3402","hebrew":"μιμητής","translit":"mimētḗs","english":"imitators"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherited"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promises"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For when God made a promise to Abraham, since he could swear by no one greater, he swore by himself,","understanding":"The first great example: Abraham. τῷ γὰρ Ἀβραὰμ ἐπαγγειλάμενος ὁ θεός, “For when God made a promise to Abraham,” ἐπεὶ κατ᾽ οὐδενὸς εἶχεν μείζονος ὀμόσαι, ὤμοσεν καθ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ, “since he could swear by no one greater, he swore by himself.” God put His own being and name behind His promise.","words":[{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"made a promise"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swear"}]},{"v":14,"text":"saying, “Surely blessing I will bless you, and multiplying I will multiply you.”","understanding":"λέγων, “saying” — the oath after Abraham offered Isaac (Genesis 22): “Surely blessing I will bless you, and multiplying I will multiply you.” The Hebrew way of doubling a verb for emphasis: ‘I will certainly, abundantly bless you.’","words":[{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessing"},{"strongs":"G4129","hebrew":"πληθύνω","translit":"plēthýnō","english":"multiplying"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Thus, having patiently endured, he obtained the promise.","understanding":"καὶ οὕτως μακροθυμήσας ἐπέτυχεν τῆς ἐπαγγελίας, “Thus, having patiently endured, he obtained the promise.” μακροθυμέω, the verb of verse 12's ‘perseverance’. Abraham waited twenty-five years for Isaac, and then was asked to give him up. He held on, and he received what God promised.","words":[{"strongs":"G3114","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","english":"patiently endured"},{"strongs":"G2013","hebrew":"ἐπιτυγχάνω","translit":"epitynchánō","english":"obtained"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For men indeed swear by a greater one, and in every dispute of theirs the oath is final for confirmation.","understanding":"ἄνθρωποι γὰρ κατὰ τοῦ μείζονος ὀμνύουσιν, “For men indeed swear by a greater one,” καὶ πάσης αὐτοῖς ἀντιλογίας πέρας εἰς βεβαίωσιν ὁ ὅρκος, “and in every dispute of theirs the oath is final for confirmation.” Human oaths end arguments, because they call on a higher authority to guarantee them.","words":[{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swear"},{"strongs":"G485","hebrew":"ἀντιλογία","translit":"antilogía","english":"dispute"},{"strongs":"G3727","hebrew":"ὅρκος","translit":"hórkos","english":"oath"},{"strongs":"G4009","hebrew":"πέρας","translit":"péras","english":"final"},{"strongs":"G951","hebrew":"βεβαίωσις","translit":"bebaíōsis","english":"confirmation"}]},{"v":17,"text":"In this way God, being determined to show more abundantly to the heirs of the promise the immutability of his counsel, interposed with an oath;","understanding":"ἐν ᾧ περισσότερον βουλόμενος ὁ θεὸς ἐπιδεῖξαι τοῖς κληρονόμοις τῆς ἐπαγγελίας, “In this way God, being determined to show more abundantly to the heirs of the promise” — the heirs are all believers, Abraham's spiritual children. τὸ ἀμετάθετον τῆς βουλῆς αὐτοῦ, “the immutability of his counsel” — His unchangeable purpose. ἐμεσίτευσεν ὅρκῳ, “interposed with an oath” — μεσιτεύω, to step in as a guarantor. God did not need to swear; He did it for our sake, to make our assurance doubly sure.","words":[{"strongs":"G4054","hebrew":"περισσότερον","translit":"perissóteron","english":"more abundantly"},{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"being determined"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1925","hebrew":"ἐπιδείκνυμι","translit":"epideíknymi","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G2818","hebrew":"κληρονόμος","translit":"klēronómos","english":"heirs"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G276","hebrew":"ἀμετάθετος","translit":"ametáthetos","english":"immutability"},{"strongs":"G1012","hebrew":"βουλή","translit":"boulḗ","english":"counsel"},{"strongs":"G3315","hebrew":"μεσιτεύω","translit":"mesiteúō","english":"interposed"},{"strongs":"G3727","hebrew":"ὅρκος","translit":"hórkos","english":"oath"}]},{"v":18,"text":"that by two immutable things, in which it is impossible for God to lie, we may have a strong encouragement, who have fled for refuge to take hold of the hope set before us.","understanding":"ἵνα διὰ δύο πραγμάτων ἀμεταθέτων, “that by two immutable things” — God's promise and God's oath. ἐν οἷς ἀδύνατον ψεύσασθαι θεόν, “in which it is impossible for God to lie.” Notice the two ‘impossibles’ of this chapter: impossible to renew those who fall away (v. 4), and impossible for God to lie (v. 18). ἰσχυρὰν παράκλησιν ἔχωμεν, “we may have a strong encouragement,” οἱ καταφυγόντες, “who have fled for refuge” — like someone fleeing to the cities of refuge in Israel. κρατῆσαι τῆς προκειμένης ἐλπίδος, “to take hold of the hope set before us” — κρατέω from the hold family: grip it with strength (as in 4:14).","words":[{"strongs":"G276","hebrew":"ἀμετάθετος","translit":"ametáthetos","english":"immutable"},{"strongs":"G4229","hebrew":"πρᾶγμα","translit":"prâgma","english":"things"},{"strongs":"G102","hebrew":"ἀδύνατος","translit":"adýnatos","english":"impossible"},{"strongs":"G5574","hebrew":"ψεύδομαι","translit":"pseúdomai","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"strong"},{"strongs":"G3874","hebrew":"παράκλησις","translit":"paráklēsis","english":"encouragement"},{"strongs":"G2703","hebrew":"καταφεύγω","translit":"katapheúgō","english":"fled for refuge"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"take hold"},{"strongs":"G4295","hebrew":"πρόκειμαι","translit":"prókeimai","english":"set before"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"}]},{"v":19,"text":"This hope we have as an anchor of the soul, a hope both sure and steadfast and entering into that which is within the veil;","understanding":"ἣν ὡς ἄγκυραν ἔχομεν τῆς ψυχῆς, “This hope we have as an anchor of the soul” — ψυχή from the life family: the inner self. In early Christian art the anchor became a symbol of hope. ἀσφαλῆ τε καὶ βεβαίαν, “a hope both sure and steadfast” — it will not slip and will not break. But this anchor is thrown not down into the sea but up into heaven: καὶ εἰσερχομένην εἰς τὸ ἐσώτερον τοῦ καταπετάσματος, “and entering into that which is within the veil” — the Most Holy Place, God's own presence.","words":[{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G45","hebrew":"ἄγκυρα","translit":"ánkyra","english":"anchor"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G804","hebrew":"ἀσφαλής","translit":"asphalḗs","english":"sure"},{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"steadfast"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entering"},{"strongs":"G2082","hebrew":"ἐσώτερος","translit":"esṓteros","english":"within"},{"strongs":"G2665","hebrew":"καταπέτασμα","translit":"katapétasma","english":"veil"}]},{"v":20,"text":"where as a forerunner Jesus entered for us, having become a high priest forever after the order of Melchizedek.","understanding":"ὅπου πρόδρομος ὑπὲρ ἡμῶν εἰσῆλθεν Ἰησοῦς, “where as a forerunner Jesus entered for us” — πρόδρομος, a runner who goes ahead, a scout. The old high priest went in alone and came out again; Jesus went in as a forerunner, so that His people will follow. κατὰ τὴν τάξιν Μελχισέδεκ ἀρχιερεὺς γενόμενος εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα, “having become a high priest forever after the order of Melchizedek” — αἰών from the time family. The writer is finally ready to explain Melchizedek.","words":[{"strongs":"G4274","hebrew":"πρόδρομος","translit":"pródromos","english":"forerunner"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entered"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Same Rain, Different Fields","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture two fields side by side in the same valley. The same clouds pass over both; the same rain falls on both, season after season. One field is green with wheat, rows of grain bending under the weight of harvest. The other is a tangle of thorns and thistles. At harvest time the farmer gathers the grain from the first and sets fire to the second.","meaning":"“For the land which has drunk the rain that comes often on it, and produces a crop suitable for them for whose sake it is also tilled, receives blessing from God” (v. 7); “but if it bears thorns and thistles, it is rejected and near being cursed” (v. 8). Many people receive the same gospel rain — teaching, worship, the Spirit's work in the church. What reveals the heart is the fruit. Ask the Spirit to make your life fruitful soil."},{"title":"Beloved, Better Things","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture a father standing at the edge of a cliff with his children, shouting a sharp warning: ‘Stay back! People have fallen here!’ The children freeze. Then he kneels, puts his arms around them, and says gently, ‘I know you. I've watched you care for your little brother all week. I'm not afraid for you — I just want you safe.’","meaning":"After the sternest warning in the letter, the writer says, “But, beloved, we are persuaded of better things for you, and things that accompany salvation, even though we speak like this” (v. 9). “For God is not unrighteous, so as to forget your work and the labor of love which you showed toward his name” (v. 10). Warnings in Scripture come from love. If you are bearing the fruit of love, take courage; God sees and remembers."},{"title":"The Anchor Thrown Upward","verseRef":"v. 18-20","image":"Picture a small ship caught in a storm near a rocky coast. The waves are huge; the wind drives it toward the rocks. Then a sailor heaves the anchor overboard — but instead of sinking into the sea, it flies upward, through the clouds, into the sky, and lodges firmly in the throne room of heaven. The chain draws taut. The ship still pitches in the storm, but it cannot be driven onto the rocks.","meaning":"“This hope we have as an anchor of the soul, a hope both sure and steadfast and entering into that which is within the veil” (v. 19), “where as a forerunner Jesus entered for us” (v. 20). Your hope is not anchored in your circumstances or feelings, but in Jesus Himself, who has gone into God's presence on your behalf. Storms will come, but you are held."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Press On to Maturity (v. 1-3)","body":"Leaving the elementary teaching about Christ, believers should press on to maturity, not laying again the foundation of repentance from dead works and faith in God, teaching about washings, laying on of hands, the resurrection of the dead and eternal judgement — and this, God permitting, they will do."},{"label":"The Fourth Warning: No Return From Rejection (v. 4-8)","body":"It is impossible to restore to repentance those who have been enlightened, tasted the heavenly gift, shared in the Holy Spirit, tasted God's good word and the powers of the coming age, and then fallen away, because they are crucifying the Son of God again and holding Him up to contempt; land that drinks rain and bears useful crops is blessed, but land that bears thorns and thistles is worthless and ends up burned."},{"label":"Confidence and Encouragement (v. 9-12)","body":"The writer is confident of better things for his beloved readers, things that belong with salvation, because God will not forget their work and love shown in serving the saints; he wants each to show the same diligence to the end and to imitate those who inherit the promises through faith and patience."},{"label":"The Certainty of God's Promise (v. 13-20)","body":"God swore by Himself to Abraham, who patiently waited and received the promise; wanting to show the heirs of promise the unchangeable character of His purpose, God confirmed it with an oath, so that by two unchangeable things in which it is impossible for God to lie, believers who have fled for refuge have strong encouragement to hold fast the hope set before them — a sure and steady anchor that enters behind the curtain, where Jesus has gone as forerunner, a high priest forever after the order of Melchizedek."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Leave the Foundation, Build the House","paragraphs":["Chapter 6 begins with a call to growth: “Therefore leaving the teaching of the first principles of Christ, let us press on to perfection” (v. 1). ‘Leaving’ does not mean abandoning; a builder leaves the foundation by building on it. The foundation is listed in three pairs: repentance and faith (turning from dead works and toward God); washings and laying on of hands (the practices of entry into the community); and resurrection and eternal judgement (the future). Every believer needs these basics, but no one should stay at the foundation forever.","The word ‘perfection’ (τελειότης) belongs to the perfect family, which runs through this section. Christ was ‘made perfect’ (5:9); the mature are ‘full grown’ (5:14); now believers are called on to ‘perfection’ (6:1). The goal of the Christian life is to reach the goal God has set: maturity in Christ. “This will we do, if God permits” (v. 3). Growth is our responsibility, but it is God who makes it possible.","Paul expressed the same confidence: “being confident of this very thing, that he who began a good work in you will complete it until the day of Jesus Christ” (Philippians 1:6). God does not leave the building half-finished."]},{"heading":"The Solemn Warning","paragraphs":["Verses 4–6 contain one of the most debated warnings in the Bible. The writer describes people who “were once enlightened and tasted of the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Spirit” (v. 4), “and tasted the good word of God, and the powers of the age to come” (v. 5), “and then fell away” (v. 6). For them, he says, “it is impossible to renew them again to repentance; seeing they crucify the Son of God for themselves again, and put him to open shame” (v. 6). This is not about a believer who stumbles into sin and repents; it is about a deliberate, public repudiation of Christ.","Christians have understood this passage in different ways, and honesty requires saying so. Some believe it teaches that genuine believers can lose their salvation. Others believe it describes people who experienced a great deal of the Spirit's work in the church without truly being born again, like the seed on rocky ground that “endures for a while” (Matthew 13:21), or those of whom John wrote, “They went out from us, but they didn’t belong to us” (1 John 2:19). Others see it as a real warning that God uses to preserve His people. All agree that the warning is serious and meant to be heeded.","The farming picture of verses 7–8 points the way. The same rain falls on two fields. One produces a crop and receives blessing; the other produces thorns and is burned. The difference is shown by fruit. Jesus said, “By their fruits you will know them” (Matthew 7:16). The writer is not trying to make sincere, fruitful believers doubt; he is warning those who are drifting toward the edge of final rejection."]},{"heading":"Better Things and a Sure Anchor","paragraphs":["Immediately the writer reassures his readers: “But, beloved, we are persuaded of better things for you, and things that accompany salvation, even though we speak like this” (v. 9). Why? Because their lives showed fruit: “For God is not unrighteous, so as to forget your work and the labor of love which you showed toward his name, in that you served the saints, and still do serve them” (v. 10). Two work-family words — work and toil — describe their love in action. Jesus said that what is done for the least of His brothers is done for Him (Matthew 25:40).","Then the writer turns to the ground of all assurance: God's promise. God swore by Himself to Abraham, “Surely blessing I will bless you, and multiplying I will multiply you” (v. 14), “Thus, having patiently endured, he obtained the promise” (v. 15). Paul says Abraham “didn’t waver through unbelief, but grew strong through faith” (Romans 4:20), “being fully assured that what he had promised, he was also able to perform” (Romans 4:21). God's promise and God's oath are “two immutable things, in which it is impossible for God to lie” (v. 18).","That gives believers “a strong encouragement, who have fled for refuge to take hold of the hope set before us” (v. 18). “This hope we have as an anchor of the soul, a hope both sure and steadfast and entering into that which is within the veil” (v. 19), “where as a forerunner Jesus entered for us” (v. 20). The same chapter that contains the sternest warning contains the strongest assurance. Jesus said, “I give eternal life to them. They will never perish, and no one will snatch them out of my hand” (John 10:28). The anchor holds because it is fastened to Him."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Warnings, Fruit and Unbreakable Hope","paragraphs":["The New Testament holds warnings and assurance together. Jesus warned, “If a man doesn’t remain in me, he is thrown out as a branch, and is withered” (John 15:6), and that many who did mighty works in His name will hear, “I never knew you. Depart from me, you who work iniquity” (Matthew 7:23). Peter warned that for some who had known the way, “the last state has become worse for them than the first” (2 Peter 2:20). Simon the magician “believed” and was baptized (Acts 8:13), yet Peter told him, “your heart isn’t right before God” (Acts 8:21).","Yet the same Scriptures give unshakeable assurance to those who belong to Christ. “For I am persuaded that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor powers” (Romans 8:38), “nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing, will be able to separate us from God’s love, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord” (Romans 8:39). “My Father, who has given them to me, is greater than all. No one is able to snatch them out of my Father’s hand” (John 10:29).","And believers share Abraham's inheritance: “If you are Christ’s, then you are Abraham’s offspring and heirs according to promise” (Galatians 3:29). Our hope is “to an incorruptible and undefiled inheritance that doesn’t fade away, reserved in Heaven for you” (1 Peter 1:4). At Jesus' death “the veil of the temple was torn in two from the top to the bottom” (Matthew 27:51): the way into God's presence, where our anchor holds, is open."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Enlighten / give light","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","strongs":"G5461","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to shed rays, i.e. to shine or (transitively) to brighten up (literally or figuratively).” John uses it of the true light that ‘enlightens everyone’. Early Christians sometimes called baptism ‘enlightenment’. In Hebrews 6:4 and 10:32 it describes people who have received the light of the gospel and understood its truth. Light received brings responsibility."},{"english":"Fall away / fall aside","hebrew":"παραπίπτω","translit":"parapíptō","strongs":"G3895","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “to fall aside, i.e. (figuratively) to apostatize.” It appears only here in the New Testament. It is not the ordinary stumbling of believers who sin and repent, but a turning aside from Christ altogether. Its seriousness is shown by the explanation that follows: such people are re-crucifying the Son of God and exposing Him to public shame."},{"english":"Crucify again","hebrew":"ἀνασταυρόω","translit":"anastauróō","strongs":"G388","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “to recrucify (figuratively).” In ordinary Greek ἀνασταυρόω simply meant ‘to crucify’ (to raise up on a cross); the context here gives it the sense ‘crucify again’. Those who reject Christ after knowing Him take the side of those who crucified Him, declaring Him worthy of the cross. Only here in the New Testament."},{"english":"Anchor","hebrew":"ἄγκυρα","translit":"ánkyra","strongs":"G45","verses":"v. 19","insight":"Strong's: “an \"anchor\" (as crooked).” Luke uses it of the anchors dropped from Paul's ship in the storm (Acts 27). In Hebrews 6:19 the anchor is hope, and unusually it is fixed not in the sea floor but in heaven, behind the veil, where Jesus has entered. Early Christians carved anchors in the catacombs as a symbol of this hope."},{"english":"Forerunner / runner ahead","hebrew":"πρόδρομος","translit":"pródromos","strongs":"G4274","verses":"v. 20","insight":"Strong's: “a runner ahead, i.e. scout (figuratively, precursor).” It was used of advance scouts sent ahead of an army, and of the first fruits that ripen ahead of the main harvest. The Old Testament high priest entered God's presence alone and for himself and the people, but did not open the way for others. Jesus entered as a forerunner, so that His people will follow Him in."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"Through this chapter the Holy Spirit calls you to grow, warns you lovingly, and anchors your hope in Christ.","items":[{"title":"Build on the Foundation","body":"The Spirit does not want you to keep relaying the foundation — wondering again and again whether you are saved, or only ever hearing basic truths. He wants to build on it. Ask Him where He is calling you to grow beyond the basics: in understanding, in obedience, in service."},{"title":"Take the Warning Seriously, Not Fearfully","body":"The Spirit's warnings are acts of love. If you have been drifting toward indifference or bitterness against Christ, He is calling you back today. And if you are bearing fruit of love and longing for Christ, receive His reassurance: He is persuaded of better things for you."},{"title":"Anchor Your Hope in Jesus","body":"Storms of doubt, suffering and fear will come. The Spirit wants your hope fastened not to circumstances but to Jesus, who has gone before you into God's presence. When you feel shaken, remember the two unchangeable things: God's promise and God's oath. It is impossible for Him to lie."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Foundation Check","body":"Can you explain the six foundation truths of verses 1–2? Choose one you are unclear on and study it this week, so you can build on it."},{"title":"The Fruit Check","body":"Like the field in verse 7, what fruit is your life producing from the ‘rain’ of God's word and Spirit? Name one evidence of fruit and one area of thorns."},{"title":"The Hope Check","body":"Where is your hope anchored right now — in a job, a relationship, a plan? Write verse 19 on a card and place it where you will see it when you feel anxious."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Serve the Saints","body":"Verse 10 commends believers who ‘served the saints, and still do serve them’. This month, choose one practical, ongoing way to serve other believers: visiting someone who is ill, helping a struggling family, supporting a church ministry. Keep doing it beyond the month. Love in action is evidence of genuine faith."},{"title":"Memorise the Anchor","body":"Memorise Hebrews 6:17–20 over two weeks. Each time you feel insecure in your faith, say it aloud: God's promise and oath are unchangeable; it is impossible for Him to lie; your hope is an anchor fixed where Jesus has entered for you. Share these verses with a believer who is struggling with doubt."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I do not want to stay at the foundation forever. Carry me on to maturity. I thank You for the basics of the faith — repentance from dead works, faith in You, and the hope of resurrection and judgement — and I ask You to build on them in my life. I hear Your solemn warning, and I ask You to keep me from ever turning away from Christ. Let me never crucify Him again by rejecting Him after tasting Your goodness. Make my life a fruitful field that drinks in Your rain and produces a harvest for others. Thank You that You do not forget the work and the labour of love shown in serving Your people. Make me diligent to the end, an imitator of those who through faith and patience inherit Your promises. Thank You that You swore by Yourself, and that it is impossible for You to lie. I have fled to You for refuge. I take hold of the hope set before me — a sure and steady anchor for my soul, fastened behind the veil where Jesus my forerunner has entered for me. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"God's Oath to Abraham After Mount Moriah","ref":"Genesis 22:1-18","relatedVerses":"v. 13-15","summary":"God tested Abraham by telling him to offer Isaac, the son of promise, on a mountain in Moriah. Abraham obeyed, travelled three days, built the altar and raised the knife — then the angel of the LORD stopped him, and Abraham saw a ram caught in a thicket and offered it instead. Then the LORD swore by Himself that because Abraham had not withheld his only son, He would surely bless him and multiply his descendants like the stars of heaven and the sand on the seashore, and that all nations would be blessed through his offspring. Hebrews 6:13–15 recalls this oath: God swore by Himself because there was no one greater, and Abraham, having patiently endured, received the promise."},{"title":"The Parable of the Sower","ref":"Matthew 13:1-23","relatedVerses":"v. 4-8","summary":"Jesus told of a farmer whose seed fell on four kinds of ground. Some fell on the path and birds ate it; some on rocky ground, where it sprang up quickly but withered in the sun; some among thorns, which choked it; and some on good soil, where it produced a great harvest. Explaining it, Jesus said the rocky ground is the one who “hears the word, and immediately with joy receives it” (Matthew 13:20), “yet he has no root in himself, but endures for a while” (Matthew 13:21). Hebrews 6 uses a similar picture: the same rain falls on different ground, and the fruit reveals the reality."},{"title":"The Cities of Refuge","ref":"Numbers 35:9-28; Joshua 20","relatedVerses":"v. 18","summary":"God commanded Israel to set apart six cities of refuge throughout the land. Anyone who killed another person accidentally could flee to one of these cities and be safe from the avenger of blood until his case was judged. Roads to the cities were kept clear, and the gates were open. As long as the fugitive stayed within the city, he was protected. Hebrews 6:18 describes believers as those ‘who have fled for refuge to take hold of the hope set before us’. Christ is our refuge; in Him we are safe from the judgement we deserve."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":7,"chapterTitle":"A Priest Forever After the Order of Melchizedek","themeTitle":"“He Is Able to Save to the Uttermost”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"For this Melchizedek, king of Salem, priest of God Most High, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him,","understanding":"οὗτος γὰρ ὁ Μελχισέδεκ, “For this Melchizedek” — now the writer explains the figure he has mentioned since 5:6. βασιλεὺς Σαλήμ, ἱερεὺς τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ ὑψίστου, “king of Salem, priest of God Most High” — both king and priest, roles kept separate in Israel. Salem is generally identified with Jerusalem. ὁ συναντήσας Ἀβραὰμ ὑποστρέφοντι ἀπὸ τῆς κοπῆς τῶν βασιλέων καὶ εὐλογήσας αὐτόν, “who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him” (the account in Genesis 14).","words":[{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"},{"strongs":"G4532","hebrew":"Σαλήμ","translit":"Salḗm","english":"Salem"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5310","hebrew":"ὕψιστος","translit":"hýpsistos","english":"Most High"},{"strongs":"G4876","hebrew":"συναντάω","translit":"synantáō","english":"met"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G5290","hebrew":"ὑποστρέφω","translit":"hypostréphō","english":"returning"},{"strongs":"G2871","hebrew":"κοπή","translit":"kopḗ","english":"slaughter"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessed"}]},{"v":2,"text":"to whom also Abraham divided a tenth part of all (being first, by interpretation, “king of righteousness”, and then also “king of Salem”, which means “king of peace”;","understanding":"ᾧ καὶ δεκάτην ἀπὸ πάντων ἐμέρισεν Ἀβραάμ, “to whom also Abraham divided a tenth part of all.” Then the meaning of his name and title: ‘Melchizedek’ means “king of righteousness” — δικαιοσύνη; and ‘king of Salem’ means “king of peace” — εἰρήνη, from the Hebrew shalom. Righteousness first, then peace: the order of the gospel.","words":[{"strongs":"G1181","hebrew":"δεκάτη","translit":"dekátē","english":"tenth"},{"strongs":"G3307","hebrew":"μερίζω","translit":"merízō","english":"divided"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G2058","hebrew":"ἑρμηνεία","translit":"hermēneía","english":"interpretation"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G4532","hebrew":"Σαλήμ","translit":"Salḗm","english":"Salem"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":3,"text":"without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but made like the Son of God), remains a priest continually.","understanding":"ἀπάτωρ, ἀμήτωρ, ἀγενεαλόγητος, “without father, without mother, without genealogy” — Genesis records none of these, which was striking because a priest's descent was everything. μήτε ἀρχὴν ἡμερῶν μήτε ζωῆς τέλος ἔχων, “having neither beginning of days nor end of life” — no recorded birth or death. ἀφωμοιωμένος δὲ τῷ υἱῷ τοῦ θεοῦ, “but made like the Son of God” — made to resemble Him. The writer reads the silence of Scripture as a deliberate picture: Melchizedek is a type, shaped to point to the eternal Son. Some think he was an appearance of Christ Himself before His birth; but ‘made like the Son’ suggests a likeness, not identity. μένει ἱερεὺς εἰς τὸ διηνεκές, “remains a priest continually.”","words":[{"strongs":"G540","hebrew":"ἀπάτωρ","translit":"apátōr","english":"without father"},{"strongs":"G282","hebrew":"ἀμήτωρ","translit":"amḗtōr","english":"without mother"},{"strongs":"G35","hebrew":"ἀγενεαλόγητος","translit":"agenealógētos","english":"without genealogy"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"beginning"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G871","hebrew":"ἀφομοιόω","translit":"aphomoióō","english":"made like"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G1336","hebrew":"διηνεκής","translit":"diēnekḗs","english":"continually"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Now consider how great this man was, to whom even Abraham, the patriarch, gave a tenth out of the best plunder.","understanding":"θεωρεῖτε δὲ πηλίκος οὗτος, “Now consider how great this man was” — θεωρέω from the see family: look carefully, contemplate. ᾧ καὶ δεκάτην Ἀβραὰμ ἔδωκεν ἐκ τῶν ἀκροθινίων ὁ πατριάρχης, “to whom even Abraham, the patriarch, gave a tenth out of the best plunder” — ἀκροθίνια, the top of the heap, the choicest spoils. The great father of Israel honoured Melchizedek as his superior.","words":[{"strongs":"G2334","hebrew":"θεωρέω","translit":"theōréō","english":"consider"},{"strongs":"G4080","hebrew":"πηλίκος","translit":"pēlíkos","english":"how great"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G3966","hebrew":"πατριάρχης","translit":"patriárchēs","english":"patriarch"},{"strongs":"G1181","hebrew":"δεκάτη","translit":"dekátē","english":"tenth"}]},{"v":5,"text":"They indeed of the sons of Levi who receive the priest’s office have a commandment to take tithes of the people according to the law, that is, of their brothers, though these have come out of the body of Abraham,","understanding":"καὶ οἱ μὲν ἐκ τῶν υἱῶν Λευῒ τὴν ἱερατείαν λαμβάνοντες, “They indeed of the sons of Levi who receive the priest’s office” — Aaron's line, from the tribe of Levi. ἐντολὴν ἔχουσιν ἀποδεκατοῦν τὸν λαὸν κατὰ τὸν νόμον, “have a commandment to take tithes of the people according to the law,” τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν τοὺς ἀδελφοὺς αὐτῶν, “that is, of their brothers” — they were authorised by law to collect from fellow descendants of Abraham.","words":[{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"sons"},{"strongs":"G3017","hebrew":"Λευΐ","translit":"Leuḯ","english":"Levi"},{"strongs":"G2405","hebrew":"ἱερατεία","translit":"hierateía","english":"priest’s office"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G586","hebrew":"ἀποδεκατόω","translit":"apodekatóō","english":"take tithes"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"}]},{"v":6,"text":"but he whose genealogy is not counted from them has accepted tithes from Abraham, and has blessed him who has the promises.","understanding":"ὁ δὲ μὴ γενεαλογούμενος ἐξ αὐτῶν, “but he whose genealogy is not counted from them” — Melchizedek had no Levitical credentials at all. δεδεκάτωκεν Ἀβραάμ, “has accepted tithes from Abraham,” καὶ τὸν ἔχοντα τὰς ἐπαγγελίας εὐλόγηκεν, “and has blessed him who has the promises.” He received from, and blessed, the very man who carried God's promises.","words":[{"strongs":"G1075","hebrew":"γενεαλογέω","translit":"genealogéō","english":"genealogy"},{"strongs":"G1183","hebrew":"δεκατόω","translit":"dekatóō","english":"accepted tithes"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promises"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But without any dispute the lesser is blessed by the greater.","understanding":"χωρὶς δὲ πάσης ἀντιλογίας, “But without any dispute” — an accepted principle: τὸ ἔλαττον ὑπὸ τοῦ κρείττονος εὐλογεῖται, “the lesser is blessed by the greater.” κρείττων, ‘better’, the letter's key word again. If Melchizedek blessed Abraham, Melchizedek was the greater.","words":[{"strongs":"G485","hebrew":"ἀντιλογία","translit":"antilogía","english":"dispute"},{"strongs":"G1640","hebrew":"ἐλάσσων","translit":"elássōn","english":"lesser"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"greater"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Here people who die receive tithes, but there one receives tithes of whom it is testified that he lives.","understanding":"καὶ ὧδε μὲν δεκάτας ἀποθνῄσκοντες ἄνθρωποι λαμβάνουσιν, “Here people who die receive tithes” — the Levitical priests were mortal. ἐκεῖ δὲ μαρτυρούμενος ὅτι ζῇ, “but there one receives tithes of whom it is testified that he lives” — Scripture records no death for Melchizedek, so he stands as a picture of a priest who lives.","words":[{"strongs":"G1181","hebrew":"δεκάτη","translit":"dekátē","english":"tithes"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"}]},{"v":9,"text":"We can say that through Abraham even Levi, who receives tithes, has paid tithes,","understanding":"καὶ ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν, “We can say” — ‘so to speak’: the writer knows this is a figure of speech. διὰ Ἀβραὰμ καὶ Λευῒς ὁ δεκάτας λαμβάνων δεδεκάτωται, “that through Abraham even Levi, who receives tithes, has paid tithes.”","words":[{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G3017","hebrew":"Λευΐ","translit":"Leuḯ","english":"Levi"},{"strongs":"G1181","hebrew":"δεκάτη","translit":"dekátē","english":"tithes"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receives"},{"strongs":"G1183","hebrew":"δεκατόω","translit":"dekatóō","english":"paid tithes"}]},{"v":10,"text":"for he was yet in the body of his father when Melchizedek met him.","understanding":"ἔτι γὰρ ἐν τῇ ὀσφύϊ τοῦ πατρὸς ἦν, “for he was yet in the body of his father” — literally ‘in the loins’: Levi, Abraham's great-grandson, was not yet born. ὅτε συνήντησεν αὐτῷ Μελχισέδεκ, “when Melchizedek met him.” In the ancient way of thinking, an ancestor acts for his descendants. The Levitical priesthood itself bowed before Melchizedek.","words":[{"strongs":"G3751","hebrew":"ὀσφῦς","translit":"osphŷs","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"father"},{"strongs":"G4876","hebrew":"συναντάω","translit":"synantáō","english":"met"},{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Now if there were perfection through the Levitical priesthood (for under it the people have received the law), what further need was there for another priest to arise after the order of Melchizedek, and not be called after the order of Aaron?","understanding":"εἰ μὲν οὖν τελείωσις διὰ τῆς Λευιτικῆς ἱερωσύνης ἦν, “Now if there were perfection through the Levitical priesthood” — τελείωσις from the perfect family: bringing people to the goal, to full access to God. ὁ λαὸς γὰρ ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς νενομοθέτηται, “(for under it the people have received the law)” — the law and the priesthood stood together. τίς ἔτι χρεία κατὰ τὴν τάξιν Μελχισέδεκ ἕτερον ἀνίστασθαι ἱερέα, “what further need was there for another priest to arise after the order of Melchizedek” — ἕτερος from the another family: another of a different kind, not just one more Levite (ἄλλος). Psalm 110 promised a different priesthood, so the first could not have been final.","words":[{"strongs":"G5050","hebrew":"τελείωσις","translit":"teleíōsis","english":"perfection"},{"strongs":"G3020","hebrew":"Λευϊτικός","translit":"Leuïtikós","english":"Levitical"},{"strongs":"G2420","hebrew":"ἱερωσύνη","translit":"hierōsýnē","english":"priesthood"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G3549","hebrew":"νομοθετέω","translit":"nomothetéō","english":"received the law"},{"strongs":"G2087","hebrew":"ἕτερος","translit":"héteros","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G450","hebrew":"ἀνίστημι","translit":"anístēmi","english":"arise"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G2","hebrew":"Ἀαρών","translit":"Aarṓn","english":"Aaron"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For the priesthood being changed, there is of necessity a change made also in the law.","understanding":"μετατιθεμένης γὰρ τῆς ἱερωσύνης, “For the priesthood being changed,” ἐξ ἀνάγκης καὶ νόμου μετάθεσις γίνεται, “there is of necessity a change made also in the law.” The law of Moses and the Levitical priesthood were bound together; changing one changes the other. This is a radical claim: the Mosaic covenant was temporary.","words":[{"strongs":"G2420","hebrew":"ἱερωσύνη","translit":"hierōsýnē","english":"priesthood"},{"strongs":"G3346","hebrew":"μετατίθημι","translit":"metatíthēmi","english":"changed"},{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"necessity"},{"strongs":"G3331","hebrew":"μετάθεσις","translit":"metáthesis","english":"change"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For he of whom these things are said belongs to another tribe, from which no one has officiated at the altar.","understanding":"ἐφ᾽ ὃν γὰρ λέγεται ταῦτα, “For he of whom these things are said” — Jesus. φυλῆς ἑτέρας μετέσχηκεν, “belongs to another tribe” — ἕτερος again: a different tribe. ἀφ᾽ ἧς οὐδεὶς προσέσχηκεν τῷ θυσιαστηρίῳ, “from which no one has officiated at the altar.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3004","hebrew":"λέγω","translit":"légō","english":"said"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G2087","hebrew":"ἕτερος","translit":"héteros","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G3348","hebrew":"μετέχω","translit":"metéchō","english":"belongs"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For it is evident that our Lord has sprung out of Judah, about which tribe Moses spoke nothing concerning priesthood.","understanding":"πρόδηλον γὰρ ὅτι ἐξ Ἰούδα ἀνατέταλκεν ὁ κύριος ἡμῶν, “For it is evident that our Lord has sprung out of Judah” — ἀνατέλλω, to rise, like the sun or a shoot from the ground. Judah was the royal tribe, the tribe of David. εἰς ἣν φυλὴν περὶ ἱερέων οὐδὲν Μωϋσῆς ἐλάλησεν, “about which tribe Moses spoke nothing concerning priesthood.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4271","hebrew":"πρόδηλος","translit":"pródēlos","english":"evident"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G393","hebrew":"ἀνατέλλω","translit":"anatéllō","english":"sprung"},{"strongs":"G2455","hebrew":"Ἰούδας","translit":"Ioúdas","english":"Judah"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"}]},{"v":15,"text":"This is yet more abundantly evident, if after the likeness of Melchizedek there arises another priest,","understanding":"καὶ περισσότερον ἔτι κατάδηλόν ἐστιν, “This is yet more abundantly evident,” εἰ κατὰ τὴν ὁμοιότητα Μελχισέδεκ ἀνίσταται ἱερεὺς ἕτερος, “if after the likeness of Melchizedek there arises another priest” — ἕτερος a third time in this passage. The priest of Psalm 110 is of a different kind altogether.","words":[{"strongs":"G2612","hebrew":"κατάδηλος","translit":"katádēlos","english":"evident"},{"strongs":"G3665","hebrew":"ὁμοιότης","translit":"homoiótēs","english":"likeness"},{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"},{"strongs":"G450","hebrew":"ἀνίστημι","translit":"anístēmi","english":"arises"},{"strongs":"G2087","hebrew":"ἕτερος","translit":"héteros","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"}]},{"v":16,"text":"who has been made, not after the law of a fleshly commandment, but after the power of an endless life:","understanding":"ὃς οὐ κατὰ νόμον ἐντολῆς σαρκίνης γέγονεν, “who has been made, not after the law of a fleshly commandment” — a rule about physical descent. ἀλλὰ κατὰ δύναμιν ζωῆς ἀκαταλύτου, “but after the power of an endless life.” Two word families meet: δύναμις from the power family and ζωή from the life family. ἀκατάλυτος, indestructible, cannot be dissolved. Jesus' priesthood rests on the power of a life that even death could not destroy.","words":[{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G4559","hebrew":"σαρκικός","translit":"sarkikós","english":"fleshly"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G179","hebrew":"ἀκατάλυτος","translit":"akatálytos","english":"endless"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"}]},{"v":17,"text":"for it is testified, “You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek.”","understanding":"μαρτυρεῖται γάρ, “for it is testified” — the proof text of the chapter, Psalm 110: “You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek.” εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα — αἰών from the time family: ‘forever’ is the word that matters.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G5010","hebrew":"τάξις","translit":"táxis","english":"order"},{"strongs":"G3198","hebrew":"Μελχισεδέκ","translit":"Melchisedék","english":"Melchizedek"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For there is an annulling of a foregoing commandment because of its weakness and uselessness","understanding":"ἀθέτησις μὲν γὰρ γίνεται προαγούσης ἐντολῆς, “For there is an annulling of a foregoing commandment” — ἀθέτησις, setting aside, cancelling (9:26 uses it of putting away sin). διὰ τὸ αὐτῆς ἀσθενὲς καὶ ἀνωφελές, “because of its weakness and uselessness” — not that the law was evil, but that it could not do what was most needed.","words":[{"strongs":"G115","hebrew":"ἀθέτησις","translit":"athétēsis","english":"annulling"},{"strongs":"G4254","hebrew":"προάγω","translit":"proágō","english":"foregoing"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weakness"},{"strongs":"G512","hebrew":"ἀνωφελής","translit":"anōphelḗs","english":"uselessness"}]},{"v":19,"text":"(for the law made nothing perfect), and a bringing in of a better hope, through which we draw near to God.","understanding":"οὐδὲν γὰρ ἐτελείωσεν ὁ νόμος, “(for the law made nothing perfect)” — τελειόω from the perfect family: the law could not bring anyone to the goal of full access to God. ἐπεισαγωγὴ δὲ κρείττονος ἐλπίδος, “and a bringing in of a better hope” — κρείττων, ‘better’. δι᾽ ἧς ἐγγίζομεν τῷ θεῷ, “through which we draw near to God.” That is the goal of all religion — and only Christ achieves it.","words":[{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G1898","hebrew":"ἐπεισαγωγή","translit":"epeisagōgḗ","english":"bringing in"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G1448","hebrew":"ἐγγίζω","translit":"engízō","english":"draw near"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Inasmuch as he was not made priest without the taking of an oath","understanding":"καὶ καθ᾽ ὅσον οὐ χωρὶς ὁρκωμοσίας, “Inasmuch as he was not made priest without the taking of an oath” — the second argument: God's oath.","words":[{"strongs":"G3728","hebrew":"ὁρκωμοσία","translit":"horkōmosía","english":"taking of an oath"}]},{"v":21,"text":"(for they indeed have been made priests without an oath), but he with an oath by him that says of him, “The Lord swore and will not change his mind, ‘You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek.’”","understanding":"οἱ μὲν γὰρ χωρὶς ὁρκωμοσίας εἰσὶν ἱερεῖς γεγονότες, “(for they indeed have been made priests without an oath)” — Aaron's sons became priests by birth and law. But Christ was made priest by oath, and the writer quotes Psalm 110 in full: “The Lord swore and will not change his mind, ‘You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek.’” μεταμέλομαι, to change one's mind, regret. This priesthood will never be revoked.","words":[{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priests"},{"strongs":"G3728","hebrew":"ὁρκωμοσία","translit":"horkōmosía","english":"oath"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swore"},{"strongs":"G3338","hebrew":"μεταμέλλομαι","translit":"metaméllomai","english":"change his mind"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":22,"text":"By so much, Jesus has become the collateral of a better covenant.","understanding":"κατὰ τοσοῦτο κρείττονος διαθήκης γέγονεν ἔγγυος Ἰησοῦς, “By so much, Jesus has become the collateral of a better covenant.” ἔγγυος, a guarantor who pledges himself for another's debt — the only use in the New Testament. Jesus Himself is the pledge that the new covenant will be kept. διαθήκη, ‘covenant’, appears here for the first time in Hebrews; it will dominate chapters 8–10. κρείττων: a better covenant.","words":[{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G1450","hebrew":"ἔγγυος","translit":"éngyos","english":"collateral"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Many, indeed, have been made priests, because they are hindered from continuing by death.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ μὲν πλείονές εἰσιν γεγονότες ἱερεῖς, “Many, indeed, have been made priests” — Jewish historians counted about eighty high priests from Aaron to the fall of the temple. διὰ τὸ θανάτῳ κωλύεσθαι παραμένειν, “because they are hindered from continuing by death.” Each one died and had to be replaced.","words":[{"strongs":"G4119","hebrew":"πλείων","translit":"pleíōn","english":"Many"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priests"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2967","hebrew":"κωλύω","translit":"kōlýō","english":"hindered"},{"strongs":"G3887","hebrew":"παραμένω","translit":"paraménō","english":"continuing"}]},{"v":24,"text":"But he, because he lives forever, has his priesthood unchangeable.","understanding":"ὁ δὲ διὰ τὸ μένειν αὐτὸν εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα, “But he, because he lives forever” — literally ‘remains’: αἰών from the time family. ἀπαράβατον ἔχει τὴν ἱερωσύνην, “has his priesthood unchangeable” — ἀπαράβατος, not passing to another, permanent. He never needs a successor.","words":[{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G531","hebrew":"ἀπαράβατος","translit":"aparábatos","english":"unchangeable"},{"strongs":"G2420","hebrew":"ἱερωσύνη","translit":"hierōsýnē","english":"priesthood"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through him, seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them.","understanding":"ὅθεν καὶ σῴζειν εἰς τὸ παντελὲς δύναται, “Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost” — παντελής, completely, entirely, and also for all time. τοὺς προσερχομένους δι᾽ αὐτοῦ τῷ θεῷ, “those who draw near to God through him.” πάντοτε ζῶν εἰς τὸ ἐντυγχάνειν ὑπὲρ αὐτῶν, “seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them” — ἐντυγχάνω from the pray family: to approach on someone's behalf, to plead. This is the anchor verse of that word in the family. He is not merely alive; He lives to pray for His people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G3838","hebrew":"παντελής","translit":"pantelḗs","english":"uttermost"},{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"draw near"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3842","hebrew":"πάντοτε","translit":"pántote","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G1793","hebrew":"ἐντυγχάνω","translit":"entynchánō","english":"make intercession"}]},{"v":26,"text":"For such a high priest was fitting for us: holy, guiltless, undefiled, separated from sinners, and made higher than the heavens;","understanding":"τοιοῦτος γὰρ ἡμῖν καὶ ἔπρεπεν ἀρχιερεύς, “For such a high priest was fitting for us” — exactly the one we needed. ὅσιος, “holy” — devout toward God; ἄκακος, “guiltless” — without evil, innocent (κακός from the evil family, negated); ἀμίαντος, “undefiled” — unstained; κεχωρισμένος ἀπὸ τῶν ἁμαρτωλῶν, “separated from sinners” — not in fellowship (He was ‘friend of sinners’) but in character and now in position; καὶ ὑψηλότερος τῶν οὐρανῶν γενόμενος, “and made higher than the heavens.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4241","hebrew":"πρέπω","translit":"prépō","english":"fitting"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G3741","hebrew":"ὅσιος","translit":"hósios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G172","hebrew":"ἄκακος","translit":"ákakos","english":"guiltless"},{"strongs":"G283","hebrew":"ἀμίαντος","translit":"amíantos","english":"undefiled"},{"strongs":"G5563","hebrew":"χωρίζω","translit":"chōrízō","english":"separated"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinners"},{"strongs":"G5308","hebrew":"ὑψηλός","translit":"hypsēlós","english":"higher"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"}]},{"v":27,"text":"who doesn’t need, like those high priests, to offer up sacrifices daily, first for his own sins, and then for the sins of the people. For he did this once for all, when he offered up himself.","understanding":"ὃς οὐκ ἔχει καθ᾽ ἡμέραν ἀνάγκην, “who doesn’t need... daily” — ὥσπερ οἱ ἀρχιερεῖς, πρότερον ὑπὲρ τῶν ἰδίων ἁμαρτιῶν θυσίας ἀναφέρειν, “like those high priests, to offer up sacrifices daily, first for his own sins.” τοῦτο γὰρ ἐποίησεν ἐφάπαξ ἑαυτὸν ἀνενέγκας, “For he did this once for all, when he offered up himself.” ἐφάπαξ, once for all — a key word of chapters 7–10. ἀναφέρω, added to the offering family: to carry up onto the altar. Christ is both priest and sacrifice: He offered Himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"daily"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priests"},{"strongs":"G399","hebrew":"ἀναφέρω","translit":"anaphérō","english":"offer up"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2178","hebrew":"ἐφάπαξ","translit":"ephápax","english":"once for all"},{"strongs":"G1438","hebrew":"ἑαυτοῦ","translit":"heautoû","english":"himself"}]},{"v":28,"text":"For the law appoints men as high priests who have weakness, but the word of the oath which came after the law appoints a Son forever who has been perfected.","understanding":"ὁ νόμος γὰρ ἀνθρώπους καθίστησιν ἀρχιερεῖς ἔχοντας ἀσθένειαν, “For the law appoints men as high priests who have weakness,” ὁ λόγος δὲ τῆς ὁρκωμοσίας τῆς μετὰ τὸν νόμον, “but the word of the oath which came after the law” — Psalm 110 was written centuries after Moses, so it supersedes the law's arrangement. υἱὸν εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα τετελειωμένον, “appoints a Son forever who has been perfected” — τελειόω, the perfect family's verb, for the third time in this chapter. Weak men versus the perfected Son.","words":[{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G2525","hebrew":"καθίστημι","translit":"kathístēmi","english":"appoints"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priests"},{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"weakness"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G3728","hebrew":"ὁρκωμοσία","translit":"horkōmosía","english":"oath"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfected"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Bread, Wine and a Blessing","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture Abraham returning from battle, weary and dusty, leading his men and the rescued captives. In a valley near the city of Salem, a king comes out to meet him — not with an army, but with bread and wine. He is also a priest of God Most High. He lifts his hands and blesses Abraham, and Abraham bows and gives him a tenth of everything. Then the king-priest disappears from the story as suddenly as he came, with no record of his birth or death.","meaning":"“For this Melchizedek, king of Salem, priest of God Most High, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him” (v. 1) was “made like the Son of God” (v. 3). Melchizedek is a picture of Jesus: King of righteousness and King of peace, priest without a recorded beginning or end, who brings bread and wine and blesses God's people. Let this ancient scene lift your eyes to your eternal King-Priest."},{"title":"A Line of Graves","verseRef":"v. 23-25","image":"Picture a long row of tombs in an ancient cemetery outside Jerusalem. Each bears the name of a high priest: Aaron, Eleazar, Phinehas, and on and on — dozens of them. Each served faithfully for a while, then died and was replaced. Now picture, at the end of the row, an empty tomb, and beyond it a throne where a Priest sits who will never need a successor, because He lives forever.","meaning":"“Many, indeed, have been made priests, because they are hindered from continuing by death” (v. 23). “But he, because he lives forever, has his priesthood unchangeable” (v. 24). “Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through him, seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them” (v. 25). Your High Priest never dies, never retires and never stops praying for you."},{"title":"Once for All","verseRef":"v. 27","image":"Picture the temple altar at dawn. Smoke rises from the morning sacrifice. Priests are already preparing the evening one. Tomorrow it will happen again, and the next day, and the next — for centuries. The blood never stops flowing; the work is never done. Then picture a hill outside the city where one Man hangs on a cross, offering not an animal but Himself. When He cries, ‘It is finished,’ the altar fire is no longer needed.","meaning":"“who doesn’t need, like those high priests, to offer up sacrifices daily, first for his own sins, and then for the sins of the people. For he did this once for all, when he offered up himself” (v. 27). There is nothing to add to Christ's sacrifice. You do not need to repeat it or supplement it. Rest in what He did once for all."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Melchizedek, King and Priest (v. 1-3)","body":"Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of God Most High, met Abraham after the defeat of the kings, blessed him and received a tenth; his name means king of righteousness and his title king of peace, and with no recorded parents, genealogy, birth or death, he resembles the Son of God and remains a priest perpetually."},{"label":"Greater Than Levi (v. 4-10)","body":"Melchizedek's greatness is shown in that Abraham the patriarch gave him a tenth and was blessed by him, and the lesser is blessed by the greater; the Levitical priests, who are mortal and descended from Abraham, collect tithes, but in a sense Levi himself paid tithes to Melchizedek through Abraham."},{"label":"A New Priesthood Means a New Law (v. 11-19)","body":"If perfection had come through the Levitical priesthood, there would be no need for another priest of Melchizedek's order; the change of priesthood requires a change of law, since Jesus comes from Judah, not Levi, and is a priest by the power of an indestructible life; the former commandment is set aside because it was weak and could perfect nothing, and a better hope is introduced, through which we draw near to God."},{"label":"A Permanent, Perfect High Priest (v. 20-28)","body":"Jesus was made priest with God's oath, becoming the guarantor of a better covenant; unlike the many priests who died, He lives forever and holds His priesthood permanently, so He can save completely those who come to God through Him, always living to intercede; He is holy, innocent, undefiled and exalted, and offered Himself once for all, a Son made perfect forever."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Mysterious King-Priest","paragraphs":["Melchizedek appears in only a few verses of Genesis 14, yet he fascinated later readers. He was “king of Salem, priest of God Most High” (v. 1). His name means “king of righteousness” (v. 2), and his title means “king of peace” (v. 2). Long before the law of Moses or the priesthood of Aaron, he was a priest of the true God and a king at the same time — something that could never happen under the law, where kings came from Judah and priests from Levi.","The writer notices what Genesis does not say. It gives Melchizedek no genealogy, no birth and no death. For a priest, whose right to serve depended on descent, that silence is remarkable. So the writer describes him as “without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but made like the Son of God” (v. 3). He is a living picture, shaped by the way Scripture tells his story, of the eternal Son who would be a priest forever.","Melchizedek's greatness is shown in his meeting with Abraham. Abraham gave him a tenth of the best spoils, and Melchizedek blessed Abraham. “But without any dispute the lesser is blessed by the greater” (v. 7). Even Levi, Abraham's great-grandson and ancestor of the priests, “was yet in the body of his father when Melchizedek met him” (v. 10). The whole Levitical priesthood, in its forefather, honoured a greater priest."]},{"heading":"Why a New Priesthood?","paragraphs":["The writer's argument turns on Psalm 110: “You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek” (v. 17). If the Levitical priesthood had been able to bring people to God completely, why would God, centuries later, promise a different priest? “Now if there were perfection through the Levitical priesthood... what further need was there for another priest to arise after the order of Melchizedek” (v. 11)? The word ‘another’ is ἕτερος, another of a different kind. The old priesthood was not the final word.","And a new priesthood means a new arrangement of law: “For the priesthood being changed, there is of necessity a change made also in the law” (v. 12). Jesus did not come from Levi but from Judah (v. 14), so under the law of Moses He could not be a priest. His priesthood rests on something greater: He was made a priest “not after the law of a fleshly commandment, but after the power of an endless life” (v. 16). The resurrection is the foundation of His priesthood.","The law had value, but it had a limit: “for the law made nothing perfect” (v. 19). It could show sin and point to the remedy, but it could not bring anyone fully into God's presence. Paul says, “For what the law couldn’t do, in that it was weak through the flesh, God did, sending his own Son” (Romans 8:3). Now there is “a bringing in of a better hope, through which we draw near to God” (v. 19). Three times in this chapter the perfect-family words appear (vv. 11, 19, 28): the law could not bring to the goal, but the perfected Son does."]},{"heading":"Able to Save to the Uttermost","paragraphs":["Jesus' priesthood was confirmed by God's oath: “The Lord swore and will not change his mind” (v. 21). That makes Him “the collateral of a better covenant” (v. 22): the guarantor who personally pledges that the covenant will stand.","And He never dies. The old priests were many, “because they are hindered from continuing by death” (v. 23). “But he, because he lives forever, has his priesthood unchangeable” (v. 24). “Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through him, seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them” (v. 25). ‘To the uttermost’ means completely and for all time. Jesus told Peter, “but I prayed for you, that your faith wouldn’t fail” (Luke 22:32). He is still praying for His people.","He is exactly the High Priest we need: “holy, guiltless, undefiled, separated from sinners, and made higher than the heavens” (v. 26). Unlike the old priests, He did not need to sacrifice for His own sins, “For he did this once for all, when he offered up himself” (v. 27). Peter writes, “Because Christ also suffered for sins once, the righteous for the unrighteous, that he might bring you to God” (1 Peter 3:18). The law appointed weak men; God's oath appointed “a Son forever who has been perfected” (v. 28)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Interceding, Once-for-All High Priest","paragraphs":["Jesus' ongoing intercession is a great comfort of the New Testament. “It is Christ who died, yes rather, who was raised from the dead, who is at the right hand of God, who also makes intercession for us” (Romans 8:34). In His prayer before the cross He said, “Not for these only do I pray, but for those also who will believe in me through their word” (John 17:20). And John writes, “If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father, Jesus Christ, the righteous” (1 John 2:1).","His sinlessness qualifies Him as priest and sacrifice. He challenged His opponents, “Which of you convicts me of sin?” (John 8:46). Peter says He “did not sin” (1 Peter 2:22). And Paul writes, “For him who knew no sin he made to be sin on our behalf; so that in him we might become the righteousness of God” (2 Corinthians 5:21). His death was once for all: “For the death that he died, he died to sin one time” (Romans 6:10).","Jesus is the guarantor of the new covenant He instituted at the Last Supper: “This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you” (Luke 22:20). Through Him “everyone who believes is justified from all things, from which you could not be justified by the law of Moses” (Acts 13:39). And He came from Judah, as the elder in heaven announced: “Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has overcome” (Revelation 5:5)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Made like / closely resembling","hebrew":"ἀφομοιόω","translit":"aphomoióō","strongs":"G871","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “to assimilate closely.” It appears only here in the New Testament. Melchizedek was ‘made like’ the Son of God — shaped, in the way Scripture records him, to resemble Christ. The Son is the original; Melchizedek is the likeness. This is why the writer can use his story to teach about Christ's eternal priesthood without claiming that Melchizedek literally had no parents."},{"english":"Endless / indestructible","hebrew":"ἀκατάλυτος","translit":"akatálytos","strongs":"G179","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “indissoluble, i.e. (figuratively) permanent.” It comes from καταλύω, to dissolve or destroy (used of tearing down the temple). Jesus' life could not be dissolved, even by death. His priesthood does not rest on a legal pedigree but on the power of a life that rose from the grave and cannot die again."},{"english":"Guarantor / collateral / surety","hebrew":"ἔγγυος","translit":"éngyos","strongs":"G1450","verses":"v. 22","insight":"Strong's: “pledged (as if articulated by a member), i.e. a bondsman.” In legal documents of the time it described someone who guaranteed a loan or contract with his own property or person. Only here in the New Testament. Jesus does not merely announce the new covenant; He personally guarantees it. His own life is the security that God's promises will be kept."},{"english":"Uttermost / completely / forever","hebrew":"παντελής","translit":"pantelḗs","strongs":"G3838","verses":"v. 25","insight":"Strong's: “full-ended, i.e. entire (neuter as noun, completion).” The phrase εἰς τὸ παντελές can mean ‘completely’ or ‘for all time’, and both fit here. It is used in Luke 13:11 of a woman who could not ‘fully’ straighten up until Jesus healed her. Jesus saves fully and forever: no sin too deep, no need too great, no time too long."},{"english":"Once for all","hebrew":"ἐφάπαξ","translit":"ephápax","strongs":"G2178","verses":"v. 27","insight":"Strong's: “upon one occasion (only).” It is a key word of Hebrews 7–10, used of Christ's offering at 7:27, 9:12 and 10:10, and Paul uses it of Christ's death in Romans 6:10. It contrasts Christ's single, sufficient sacrifice with the repeated sacrifices of the old priests. Because His offering was once for all, it never needs to be repeated, and nothing can be added to it."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you a High Priest who never dies, never fails and never stops praying for you.","items":[{"title":"Righteousness, Then Peace","body":"Melchizedek was king of righteousness, then king of peace. The Spirit wants you to know that true peace comes only after God has dealt with sin. Ask Him to show you whether you are seeking peace while avoiding righteousness, and to lead you to the peace that comes through Christ's righteousness."},{"title":"Saved to the Uttermost","body":"No situation is beyond Jesus' power to save. The Spirit wants you to believe that Jesus can save you completely — every sin, every bondage, every part of your life — and keep you forever. Bring Him the area you think is too far gone, and trust His complete salvation."},{"title":"He Is Praying for You Now","body":"Jesus lives to intercede for you. The Spirit reminds you that when you are weak, tempted or failing, your High Priest is praying for you. Let this give you courage to keep going and to draw near to God through Him."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Access Check","body":"Are you relying on anything besides Jesus to draw near to God — rituals, good deeds, a pastor? Write down verse 19 and verse 25, and draw near through Him alone."},{"title":"The Generosity Check","body":"Abraham gave a tenth of the best to Melchizedek in gratitude. How does your giving reflect gratitude to God? Consider one step in generous, grateful giving (vv. 2, 4)."},{"title":"The Assurance Check","body":"Do you live as though your salvation depends on your own strength to hold on? Meditate on verse 25 and thank Jesus that He holds on to you through His intercession."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Pray With Your Interceding Priest","body":"Jesus lives to intercede for His people. For the next two weeks, as you pray for others, imagine yourself joining Jesus' prayers for them. Begin each prayer time by reading Hebrews 7:25, then pray for your family, church and friends, confident that your High Priest is praying too."},{"title":"Once-for-All Thanksgiving","body":"Write a list of the sins, failures and burdens you have carried. Next to each, write ‘once for all’ (v. 27). Then thank Jesus aloud that His single sacrifice covers each one completely. Destroy the list as a sign that nothing needs to be added to His work."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are my great High Priest after the order of Melchizedek — King of righteousness and King of peace. Like Abraham, I honour You and give You the best of what I have. You were not made priest by human descent, but by the power of an indestructible life. The law could make nothing perfect, but You have brought in a better hope through which I draw near to God. Thank You that You were made priest with God's oath, and that You are the guarantor of a better covenant. The old priests died one after another, but You live forever, and Your priesthood never passes to another. Thank You that You are able to save me completely, to the uttermost, because You always live to make intercession for me. You are holy, innocent, undefiled, separated from sinners and exalted above the heavens. You do not need to offer sacrifices daily, because You offered Yourself once for all. I rest in Your finished work and in Your unending prayers. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Melchizedek Meets Abraham","ref":"Genesis 14:1-24","relatedVerses":"v. 1-10","summary":"Four kings from the east invaded the region of Sodom, defeated its kings and carried off Abraham's nephew Lot. Abraham gathered 318 trained men, pursued the invaders by night, defeated them and recovered all the people and goods. On his way back, Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of God Most High, came out with bread and wine and blessed Abraham by God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth, who had delivered his enemies into his hand. Abraham gave him a tenth of everything. Then Abraham refused any reward from the king of Sodom. Hebrews 7 builds its whole argument on these few verses."},{"title":"Peter's Failure and Jesus' Prayer","ref":"Luke 22:31-34, 54-62","relatedVerses":"v. 25","summary":"At the Last Supper Jesus told Peter that Satan had asked to sift the disciples like wheat, but added, “but I prayed for you, that your faith wouldn’t fail. You, when once you have turned again, establish your brothers” (Luke 22:32). That night Peter denied Jesus three times, then went out and wept bitterly. But his faith did not finally fail. After the resurrection Jesus restored him, and Peter became a leader of the church. Peter's story shows what it means that Jesus ‘lives forever to make intercession’ for His people: even when we fall, His prayers hold us."},{"title":"The Last Supper and the New Covenant","ref":"Luke 22:14-20","relatedVerses":"v. 22, 27","summary":"On the night He was betrayed, Jesus shared the Passover meal with His disciples. He took bread, gave thanks, broke it and said it was His body given for them. Then He took the cup after supper, saying, “This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you” (Luke 22:20). Like Melchizedek, He offered bread and wine. Unlike any other priest, He was about to offer Himself once for all. Hebrews 7:22 calls Him the guarantor of this better covenant."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":8,"chapterTitle":"The True Tabernacle and the New Covenant","themeTitle":"“I Will Put My Laws Into Their Mind”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now in the things which we are saying, the main point is this. We have such a high priest, who sat down on the right hand of the throne of the Majesty in the heavens,","understanding":"κεφάλαιον δὲ ἐπὶ τοῖς λεγομένοις, “Now in the things which we are saying, the main point is this.” κεφάλαιον, the headline, the sum of the argument. τοιοῦτον ἔχομεν ἀρχιερέα, “We have such a high priest” — not ‘we used to have’ or ‘we will have’, but now. ὃς ἐκάθισεν ἐν δεξιᾷ τοῦ θρόνου τῆς μεγαλωσύνης ἐν τοῖς οὐρανοῖς, “who sat down on the right hand of the throne of the Majesty in the heavens.” He is seated: His sacrifice is complete. He is at the right hand: He has the place of honour and power.","words":[{"strongs":"G2774","hebrew":"κεφάλαιον","translit":"kephálaion","english":"main point"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G2523","hebrew":"καθίζω","translit":"kathízō","english":"sat down"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G3172","hebrew":"μεγαλωσύνη","translit":"megalōsýnē","english":"Majesty"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"}]},{"v":2,"text":"a servant of the sanctuary, and of the true tabernacle, which the Lord pitched, not man.","understanding":"τῶν ἁγίων λειτουργός, “a servant of the sanctuary” — λειτουργός from the servant family: one who carries out priestly, public service (the angels were ‘servants’ in 1:7; now the enthroned Christ serves as priest). καὶ τῆς σκηνῆς τῆς ἀληθινῆς, “and of the true tabernacle” — ἀληθινός, genuine, real, as opposed to a copy. ἣν ἔπηξεν ὁ κύριος, οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, “which the Lord pitched, not man” — πήγνυμι, to drive in the tent pegs. Moses' tent was made by craftsmen; the true one by God.","words":[{"strongs":"G39","hebrew":"ἅγιον","translit":"hágion","english":"sanctuary"},{"strongs":"G3011","hebrew":"λειτουργός","translit":"leitourgós","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G4078","hebrew":"πήγνυμι","translit":"pḗgnymi","english":"pitched"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For every high priest is appointed to offer both gifts and sacrifices. Therefore it is necessary that this high priest also have something to offer.","understanding":"πᾶς γὰρ ἀρχιερεὺς εἰς τὸ προσφέρειν δῶρά τε καὶ θυσίας καθίσταται, “For every high priest is appointed to offer both gifts and sacrifices” — three offering-family words again: προσφέρω, δῶρον, θυσία. ὅθεν ἀναγκαῖον ἔχειν τι καὶ τοῦτον ὃ προσενέγκῃ, “Therefore it is necessary that this high priest also have something to offer.” The writer will reveal what it is: Himself (9:14).","words":[{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offer"},{"strongs":"G1435","hebrew":"δῶρον","translit":"dōron","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G2525","hebrew":"καθίστημι","translit":"kathístēmi","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G316","hebrew":"ἀναγκαῖος","translit":"anankaîos","english":"necessary"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For if he were on earth, he would not be a priest at all, seeing there are priests who offer the gifts according to the law;","understanding":"εἰ μὲν οὖν ἦν ἐπὶ γῆς, οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἦν ἱερεύς, “For if he were on earth, he would not be a priest at all” — because He was from Judah, not Levi (7:14). ὄντων τῶν προσφερόντων κατὰ νόμον τὰ δῶρα, “seeing there are priests who offer the gifts according to the law” — the present tense suggests the temple was still standing when Hebrews was written, before AD 70.","words":[{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offer"},{"strongs":"G1435","hebrew":"δῶρον","translit":"dōron","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":5,"text":"who serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things, even as Moses was warned by God when he was about to make the tabernacle, for he said, “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain.”","understanding":"οἵτινες ὑποδείγματι καὶ σκιᾷ λατρεύουσιν τῶν ἐπουρανίων, “who serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things.” Three words of the new pattern family meet in this verse: ὑπόδειγμα, a copy or sketch; σκιά, a shadow cast by something real; and, in the quotation, τύπος, the original pattern. λατρεύω, added to the servant family: to serve in worship. Moses was told, as Exodus 25 records: “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain.” The earthly tabernacle was a careful copy of a heavenly reality.","words":[{"strongs":"G5262","hebrew":"ὑπόδειγμα","translit":"hypódeigma","english":"copy"},{"strongs":"G4639","hebrew":"σκιά","translit":"skiá","english":"shadow"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G5537","hebrew":"χρηματίζω","translit":"chrēmatízō","english":"warned by God"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"See"},{"strongs":"G5179","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","english":"pattern"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"shown"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But now he has obtained a more excellent ministry, by so much as he is also the mediator of a better covenant, which on better promises has been given as law.","understanding":"νυνὶ δὲ διαφορωτέρας τέτυχεν λειτουργίας, “But now he has obtained a more excellent ministry” — λειτουργία, priestly service (our ‘liturgy’). ὅσῳ καὶ κρείττονός ἐστιν διαθήκης μεσίτης, “by so much as he is also the mediator of a better covenant” — μεσίτης, the go-between who brings two parties together. ἥτις ἐπὶ κρείττοσιν ἐπαγγελίαις νενομοθέτηται, “which on better promises has been given as law.” κρείττων twice: better covenant, better promises.","words":[{"strongs":"G1313","hebrew":"διάφορος","translit":"diáphoros","english":"more excellent"},{"strongs":"G5177","hebrew":"τυγχάνω","translit":"tynchánō","english":"obtained"},{"strongs":"G3009","hebrew":"λειτουργία","translit":"leitourgía","english":"ministry"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G3316","hebrew":"μεσίτης","translit":"mesítēs","english":"mediator"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promises"},{"strongs":"G3549","hebrew":"νομοθετέω","translit":"nomothetéō","english":"given as law"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For if that first covenant had been faultless, then no place would have been sought for a second.","understanding":"εἰ γὰρ ἡ πρώτη ἐκείνη ἦν ἄμεμπτος, “For if that first covenant had been faultless,” οὐκ ἂν δευτέρας ἐζητεῖτο τόπος, “then no place would have been sought for a second.” The same logic as 7:11: God promising something new shows that the old was not final.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G273","hebrew":"ἄμεμπτος","translit":"ámemptos","english":"faultless"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"sought"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For finding fault with them, he said, “Behold, the days come”, says the Lord, “that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah;","understanding":"μεμφόμενος γὰρ αὐτοῖς λέγει, “For finding fault with them, he said” — the fault lay with the people (‘them’), not with God's law. Then the longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament, from Jeremiah 31: “Behold, the days come”, says the Lord, “that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah.” καινός from the new family: new in kind, not merely recent. συντελέω, to bring to completion.","words":[{"strongs":"G3201","hebrew":"μέμφομαι","translit":"mémphomai","english":"finding fault"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4931","hebrew":"συντελέω","translit":"synteléō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2474","hebrew":"Ἰσραήλ","translit":"Israḗl","english":"Israel"},{"strongs":"G2455","hebrew":"Ἰούδας","translit":"Ioúdas","english":"Judah"}]},{"v":9,"text":"not according to the covenant that I made with their fathers, in the day that I took them by the hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt; for they didn’t continue in my covenant, and I disregarded them,” says the Lord.","understanding":"“not according to the covenant that I made with their fathers, in the day that I took them by the hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt” — a tender picture: God leading Israel like a father leading a child. ὅτι αὐτοὶ οὐκ ἐνέμειναν ἐν τῇ διαθήκῃ μου, “for they didn’t continue in my covenant” — the problem was persistence. κἀγὼ ἠμέλησα αὐτῶν, “and I disregarded them” — ἀμελέω, the very word used in 2:3 for those who ‘neglect so great a salvation’. Those who neglect God find themselves neglected.","words":[{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"made"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G1949","hebrew":"ἐπιλαμβάνομαι","translit":"epilambánomai","english":"took them"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G1806","hebrew":"ἐξάγω","translit":"exágō","english":"lead them out"},{"strongs":"G125","hebrew":"Αἴγυπτος","translit":"Aígyptos","english":"Egypt"},{"strongs":"G1696","hebrew":"ἐμμένω","translit":"emménō","english":"continue"},{"strongs":"G272","hebrew":"ἀμελέω","translit":"ameléō","english":"disregarded"}]},{"v":10,"text":"“For this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel. After those days,” says the Lord; “I will put my laws into their mind, I will also write them on their heart. I will be their God, and they will be my people.","understanding":"The heart of the new covenant: “For this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel. After those days,” says the Lord; “I will put my laws into their mind, I will also write them on their heart.” The old law was written on stone tablets outside the people; the new is written inside, in the mind (διάνοια) and heart (καρδία). God does not lower His standard; He changes the person. “I will be their God, and they will be my people” — the covenant promise from Exodus onward, now fully realised.","words":[{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G1303","hebrew":"διατίθεμαι","translit":"diatíthemai","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2474","hebrew":"Ἰσραήλ","translit":"Israḗl","english":"Israel"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"put"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"laws"},{"strongs":"G1271","hebrew":"διάνοια","translit":"diánoia","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G1924","hebrew":"ἐπιγράφω","translit":"epigráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":11,"text":"They will not teach every man his fellow citizen, and every man his brother, saying, ‘Know the Lord,’ for all will know me, from their least to their greatest.","understanding":"Two members of the know family in one verse: “They will not teach every man his fellow citizen, and every man his brother, saying, ‘Know the Lord,’” — γνῶθι, from γινώσκω: come to know, learn by experience. “for all will know me, from their least to their greatest” — εἰδήσουσιν, from οἶδα: know with settled, inward certainty. Under the old covenant, knowing God was mediated by priests and teachers; in the new, every believer, from the least to the greatest, knows God personally.","words":[{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teach"},{"strongs":"G4177","hebrew":"πολίτης","translit":"polítēs","english":"fellow citizen"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"Know"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"least"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"greatest"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness. I will remember their sins and lawless deeds no more.”","understanding":"And the foundation of it all: “For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness. I will remember their sins and lawless deeds no more.” ἵλεως, gracious, propitious — related to the atonement word of 2:17. Three members of the sin family: ἀδικία, ‘unrighteousness’ — wrong done to others; ἁμαρτία, ‘sins’ — missing the mark; ἀνομία, ‘lawless deeds’ — rebellion against God's law. (The words ‘and lawless deeds’ are in the manuscripts the WEB follows; many older ones omit them.) οὐ μὴ μνησθῶ ἔτι — a double negative: ‘I will certainly never remember’. God does not forget as we forget; He chooses never to bring them up against us again.","words":[{"strongs":"G2436","hebrew":"ἵλεως","translit":"híleōs","english":"merciful"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"unrighteousness"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G458","hebrew":"ἀνομία","translit":"anomía","english":"lawless deeds"},{"strongs":"G3415","hebrew":"μνάομαι","translit":"mnáomai","english":"remember"}]},{"v":13,"text":"In that he says, “A new covenant”, he has made the first old. But that which is becoming old and grows aged is near to vanishing away.","understanding":"ἐν τῷ λέγειν, “In that he says” — the single word ‘new’ carries the argument: “A new covenant”. πεπαλαίωκεν τὴν πρώτην, “he has made the first old.” τὸ δὲ παλαιούμενον καὶ γηράσκον ἐγγὺς ἀφανισμοῦ, “But that which is becoming old and grows aged is near to vanishing away” — ἀφανισμός, disappearance. Within a few years of this letter, the temple was destroyed and the sacrifices ceased (AD 70).","words":[{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G3822","hebrew":"παλαιόω","translit":"palaióō","english":"made the first old"},{"strongs":"G1095","hebrew":"γηράσκω","translit":"gēráskō","english":"grows aged"},{"strongs":"G1451","hebrew":"ἐγγύς","translit":"engýs","english":"near"},{"strongs":"G854","hebrew":"ἀφανισμός","translit":"aphanismós","english":"vanishing away"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Model and the Building","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture an architect's office. On the table stands a detailed scale model of a great cathedral — every arch, window and spire carefully made of wood and card. People come to admire it and learn from it. Then picture walking outside to see the cathedral itself, finished and full of light, a thousand times larger and more glorious than the model. The model was accurate and useful, but it was never meant to be lived in.","meaning":"Earthly priests “serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things” (v. 5), but Jesus is “a servant of the sanctuary, and of the true tabernacle, which the Lord pitched, not man” (v. 2). The tabernacle, priests and sacrifices were God-given models. Jesus ministers in the reality. Do not cling to the model when the cathedral is open."},{"title":"Written on the Heart","verseRef":"v. 10","image":"Picture two tablets of stone, engraved with God's commandments, carried in a golden box. They are holy and true, but they stand outside the people, pointing at them, telling them what they should be. Now picture the same words being traced, letter by letter, by a gentle hand of light onto a living human heart — so that the commands become desires, and what was once ‘you must’ becomes ‘I want to’.","meaning":"“I will put my laws into their mind, I will also write them on their heart. I will be their God, and they will be my people” (v. 10). The new covenant does not abolish God's will; it moves it inside. By His Spirit, God gives new desires and new power to obey. Ask Him to write His ways more deeply on your heart."},{"title":"Remembered No More","verseRef":"v. 12","image":"Picture a huge courtroom file, thick with records of every wrong you have done — every lie, every unkind word, every rebellion. The judge opens it, looks at you, and then, deliberately, feeds every page into a fire until nothing is left but ash. He says, ‘I will never bring these up again.’","meaning":"“For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness. I will remember their sins and lawless deeds no more” (v. 12). Unrighteousness, sins, lawless deeds — every kind of wrong is covered. God does not merely file them away; He chooses never to hold them against you again. Stop bringing up what God has chosen to forget."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Main Point: A Heavenly High Priest (v. 1-6)","body":"The main point is that we have a high priest seated at God's right hand in heaven, serving in the true tabernacle set up by the Lord; every high priest offers gifts and sacrifices, so He too must have something to offer; on earth He would not be a priest at all, since the Levitical priests serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly sanctuary, as Moses was told to follow the pattern shown on the mountain; but Christ's ministry is far better, as the mediator of a better covenant based on better promises."},{"label":"The Promised New Covenant (v. 7-13)","body":"If the first covenant had been faultless, there would have been no need for a second; God, finding fault with the people, promised through Jeremiah a new covenant, unlike the one Israel broke, in which He would write His laws on their minds and hearts, be their God, give every one of them personal knowledge of Him, and remember their sins no more; by calling it new He has made the first obsolete, and what is obsolete is about to disappear."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Main Point","paragraphs":["After seven chapters, the writer pauses to give the headline: “Now in the things which we are saying, the main point is this. We have such a high priest, who sat down on the right hand of the throne of the Majesty in the heavens” (v. 1). Everything so far — the Son's greatness, His humanity, His sympathy, His Melchizedek priesthood — comes to this: we have Him, now, enthroned and serving.","He ministers in “the true tabernacle, which the Lord pitched, not man” (v. 2). The word ‘true’ (ἀληθινός) means genuine, the original as opposed to a copy. The earthly tabernacle was real and God-given, but it was a copy. The priests on earth “serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things” (v. 5). God told Moses, “See, you shall make everything according to the pattern that was shown to you on the mountain” (v. 5). Stephen also reminded the council that the tabernacle was made “according to the pattern that he had seen” (Acts 7:44).","The pattern-family words help us read the whole Old Testament worship system. τύπος is the original pattern; ὑπόδειγμα the copy; σκιά the shadow. Paul says the same of the festivals and Sabbaths: they “are a shadow of the things to come; but the body is Christ’s” (Colossians 2:17). Shadows are not bad; they show that something real is near. But no one would choose to hug a shadow when the person is standing in front of them."]},{"heading":"A Better Covenant With Better Promises","paragraphs":["Christ “has obtained a more excellent ministry, by so much as he is also the mediator of a better covenant, which on better promises has been given as law” (v. 6). A mediator stands between two parties and brings them together. Paul writes, “For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 2:5). Jesus not only announces the new covenant; He establishes it with His own blood.","Why was a new covenant needed? “For if that first covenant had been faultless, then no place would have been sought for a second” (v. 7). But notice where the fault lay: “For finding fault with them” (v. 8). The law was holy, but the people “didn’t continue in my covenant” (v. 9). The old covenant told people what to do but did not change their hearts. Paul says, “For the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life” (2 Corinthians 3:6).","So God promised through Jeremiah, as quoted in verse 8, “that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah” (v. 8). Jesus announced its arrival at the Last Supper: “This cup is the new covenant in my blood” (1 Corinthians 11:25). The new covenant is not a different God or a lower standard, but the same God doing what the old could not."]},{"heading":"Four Promises of the New Covenant","paragraphs":["The new covenant brings four great promises. First, an inner law: “I will put my laws into their mind, I will also write them on their heart” (v. 10). Paul says believers are “a letter of Christ... written not with ink, but with the Spirit of the living God; not in tablets of stone, but in tablets that are hearts of flesh” (2 Corinthians 3:3). Second, a relationship: “I will be their God, and they will be my people” (v. 10).","Third, personal knowledge of God for everyone: “for all will know me, from their least to their greatest” (v. 11). Two know-family words make the point: no one will need to be told ‘get to know (γινώσκω) the Lord’, because all will ‘know’ (οἶδα) Him. Jesus said, quoting the prophets, “They will all be taught by God” (John 6:45). Fourth, complete forgiveness: “For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness. I will remember their sins and lawless deeds no more” (v. 12). Three sin-family words — injustice, sin, lawlessness — are all covered.","The chapter ends: “In that he says, ‘A new covenant’, he has made the first old. But that which is becoming old and grows aged is near to vanishing away” (v. 13). The word ‘new’ (καινός) means new in kind. The old covenant, with its temple and sacrifices, was about to disappear — and within a few years of this letter, the temple was destroyed. Those tempted to go back were clinging to something that was passing away."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The New Covenant in Jesus' Blood","paragraphs":["Jesus instituted the new covenant at the Last Supper: “for this is my blood of the new covenant, which is poured out for many for the remission of sins” (Matthew 26:28). Luke records, “This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you” (Luke 22:20). Paul calls apostles “servants of a new covenant; not of the letter, but of the Spirit” (2 Corinthians 3:6), and quotes God's promise for Israel: “This is my covenant with them, when I will take away their sins” (Romans 11:27).","The heavenly reality behind the earthly copies is a theme of Hebrews. The law was “a shadow of the good to come, not the very image of the things” (Hebrews 10:1). The earthly holy places were “representations of the true” (Hebrews 9:24). And Jesus is “the mediator of a new covenant” (Hebrews 12:24) — there using νέος, ‘new’ in the sense of freshly given, alongside καινός, ‘new in kind’, here in chapter 8.","The inner knowledge of God promised in verse 11 is the work of the Spirit. John writes, “the anointing which you received from him remains in you, and you don’t need for anyone to teach you” (1 John 2:27). This does not abolish teachers (Hebrews itself is teaching!), but it means every believer, not just priests and experts, has direct, personal access to God and real knowledge of Him."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Shadow","hebrew":"σκιά","translit":"skiá","strongs":"G4639","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “\"shade\" or a shadow (literally or figuratively (darkness of error or an adumbration)).” Hebrews uses it twice: earthly priests serve a ‘shadow’ of heavenly things (8:5), and the law has a ‘shadow’ of good things to come (10:1). Colossians 2:17 uses it of festivals and Sabbaths. A shadow is real and has the shape of what casts it, but it is not the substance. Christ is the substance. This study tags it in the new pattern family."},{"english":"Pattern / type","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","strongs":"G5179","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “a die (as struck), i.e. (by implication) a stamp or scar; by analogy, a shape, i.e. a statue, (figuratively) style or resemblance; specially, a sampler (\"type\"), i.e. a model (for imitation) or instance (for warning).” It could mean the mark left by a blow (Thomas wanted to see the ‘print’ of the nails), a model to copy, or a type that foreshadows. In 8:5 it is the heavenly original that Moses' tabernacle copied."},{"english":"Mediator / go-between","hebrew":"μεσίτης","translit":"mesítēs","strongs":"G3316","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “a go-between, i.e. (simply) an internunciator, or (by implication) a reconciler (intercessor).” Moses was the mediator of the old covenant at Sinai (Galatians 3:19). Jesus is the mediator of the new and better covenant (8:6; 9:15; 12:24). Paul says there is ‘one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus’. Because He is both God and man, He alone can bring the two together."},{"english":"Covenant / will / testament","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","strongs":"G1242","verses":"v. 6, 8, 9, 10","insight":"Strong's: “properly, a disposition, i.e. (specially) a contract (especially a devisory will).” In ordinary Greek it usually meant a last will and testament. The Greek Old Testament used it for God's covenants, which are not bargains between equals but arrangements God sovereignly sets up. Hebrews 9:16–17 will use both senses: a covenant that is also a will, put into effect by the death of the one who made it. It gives us the names ‘Old Testament’ and ‘New Testament’."},{"english":"Vanishing away / disappearance","hebrew":"ἀφανισμός","translit":"aphanismós","strongs":"G854","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “disappearance, i.e. (figuratively) abrogation.” It appears only here in the New Testament. The old covenant was ‘near to vanishing away’ — and within a few years of this letter, the Romans destroyed the temple in Jerusalem (AD 70) and the sacrificial system ended. The shadow disappeared because the reality had come."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"Through this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you where Jesus is serving now and what He has written on your heart.","items":[{"title":"We Have Such a High Priest — Now","body":"The main point is not a doctrine but a Person who is serving for you right now in heaven. The Spirit wants you to live in the present tense of Jesus' priesthood. Whatever you face today, He is seated, enthroned and ministering on your behalf."},{"title":"Let Him Write on Your Heart","body":"In the new covenant, God's will is not only commanded but written within. The Spirit is the One who writes. Where do you still experience God's ways as outside rules rather than inner desires? Ask Him to write His law more deeply on your heart, so obedience becomes delight."},{"title":"Believe That He Has Forgotten","body":"God has promised to remember your sins no more. The Spirit wants to free you from replaying forgiven failures. When the memory of a confessed sin accuses you, answer with verse 12: ‘God has chosen never to bring this up again.’"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Shadow Check","body":"Are you holding on to any ‘shadow’ — a ritual, tradition or religious habit — as if it were the substance? Ask how it points you to Christ, and focus on Him (v. 5)."},{"title":"The Heart Check","body":"Which of God's commands do you obey only reluctantly, as outside rules? Pray specifically for Him to write that command on your heart (v. 10)."},{"title":"The Forgiveness Check","body":"Is there a forgiven sin you keep bringing up before God? Read verse 12 aloud and thank Him that He remembers it no more."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Know the Lord Personally","body":"Every believer can know God personally (v. 11). This month, set aside a regular time to talk with God simply and honestly, as a child to a Father. Read a short passage, then tell Him what you notice about Him, and listen. Keep a journal of what you learn about God Himself, not just about rules."},{"title":"Celebrate the New Covenant","body":"Next time you share in the Lord's Supper, prepare beforehand by reading Hebrews 8:10–12 and Luke 22:20. As you take the cup, remember the four promises: an inner law, a relationship, personal knowledge of God, and complete forgiveness. Thank Jesus, the mediator, who sealed them with His blood."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, this is the main point: I have a High Priest seated at the right hand of the throne of the Majesty in heaven. You serve in the true tabernacle that the Lord pitched, not human hands. Thank You that the tabernacle, priests and sacrifices of the old covenant were a copy and a shadow, and that You are the reality. You are the mediator of a better covenant, founded on better promises. Father, thank You for the new covenant. Put Your laws into my mind and write them on my heart. Be my God, and let me live as one of Your people. Thank You that I can know You personally, whether I am least or greatest. Thank You that You are merciful to my unrighteousness and that You will remember my sins and lawless deeds no more. Help me let go of every shadow and hold fast to Christ, the substance. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Moses on the Mountain: The Pattern of the Tabernacle","ref":"Exodus 24:12-18; 25:1-9, 40","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 5","summary":"God called Moses up Mount Sinai, and the glory of the LORD settled on the mountain like a consuming fire. Moses stayed forty days and nights, and God gave him detailed instructions for building a sanctuary so that He could dwell among His people: the ark, the table, the lampstand, the curtains, the altar. Again and again God told Moses to make everything exactly according to the pattern shown to him on the mountain. Hebrews 8:5 quotes this command and explains its meaning: the earthly tabernacle was a careful copy of a heavenly reality, where Jesus now ministers as High Priest."},{"title":"The Covenant Broken at the Golden Calf","ref":"Exodus 32:1-20","relatedVerses":"v. 8-9","summary":"While Moses was on the mountain receiving the law, the people grew impatient. They persuaded Aaron to make a golden calf and worshipped it, saying it was the god who brought them out of Egypt. When Moses came down and saw the idolatry, he threw down the two stone tablets and broke them at the foot of the mountain. The covenant had barely been made before it was broken. Hebrews 8:9 quotes God's verdict through Jeremiah: ‘they didn’t continue in my covenant’. The problem was not the law but the people's hearts — which is why God promised a new covenant written on hearts."},{"title":"The Last Supper","ref":"Matthew 26:26-29; 1 Corinthians 11:23-26","relatedVerses":"v. 6-13","summary":"On the night He was betrayed, Jesus took bread, gave thanks, broke it and said, ‘This is my body.’ Then He took the cup and said, “for this is my blood of the new covenant, which is poured out for many for the remission of sins” (Matthew 26:28). Paul passed on the same words to the Corinthians: “This cup is the new covenant in my blood. Do this, as often as you drink, in memory of me” (1 Corinthians 11:25). At that table, the new covenant promised in Jeremiah 31 and quoted in Hebrews 8 was inaugurated, sealed the next day by Jesus' death."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":9,"chapterTitle":"The Earthly Tent and the Blood of Christ","themeTitle":"“Through His Own Blood, Once for All”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now indeed even the first covenant had ordinances of divine service, and an earthly sanctuary.","understanding":"εἶχε μὲν οὖν καὶ ἡ πρώτη δικαιώματα λατρείας, “Now indeed even the first covenant had ordinances of divine service” — δικαίωμα, a regulation; λατρεία, worship (related to λατρεύω in the servant family). τό τε ἅγιον κοσμικόν, “and an earthly sanctuary” — κοσμικός, of this world (κόσμος, world family). The old covenant was not worthless; it had God-given worship and a God-given sanctuary. But it was ‘of this world’.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G1345","hebrew":"δικαίωμα","translit":"dikaíōma","english":"ordinances"},{"strongs":"G2999","hebrew":"λατρεία","translit":"latreía","english":"divine service"},{"strongs":"G39","hebrew":"ἅγιον","translit":"hágion","english":"sanctuary"},{"strongs":"G2886","hebrew":"κοσμικός","translit":"kosmikós","english":"earthly"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For a tabernacle was prepared. In the first part were the lamp stand, the table, and the show bread; which is called the Holy Place.","understanding":"σκηνὴ γὰρ κατεσκευάσθη ἡ πρώτη, “For a tabernacle was prepared. In the first part” — the outer room. ἐν ᾗ ἥ τε λυχνία καὶ ἡ τράπεζα καὶ ἡ πρόθεσις τῶν ἄρτων, “were the lamp stand, the table, and the show bread” — literally ‘the setting-forth of the loaves’: twelve loaves set before God each Sabbath. ἥτις λέγεται ἅγια, “which is called the Holy Place.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G2680","hebrew":"κατασκευάζω","translit":"kataskeuázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stand"},{"strongs":"G5132","hebrew":"τράπεζα","translit":"trápeza","english":"table"},{"strongs":"G4286","hebrew":"πρόθεσις","translit":"próthesis","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G740","hebrew":"ἄρτος","translit":"ártos","english":"bread"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"}]},{"v":3,"text":"After the second veil was the tabernacle which is called the Holy of Holies,","understanding":"μετὰ δὲ τὸ δεύτερον καταπέτασμα, “After the second veil” — the inner curtain, the one torn at Jesus' death. σκηνὴ ἡ λεγομένη ἅγια ἁγίων, “was the tabernacle which is called the Holy of Holies” — the Most Holy Place, where God's presence dwelt above the ark.","words":[{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G2665","hebrew":"καταπέτασμα","translit":"katapétasma","english":"veil"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy of Holies"}]},{"v":4,"text":"having a golden altar of incense, and the ark of the covenant overlaid on all sides with gold, in which was a golden pot holding the manna, Aaron’s rod that budded, and the tablets of the covenant;","understanding":"χρυσοῦν ἔχουσα θυμιατήριον, “having a golden altar of incense” — the incense altar actually stood just outside the veil, but it belonged closely to the inner room, since its incense was carried inside on the Day of Atonement; some translate the word ‘censer’. καὶ τὴν κιβωτὸν τῆς διαθήκης περικεκαλυμμένην πάντοθεν χρυσίῳ, “and the ark of the covenant overlaid on all sides with gold.” Inside: στάμνος χρυσῆ ἔχουσα τὸ μάννα, “a golden pot holding the manna” — God's provision; ἡ ῥάβδος Ἀαρὼν ἡ βλαστήσασα, “Aaron’s rod that budded” — God's chosen priesthood; καὶ αἱ πλάκες τῆς διαθήκης, “and the tablets of the covenant” — God's law.","words":[{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G2369","hebrew":"θυμιαστήριον","translit":"thymiastḗrion","english":"altar of incense"},{"strongs":"G2787","hebrew":"κιβωτός","translit":"kibōtós","english":"ark"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G4028","hebrew":"περικαλύπτω","translit":"perikalýptō","english":"overlaid"},{"strongs":"G4713","hebrew":"στάμνος","translit":"stámnos","english":"pot"},{"strongs":"G3131","hebrew":"μάννα","translit":"mánna","english":"manna"},{"strongs":"G2","hebrew":"Ἀαρών","translit":"Aarṓn","english":"Aaron"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"rod"},{"strongs":"G985","hebrew":"βλαστάνω","translit":"blastánō","english":"budded"},{"strongs":"G4109","hebrew":"πλάξ","translit":"pláx","english":"tablets"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and above it cherubim of glory overshadowing the mercy seat, of which things we can’t speak now in detail.","understanding":"ὑπεράνω δὲ αὐτῆς Χερουβὶν δόξης κατασκιάζοντα τὸ ἱλαστήριον, “and above it cherubim of glory overshadowing the mercy seat.” ἱλαστήριον, the place of atonement, the golden lid of the ark where blood was sprinkled — the same word Paul uses of Christ in Romans 3:25. περὶ ὧν οὐκ ἔστιν νῦν λέγειν κατὰ μέρος, “of which things we can’t speak now in detail” — the writer could say much more, but his point is elsewhere.","words":[{"strongs":"G5231","hebrew":"ὑπεράνω","translit":"hyperánō","english":"above"},{"strongs":"G5502","hebrew":"χερουβίμ","translit":"cheroubím","english":"cherubim"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2683","hebrew":"κατασκιάζω","translit":"kataskiázō","english":"overshadowing"},{"strongs":"G2435","hebrew":"ἱλαστήριον","translit":"hilastḗrion","english":"mercy seat"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"detail"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Now these things having been thus prepared, the priests go in continually into the first tabernacle, accomplishing the services,","understanding":"τούτων δὲ οὕτως κατεσκευασμένων, “Now these things having been thus prepared,” εἰς μὲν τὴν πρώτην σκηνὴν διὰ παντὸς εἰσίασιν οἱ ἱερεῖς τὰς λατρείας ἐπιτελοῦντες, “the priests go in continually into the first tabernacle, accomplishing the services” — trimming lamps, burning incense, changing the bread. Daily, endless activity.","words":[{"strongs":"G2680","hebrew":"κατασκευάζω","translit":"kataskeuázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G1275","hebrew":"διαπαντός","translit":"diapantós","english":"continually"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priests"},{"strongs":"G2005","hebrew":"ἐπιτελέω","translit":"epiteléō","english":"accomplishing"},{"strongs":"G2999","hebrew":"λατρεία","translit":"latreía","english":"services"}]},{"v":7,"text":"but into the second the high priest alone, once in the year, not without blood, which he offers for himself, and for the errors of the people.","understanding":"εἰς δὲ τὴν δευτέραν ἅπαξ τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ μόνος ὁ ἀρχιερεύς, “but into the second the high priest alone, once in the year” — on the Day of Atonement (Leviticus 16). οὐ χωρὶς αἵματος, “not without blood,” ὃ προσφέρει ὑπὲρ ἑαυτοῦ καὶ τῶν τοῦ λαοῦ ἀγνοημάτων, “which he offers for himself, and for the errors of the people.” ἀγνόημα, added to the sin family: sins of ignorance, wrongs done without full knowledge. One man, one day a year, with blood: access to God was severely restricted.","words":[{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"year"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offers"},{"strongs":"G51","hebrew":"ἀγνόημα","translit":"agnóēma","english":"errors"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The Holy Spirit is indicating this, that the way into the Holy Place wasn’t yet revealed while the first tabernacle was still standing;","understanding":"τοῦτο δηλοῦντος τοῦ πνεύματος τοῦ ἁγίου, “The Holy Spirit is indicating this” — the Spirit was teaching through the very layout of the tabernacle. μήπω πεφανερῶσθαι τὴν τῶν ἁγίων ὁδόν, “that the way into the Holy Place wasn’t yet revealed” — φανερόω from the appear family. ἔτι τῆς πρώτης σκηνῆς ἐχούσης στάσιν, “while the first tabernacle was still standing.” The curtain itself preached a sermon: ‘Not yet. The way is not open.’","words":[{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1213","hebrew":"δηλόω","translit":"dēlóō","english":"indicating"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"}]},{"v":9,"text":"which is a symbol of the present age, where gifts and sacrifices are offered that are incapable, concerning the conscience, of making the worshiper perfect;","understanding":"ἥτις παραβολὴ εἰς τὸν καιρὸν τὸν ἐνεστηκότα, “which is a symbol of the present age” — παραβολή from the pattern family: a picture set alongside. A word-family point: ‘age’ here is καιρός, the time family's word for a season with a particular character — not αἰών, the word for the ages in verse 26. καθ᾽ ἣν δῶρά τε καὶ θυσίαι προσφέρονται, “where gifts and sacrifices are offered” — the offering family again. μὴ δυνάμεναι κατὰ συνείδησιν τελειῶσαι τὸν λατρεύοντα, “that are incapable, concerning the conscience, of making the worshiper perfect” — τελειόω from the perfect family. They could cleanse the body ritually but could not reach the conscience.","words":[{"strongs":"G3850","hebrew":"παραβολή","translit":"parabolḗ","english":"symbol"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"age"},{"strongs":"G1764","hebrew":"ἐνίστημι","translit":"enístēmi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G1435","hebrew":"δῶρον","translit":"dōron","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"incapable"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"worshiper"}]},{"v":10,"text":"being only (with meats and drinks and various washings) fleshly ordinances, imposed until a time of reformation.","understanding":"μόνον ἐπὶ βρώμασιν καὶ πόμασιν καὶ διαφόροις βαπτισμοῖς, “being only (with meats and drinks and various washings)” — βαπτισμός, ritual washings (as in 6:2). δικαιώματα σαρκός, “fleshly ordinances” — rules for the body. μέχρι καιροῦ διορθώσεως ἐπικείμενα, “imposed until a time of reformation” — καιρός again: the appointed season of διόρθωσις, setting things straight. That season arrived with Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"meats"},{"strongs":"G4188","hebrew":"πόμα","translit":"póma","english":"drinks"},{"strongs":"G1313","hebrew":"διάφορος","translit":"diáphoros","english":"various"},{"strongs":"G909","hebrew":"βαπτισμός","translit":"baptismós","english":"washings"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"fleshly"},{"strongs":"G1345","hebrew":"δικαίωμα","translit":"dikaíōma","english":"ordinances"},{"strongs":"G1945","hebrew":"ἐπίκειμαι","translit":"epíkeimai","english":"imposed"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G1357","hebrew":"διόρθωσις","translit":"diórthōsis","english":"reformation"}]},{"v":11,"text":"But Christ having come as a high priest of the coming good things, through the greater and more perfect tabernacle, not made with hands, that is to say, not of this creation,","understanding":"Χριστὸς δὲ παραγενόμενος ἀρχιερεὺς τῶν γενομένων ἀγαθῶν, “But Christ having come as a high priest of the coming good things” — ἀγαθός from the good family (some manuscripts read ‘the good things that have come’). διὰ τῆς μείζονος καὶ τελειοτέρας σκηνῆς, “through the greater and more perfect tabernacle” — τέλειος from the perfect family. οὐ χειροποιήτου, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν οὐ ταύτης τῆς κτίσεως, “not made with hands, that is to say, not of this creation.”","words":[{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G3854","hebrew":"παραγίνομαι","translit":"paragínomai","english":"having come"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"more perfect"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G5499","hebrew":"χειροποίητος","translit":"cheiropoíētos","english":"made with hands"},{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"creation"}]},{"v":12,"text":"nor yet through the blood of goats and calves, but through his own blood, entered in once for all into the Holy Place, having obtained eternal redemption.","understanding":"οὐδὲ δι᾽ αἵματος τράγων καὶ μόσχων, “nor yet through the blood of goats and calves,” διὰ δὲ τοῦ ἰδίου αἵματος, “but through his own blood” — the decisive difference. εἰσῆλθεν ἐφάπαξ εἰς τὰ ἅγια, “entered in once for all into the Holy Place” — ἐφάπαξ, once for all, never to be repeated. αἰωνίαν λύτρωσιν εὑράμενος, “having obtained eternal redemption” — λύτρωσις, release by ransom; αἰώνιος, eternal. The old high priest's work lasted a year; Christ's lasts forever.","words":[{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5131","hebrew":"τράγος","translit":"trágos","english":"goats"},{"strongs":"G3448","hebrew":"μόσχος","translit":"móschos","english":"calves"},{"strongs":"G2398","hebrew":"ἴδιος","translit":"ídios","english":"own"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entered"},{"strongs":"G2178","hebrew":"ἐφάπαξ","translit":"ephápax","english":"once for all"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy Place"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G3085","hebrew":"λύτρωσις","translit":"lýtrōsis","english":"redemption"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"obtained"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For if the blood of goats and bulls, and the ashes of a heifer sprinkling those who have been defiled, sanctify to the cleanness of the flesh:","understanding":"εἰ γὰρ τὸ αἷμα τράγων καὶ ταύρων, “For if the blood of goats and bulls” — the Day of Atonement — καὶ σποδὸς δαμάλεως ῥαντίζουσα τοὺς κεκοινωμένους, “and the ashes of a heifer sprinkling those who have been defiled” — the red heifer of Numbers 19, whose ashes mixed with water cleansed those defiled by contact with death. ἁγιάζει πρὸς τὴν τῆς σαρκὸς καθαρότητα, “sanctify to the cleanness of the flesh” — they made people ritually clean on the outside.","words":[{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5131","hebrew":"τράγος","translit":"trágos","english":"goats"},{"strongs":"G5022","hebrew":"ταῦρος","translit":"taûros","english":"bulls"},{"strongs":"G4700","hebrew":"σποδός","translit":"spodós","english":"ashes"},{"strongs":"G1151","hebrew":"δάμαλις","translit":"dámalis","english":"heifer"},{"strongs":"G4472","hebrew":"ῥαντίζω","translit":"rhantízō","english":"sprinkling"},{"strongs":"G2840","hebrew":"κοινόω","translit":"koinóō","english":"defiled"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctify"},{"strongs":"G2514","hebrew":"καθαρότης","translit":"katharótēs","english":"cleanness"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]},{"v":14,"text":"how much more will the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without defect to God, cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?","understanding":"πόσῳ μᾶλλον τὸ αἷμα τοῦ Χριστοῦ, “how much more will the blood of Christ,” ὃς διὰ πνεύματος αἰωνίου ἑαυτὸν προσήνεγκεν ἄμωμον τῷ θεῷ, “who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without defect to God” — Father, Son and Spirit together in the sacrifice. ἄμωμος, unblemished, the word for a perfect sacrificial animal. καθαριεῖ τὴν συνείδησιν ἡμῶν ἀπὸ νεκρῶν ἔργων, “cleanse your conscience from dead works” — what animal blood could never reach. ἔργον from the work family: dead works are deeds that lead to death or religious acts that cannot give life. εἰς τὸ λατρεύειν θεῷ ζῶντι, “to serve the living God” — λατρεύω from the servant family. Cleansed in order to worship.","words":[{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"},{"strongs":"G299","hebrew":"ἄμωμος","translit":"ámōmos","english":"without defect"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"cleanse"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For this reason he is the mediator of a new covenant, since a death has occurred for the redemption of the transgressions that were under the first covenant, that those who have been called may receive the promise of the eternal inheritance.","understanding":"καὶ διὰ τοῦτο διαθήκης καινῆς μεσίτης ἐστίν, “For this reason he is the mediator of a new covenant” — καινός from the new family. ὅπως θανάτου γενομένου εἰς ἀπολύτρωσιν τῶν ἐπὶ τῇ πρώτῃ διαθήκῃ παραβάσεων, “since a death has occurred for the redemption of the transgressions that were under the first covenant” — παράβασις from the sin family: stepping over God's line. Christ's death reached back to cover the sins of believers under the old covenant too. τὴν ἐπαγγελίαν λάβωσιν οἱ κεκλημένοι τῆς αἰωνίου κληρονομίας, “that those who have been called may receive the promise of the eternal inheritance.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G3316","hebrew":"μεσίτης","translit":"mesítēs","english":"mediator"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G629","hebrew":"ἀπολύτρωσις","translit":"apolýtrōsis","english":"redemption"},{"strongs":"G3847","hebrew":"παράβασις","translit":"parábasis","english":"transgressions"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2817","hebrew":"κληρονομία","translit":"klēronomía","english":"inheritance"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For where a last will and testament is, there must of necessity be the death of him who made it.","understanding":"Now the writer plays on the double meaning of διαθήκη: ‘covenant’ and also, in everyday Greek, ‘will’ or ‘testament’. ὅπου γὰρ διαθήκη, “For where a last will and testament is,” θάνατον ἀνάγκη φέρεσθαι τοῦ διαθεμένου, “there must of necessity be the death of him who made it.” Some translators keep ‘covenant’ here and understand the death as that of the sacrificial animals by which covenants were ratified; the WEB follows the majority in reading ‘will’. Either way, the point is the same: the new covenant comes into effect through Christ's death.","words":[{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"last will and testament"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"necessity"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"be"},{"strongs":"G1303","hebrew":"διατίθεμαι","translit":"diatíthemai","english":"made it"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For a will is in force where there has been death, for it is never in force while he who made it lives.","understanding":"διαθήκη γὰρ ἐπὶ νεκροῖς βεβαία, “For a will is in force where there has been death” — βέβαιος, valid, binding. ἐπεὶ μήποτε ἰσχύει ὅτε ζῇ ὁ διαθέμενος, “for it is never in force while he who made it lives.” An inheritance is received only when the testator dies. Christ died so that His people could receive the ‘eternal inheritance’ (v. 15).","words":[{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"in force"},{"strongs":"G2480","hebrew":"ἰσχύω","translit":"ischýō","english":"in force"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G1303","hebrew":"διατίθεμαι","translit":"diatíthemai","english":"made it"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Therefore even the first covenant has not been dedicated without blood.","understanding":"ὅθεν οὐδὲ ἡ πρώτη χωρὶς αἵματος ἐγκεκαίνισται, “Therefore even the first covenant has not been dedicated without blood” — ἐγκαινίζω, to inaugurate, to put into effect (related to καινός, new). Death and blood stood at the beginning of the old covenant too.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G1457","hebrew":"ἐγκαινίζω","translit":"enkainízō","english":"dedicated"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For when every commandment had been spoken by Moses to all the people according to the law, he took the blood of the calves and the goats, with water and scarlet wool and hyssop, and sprinkled both the book itself and all the people,","understanding":"λαληθείσης γὰρ πάσης ἐντολῆς κατὰ νόμον ὑπὸ Μωϋσέως παντὶ τῷ λαῷ, “For when every commandment had been spoken by Moses to all the people according to the law” — at Sinai (Exodus 24). λαβὼν τὸ αἷμα τῶν μόσχων καὶ τῶν τράγων, “he took the blood of the calves and the goats,” μετὰ ὕδατος καὶ ἐρίου κοκκίνου καὶ ὑσσώπου, “with water and scarlet wool and hyssop,” αὐτό τε τὸ βιβλίον καὶ πάντα τὸν λαὸν ἐράντισεν, “and sprinkled both the book itself and all the people.” The covenant was sealed with blood on both the word and the people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoken"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G3448","hebrew":"μόσχος","translit":"móschos","english":"calves"},{"strongs":"G5131","hebrew":"τράγος","translit":"trágos","english":"goats"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G2847","hebrew":"κόκκινος","translit":"kókkinos","english":"scarlet"},{"strongs":"G2053","hebrew":"ἔριον","translit":"érion","english":"wool"},{"strongs":"G5301","hebrew":"ὕσσωπος","translit":"hýssōpos","english":"hyssop"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G4472","hebrew":"ῥαντίζω","translit":"rhantízō","english":"sprinkled"}]},{"v":20,"text":"saying, “This is the blood of the covenant which God has commanded you.”","understanding":"λέγων, “saying,” “This is the blood of the covenant which God has commanded you.” Jesus echoed these words at the Last Supper: ‘This is my blood of the new covenant.’ Moses' words pointed forward to Christ's.","words":[{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1781","hebrew":"ἐντέλλομαι","translit":"entéllomai","english":"commanded"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Moreover he sprinkled the tabernacle and all the vessels of the ministry in the same way with the blood.","understanding":"καὶ τὴν σκηνὴν δὲ καὶ πάντα τὰ σκεύη τῆς λειτουργίας τῷ αἵματι ὁμοίως ἐράντισεν, “Moreover he sprinkled the tabernacle and all the vessels of the ministry in the same way with the blood.” λειτουργία, priestly service. Everything connected with approaching God had to be cleansed by blood.","words":[{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G4632","hebrew":"σκεῦος","translit":"skeûos","english":"vessels"},{"strongs":"G3009","hebrew":"λειτουργία","translit":"leitourgía","english":"ministry"},{"strongs":"G3668","hebrew":"ὁμοίως","translit":"homoíōs","english":"in the same way"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G4472","hebrew":"ῥαντίζω","translit":"rhantízō","english":"sprinkled"}]},{"v":22,"text":"According to the law, nearly everything is cleansed with blood, and apart from shedding of blood there is no remission.","understanding":"καὶ σχεδὸν ἐν αἵματι πάντα καθαρίζεται κατὰ τὸν νόμον, “According to the law, nearly everything is cleansed with blood” — ‘nearly’, because some things were cleansed with water or fire. καὶ χωρὶς αἱματεκχυσίας οὐ γίνεται ἄφεσις, “and apart from shedding of blood there is no remission.” αἱματεκχυσία, the pouring out of blood — a word perhaps coined here. ἄφεσις, release, forgiveness. Sin is a matter of life and death; it is forgiven only through a life given.","words":[{"strongs":"G4975","hebrew":"σχεδόν","translit":"schedón","english":"nearly"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"cleansed"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"apart from"},{"strongs":"G130","hebrew":"αἱματεκχυσία","translit":"haimatekchysía","english":"shedding of blood"},{"strongs":"G859","hebrew":"ἄφεσις","translit":"áphesis","english":"remission"}]},{"v":23,"text":"It was necessary therefore that the copies of the things in the heavens should be cleansed with these; but the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these.","understanding":"ἀνάγκη οὖν τὰ μὲν ὑποδείγματα τῶν ἐν τοῖς οὐρανοῖς τούτοις καθαρίζεσθαι, “It was necessary therefore that the copies of the things in the heavens should be cleansed with these” — ὑπόδειγμα from the pattern family. αὐτὰ δὲ τὰ ἐπουράνια κρείττοσιν θυσίαις παρὰ ταύτας, “but the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these” — κρείττων, ‘better’; the plural ‘sacrifices’ refers to the one sacrifice of Christ in its fullness. Why would heavenly things need cleansing? Perhaps because the way to God's presence had to be opened for sinners; the writer does not explain further.","words":[{"strongs":"G318","hebrew":"ἀνάγκη","translit":"anánkē","english":"necessary"},{"strongs":"G5262","hebrew":"ὑπόδειγμα","translit":"hypódeigma","english":"copies"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"cleansed"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"}]},{"v":24,"text":"For Christ hasn’t entered into holy places made with hands, which are representations of the true, but into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us;","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ εἰς χειροποίητα ἅγια εἰσῆλθεν Χριστός, “For Christ hasn’t entered into holy places made with hands,” ἀντίτυπα τῶν ἀληθινῶν, “which are representations of the true” — ἀντίτυπον from the pattern family: counterparts of the real. ἀλλ᾽ εἰς αὐτὸν τὸν οὐρανόν, “but into heaven itself,” νῦν ἐμφανισθῆναι τῷ προσώπῳ τοῦ θεοῦ ὑπὲρ ἡμῶν, “now to appear in the presence of God for us.” ἐμφανίζω, added to the appear family: to show oneself openly before someone. This is the first of three ‘appearings’ in verses 24–28 — His present appearing before God for us.","words":[{"strongs":"G5499","hebrew":"χειροποίητος","translit":"cheiropoíētos","english":"made with hands"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy places"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entered"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G499","hebrew":"ἀντίτυπον","translit":"antítypon","english":"representations"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G3568","hebrew":"νῦν","translit":"nŷn","english":"now"},{"strongs":"G1718","hebrew":"ἐμφανίζω","translit":"emphanízō","english":"appear"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"presence"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":25,"text":"nor yet that he should offer himself often, as the high priest enters into the holy place year by year with blood not his own,","understanding":"οὐδ᾽ ἵνα πολλάκις προσφέρῃ ἑαυτόν, “nor yet that he should offer himself often,” ὥσπερ ὁ ἀρχιερεὺς εἰσέρχεται εἰς τὰ ἅγια κατ᾽ ἐνιαυτὸν ἐν αἵματι ἀλλοτρίῳ, “as the high priest enters into the holy place year by year with blood not his own.” ἀλλότριος, belonging to another — the animal's, not his own.","words":[{"strongs":"G4178","hebrew":"πολλάκις","translit":"pollákis","english":"often"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offer"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enters"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"year by year"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G245","hebrew":"ἀλλότριος","translit":"allótrios","english":"not his own"}]},{"v":26,"text":"or else he must have suffered often since the foundation of the world. But now once at the end of the ages, he has been revealed to put away sin by the sacrifice of himself.","understanding":"ἐπεὶ ἔδει αὐτὸν πολλάκις παθεῖν ἀπὸ καταβολῆς κόσμου, “or else he must have suffered often since the foundation of the world” — κόσμος from the world family. νυνὶ δὲ ἅπαξ ἐπὶ συντελείᾳ τῶν αἰώνων, “But now once at the end of the ages” — αἰών from the time family: the climax of all history (compare καιρός, ‘the present age’, in v. 9). εἰς ἀθέτησιν ἁμαρτίας διὰ τῆς θυσίας αὐτοῦ πεφανέρωται, “he has been revealed to put away sin by the sacrifice of himself.” φανερόω from the appear family: the second ‘appearing’ — His past appearing in history, at the cross. ἀθέτησις, cancellation (as in 7:18): sin set aside, annulled.","words":[{"strongs":"G4178","hebrew":"πολλάκις","translit":"pollákis","english":"often"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G2602","hebrew":"καταβολή","translit":"katabolḗ","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G4930","hebrew":"συντέλεια","translit":"syntéleia","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"ages"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G115","hebrew":"ἀθέτησις","translit":"athétēsis","english":"put away"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifice"}]},{"v":27,"text":"Inasmuch as it is appointed for men to die once, and after this, judgment,","understanding":"καὶ καθ᾽ ὅσον ἀπόκειται τοῖς ἀνθρώποις ἅπαξ ἀποθανεῖν, “Inasmuch as it is appointed for men to die once” — ἀπόκειμαι, laid up, reserved (the same word as the ‘crown laid up’ in 2 Timothy 4:8). μετὰ δὲ τοῦτο κρίσις, “and after this, judgment.” One life, one death, then judgement — no second chances after death, no cycle of rebirth.","words":[{"strongs":"G606","hebrew":"ἀπόκειμαι","translit":"apókeimai","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"}]},{"v":28,"text":"so Christ also, having been offered once to bear the sins of many, will appear a second time, without sin, to those who are eagerly waiting for him for salvation.","understanding":"οὕτως καὶ ὁ Χριστὸς ἅπαξ προσενεχθεὶς εἰς τὸ πολλῶν ἀνενεγκεῖν ἁμαρτίας, “so Christ also, having been offered once to bear the sins of many” — ἀναφέρω from the offering family, echoing Isaiah 53: ‘he bore the sin of many’. As humans die once, Christ died once. ἐκ δευτέρου χωρὶς ἁμαρτίας ὀφθήσεται, “will appear a second time, without sin” — ὤφθη, from ὁράω in the appear family: the third ‘appearing’, His future return, not to deal with sin again but to complete salvation. τοῖς αὐτὸν ἀπεκδεχομένοις εἰς σωτηρίαν, “to those who are eagerly waiting for him for salvation.” Three appearings, three words: He appeared once, φανερόω in verse 26; He appears now for us, ἐμφανίζω in verse 24; He will appear again, ὁράω here in verse 28.","words":[{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"},{"strongs":"G399","hebrew":"ἀναφέρω","translit":"anaphérō","english":"bear"},{"strongs":"G4183","hebrew":"πολύς","translit":"polýs","english":"many"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"without"},{"strongs":"G3700","hebrew":"ὀπτάνομαι","translit":"optánomai","english":"appear"},{"strongs":"G553","hebrew":"ἀπεκδέχομαι","translit":"apekdéchomai","english":"eagerly waiting"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Behind the Curtain","verseRef":"v. 6-8","image":"Picture the tabernacle in the wilderness. In the outer room, priests come and go all day: trimming lamps, burning incense, changing bread. Behind a thick, embroidered curtain lies the inner room, silent and dark except for the glory above the ark. No one enters — except one man, once a year, trembling, carrying blood. Everyone else waits outside. The curtain itself seems to say: ‘Not yet. The way is closed.’","meaning":"“but into the second the high priest alone, once in the year, not without blood” (v. 7). “The Holy Spirit is indicating this, that the way into the Holy Place wasn’t yet revealed while the first tabernacle was still standing” (v. 8). When Jesus died, “The veil of the temple was torn in two from the top to the bottom” (Mark 15:38). Through Him, the way is open. You may come in."},{"title":"The Blood That Reaches the Conscience","verseRef":"v. 13-14","image":"Picture someone scrubbing the outside of a cup until it gleams, while the inside remains stained and foul. Then picture a stream of clear, living water poured into the cup, flowing through every corner, dissolving every stain from within. The outside washing was not useless, but only the inner washing makes the cup truly clean.","meaning":"The old sacrifices could “sanctify to the cleanness of the flesh” (v. 13). “how much more will the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without defect to God, cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?” (v. 14). Christ's blood reaches where nothing else can: the guilty conscience. Let Him wash you within, and then serve the living God with a clean heart."},{"title":"Three Appearings","verseRef":"v. 24-28","image":"Picture three windows in a cathedral. The first shows a cross on a hill, with a Man dying under a darkened sky. The second shows a throne room in heaven, where the same Man, now risen, stands before the Father's face, pleading for His people. The third shows the sky splitting open and the same Man coming in glory, while crowds lift their faces in joy.","meaning":"He “has been revealed to put away sin by the sacrifice of himself” (v. 26). He has entered “into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us” (v. 24). And He “will appear a second time, without sin, to those who are eagerly waiting for him for salvation” (v. 28). Three different Greek words, three appearings: past, present and future. Live between the cross and the return, with your High Priest appearing for you now."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Earthly Tabernacle (v. 1-10)","body":"The first covenant had regulations for worship and an earthly sanctuary: an outer room with the lampstand, table and bread of the Presence, and behind the second curtain the Most Holy Place with the golden incense altar and the ark containing manna, Aaron's staff and the tablets, beneath the cherubim over the mercy seat; priests served continually in the outer room, but only the high priest entered the inner room once a year with blood for himself and the people's unintentional sins, showing that the way into the holy place was not yet open; its gifts and sacrifices could not perfect the conscience but were external regulations until the time of reformation."},{"label":"Christ's Better Sacrifice (v. 11-14)","body":"Christ came as high priest of the good things to come, through a greater, more perfect tent not made with hands, and entered the holy place once for all by His own blood, obtaining eternal redemption; if animal blood and ashes cleansed the flesh, how much more will Christ's blood, offered through the eternal Spirit, cleanse our conscience from dead works to serve the living God."},{"label":"The Mediator's Death Puts the Covenant in Force (v. 15-22)","body":"Christ is mediator of a new covenant, since His death redeems transgressions under the first covenant so that the called may receive the eternal inheritance; as a will takes effect only after death, so the first covenant was inaugurated with blood, when Moses sprinkled the book, the people, the tabernacle and its vessels; under the law almost everything is cleansed with blood, and without the shedding of blood there is no forgiveness."},{"label":"Once for All, and Coming Again (v. 23-28)","body":"The heavenly realities needed better sacrifices than the copies; Christ entered not a man-made sanctuary but heaven itself to appear before God for us, not to offer Himself repeatedly as the high priest does each year, but once for all at the end of the ages to put away sin by His sacrifice; as people die once and then face judgement, so Christ was offered once to bear the sins of many and will appear a second time, not to deal with sin, but to bring salvation to those who eagerly wait for Him."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Sanctuary That Preached a Sermon","paragraphs":["Chapter 9 describes the tabernacle Moses built: an outer room, the Holy Place, with the lampstand, table and bread; and an inner room, the Holy of Holies, with the ark of the covenant, the mercy seat and the cherubim of glory. The priests went into the outer room every day. But “into the second the high priest alone, once in the year, not without blood, which he offers for himself, and for the errors of the people” (v. 7).","The writer sees a message in the layout: “The Holy Spirit is indicating this, that the way into the Holy Place wasn’t yet revealed while the first tabernacle was still standing” (v. 8). The curtain said, ‘Keep out.’ The once-a-year entry said, ‘Access is restricted.’ The repeated sacrifices said, ‘The problem is not solved.’ The whole system was “a symbol of the present age, where gifts and sacrifices are offered that are incapable, concerning the conscience, of making the worshiper perfect” (v. 9).","Two time-family words appear here. The ‘present age’ (v. 9) and the ‘time of reformation’ (v. 10) both use καιρός, a season with a particular character. Later, Christ appears ‘at the end of the ages’ (v. 26), using αἰών, the great eras of history. The old covenant was a season; it was “imposed until a time of reformation” (v. 10). With Christ, that time of setting things right has come."]},{"heading":"How Much More the Blood of Christ","paragraphs":["Then comes the contrast: “But Christ having come as a high priest of the coming good things, through the greater and more perfect tabernacle, not made with hands” (v. 11), “nor yet through the blood of goats and calves, but through his own blood, entered in once for all into the Holy Place, having obtained eternal redemption” (v. 12). The old high priest brought someone else's blood, entered an earthly tent, and had to return every year. Christ brought His own blood, entered heaven itself, and did it once for all.","The result is a cleansing the old system could never give: “how much more will the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without defect to God, cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?” (v. 14). Animal blood could make a person ritually clean on the outside. Christ's blood reaches the conscience, the inner sense of guilt before God. John writes, “the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” (1 John 1:7).","And this cleansing has a purpose: “to serve the living God” (v. 14). The servant-family word λατρεύω means to serve in worship. We are not cleansed merely to feel better, but to be free to worship and serve God with a clear conscience. Paul says, “in whom we have our redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of our trespasses, according to the riches of his grace” (Ephesians 1:7)."]},{"heading":"Once, Now and Again","paragraphs":["Christ's death put the new covenant into effect. The writer plays on the word διαθήκη, which means both ‘covenant’ and ‘will’: “For where a last will and testament is, there must of necessity be the death of him who made it” (v. 16). Even the old covenant began with blood, when Moses sprinkled the book and the people, saying, “This is the blood of the covenant which God has commanded you” (v. 20). The principle stands: “apart from shedding of blood there is no remission” (v. 22). Jesus said at the Last Supper, “for this is my blood of the new covenant, which is poured out for many for the remission of sins” (Matthew 26:28).","Verses 24–28 then describe three ‘appearings’ of Christ, each with a different word from the appear family. Past: “But now once at the end of the ages, he has been revealed to put away sin by the sacrifice of himself” (v. 26). Present: He has entered “into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us” (v. 24). Future: He “will appear a second time, without sin, to those who are eagerly waiting for him for salvation” (v. 28).","Between the first and third appearings comes the solemn reality of every human life: “Inasmuch as it is appointed for men to die once, and after this, judgment” (v. 27). There is one life and then judgement. But for those who belong to Christ, the judgement has already fallen on Him: He was “offered once to bear the sins of many” (v. 28). Jesus said that the one who believes “has eternal life, and doesn’t come into judgment, but has passed out of death into life” (John 5:24). Believers wait for His return not with dread but with eager longing."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Blood, the Torn Veil and the Return","paragraphs":["The New Testament repeatedly celebrates the power of Christ's blood. We were redeemed “with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (1 Peter 1:19). God reconciled all things to Himself, “having made peace through the blood of his cross” (Colossians 1:20). And Christ is the one “who loves us, and washed us from our sins by his blood” (Revelation 1:5). John the Baptist pointed to Him: “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” (John 1:29).","At the moment Jesus died, the curtain that symbolised restricted access was torn: “The veil of the temple was torn in two from the top to the bottom” (Mark 15:38). Luke adds, “The sun was darkened, and the veil of the temple was torn in two” (Luke 23:45). The torn veil announced what Hebrews 9:8 had anticipated: the way into the holy place is now open.","The promise of Christ's second appearing runs through the New Testament. The angels told the disciples, “This Jesus, who was received up from you into the sky, will come back in the same way as you saw him going into the sky” (Acts 1:11). Jesus promised, “I will come again, and will receive you to myself” (John 14:3). “For the Lord himself will descend from heaven with a shout” (1 Thessalonians 4:16). And “when he is revealed, we will be like him; for we will see him just as he is” (1 John 3:2)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Mercy seat / place of atonement","hebrew":"ἱλαστήριον","translit":"hilastḗrion","strongs":"G2435","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “an expiatory (place or thing), i.e. (concretely) an atoning victim, or (specially) the lid of the Ark (in the Temple).” It was the golden cover of the ark where the high priest sprinkled blood on the Day of Atonement, between the cherubim, where God met His people. Paul uses the same word of Christ: God ‘sent to be an atoning sacrifice’ (Romans 3:25). Jesus is both the place where God meets sinners and the sacrifice that makes it possible."},{"english":"Symbol / parable","hebrew":"παραβολή","translit":"parabolḗ","strongs":"G3850","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “a similitude (\"parable\"), i.e. (symbolic) fictitious narrative (of common life conveying a moral), apothegm or adage.” It literally means something ‘thrown alongside’ for comparison — the word for Jesus' parables. In Hebrews 9:9 the whole tabernacle system is a ‘parable’ of its time: a picture that teaches by comparison. This study tags it in the pattern family."},{"english":"Redemption / ransoming","hebrew":"λύτρωσις","translit":"lýtrōsis","strongs":"G3085","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “a ransoming (figuratively).” It describes release by payment of a price, as a slave or prisoner was freed. Zechariah and Anna used it of the redemption Israel longed for (Luke 1:68; 2:38). Christ obtained ‘eternal redemption’ by His own blood: the price was paid in full, and the freedom lasts forever."},{"english":"Shedding of blood","hebrew":"αἱματεκχυσία","translit":"haimatekchysía","strongs":"G130","verses":"v. 22","insight":"Strong's: “an effusion of blood.” It may have been coined by the writer, combining αἷμα (blood) and ἐκχέω (to pour out). It sums up the whole sacrificial system: forgiveness requires a life poured out. The principle points to the cross, where Christ's blood was ‘poured out for many for the remission of sins’ (Matthew 26:28)."},{"english":"Eagerly wait / await expectantly","hebrew":"ἀπεκδέχομαι","translit":"apekdéchomai","strongs":"G553","verses":"v. 28","insight":"Strong's: “to expect fully.” It combines three parts — ἀπό, ἐκ and δέχομαι, to receive — and describes eager, intent waiting, like someone leaning forward to watch for an arrival. Paul uses it of creation and believers waiting for the redemption of the body, and of waiting for the Saviour from heaven. Christians are not just people who believe in the second coming; they eagerly await it."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you the open way, the cleansing blood, and the coming King.","items":[{"title":"The Way Is Open","body":"The Spirit once taught through a closed curtain that the way was not yet open. Now He testifies that the way is open through Christ's blood. Are you still living as if God's presence were off limits to you? Ask the Spirit to give you confidence to come close."},{"title":"A Clean Conscience","body":"Christ's blood cleanses the conscience from dead works. The Spirit wants to free you from guilt for confessed sin and from the deadness of religious performance. Bring to Him whatever still weighs on your conscience, and receive cleansing so you can serve the living God with joy."},{"title":"Eagerly Waiting","body":"Christ will appear a second time to bring salvation to those who eagerly wait for Him. The Spirit wants to stir in you a longing for Jesus' return. Ask Him to make that hope bright and to shape how you live now."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Access Check","body":"Do you approach God as if the curtain were still closed — distant, fearful, unsure of welcome? Meditate on verse 12 and draw near with confidence."},{"title":"The Conscience Check","body":"Is there anything on your conscience — confessed sin you still feel guilty for, or dead religious habits? Pray verse 14 over it specifically."},{"title":"The Readiness Check","body":"Verse 27 says everyone dies once and then faces judgement. Are you ready? Is there someone you need to talk to about this?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Meditate on the Three Appearings","body":"For three days, take one ‘appearing’ each day: Christ revealed at the cross (v. 26), Christ appearing before God for you now (v. 24), and Christ appearing again (v. 28). Read the verse, thank Him for that aspect of His work, and write down how it changes the way you will live that day."},{"title":"Clean-Conscience Service","body":"Because Christ cleansed your conscience ‘to serve the living God’ (v. 14), choose one act of service this week offered as worship — not to earn God's favour but in gratitude for cleansing. Before you do it, pray, ‘Lord, I serve You freely because You have made me clean.’"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, thank You that You are the High Priest of the good things to come. The old tabernacle was a picture, with a curtain that said the way was not yet open. But You entered, not with the blood of goats and calves, but with Your own blood, once for all into the holy place, and You obtained eternal redemption. Through the eternal Spirit You offered Yourself without blemish to God. Cleanse my conscience from dead works, so that I may serve the living God. Thank You for being the mediator of a new covenant, sealed by Your death, so that I may receive the promised eternal inheritance. Without the shedding of blood there is no forgiveness, and You poured out Your blood for me. You appeared once at the end of the ages to put away sin by Your sacrifice. You appear now in God's presence for me. And You will appear a second time, not to deal with sin, but to bring salvation. I eagerly wait for You. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Day of Atonement","ref":"Leviticus 16","relatedVerses":"v. 7, 12, 25","summary":"Once a year, on the Day of Atonement, the high priest bathed, put on simple linen garments and offered a bull for his own sins. He took a censer of burning coals and incense into the Most Holy Place, so that the cloud would cover the mercy seat, and sprinkled the bull's blood on and before it. Then he killed a goat for the people's sins and did the same with its blood. A second goat, the scapegoat, had the people's sins confessed over it and was sent away into the wilderness. Hebrews 9 contrasts this yearly ritual with Christ, who entered heaven itself once for all with His own blood."},{"title":"Moses Seals the Covenant With Blood","ref":"Exodus 24:1-8","relatedVerses":"v. 18-20","summary":"After God gave the law at Sinai, Moses wrote down all the LORD's words and built an altar at the foot of the mountain. Young men offered burnt offerings and fellowship offerings. Moses took half the blood and splashed it on the altar. Then he read the Book of the Covenant to the people, who promised to obey. Moses took the blood, sprinkled it on the people, and said it was the blood of the covenant the LORD had made with them. Hebrews 9:19–20 recalls this moment to show that covenants are inaugurated with blood — and that Christ's blood inaugurates the new covenant."},{"title":"The Veil Torn at the Cross","ref":"Mark 15:33-39","relatedVerses":"v. 3, 8, 24","summary":"From noon until three in the afternoon, darkness covered the land as Jesus hung on the cross. At three He cried out with a loud voice and breathed His last. At that moment, “The veil of the temple was torn in two from the top to the bottom” (Mark 15:38) — torn from above, by God. The centurion who saw how Jesus died said, ‘Truly this man was the Son of God!’ Hebrews 9 explains what the torn veil means: the way into God's presence, closed while the first tabernacle stood, has been opened by Christ's death."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":10,"chapterTitle":"One Sacrifice Forever — Therefore Draw Near and Hold Fast","themeTitle":"“A New and Living Way”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"For the law, having a shadow of the good to come, not the very image of the things, can never with the same sacrifices year by year, which they offer continually, make perfect those who draw near.","understanding":"σκιὰν γὰρ ἔχων ὁ νόμος τῶν μελλόντων ἀγαθῶν, “For the law, having a shadow of the good to come” — σκιά from the pattern family. οὐκ αὐτὴν τὴν εἰκόνα τῶν πραγμάτων, “not the very image of the things” — εἰκών from the image family: the true likeness, the reality itself. Two picture-words set against each other: a shadow is an outline; an image is the real form. κατ᾽ ἐνιαυτὸν ταῖς αὐταῖς θυσίαις ἃς προσφέρουσιν εἰς τὸ διηνεκές, “can never with the same sacrifices year by year, which they offer continually,” οὐδέποτε δύναται τοὺς προσερχομένους τελειῶσαι, “make perfect those who draw near” — τελειόω from the perfect family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G4639","hebrew":"σκιά","translit":"skiá","english":"shadow"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G4229","hebrew":"πρᾶγμα","translit":"prâgma","english":"things"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"year by year"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offer"},{"strongs":"G1336","hebrew":"διηνεκής","translit":"diēnekḗs","english":"continually"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"can"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"make perfect"},{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"draw near"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Or else wouldn’t they have ceased to be offered, because the worshipers, having been once cleansed, would have had no more consciousness of sins?","understanding":"ἐπεὶ οὐκ ἂν ἐπαύσαντο προσφερόμεναι, “Or else wouldn’t they have ceased to be offered” — the very repetition proves the inadequacy. διὰ τὸ μηδεμίαν ἔχειν ἔτι συνείδησιν ἁμαρτιῶν τοὺς λατρεύοντας ἅπαξ κεκαθαρισμένους, “because the worshipers, having been once cleansed, would have had no more consciousness of sins?” λατρεύω from the servant family. If a sacrifice really dealt with sin, the conscience would be at rest and no more would be needed.","words":[{"strongs":"G3973","hebrew":"παύω","translit":"paúō","english":"ceased"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"worshipers"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"cleansed"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"consciousness"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":3,"text":"But in those sacrifices there is a yearly reminder of sins.","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ ἐν αὐταῖς ἀνάμνησις ἁμαρτιῶν κατ᾽ ἐνιαυτόν, “But in those sacrifices there is a yearly reminder of sins.” ἀνάμνησις, remembrance — the same word Jesus used at the Last Supper: ‘Do this in memory of me.’ The old sacrifices brought sins to mind every year; the Lord's Supper brings Christ to mind, the One who removed them.","words":[{"strongs":"G364","hebrew":"ἀνάμνησις","translit":"anámnēsis","english":"reminder"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"yearly"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For it is impossible that the blood of bulls and goats should take away sins.","understanding":"ἀδύνατον γὰρ αἷμα ταύρων καὶ τράγων ἀφαιρεῖν ἁμαρτίας, “For it is impossible that the blood of bulls and goats should take away sins.” A blunt statement. Animal blood was God's appointed picture and pledge, but it could never itself remove human guilt. Only a human, sinless, willing sacrifice could do that.","words":[{"strongs":"G102","hebrew":"ἀδύνατος","translit":"adýnatos","english":"impossible"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5022","hebrew":"ταῦρος","translit":"taûros","english":"bulls"},{"strongs":"G5131","hebrew":"τράγος","translit":"trágos","english":"goats"},{"strongs":"G851","hebrew":"ἀφαιρέω","translit":"aphairéō","english":"take away"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Therefore when he comes into the world, he says, “Sacrifice and offering you didn’t desire, but you prepared a body for me.","understanding":"διὸ εἰσερχόμενος εἰς τὸν κόσμον λέγει, “Therefore when he comes into the world, he says” — the writer hears the Son speaking Psalm 40 at His incarnation. κόσμος from the world family. “Sacrifice and offering you didn’t desire, but you prepared a body for me.” Two offering-family words: θυσία, a slain sacrifice, and προσφορά, an offering. The writer quotes the Greek Old Testament, which reads ‘a body you prepared’; the Hebrew reads ‘my ears you have opened’ — a willing, listening servant. Both point to the same truth: God wanted obedience, and the Son came in a body to obey to the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"comes"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"Sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G4376","hebrew":"προσφορά","translit":"prosphorá","english":"offering"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G2675","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","english":"prepared"}]},{"v":6,"text":"You had no pleasure in whole burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin.","understanding":"“You had no pleasure in whole burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin.” A third offering-family word: ὁλοκαύτωμα, the whole burnt offering, entirely consumed; and ‘sacrifices for sin’ (περὶ ἁμαρτίας), the sin offering. εὐδοκέω, to take delight. God commanded these sacrifices, but they were never what He ultimately wanted.","words":[{"strongs":"G3646","hebrew":"ὁλοκαύτωμα","translit":"holokaútōma","english":"whole burnt offerings"},{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"pleasure"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Then I said, ‘Behold, I have come (in the scroll of the book it is written of me) to do your will, O God.’”","understanding":"“Then I said, ‘Behold, I have come (in the scroll of the book it is written of me) to do your will, O God.’” κεφαλίδι βιβλίου, ‘in the roll of the book’ — the Scriptures were written about Him. θέλημα, will. What God desired was not more animals but a perfectly obedient Son.","words":[{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"scroll"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Previously saying, “Sacrifices and offerings and whole burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin you didn’t desire, neither had pleasure in them” (those which are offered according to the law),","understanding":"The writer repeats the quotation, now listing all four offering-family words together: “Sacrifices and offerings and whole burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin you didn’t desire, neither had pleasure in them” — θυσίαι, προσφοραί, ὁλοκαυτώματα, περὶ ἁμαρτίας. Every kind of offering in the Levitical system is named, and every one is set aside. αἵτινες κατὰ νόμον προσφέρονται, ‘(those which are offered according to the law)’ — even though the law itself commanded them.","words":[{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"Sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G4376","hebrew":"προσφορά","translit":"prosphorá","english":"offerings"},{"strongs":"G3646","hebrew":"ὁλοκαύτωμα","translit":"holokaútōma","english":"whole burnt offerings"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"pleasure"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"}]},{"v":9,"text":"then he has said, “Behold, I have come to do your will.” He takes away the first, that he may establish the second,","understanding":"τότε εἴρηκεν, “then he has said, ‘Behold, I have come to do your will.’” ἀναιρεῖ τὸ πρῶτον ἵνα τὸ δεύτερον στήσῃ, “He takes away the first, that he may establish the second.” ἀναιρέω, to take away, abolish. The first (the system of animal sacrifices) is removed; the second (the will of God done by Christ) is established.","words":[{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G337","hebrew":"ἀναιρέω","translit":"anairéō","english":"takes away"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"establish"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"}]},{"v":10,"text":"by which will we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all.","understanding":"ἐν ᾧ θελήματι ἡγιασμένοι ἐσμέν, “by which will we have been sanctified” — perfect tense: made holy, set apart for God, a completed act with lasting effect. διὰ τῆς προσφορᾶς τοῦ σώματος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ ἐφάπαξ, “through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all.” προσφορά from the offering family: the ‘body prepared’ (v. 5) was the offering. ἐφάπαξ, once for all.","words":[{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G4376","hebrew":"προσφορά","translit":"prosphorá","english":"offering"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G2178","hebrew":"ἐφάπαξ","translit":"ephápax","english":"once for all"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Every priest indeed stands day by day serving and often offering the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins,","understanding":"καὶ πᾶς μὲν ἱερεὺς ἕστηκεν καθ᾽ ἡμέραν λειτουργῶν, “Every priest indeed stands day by day serving” — standing, because the work was never done. καὶ τὰς αὐτὰς πολλάκις προσφέρων θυσίας, “and often offering the same sacrifices,” αἵτινες οὐδέποτε δύνανται περιελεῖν ἁμαρτίας, “which can never take away sins” — περιαιρέω, to strip off, remove entirely.","words":[{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stands"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day by day"},{"strongs":"G3008","hebrew":"λειτουργέω","translit":"leitourgéō","english":"serving"},{"strongs":"G4178","hebrew":"πολλάκις","translit":"pollákis","english":"often"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offering"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G4014","hebrew":"περιαιρέω","translit":"periairéō","english":"take away"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":12,"text":"but he, when he had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, sat down on the right hand of God;","understanding":"οὗτος δὲ μίαν ὑπὲρ ἁμαρτιῶν προσενέγκας θυσίαν εἰς τὸ διηνεκές, “but he, when he had offered one sacrifice for sins forever” — μία, one, against the ‘often’ of verse 11. ἐκάθισεν ἐν δεξιᾷ τοῦ θεοῦ, “sat down on the right hand of God.” The priests stood; Christ sat. The contrast between verses 11 and 12 is the whole gospel in two postures.","words":[{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G1336","hebrew":"διηνεκής","translit":"diēnekḗs","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G2523","hebrew":"καθίζω","translit":"kathízō","english":"sat down"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"}]},{"v":13,"text":"from that time waiting until his enemies are made the footstool of his feet.","understanding":"τὸ λοιπὸν ἐκδεχόμενος ἕως τεθῶσιν οἱ ἐχθροὶ αὐτοῦ ὑποπόδιον τῶν ποδῶν αὐτοῦ, “from that time waiting until his enemies are made the footstool of his feet” — Psalm 110 again (1:13). His sacrifice is finished; His victory is certain; He waits for its full display.","words":[{"strongs":"G1551","hebrew":"ἐκδέχομαι","translit":"ekdéchomai","english":"waiting"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemies"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"made"},{"strongs":"G5286","hebrew":"ὑποπόδιον","translit":"hypopódion","english":"footstool"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For by one offering he has perfected forever those who are being sanctified.","understanding":"μιᾷ γὰρ προσφορᾷ τετελείωκεν εἰς τὸ διηνεκὲς τοὺς ἁγιαζομένους, “For by one offering he has perfected forever those who are being sanctified.” Two tenses held together. τετελείωκεν, perfect tense of τελειόω from the perfect family: He has already brought them to the goal — fully and permanently accepted before God. ἁγιαζομένους, present tense: they are being made holy in daily life. Our standing is complete; our growth continues.","words":[{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G4376","hebrew":"προσφορά","translit":"prosphorá","english":"offering"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfected"},{"strongs":"G1336","hebrew":"διηνεκής","translit":"diēnekḗs","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"being sanctified"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The Holy Spirit also testifies to us, for after saying,","understanding":"μαρτυρεῖ δὲ ἡμῖν καὶ τὸ πνεῦμα τὸ ἅγιον, “The Holy Spirit also testifies to us” — again Scripture is the Spirit's present testimony. μετὰ γὰρ τὸ εἰρηκέναι, “for after saying” — the writer returns to Jeremiah 31 from chapter 8.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testifies"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":16,"text":"“This is the covenant that I will make with them: ‘After those days,’ says the Lord, ‘I will put my laws on their heart, I will also write them on their mind;’” then he says,","understanding":"“This is the covenant that I will make with them: ‘After those days,’ says the Lord, ‘I will put my laws on their heart, I will also write them on their mind;’” then he says — the inner law of the new covenant (8:10), now quoted to make a different point: what comes next.","words":[{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G1303","hebrew":"διατίθεμαι","translit":"diatíthemai","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"put"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"laws"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G1271","hebrew":"διάνοια","translit":"diánoia","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G1924","hebrew":"ἐπιγράφω","translit":"epigráphō","english":"write"}]},{"v":17,"text":"“I will remember their sins and their iniquities no more.”","understanding":"“I will remember their sins and their iniquities no more.” Two sin-family words: ἁμαρτία, missing the mark, and ἀνομία, lawlessness. This is the promise the writer wanted to reach.","words":[{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G458","hebrew":"ἀνομία","translit":"anomía","english":"iniquities"},{"strongs":"G3415","hebrew":"μνάομαι","translit":"mnáomai","english":"remember"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Now where remission of these is, there is no more offering for sin.","understanding":"ὅπου δὲ ἄφεσις τούτων, “Now where remission of these is” — ἄφεσις, release, forgiveness (9:22). οὐκέτι προσφορὰ περὶ ἁμαρτίας, “there is no more offering for sin.” If God has forgiven and will not remember, there is nothing left to sacrifice for. The argument of chapters 7–10 is complete.","words":[{"strongs":"G859","hebrew":"ἄφεσις","translit":"áphesis","english":"remission"},{"strongs":"G3765","hebrew":"οὐκέτι","translit":"oukéti","english":"no more"},{"strongs":"G4376","hebrew":"προσφορά","translit":"prosphorá","english":"offering"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Having therefore, brothers, boldness to enter into the holy place by the blood of Jesus,","understanding":"Now the great ‘therefore’. ἔχοντες οὖν, ἀδελφοί, παρρησίαν εἰς τὴν εἴσοδον τῶν ἁγίων ἐν τῷ αἵματι Ἰησοῦ, “Having therefore, brothers, boldness to enter into the holy place by the blood of Jesus” — παρρησία, confidence, freedom. What only the high priest could do once a year, all believers may do at any time.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"Having"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G1529","hebrew":"εἴσοδος","translit":"eísodos","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy place"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"}]},{"v":20,"text":"by the way which he dedicated for us, a new and living way, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh;","understanding":"ἣν ἐνεκαίνισεν ἡμῖν ὁδὸν πρόσφατον καὶ ζῶσαν, “by the way which he dedicated for us, a new and living way” — πρόσφατος, added to the new family: originally ‘freshly slain’, then ‘new, recent’. A way opened by a fresh sacrifice, and yet ‘living’, because the One who opened it is alive. διὰ τοῦ καταπετάσματος, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν τῆς σαρκὸς αὐτοῦ, “through the veil, that is to say, his flesh” — when His body was torn, the curtain was torn.","words":[{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G1457","hebrew":"ἐγκαινίζω","translit":"enkainízō","english":"dedicated"},{"strongs":"G4372","hebrew":"πρόσφατος","translit":"prósphatos","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G2665","hebrew":"καταπέτασμα","translit":"katapétasma","english":"veil"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]},{"v":21,"text":"and having a great priest over God’s house,","understanding":"καὶ ἱερέα μέγαν ἐπὶ τὸν οἶκον τοῦ θεοῦ, “and having a great priest over God’s house” — the Son over the house (3:6).","words":[{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priest"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":22,"text":"let’s draw near with a true heart in fullness of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and having our body washed with pure water,","understanding":"Three ‘let us’ commands follow, built on faith, hope and love. First, faith: προσερχώμεθα μετὰ ἀληθινῆς καρδίας ἐν πληροφορίᾳ πίστεως, “let’s draw near with a true heart in fullness of faith.” ῥεραντισμένοι τὰς καρδίας ἀπὸ συνειδήσεως πονηρᾶς, “having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience” — πονηρός from the evil family: a guilty conscience; the sprinkling echoes the priests' consecration. καὶ λελουσμένοι τὸ σῶμα ὕδατι καθαρῷ, “and having our body washed with pure water” — probably pointing to baptism, the outward sign of inward cleansing.","words":[{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"draw near"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G4136","hebrew":"πληροφορία","translit":"plērophoría","english":"fullness"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4472","hebrew":"ῥαντίζω","translit":"rhantízō","english":"sprinkled"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G3068","hebrew":"λούω","translit":"loúō","english":"washed"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"}]},{"v":23,"text":"let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering; for he who promised is faithful.","understanding":"Second, hope: κατέχωμεν τὴν ὁμολογίαν τῆς ἐλπίδος ἀκλινῆ, “let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering” — κατέχω from the hold family; ἀκλινής, not leaning to either side. πιστὸς γὰρ ὁ ἐπαγγειλάμενος, “for he who promised is faithful.” We hold on because He holds firm.","words":[{"strongs":"G2722","hebrew":"κατέχω","translit":"katéchō","english":"hold fast"},{"strongs":"G3671","hebrew":"ὁμολογία","translit":"homología","english":"confession"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G186","hebrew":"ἀκλινής","translit":"aklinḗs","english":"without wavering"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promised"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Let us consider how to provoke one another to love and good works,","understanding":"Third, love: καὶ κατανοῶμεν ἀλλήλους, “Let us consider” one another — the word used of considering Jesus (3:1). εἰς παροξυσμὸν ἀγάπης καὶ καλῶν ἔργων, “how to provoke one another to love and good works.” παροξυσμός usually means a sharp quarrel (used of Paul and Barnabas, Acts 15:39) — here it is turned to good: stir each other up! Three families meet: ἀγάπη (love), καλός (good), ἔργον (work).","words":[{"strongs":"G2657","hebrew":"κατανοέω","translit":"katanoéō","english":"consider"},{"strongs":"G240","hebrew":"ἀλλήλων","translit":"allḗlōn","english":"one another"},{"strongs":"G3948","hebrew":"παροξυσμός","translit":"paroxysmós","english":"provoke"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":25,"text":"not forsaking our own assembling together, as the custom of some is, but exhorting one another; and so much the more, as you see the Day approaching.","understanding":"μὴ ἐγκαταλείποντες τὴν ἐπισυναγωγὴν ἑαυτῶν, “not forsaking our own assembling together” — ἐπισυναγωγή, gathering together (the same word Paul uses for our ‘gathering together to him’ at Christ's coming). καθὼς ἔθος τισίν, “as the custom of some is” — some had already stopped coming. ἀλλὰ παρακαλοῦντες, “but exhorting one another;” καὶ τοσούτῳ μᾶλλον ὅσῳ βλέπετε ἐγγίζουσαν τὴν ἡμέραν, “and so much the more, as you see the Day approaching” — ἡμέρα from the time family: the Day of Christ's return.","words":[{"strongs":"G1459","hebrew":"ἐγκαταλείπω","translit":"enkataleípō","english":"forsaking"},{"strongs":"G1997","hebrew":"ἐπισυναγωγή","translit":"episynagōgḗ","english":"assembling together"},{"strongs":"G1485","hebrew":"ἔθος","translit":"éthos","english":"custom"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhorting"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"Day"},{"strongs":"G1448","hebrew":"ἐγγίζω","translit":"engízō","english":"approaching"}]},{"v":26,"text":"For if we sin willfully after we have received the knowledge of the truth, there remains no more a sacrifice for sins,","understanding":"The fifth and sternest warning. ἑκουσίως γὰρ ἁμαρτανόντων ἡμῶν, “For if we sin willfully” — present tense: keep on deliberately sinning, a settled choice; ἁμαρτάνω from the sin family. μετὰ τὸ λαβεῖν τὴν ἐπίγνωσιν τῆς ἀληθείας, “after we have received the knowledge of the truth” — ἐπίγνωσις from the know family: full knowledge. οὐκέτι περὶ ἁμαρτιῶν ἀπολείπεται θυσία, “there remains no more a sacrifice for sins.” The context (v. 29) shows the sin in view: rejecting Christ Himself. If you reject the only sacrifice, there is no other.","words":[{"strongs":"G1596","hebrew":"ἑκουσίως","translit":"hekousíōs","english":"willfully"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G3765","hebrew":"οὐκέτι","translit":"oukéti","english":"no more"},{"strongs":"G620","hebrew":"ἀπολείπω","translit":"apoleípō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":27,"text":"but a certain fearful expectation of judgment, and a fierceness of fire which will devour the adversaries.","understanding":"φοβερὰ δέ τις ἐκδοχὴ κρίσεως, “but a certain fearful expectation of judgment,” καὶ πυρὸς ζῆλος ἐσθίειν μέλλοντος τοὺς ὑπεναντίους, “and a fierceness of fire which will devour the adversaries” — ζῆλος, zeal: God's jealous holiness pictured as fire. Those who make themselves God's enemies by rejecting His Son face His judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G5398","hebrew":"φοβερός","translit":"phoberós","english":"fearful"},{"strongs":"G1561","hebrew":"ἐκδοχή","translit":"ekdochḗ","english":"expectation"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2205","hebrew":"ζῆλος","translit":"zēlos","english":"fierceness"},{"strongs":"G2068","hebrew":"ἐσθίω","translit":"esthíō","english":"devour"},{"strongs":"G5227","hebrew":"ὑπεναντίος","translit":"hypenantíos","english":"adversaries"}]},{"v":28,"text":"A man who disregards Moses’ law dies without compassion on the word of two or three witnesses.","understanding":"ἀθετήσας τις νόμον Μωϋσέως, “A man who disregards Moses’ law” — ἀθετέω, to set aside, reject (as in the case of idolatry in Deuteronomy 17). χωρὶς οἰκτιρμῶν ἐπὶ δυσὶν ἢ τρισὶν μάρτυσιν ἀποθνῄσκει, “dies without compassion on the word of two or three witnesses.”","words":[{"strongs":"G114","hebrew":"ἀθετέω","translit":"athetéō","english":"disregards"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"without"},{"strongs":"G3628","hebrew":"οἰκτιρμός","translit":"oiktirmós","english":"compassion"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"},{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witnesses"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"dies"}]},{"v":29,"text":"How much worse punishment do you think he will be judged worthy of who has trodden under foot the Son of God, and has counted the blood of the covenant with which he was sanctified an unholy thing, and has insulted the Spirit of grace?","understanding":"πόσῳ δοκεῖτε χείρονος ἀξιωθήσεται τιμωρίας, “How much worse punishment do you think he will be judged worthy of” — three charges. ὁ τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ θεοῦ καταπατήσας, “who has trodden under foot the Son of God” — treated Him with contempt; καὶ τὸ αἷμα τῆς διαθήκης κοινὸν ἡγησάμενος ἐν ᾧ ἡγιάσθη, “and has counted the blood of the covenant with which he was sanctified an unholy thing” — κοινός, common, ordinary; καὶ τὸ πνεῦμα τῆς χάριτος ἐνυβρίσας, “and has insulted the Spirit of grace.” This is not a believer struggling with sin, but a deliberate, contemptuous rejection of the Son, His blood and His Spirit. As with 6:4–6, Christians differ on whether such a person was ever truly born again (‘with which he was sanctified’ may describe the outward setting apart of a member of the covenant community); all agree the warning is real.","words":[{"strongs":"G5501","hebrew":"χείρων","translit":"cheírōn","english":"worse"},{"strongs":"G5098","hebrew":"τιμωρία","translit":"timōría","english":"punishment"},{"strongs":"G515","hebrew":"ἀξιόω","translit":"axióō","english":"judged worthy"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2662","hebrew":"καταπατέω","translit":"katapatéō","english":"trodden under foot"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G2840","hebrew":"κοινόω","translit":"koinóō","english":"unholy"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"counted"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G1796","hebrew":"ἐνυβρίζω","translit":"enybrízō","english":"insulted"}]},{"v":30,"text":"For we know him who said, “Vengeance belongs to me,” says the Lord, “I will repay.” Again, “The Lord will judge his people.”","understanding":"οἴδαμεν γὰρ τὸν εἰπόντα, “For we know him who said” — οἶδα from the know family. Two quotations from the Song of Moses (Deuteronomy 32): “Vengeance belongs to me,” says the Lord, “I will repay.” Again, “The Lord will judge his people.” Paul quotes the first to tell believers not to take revenge (Romans 12:19). Here it warns that God Himself will judge those who despise His Son — even among His people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1557","hebrew":"ἐκδίκησις","translit":"ekdíkēsis","english":"Vengeance"},{"strongs":"G467","hebrew":"ἀνταποδίδωμι","translit":"antapodídōmi","english":"repay"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":31,"text":"It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.","understanding":"φοβερὸν τὸ ἐμπεσεῖν εἰς χεῖρας θεοῦ ζῶντος, “It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.” The living God is not an idea to be managed, but a holy Person. For those who reject His mercy, His hands mean judgement; for those who trust Him, the same hands mean safety (John 10:29).","words":[{"strongs":"G5398","hebrew":"φοβερός","translit":"phoberós","english":"fearful"},{"strongs":"G1706","hebrew":"ἐμπίπτω","translit":"empíptō","english":"fall"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":32,"text":"But remember the former days, in which, after you were enlightened, you endured a great struggle with sufferings;","understanding":"Then encouragement. ἀναμιμνῄσκεσθε δὲ τὰς πρότερον ἡμέρας, “But remember the former days” — ἀναμιμνῄσκω, call to mind. ἐν αἷς φωτισθέντες, “in which, after you were enlightened” — the same word as 6:4: they had received the light. πολλὴν ἄθλησιν ὑπεμείνατε παθημάτων, “you endured a great struggle with sufferings” — ἄθλησις, an athletic contest (our ‘athlete’). They had already competed well.","words":[{"strongs":"G363","hebrew":"ἀναμιμνήσκω","translit":"anamimnḗskō","english":"remember"},{"strongs":"G4387","hebrew":"πρότερος","translit":"próteros","english":"former"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"enlightened"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G119","hebrew":"ἄθλησις","translit":"áthlēsis","english":"struggle"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"}]},{"v":33,"text":"partly, being exposed to both reproaches and oppressions; and partly, becoming partakers with those who were treated so.","understanding":"τοῦτο μὲν ὀνειδισμοῖς τε καὶ θλίψεσιν θεατριζόμενοι, “partly, being exposed to both reproaches and oppressions” — θεατρίζω, made a public spectacle, as in a theatre. τοῦτο δὲ κοινωνοὶ τῶν οὕτως ἀναστρεφομένων γενηθέντες, “and partly, becoming partakers with those who were treated so” — they stood with others who suffered, rather than distancing themselves.","words":[{"strongs":"G3680","hebrew":"ὀνειδισμός","translit":"oneidismós","english":"reproaches"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"oppressions"},{"strongs":"G2301","hebrew":"θεατρίζω","translit":"theatrízō","english":"exposed"},{"strongs":"G2844","hebrew":"κοινωνός","translit":"koinōnós","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G390","hebrew":"ἀναστρέφω","translit":"anastréphō","english":"treated"}]},{"v":34,"text":"For you both had compassion on me in my chains, and joyfully accepted the plundering of your possessions, knowing that you have for yourselves a better possession and an enduring one in the heavens.","understanding":"καὶ γὰρ τοῖς δεσμοῖς μου συνεπαθήσατε, “For you both had compassion on me in my chains” — συμπαθέω, the word used of Jesus sympathising with our weakness (4:15). (Many manuscripts read ‘on the prisoners’ rather than ‘on me in my chains’.) καὶ τὴν ἁρπαγὴν τῶν ὑπαρχόντων ὑμῶν μετὰ χαρᾶς προσεδέξασθε, “and joyfully accepted the plundering of your possessions” — they let their goods be seized with joy! γινώσκοντες ἔχειν ἑαυτοῖς κρείττονα ὕπαρξιν καὶ μένουσαν, “knowing that you have for yourselves a better possession and an enduring one in the heavens” — κρείττων, ‘better’; γινώσκω from the know family.","words":[{"strongs":"G4834","hebrew":"συμπαθέω","translit":"sympathéō","english":"had compassion"},{"strongs":"G1199","hebrew":"δεσμόν","translit":"desmón","english":"chains"},{"strongs":"G724","hebrew":"ἁρπαγή","translit":"harpagḗ","english":"plundering"},{"strongs":"G5224","hebrew":"ὑπάρχοντα","translit":"hypárchonta","english":"possessions"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joyfully"},{"strongs":"G4327","hebrew":"προσδέχομαι","translit":"prosdéchomai","english":"accepted"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G5223","hebrew":"ὕπαρξις","translit":"hýparxis","english":"possession"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"enduring"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"}]},{"v":35,"text":"Therefore don’t throw away your boldness, which has a great reward.","understanding":"μὴ ἀποβάλητε οὖν τὴν παρρησίαν ὑμῶν, “Therefore don’t throw away your boldness” — ἀποβάλλω, to fling away, like a soldier throwing away his shield in panic. ἥτις ἔχει μεγάλην μισθαποδοσίαν, “which has a great reward.” The same confidence (παρρησία) that lets us enter God's presence (v. 19) must not be discarded.","words":[{"strongs":"G577","hebrew":"ἀποβάλλω","translit":"apobállō","english":"throw away"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3405","hebrew":"μισθαποδοσία","translit":"misthapodosía","english":"reward"}]},{"v":36,"text":"For you need endurance so that, having done the will of God, you may receive the promise.","understanding":"ὑπομονῆς γὰρ ἔχετε χρείαν, “For you need endurance” — ὑπομονή, staying power under pressure. ἵνα τὸ θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ ποιήσαντες κομίσησθε τὴν ἐπαγγελίαν, “so that, having done the will of God, you may receive the promise.” Like Christ, who came ‘to do your will’ (v. 7), believers do God's will and wait for the promise.","words":[{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"endurance"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"done"},{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"}]},{"v":37,"text":"“In a very little while, he who comes will come, and will not wait.","understanding":"Now Habakkuk 2, in its Greek form: “In a very little while, he who comes will come, and will not wait.” μικρὸν ὅσον ὅσον, ‘a little, how little!’ — the wait is short. χρονίζω, to delay — from χρόνος in the time family. He will not take more time than necessary.","words":[{"strongs":"G3397","hebrew":"μικρόν","translit":"mikrón","english":"little while"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"comes"},{"strongs":"G5549","hebrew":"χρονίζω","translit":"chronízō","english":"wait"}]},{"v":38,"text":"But the righteous will live by faith. If he shrinks back, my soul has no pleasure in him.”","understanding":"“But the righteous will live by faith.” The verse Paul used to show that righteousness comes by faith (Romans 1:17; Galatians 3:11); here it describes the life of faith that endures. “If he shrinks back, my soul has no pleasure in him.” ὑποστέλλω, to draw back, like sailors shortening sail in fear of a storm. ψυχή from the life family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5288","hebrew":"ὑποστέλλω","translit":"hypostéllō","english":"shrinks back"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"pleasure"}]},{"v":39,"text":"But we are not of those who shrink back to destruction, but of those who have faith to the saving of the soul.","understanding":"ἡμεῖς δὲ οὐκ ἐσμὲν ὑποστολῆς εἰς ἀπώλειαν, “But we are not of those who shrink back to destruction,” ἀλλὰ πίστεως εἰς περιποίησιν ψυχῆς, “but of those who have faith to the saving of the soul.” The warning ends in confidence: ‘we’ — the writer includes himself with the readers among those who believe. And this leads straight into the great chapter on faith.","words":[{"strongs":"G5289","hebrew":"ὑποστολή","translit":"hypostolḗ","english":"shrink back"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4047","hebrew":"περιποίησις","translit":"peripoíēsis","english":"saving"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Standing and Sitting","verseRef":"v. 11-14","image":"Picture the temple court at sunset. A priest stands at the altar, exhausted, sleeves stained, the smoke of the evening sacrifice rising. Tomorrow he will stand here again, and the day after, offering the same sacrifices that never quite finish the job. There is no chair for him. Now picture a throne in heaven. On it sits One who made one offering, once, and then sat down — not from tiredness, but because the work was complete.","meaning":"“Every priest indeed stands day by day serving and often offering the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins” (v. 11), “but he, when he had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, sat down on the right hand of God” (v. 12). “For by one offering he has perfected forever those who are being sanctified” (v. 14). Your acceptance before God does not depend on daily sacrifices. Rest in the One who sat down."},{"title":"The Curtain Torn, the Way Open","verseRef":"v. 19-22","image":"Picture a great curtain hanging in a dark temple, thick and heavy, embroidered with cherubim. For centuries it has barred the way. Then picture it ripped from top to bottom, and light flooding out from the inner room. A crowd of ordinary people — not priests — walk forward, hesitant at first, then confident, into the presence of God.","meaning":"“Having therefore, brothers, boldness to enter into the holy place by the blood of Jesus” (v. 19), “by the way which he dedicated for us, a new and living way, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh” (v. 20), “let’s draw near with a true heart in fullness of faith” (v. 22). Jesus' torn body opened the way. You are invited in. Do not stand outside."},{"title":"Don't Throw Away Your Shield","verseRef":"v. 32-39","image":"Picture an ancient battlefield. Early in the fight a soldier stood firm, shield raised, taking blow after blow. Now, hours later, exhausted and wounded, he is tempted to fling his shield away and run. His commander shouts across the field: ‘Remember how you stood at the beginning! Don't throw it away! Reinforcements are coming — hold on a little longer!’","meaning":"“But remember the former days, in which, after you were enlightened, you endured a great struggle with sufferings” (v. 32). “Therefore don’t throw away your boldness, which has a great reward” (v. 35). “For you need endurance so that, having done the will of God, you may receive the promise” (v. 36). “In a very little while, he who comes will come, and will not wait” (v. 37). Remember God's past faithfulness, hold your confidence, and endure."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Law's Shadow and Its Limits (v. 1-4)","body":"The law had only a shadow of the good things to come, not their true form, so its repeated yearly sacrifices could never perfect worshippers; otherwise they would have stopped, since cleansed worshippers would have no more consciousness of sin; instead the sacrifices were a yearly reminder of sin, because animal blood cannot take sins away."},{"label":"The Obedient Son's Single Sacrifice (v. 5-18)","body":"Coming into the world, Christ said in Psalm 40 that God did not desire sacrifices and offerings but prepared a body for Him to do God's will; He removes the first to establish the second, and by God's will we are sanctified through the once-for-all offering of His body; the priests stand daily offering sacrifices that cannot remove sins, but Christ offered one sacrifice and sat down at God's right hand, perfecting forever those being sanctified, as the Spirit testifies in the new-covenant promise that God will remember sins no more — so no further offering for sin remains."},{"label":"Draw Near, Hold Fast, Stir Up (v. 19-25)","body":"Since we have confidence to enter the holy place by Jesus' blood through the new and living way of His flesh, and a great priest over God's house, let us draw near with true hearts in full assurance of faith, hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering, and consider how to stir one another to love and good works, not neglecting to meet together but encouraging one another, all the more as the Day approaches."},{"label":"The Fifth Warning (v. 26-31)","body":"If we go on sinning deliberately after receiving the knowledge of the truth, no sacrifice for sins remains, only a fearful expectation of judgement; if rejecting Moses' law brought death, how much worse for one who has trampled the Son of God, profaned the covenant blood and insulted the Spirit of grace; it is fearful to fall into the hands of the living God."},{"label":"Remember, Endure, Believe (v. 32-39)","body":"Remember the earlier days when, after being enlightened, you endured hard struggles, public insults and the seizure of property with joy, knowing you had a better and lasting possession; do not throw away your confidence, but endure, because the coming One will come soon, the righteous live by faith, and we are not those who shrink back to destruction but those who believe and are saved."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Shadow and Substance, Standing and Sitting","paragraphs":["Chapter 10 completes the argument about sacrifice. “For the law, having a shadow of the good to come, not the very image of the things, can never with the same sacrifices year by year, which they offer continually, make perfect those who draw near” (v. 1). Two picture-words make the contrast: the law was a σκιά, a shadow, not the εἰκών, the real form. The proof is repetition: if the sacrifices had worked, they would have stopped. Instead, “in those sacrifices there is a yearly reminder of sins” (v. 3), because “it is impossible that the blood of bulls and goats should take away sins” (v. 4).","What God wanted was not more animals but a perfectly obedient person. So the Son, coming into the world, says in Psalm 40, “Sacrifice and offering you didn’t desire, but you prepared a body for me” (v. 5), and “Behold, I have come (in the scroll of the book it is written of me) to do your will, O God” (v. 7). All four offering-family words appear in verse 8, and all are set aside. “He takes away the first, that he may establish the second” (v. 9). “by which will we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all” (v. 10).","The contrast between the old priests and Christ is captured in their posture: “Every priest indeed stands day by day serving and often offering the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins” (v. 11), “but he, when he had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, sat down on the right hand of God” (v. 12). The result: “For by one offering he has perfected forever those who are being sanctified” (v. 14). Believers are already perfected in standing, and are being sanctified in practice. Because God promises, “I will remember their sins and their iniquities no more” (v. 17), “there is no more offering for sin” (v. 18)."]},{"heading":"Draw Near, Hold Fast, Stir One Another Up","paragraphs":["Now comes the great application. “Having therefore, brothers, boldness to enter into the holy place by the blood of Jesus” (v. 19), “by the way which he dedicated for us, a new and living way, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh” (v. 20), and “a great priest over God’s house” (v. 21) — three ‘let us’ commands follow, shaped by faith, hope and love.","Faith: “let’s draw near with a true heart in fullness of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and having our body washed with pure water” (v. 22). Hope: “let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering; for he who promised is faithful” (v. 23). Love: “Let us consider how to provoke one another to love and good works” (v. 24). The hold-family word κατέχω calls for a firm grip; the word for ‘provoke’ usually describes quarrels, but here it means stirring each other up to love.","And this happens in community: “not forsaking our own assembling together, as the custom of some is, but exhorting one another; and so much the more, as you see the Day approaching” (v. 25). Some had stopped meeting. The writer knows that isolated believers drift; gathered believers encourage each other. The early church “continued steadfastly in the apostles’ teaching and fellowship, in the breaking of bread, and prayer” (Acts 2:42)."]},{"heading":"A Fearful Warning and a Confident Hope","paragraphs":["The fifth warning is the most severe: “For if we sin willfully after we have received the knowledge of the truth, there remains no more a sacrifice for sins” (v. 26), “but a certain fearful expectation of judgment, and a fierceness of fire which will devour the adversaries” (v. 27). This does not describe ordinary sins that believers confess, but a settled, deliberate rejection: one who “has trodden under foot the Son of God, and has counted the blood of the covenant with which he was sanctified an unholy thing, and has insulted the Spirit of grace” (v. 29). If you reject the only sacrifice, there is no other. “It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God” (v. 31). As with chapter 6, sincere Christians interpret the passage differently, but its warning is plain and real.","Then the writer turns, as he did in chapter 6, to encouragement. “But remember the former days, in which, after you were enlightened, you endured a great struggle with sufferings” (v. 32). They had been publicly insulted, had stood with those in prison, and had “joyfully accepted the plundering of your possessions, knowing that you have for yourselves a better possession and an enduring one in the heavens” (v. 34). Jesus said, “Rejoice, and be exceedingly glad, for great is your reward in heaven” (Matthew 5:12).","So: “Therefore don’t throw away your boldness, which has a great reward” (v. 35). “For you need endurance so that, having done the will of God, you may receive the promise” (v. 36). The Lord is coming soon, and “the righteous will live by faith” (v. 38). The chapter ends with confidence: “But we are not of those who shrink back to destruction, but of those who have faith to the saving of the soul” (v. 39). That word ‘faith’ opens the door to chapter 11."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Remembrance, Faith and Endurance","paragraphs":["The ‘reminder of sins’ (ἀνάμνησις) in 10:3 is the same word Jesus used at the Last Supper: “This is my body which is given for you. Do this in memory of me” (Luke 22:19). Paul records, “This is my body, which is broken for you. Do this in memory of me” (1 Corinthians 11:24). The old sacrifices reminded people of their sins; the Lord's Supper reminds believers of the One who took them away.","The verse “the righteous will live by faith” (10:38) is quoted by Paul as the heart of the gospel: “But the righteous shall live by faith” (Romans 1:17), and “The righteous will live by faith” (Galatians 3:11). The words about vengeance in 10:30 are used by Paul to tell believers not to retaliate: “Don’t seek revenge yourselves, beloved, but give place to God’s wrath” (Romans 12:19). Judgement belongs to God, not to us.","The call to endure suffering with joy echoes throughout the New Testament. The apostles left the council “rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for Jesus’ name” (Acts 5:41). Peter says trials prove faith “more precious than gold that perishes” (1 Peter 1:7). Jesus said, “lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust consume” (Matthew 6:20). And James urges, “You also be patient. Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord is at hand” (James 5:8)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Reminder / remembrance","hebrew":"ἀνάμνησις","translit":"anámnēsis","strongs":"G364","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “recollection.” It is used only four times in the New Testament: three times in Jesus' words at the Last Supper, ‘Do this in memory of me’, and here in Hebrews 10:3. The contrast is striking. The old sacrifices were a yearly remembrance of sins, keeping guilt in view. The Lord's Supper is a remembrance of Christ, keeping His finished sacrifice in view."},{"english":"New / freshly made","hebrew":"πρόσφατος","translit":"prósphatos","strongs":"G4372","verses":"v. 20","insight":"Strong's: “previously (recently) slain (fresh), i.e. (figuratively) lately made.” Its original meaning was ‘freshly slaughtered’, though by New Testament times it usually meant simply ‘new’ or ‘recent’. Either sense fits Hebrews 10:20: the way into God's presence was opened by a fresh sacrifice, and it is always new — never stale, never closed. This study adds it to the new family beside καινός and νέος."},{"english":"Without wavering / unbending","hebrew":"ἀκλινής","translit":"aklinḗs","strongs":"G186","verses":"v. 23","insight":"Strong's: “not leaning, i.e. (figuratively) firm.” It appears only here in the New Testament. It pictures something standing upright, not tilting to one side under pressure. Believers are to hold the confession of their hope without leaning toward doubt or compromise — not because they are strong, but because ‘he who promised is faithful’."},{"english":"Provoke / stir up","hebrew":"παροξυσμός","translit":"paroxysmós","strongs":"G3948","verses":"v. 24","insight":"Strong's: “incitement (to good), or dispute (in anger).” Its only other New Testament use is the ‘sharp contention’ between Paul and Barnabas (Acts 15:39). Hebrews turns a word for conflict into a word for love: believers should put as much energy into stirring each other up to love and good works as people usually put into arguments."},{"english":"Shrink back / draw back","hebrew":"ὑποστέλλω","translit":"hypostéllō","strongs":"G5288","verses":"v. 38","insight":"Strong's: “to withhold under (out of sight), i.e. (reflexively) to cower or shrink, (figuratively) to conceal (reserve).” Paul used it when he said he had ‘not shrunk back’ from declaring the whole counsel of God (Acts 20:27). It pictures timid retreat when faith is costly. The writer is confident that his readers are not those who shrink back, but those who believe."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you a finished sacrifice, an open way, and the endurance faith requires.","items":[{"title":"He Sat Down — So Can You","body":"Christ's work is finished; He sat down. The Spirit wants you to stop standing anxiously before God as if your acceptance still depended on your offerings. You have been perfected forever by His one offering. Rest in that, and let your sanctification flow from security rather than fear."},{"title":"Come Near, Hold On, Stir Up","body":"The Spirit calls you to three actions: draw near to God in faith, hold fast to your hope, and stir up others to love. Ask Him which of these you are neglecting. Is there someone you should invite back into fellowship this week?"},{"title":"Do Not Throw Away Your Confidence","body":"Perhaps you are weary after a long struggle. The Spirit reminds you of past faithfulness — yours and His — and urges you not to throw away your confidence now. The One who is coming will come. Ask Him for fresh endurance today."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Rest Check","body":"Do you still feel you must ‘do something’ to stay acceptable to God? Read verses 12 and 14 slowly, and thank Christ for His finished work."},{"title":"The Fellowship Check","body":"Have you been neglecting to meet with other believers (v. 25)? What would it take to be consistently present? Who could you encourage to come with you?"},{"title":"The Endurance Check","body":"Where are you tempted to throw away your confidence or shrink back (vv. 35, 38)? Write down one way God has been faithful in the past, and let it strengthen you now."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Provoke Someone to Love","body":"Each week this month, deliberately ‘provoke’ one believer to love and good works (v. 24): invite them to serve with you, share a need they could meet, or simply tell them how you have seen God use them. Keep track and pray for each one."},{"title":"Remember the Former Days","body":"Write a short account of your early days of faith — what drew you to Christ, what you endured, how God sustained you (v. 32). Read it when you feel weary. Share it with a younger believer as an encouragement to endure."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You that the law's shadows have given way to the reality in Christ. Animal blood could never take away sins, but Your Son came into the world, saying, ‘I have come to do your will,’ and offered His body once for all. Thank You that I have been sanctified by that offering, and that by one offering He has perfected forever those who are being sanctified. He sat down, because His work is finished. You remember my sins and lawless deeds no more. So I come with confidence into Your presence by the blood of Jesus, through the new and living way He opened through His flesh. I draw near with a sincere heart in full assurance of faith. I hold fast the confession of my hope without wavering, because You who promised are faithful. Help me stir up others to love and good works, and never to neglect meeting with Your people. Keep me from ever trampling the Son of God or insulting the Spirit of grace. When I grow weary, help me remember the former days, refuse to throw away my confidence, and endure until I receive what You have promised. I am not one who shrinks back, but one who believes. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Psalm 40: ‘I Have Come to Do Your Will’","ref":"Psalm 40:6-8","relatedVerses":"v. 5-10","summary":"In Psalm 40 David praises God for lifting him out of a pit and putting a new song in his mouth. Then he says that God did not delight in sacrifices and offerings; instead, he comes willingly to do God's will, for God's law is within his heart. Hebrews 10 hears the Son speaking these words as He enters the world. What David desired imperfectly, Jesus did perfectly: He came in a body prepared for Him, delighted to do the Father's will, and offered that body once for all."},{"title":"Gethsemane: ‘Not My Will, But Yours’","ref":"Luke 22:39-46","relatedVerses":"v. 7, 9-10","summary":"On the night before the cross, Jesus knelt in the garden and prayed, ‘Father, if you are willing, remove this cup from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but yours, be done.’ An angel strengthened Him, and in anguish He prayed more earnestly. Then He rose to face His betrayer. Hebrews 10 says, ‘Behold, I have come to do your will.’ In Gethsemane we see the cost of that obedience, and on the cross we see its fulfilment: ‘by which will we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all’ (v. 10)."},{"title":"The Apostles Rejoice in Suffering","ref":"Acts 5:17-42","relatedVerses":"v. 32-35","summary":"The high priest had the apostles arrested for preaching about Jesus. An angel released them, and they went straight back to teaching in the temple. Brought before the council again, Peter declared that they must obey God rather than men. After advice from Gamaliel, the council had them beaten and ordered them not to speak in Jesus' name. “They therefore departed from the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for Jesus’ name” (Acts 5:41). Like the readers of Hebrews in their early days, they accepted suffering with joy, knowing they had a better possession."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":11,"chapterTitle":"The Great Cloud of Faith","themeTitle":"“Faith Is Assurance of Things Hoped For”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, proof of things not seen.","understanding":"ἔστιν δὲ πίστις ἐλπιζομένων ὑπόστασις, “Now faith is assurance of things hoped for” — ὑπόστασις, the same word as ‘substance’ in 1:3 and ‘confidence’ in 3:14: the solid ground beneath, the title deed. Faith treats God's future promises as present realities. πραγμάτων ἔλεγχος οὐ βλεπομένων, “proof of things not seen” — ἔλεγχος, conviction, evidence that convinces (the ‘reproof’ word of 2 Timothy 3:16). βλέπω from the see family: what cannot yet be seen with the eyes. This is not a full definition of faith but a description of what it does.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5287","hebrew":"ὑπόστασις","translit":"hypóstasis","english":"assurance"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"hoped for"},{"strongs":"G1650","hebrew":"ἔλεγχος","translit":"élenchos","english":"proof"},{"strongs":"G4229","hebrew":"πρᾶγμα","translit":"prâgma","english":"things"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"seen"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For by this, the elders obtained testimony.","understanding":"ἐν ταύτῃ γὰρ ἐμαρτυρήθησαν οἱ πρεσβύτεροι, “For by this, the elders obtained testimony” — οἱ πρεσβύτεροι, the people of old, the ancestors. μαρτυρέω, God bore witness about them, approving them. The rest of the chapter shows how.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"obtained testimony"}]},{"v":3,"text":"By faith, we understand that the universe has been framed by the word of God, so that what is seen has not been made out of things which are visible.","understanding":"πίστει νοοῦμεν κατηρτίσθαι τοὺς αἰῶνας ῥήματι θεοῦ, “By faith, we understand that the universe has been framed by the word of God.” Three word-family points. αἰών, here ‘universe’ — the ages, the whole created order (as ‘worlds’ in 1:2). καταρτίζω from the perfect family: fitted together, set in order. ῥῆμα from the word family: God's spoken command (‘Let there be light’), not λόγος, the message. εἰς τὸ μὴ ἐκ φαινομένων τὸ βλεπόμενον γεγονέναι, “so that what is seen has not been made out of things which are visible” — φαίνω from the appear family and βλέπω from the see family. The visible world came from the invisible word.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3539","hebrew":"νοιέω","translit":"noiéō","english":"understand"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"universe"},{"strongs":"G2675","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","english":"framed"},{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"visible"}]},{"v":4,"text":"By faith, Abel offered to God a more excellent sacrifice than Cain, through which he had testimony given to him that he was righteous, God testifying with respect to his gifts; and through it he, being dead, still speaks.","understanding":"πίστει πλείονα θυσίαν Ἄβελ παρὰ Κάϊν προσήνεγκεν τῷ θεῷ, “By faith, Abel offered to God a more excellent sacrifice than Cain” — θυσία and προσφέρω from the offering family. Genesis does not say exactly why Abel's was better; Hebrews says it was faith, and John adds that Cain's ‘deeds were evil’. δι᾽ ἧς ἐμαρτυρήθη εἶναι δίκαιος, “through which he had testimony given to him that he was righteous.” καὶ δι᾽ αὐτῆς ἀποθανὼν ἔτι λαλεῖ, “and through it he, being dead, still speaks.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G6","hebrew":"Ἄβελ","translit":"Ábel","english":"Abel"},{"strongs":"G4119","hebrew":"πλείων","translit":"pleíōn","english":"more excellent"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G2535","hebrew":"Κάϊν","translit":"Káïn","english":"Cain"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G1435","hebrew":"δῶρον","translit":"dōron","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaks"}]},{"v":5,"text":"By faith, Enoch was taken away, so that he wouldn’t see death, and he was not found, because God translated him. For he has had testimony given to him that before his translation he had been well pleasing to God.","understanding":"πίστει Ἐνὼχ μετετέθη τοῦ μὴ ἰδεῖν θάνατον, “By faith, Enoch was taken away, so that he wouldn’t see death” — μετατίθημι, transferred, translated. Genesis 5 says only that ‘Enoch walked with God, and he was not, for God took him’. εὐηρεστηκέναι τῷ θεῷ, “he had been well pleasing to God” — the Greek Old Testament's way of saying he ‘walked with God’.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1802","hebrew":"Ἐνώχ","translit":"Enṓch","english":"Enoch"},{"strongs":"G3346","hebrew":"μετατίθημι","translit":"metatíthēmi","english":"taken away"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G3331","hebrew":"μετάθεσις","translit":"metáthesis","english":"translation"},{"strongs":"G2100","hebrew":"εὐαρεστέω","translit":"euarestéō","english":"well pleasing"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Without faith it is impossible to be well pleasing to him, for he who comes to God must believe that he exists, and that he is a rewarder of those who seek him.","understanding":"χωρὶς δὲ πίστεως ἀδύνατον εὐαρεστῆσαι, “Without faith it is impossible to be well pleasing to him” — the conclusion from Enoch. πιστεῦσαι γὰρ δεῖ τὸν προσερχόμενον τῷ θεῷ ὅτι ἔστιν, “for he who comes to God must believe that he exists,” καὶ τοῖς ἐκζητοῦσιν αὐτὸν μισθαποδότης γίνεται, “and that he is a rewarder of those who seek him.” Faith believes two things: that God is, and that God is good to those who seek Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"Without"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G102","hebrew":"ἀδύνατος","translit":"adýnatos","english":"impossible"},{"strongs":"G2100","hebrew":"εὐαρεστέω","translit":"euarestéō","english":"well pleasing"},{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"comes"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G3406","hebrew":"μισθαποδότης","translit":"misthapodótēs","english":"rewarder"},{"strongs":"G1567","hebrew":"ἐκζητέω","translit":"ekzētéō","english":"seek"}]},{"v":7,"text":"By faith, Noah, being warned about things not yet seen, moved with godly fear, prepared a ship for the saving of his house, through which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is according to faith.","understanding":"πίστει χρηματισθεὶς Νῶε περὶ τῶν μηδέπω βλεπομένων, “By faith, Noah, being warned about things not yet seen” — no one had ever seen a flood like it. εὐλαβηθείς, “moved with godly fear” — εὐλαβέομαι, added to the godliness family: reverent awe that acts. κατεσκεύασεν κιβωτόν εἰς σωτηρίαν τοῦ οἴκου αὐτοῦ, “prepared a ship for the saving of his house.” δι᾽ ἧς κατέκρινεν τὸν κόσμον, “through which he condemned the world” — κόσμος from the world family: his obedience exposed their unbelief. καὶ τῆς κατὰ πίστιν δικαιοσύνης ἐγένετο κληρονόμος, “and became heir of the righteousness which is according to faith.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3575","hebrew":"Νῶε","translit":"Nōe","english":"Noah"},{"strongs":"G5537","hebrew":"χρηματίζω","translit":"chrēmatízō","english":"warned"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G2125","hebrew":"εὐλαβέομαι","translit":"eulabéomai","english":"godly fear"},{"strongs":"G2680","hebrew":"κατασκευάζω","translit":"kataskeuázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G2787","hebrew":"κιβωτός","translit":"kibōtós","english":"ship"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"saving"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G2632","hebrew":"κατακρίνω","translit":"katakrínō","english":"condemned"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2818","hebrew":"κληρονόμος","translit":"klēronómos","english":"heir"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"}]},{"v":8,"text":"By faith, Abraham, when he was called, obeyed to go out to the place which he was to receive for an inheritance. He went out, not knowing where he went.","understanding":"πίστει καλούμενος Ἀβραὰμ ὑπήκουσεν ἐξελθεῖν, “By faith, Abraham, when he was called, obeyed to go out” — the call and the obedience come together; ὑπακούω, to listen and obey. εἰς τόπον ὃν ἤμελλεν λαμβάνειν εἰς κληρονομίαν, “to the place which he was to receive for an inheritance.” καὶ ἐξῆλθεν μὴ ἐπιστάμενος ποῦ ἔρχεται, “He went out, not knowing where he went” — ἐπίσταμαι, added to the know family: to know well, to understand. He did not know the destination, but he knew the One who called.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G5219","hebrew":"ὑπακούω","translit":"hypakoúō","english":"obeyed"},{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"go out"},{"strongs":"G2817","hebrew":"κληρονομία","translit":"klēronomía","english":"inheritance"},{"strongs":"G1987","hebrew":"ἐπίσταμαι","translit":"epístamai","english":"knowing"}]},{"v":9,"text":"By faith, he lived as an alien in the land of promise, as in a land not his own, dwelling in tents, with Isaac and Jacob, the heirs with him of the same promise.","understanding":"πίστει παρῴκησεν εἰς γῆν τῆς ἐπαγγελίας ὡς ἀλλοτρίαν, “By faith, he lived as an alien in the land of promise, as in a land not his own” — παροικέω, to live as a resident foreigner. ἐν σκηναῖς κατοικήσας μετὰ Ἰσαὰκ καὶ Ἰακώβ, “dwelling in tents, with Isaac and Jacob, the heirs with him of the same promise.” Three generations lived in the promised land in tents, never owning it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3939","hebrew":"παροικέω","translit":"paroikéō","english":"lived as an alien"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"land"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G245","hebrew":"ἀλλότριος","translit":"allótrios","english":"not his own"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tents"},{"strongs":"G2464","hebrew":"Ἰσαάκ","translit":"Isaák","english":"Isaac"},{"strongs":"G2384","hebrew":"Ἰακώβ","translit":"Iakṓb","english":"Jacob"},{"strongs":"G4789","hebrew":"συγκληρονόμος","translit":"synklēronómos","english":"heirs with him"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For he looked for the city which has the foundations, whose builder and maker is God.","understanding":"ἐξεδέχετο γὰρ τὴν τοὺς θεμελίους ἔχουσαν πόλιν, “For he looked for the city which has the foundations” — tents have pegs; this city has foundations. ἧς τεχνίτης καὶ δημιουργὸς ὁ θεός, “whose builder and maker is God” — τεχνίτης, a craftsman, architect; δημιουργός, a builder. Abraham looked beyond Canaan to God's eternal city.","words":[{"strongs":"G1551","hebrew":"ἐκδέχομαι","translit":"ekdéchomai","english":"looked for"},{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundations"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G5079","hebrew":"τεχνίτης","translit":"technítēs","english":"builder"},{"strongs":"G1217","hebrew":"δημιουργός","translit":"dēmiourgós","english":"maker"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":11,"text":"By faith, even Sarah herself received power to conceive, and she bore a child when she was past age, since she counted him faithful who had promised.","understanding":"πίστει καὶ αὐτὴ Σάρρα δύναμιν εἰς καταβολὴν σπέρματος ἔλαβεν, “By faith, even Sarah herself received power to conceive” — δύναμις from the power family. καὶ παρὰ καιρὸν ἡλικίας, “and she bore a child when she was past age” — καιρός from the time family: past the season. ἐπεὶ πιστὸν ἡγήσατο τὸν ἐπαγγειλάμενον, “since she counted him faithful who had promised.” (Some read the sentence with Abraham as the subject: ‘By faith Abraham, with Sarah herself, received power...’. Sarah had laughed in unbelief at first, but she came to trust.)","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4564","hebrew":"Σάῤῥα","translit":"Sárrha","english":"Sarah"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2602","hebrew":"καταβολή","translit":"katabolḗ","english":"conceive"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"past"},{"strongs":"G2244","hebrew":"ἡλικία","translit":"hēlikía","english":"age"},{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"bore a child"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promised"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Therefore as many as the stars of the sky in multitude, and as innumerable as the sand which is by the sea shore, were fathered by one man, and him as good as dead.","understanding":"διὸ καὶ ἀφ᾽ ἑνὸς ἐγεννήθησαν, καὶ ταῦτα νενεκρωμένου, “Therefore... were fathered by one man, and him as good as dead” — νεκρόω, deadened: Abraham was about a hundred. καθὼς τὰ ἄστρα τοῦ οὐρανοῦ τῷ πλήθει, “as many as the stars of the sky in multitude, and as innumerable as the sand which is by the sea shore” — God's promise fulfilled from a body that was ‘as good as dead’: a picture of resurrection.","words":[{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"fathered"},{"strongs":"G3499","hebrew":"νεκρόω","translit":"nekróō","english":"as good as dead"},{"strongs":"G798","hebrew":"ἄστρον","translit":"ástron","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G285","hebrew":"ἄμμος","translit":"ámmos","english":"sand"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G382","hebrew":"ἀναρίθμητος","translit":"anaríthmētos","english":"innumerable"}]},{"v":13,"text":"These all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them and embraced them from afar, and having confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth.","understanding":"κατὰ πίστιν ἀπέθανον οὗτοι πάντες, “These all died in faith” — they did not die in disappointment but in faith. μὴ λαβόντες τὰς ἐπαγγελίας, “not having received the promises,” ἀλλὰ πόρρωθεν αὐτὰς ἰδόντες καὶ ἀσπασάμενοι, “but having seen them and embraced them from afar” — ἀσπάζομαι, to greet, like travellers waving to a city on the horizon. ὅτι ξένοι καὶ παρεπίδημοί εἰσιν ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “and having confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promises"},{"strongs":"G4206","hebrew":"πόῤῥω","translit":"pórrhō","english":"afar"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"embraced"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confessed"},{"strongs":"G3581","hebrew":"ξένος","translit":"xénos","english":"strangers"},{"strongs":"G3927","hebrew":"παρεπίδημος","translit":"parepídēmos","english":"pilgrims"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For those who say such things make it clear that they are seeking a country of their own.","understanding":"οἱ γὰρ τοιαῦτα λέγοντες ἐμφανίζουσιν, “For those who say such things make it clear” — ἐμφανίζω from the appear family: show openly. ὅτι πατρίδα ἐπιζητοῦσιν, “that they are seeking a country of their own” — πατρίς, a fatherland, home.","words":[{"strongs":"G1718","hebrew":"ἐμφανίζω","translit":"emphanízō","english":"make it clear"},{"strongs":"G3968","hebrew":"πατρίς","translit":"patrís","english":"country"},{"strongs":"G1934","hebrew":"ἐπιζητέω","translit":"epizētéō","english":"seeking"}]},{"v":15,"text":"If indeed they had been thinking of that country from which they went out, they would have had enough time to return.","understanding":"καὶ εἰ μὲν ἐκείνης ἐμνημόνευον ἀφ᾽ ἧς ἐξέβησαν, “If indeed they had been thinking of that country from which they went out” — Ur, Haran, Mesopotamia. εἶχον ἂν καιρὸν ἀνακάμψαι, “they would have had enough time to return” — καιρός, opportunity. They could have gone back, but they did not. Faith refuses to return to the old life (as the readers were tempted to do).","words":[{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"thinking of"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G344","hebrew":"ἀνακάμπτω","translit":"anakámptō","english":"return"}]},{"v":16,"text":"But now they desire a better country, that is, a heavenly one. Therefore God is not ashamed of them, to be called their God, for he has prepared a city for them.","understanding":"νῦν δὲ κρείττονος ὀρέγονται, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν ἐπουρανίου, “But now they desire a better country, that is, a heavenly one” — κρείττων, ‘better’ again; ὀρέγομαι, to stretch out toward. διὸ οὐκ ἐπαισχύνεται αὐτοὺς ὁ θεὸς θεὸς ἐπικαλεῖσθαι αὐτῶν, “Therefore God is not ashamed of them, to be called their God” — as Jesus is not ashamed to call us brothers (2:11). ἡτοίμασεν γὰρ αὐτοῖς πόλιν, “for he has prepared a city for them.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G3713","hebrew":"ὀρέγομαι","translit":"orégomai","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G1870","hebrew":"ἐπαισχύνομαι","translit":"epaischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"}]},{"v":17,"text":"By faith, Abraham, being tested, offered up Isaac. Yes, he who had gladly received the promises was offering up his one and only son,","understanding":"πίστει προσενήνοχεν Ἀβραὰμ τὸν Ἰσαὰκ πειραζόμενος, “By faith, Abraham, being tested, offered up Isaac” — προσφέρω from the offering family; perfect tense: as good as done. καὶ τὸν μονογενῆ προσέφερεν ὁ τὰς ἐπαγγελίας ἀναδεξάμενος, “Yes, he who had gladly received the promises was offering up his one and only son” — μονογενής, unique, only, the word John uses of Jesus. The test was agonising: the son of promise was the one he was asked to give.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tested"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"offered up"},{"strongs":"G2464","hebrew":"Ἰσαάκ","translit":"Isaák","english":"Isaac"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promises"},{"strongs":"G324","hebrew":"ἀναδέχομαι","translit":"anadéchomai","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G3439","hebrew":"μονογενής","translit":"monogenḗs","english":"one and only"}]},{"v":18,"text":"to whom it was said, “your offspring will be accounted as from Isaac”;","understanding":"πρὸς ὃν ἐλαλήθη ὅτι, “to whom it was said,” “your offspring will be accounted as from Isaac” (Genesis 21). The promise depended on Isaac living. Abraham faced an apparent contradiction between God's promise and God's command.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"said"},{"strongs":"G2464","hebrew":"Ἰσαάκ","translit":"Isaák","english":"Isaac"},{"strongs":"G4690","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","english":"offspring"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"accounted"}]},{"v":19,"text":"concluding that God is able to raise up even from the dead. Figuratively speaking, he also did receive him back from the dead.","understanding":"λογισάμενος ὅτι καὶ ἐκ νεκρῶν ἐγείρειν δυνατὸς ὁ θεός, “concluding that God is able to raise up even from the dead” — λογίζομαι, he reckoned, he worked it out. ἐγείρω from the rise family. Abraham resolved the contradiction by believing in resurrection. ὅθεν αὐτὸν καὶ ἐν παραβολῇ ἐκομίσατο, “Figuratively speaking, he also did receive him back from the dead” — παραβολή from the pattern family: in a figure, a picture. Isaac's rescue on the altar was a picture of the resurrection of God's own Son.","words":[{"strongs":"G3049","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","english":"concluding"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1415","hebrew":"δυνατός","translit":"dynatós","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raise up"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G3850","hebrew":"παραβολή","translit":"parabolḗ","english":"Figuratively"},{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receive"}]},{"v":20,"text":"By faith, Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau, even concerning things to come.","understanding":"πίστει περὶ μελλόντων εὐλόγησεν Ἰσαὰκ τὸν Ἰακὼβ καὶ τὸν Ἡσαῦ, “By faith, Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau, even concerning things to come.” Even though Isaac was deceived about which son he was blessing (Genesis 27), his blessing looked forward in faith to God's future.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2464","hebrew":"Ἰσαάκ","translit":"Isaák","english":"Isaac"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G2384","hebrew":"Ἰακώβ","translit":"Iakṓb","english":"Jacob"},{"strongs":"G2269","hebrew":"Ἠσαῦ","translit":"Ēsaû","english":"Esau"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"}]},{"v":21,"text":"By faith, Jacob, when he was dying, blessed each of the sons of Joseph, and worshiped, leaning on the top of his staff.","understanding":"πίστει Ἰακὼβ ἀποθνῄσκων, “By faith, Jacob, when he was dying, blessed each of the sons of Joseph” — Ephraim and Manasseh (Genesis 48). καὶ προσεκύνησεν ἐπὶ τὸ ἄκρον τῆς ῥάβδου αὐτοῦ, “and worshiped, leaning on the top of his staff.” The Hebrew of Genesis 47:31 reads ‘the head of the bed’; the Greek Old Testament reads ‘staff’ — the same consonants with different vowels. The writer follows the Greek. Either way, the dying pilgrim worshipped.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2384","hebrew":"Ἰακώβ","translit":"Iakṓb","english":"Jacob"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"dying"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"sons"},{"strongs":"G2501","hebrew":"Ἰωσήφ","translit":"Iōsḗph","english":"Joseph"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"},{"strongs":"G206","hebrew":"ἄκρον","translit":"ákron","english":"top"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"staff"}]},{"v":22,"text":"By faith, Joseph, when his end was near, made mention of the departure of the children of Israel; and gave instructions concerning his bones.","understanding":"πίστει Ἰωσὴφ τελευτῶν περὶ τῆς ἐξόδου τῶν υἱῶν Ἰσραὴλ ἐμνημόνευσεν, “By faith, Joseph, when his end was near, made mention of the departure of the children of Israel” — ἔξοδος, the exodus, four centuries before it happened. καὶ περὶ τῶν ὀστέων αὐτοῦ ἐνετείλατο, “and gave instructions concerning his bones” — that they be carried to Canaan. Though he ruled Egypt, his heart was in the land of promise.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2501","hebrew":"Ἰωσήφ","translit":"Iōsḗph","english":"Joseph"},{"strongs":"G5053","hebrew":"τελευτάω","translit":"teleutáō","english":"end was near"},{"strongs":"G1841","hebrew":"ἔξοδος","translit":"éxodos","english":"departure"},{"strongs":"G2474","hebrew":"Ἰσραήλ","translit":"Israḗl","english":"Israel"},{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"made mention"},{"strongs":"G3747","hebrew":"ὀστέον","translit":"ostéon","english":"bones"},{"strongs":"G1781","hebrew":"ἐντέλλομαι","translit":"entéllomai","english":"gave instructions"}]},{"v":23,"text":"By faith, Moses, when he was born, was hidden for three months by his parents, because they saw that he was a beautiful child, and they were not afraid of the king’s commandment.","understanding":"πίστει Μωϋσῆς γεννηθεὶς ἐκρύβη τρίμηνον ὑπὸ τῶν πατέρων αὐτοῦ, “By faith, Moses, when he was born, was hidden for three months by his parents” — the faith here is his parents'. διότι εἶδον ἀστεῖον τὸ παιδίον, “because they saw that he was a beautiful child” — ἀστεῖος, fine, well-favoured (Stephen says ‘exceedingly handsome’). καὶ οὐκ ἐφοβήθησαν τὸ διάταγμα τοῦ βασιλέως, “and they were not afraid of the king’s commandment” — Pharaoh's order to kill Hebrew boys.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G2928","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G5150","hebrew":"τρίμηνον","translit":"trímēnon","english":"three months"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G791","hebrew":"ἀστεῖος","translit":"asteîos","english":"beautiful"},{"strongs":"G3813","hebrew":"παιδίον","translit":"paidíon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"afraid"},{"strongs":"G1297","hebrew":"διάταγμα","translit":"diátagma","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"}]},{"v":24,"text":"By faith, Moses, when he had grown up, refused to be called the son of Pharaoh’s daughter,","understanding":"πίστει Μωϋσῆς μέγας γενόμενος, “By faith, Moses, when he had grown up” — Stephen says he was about forty years old. ἠρνήσατο λέγεσθαι υἱὸς θυγατρὸς Φαραώ, “refused to be called the son of Pharaoh’s daughter” — ἀρνέομαι, renounced. He gave up royal status, wealth and power.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"grown up"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"refused"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G2364","hebrew":"θυγάτηρ","translit":"thygátēr","english":"daughter"},{"strongs":"G5328","hebrew":"Φαραώ","translit":"Pharaṓ","english":"Pharaoh"}]},{"v":25,"text":"choosing rather to share ill treatment with God’s people, than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a time;","understanding":"μᾶλλον ἑλόμενος συγκακουχεῖσθαι τῷ λαῷ τοῦ θεοῦ, “choosing rather to share ill treatment with God’s people” — αἱρέομαι, a deliberate choice. ἢ πρόσκαιρον ἔχειν ἁμαρτίας ἀπόλαυσιν, “than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a time” — πρόσκαιρος, added to the time family: built on καιρός, ‘for a season’, temporary. Sin's pleasures are real, but they are seasonal; they do not last.","words":[{"strongs":"G138","hebrew":"αἱρέομαι","translit":"hairéomai","english":"choosing"},{"strongs":"G4778","hebrew":"συγκακουχέω","translit":"synkakouchéō","english":"share ill treatment"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4340","hebrew":"πρόσκαιρος","translit":"próskairos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G619","hebrew":"ἀπόλαυσις","translit":"apólausis","english":"pleasures"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":26,"text":"accounting the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of Egypt; for he looked to the reward.","understanding":"μείζονα πλοῦτον ἡγησάμενος τῶν Αἰγύπτου θησαυρῶν τὸν ὀνειδισμὸν τοῦ Χριστοῦ, “accounting the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of Egypt.” Moses suffered the kind of reproach that God's anointed people — and ultimately the Messiah — would bear; identifying with God's people was identifying with Christ. ἀπέβλεπεν γὰρ εἰς τὴν μισθαποδοσίαν, “for he looked to the reward” — ἀποβλέπω, to look away from everything else and fix the eyes on one thing.","words":[{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"accounting"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"riches"},{"strongs":"G125","hebrew":"Αἴγυπτος","translit":"Aígyptos","english":"Egypt"},{"strongs":"G2344","hebrew":"θησαυρός","translit":"thēsaurós","english":"treasures"},{"strongs":"G3680","hebrew":"ὀνειδισμός","translit":"oneidismós","english":"reproach"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G578","hebrew":"ἀποβλέπω","translit":"apoblépō","english":"looked"},{"strongs":"G3405","hebrew":"μισθαποδοσία","translit":"misthapodosía","english":"reward"}]},{"v":27,"text":"By faith, he left Egypt, not fearing the wrath of the king; for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible.","understanding":"πίστει κατέλιπεν Αἴγυπτον, μὴ φοβηθεὶς τὸν θυμὸν τοῦ βασιλέως, “By faith, he left Egypt, not fearing the wrath of the king” — probably the Exodus itself, when he left boldly. τὸν γὰρ ἀόρατον ὡς ὁρῶν ἐκαρτέρησεν, “for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible.” ὁράω from the see family, joined with ἀόρατος, ‘invisible’ — a deliberate paradox. Faith sees the One no eye can see. καρτερέω, to be strong, steadfast.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2641","hebrew":"καταλείπω","translit":"kataleípō","english":"left"},{"strongs":"G125","hebrew":"Αἴγυπτος","translit":"Aígyptos","english":"Egypt"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fearing"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"},{"strongs":"G2594","hebrew":"καρτερέω","translit":"karteréō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seeing"},{"strongs":"G517","hebrew":"ἀόρατος","translit":"aóratos","english":"invisible"}]},{"v":28,"text":"By faith, he kept the Passover, and the sprinkling of the blood, that the destroyer of the firstborn should not touch them.","understanding":"πίστει πεποίηκεν τὸ πάσχα καὶ τὴν πρόσχυσιν τοῦ αἵματος, “By faith, he kept the Passover, and the sprinkling of the blood” — on the doorposts. ἵνα μὴ ὁ ὀλοθρεύων τὰ πρωτότοκα θίγῃ αὐτῶν, “that the destroyer of the firstborn should not touch them.” Faith trusted God's word that blood would protect. Christ is ‘our Passover’ (1 Corinthians 5:7).","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"kept"},{"strongs":"G3957","hebrew":"πάσχα","translit":"páscha","english":"Passover"},{"strongs":"G4378","hebrew":"πρόσχυσις","translit":"próschysis","english":"sprinkling"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G3645","hebrew":"ὀλοθρεύω","translit":"olothreúō","english":"destroyer"},{"strongs":"G4416","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","english":"firstborn"},{"strongs":"G2345","hebrew":"θιγγάνω","translit":"thingánō","english":"touch"}]},{"v":29,"text":"By faith, they passed through the Red Sea as on dry land. When the Egyptians tried to do so, they were swallowed up.","understanding":"πίστει διέβησαν τὴν ἐρυθρὰν θάλασσαν ὡς διὰ ξηρᾶς γῆς, “By faith, they passed through the Red Sea as on dry land.” ἧς πεῖραν λαβόντες οἱ Αἰγύπτιοι κατεπόθησαν, “When the Egyptians tried to do so, they were swallowed up.” The same sea was a road for faith and a grave for unbelief.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1224","hebrew":"διαβαίνω","translit":"diabaínō","english":"passed through"},{"strongs":"G2063","hebrew":"ἐρυθρός","translit":"erythrós","english":"Red"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"Sea"},{"strongs":"G3584","hebrew":"ξηρός","translit":"xērós","english":"dry"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"land"},{"strongs":"G124","hebrew":"Αἰγύπτιος","translit":"Aigýptios","english":"Egyptians"},{"strongs":"G3984","hebrew":"πεῖρα","translit":"peîra","english":"tried"},{"strongs":"G2666","hebrew":"καταπίνω","translit":"katapínō","english":"swallowed up"}]},{"v":30,"text":"By faith, the walls of Jericho fell down, after they had been encircled for seven days.","understanding":"πίστει τὰ τείχη Ἰεριχὼ ἔπεσαν κυκλωθέντα ἐπὶ ἑπτὰ ἡμέρας, “By faith, the walls of Jericho fell down, after they had been encircled for seven days.” Marching and shouting are not military strategy; they are obedience to a strange command, trusting God to act.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"walls"},{"strongs":"G2410","hebrew":"Ἱεριχώ","translit":"Hierichṓ","english":"Jericho"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell down"},{"strongs":"G2944","hebrew":"κυκλόω","translit":"kyklóō","english":"encircled"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"}]},{"v":31,"text":"By faith, Rahab the prostitute, didn’t perish with those who were disobedient, having received the spies in peace.","understanding":"πίστει Ῥαὰβ ἡ πόρνη οὐ συναπώλετο τοῖς ἀπειθήσασιν, “By faith, Rahab the prostitute, didn’t perish with those who were disobedient” — a Gentile woman with a sinful past, listed among the heroes. Her unbelieving neighbours ‘disobeyed’ (ἀπειθέω); she believed. δεξαμένη τοὺς κατασκόπους μετ᾽ εἰρήνης, “having received the spies in peace.” Matthew's genealogy lists her among the ancestors of Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4460","hebrew":"Ῥαάβ","translit":"Rhaáb","english":"Rahab"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G4881","hebrew":"συναπόλλυμι","translit":"synapóllymi","english":"perish"},{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G1209","hebrew":"δέχομαι","translit":"déchomai","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G2685","hebrew":"κατάσκοπος","translit":"katáskopos","english":"spies"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":32,"text":"What more shall I say? For the time would fail me if I told of Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David, Samuel, and the prophets;","understanding":"καὶ τί ἔτι λέγω; “What more shall I say?” ἐπιλείψει με γὰρ διηγούμενον ὁ χρόνος, “For the time would fail me if I told” — χρόνος from the time family: the preacher is running out of time. Then names, not in order: Γεδεών, Βαράκ, Σαμψών, Ἰεφθάε, “Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah” — flawed judges, some deeply so; Δαυίδ τε καὶ Σαμουὴλ καὶ τῶν προφητῶν, “David, Samuel, and the prophets.” God counts faith even in imperfect people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2089","hebrew":"ἔτι","translit":"éti","english":"more"},{"strongs":"G3004","hebrew":"λέγω","translit":"légō","english":"say"},{"strongs":"G1952","hebrew":"ἐπιλείπω","translit":"epileípō","english":"fail"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G1066","hebrew":"Γεδεών","translit":"Gedeṓn","english":"Gideon"},{"strongs":"G913","hebrew":"Βαράκ","translit":"Barák","english":"Barak"},{"strongs":"G4546","hebrew":"Σαμψών","translit":"Sampsṓn","english":"Samson"},{"strongs":"G2422","hebrew":"Ἰεφθάε","translit":"Iephtháe","english":"Jephthah"},{"strongs":"G1138","hebrew":"Δαβίδ","translit":"Dabíd","english":"David"},{"strongs":"G4545","hebrew":"Σαμουήλ","translit":"Samouḗl","english":"Samuel"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"}]},{"v":33,"text":"who, through faith subdued kingdoms, worked out righteousness, obtained promises, stopped the mouths of lions,","understanding":"οἳ διὰ πίστεως κατηγωνίσαντο βασιλείας, “who, through faith subdued kingdoms” — David; εἰργάσαντο δικαιοσύνην, “worked out righteousness” — ἐργάζομαι from the work family: administered justice; ἐπέτυχον ἐπαγγελιῶν, “obtained promises”; ἔφραξαν στόματα λεόντων, “stopped the mouths of lions” — Daniel.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2610","hebrew":"καταγωνίζομαι","translit":"katagōnízomai","english":"subdued"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"kingdoms"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"worked out"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2013","hebrew":"ἐπιτυγχάνω","translit":"epitynchánō","english":"obtained"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promises"},{"strongs":"G5420","hebrew":"φράσσω","translit":"phrássō","english":"stopped"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouths"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lions"}]},{"v":34,"text":"quenched the power of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, from weakness were made strong, grew mighty in war, and caused foreign armies to flee.","understanding":"ἔσβεσαν δύναμιν πυρός, “quenched the power of fire” — Daniel's three friends in the furnace; ἔφυγον στόματα μαχαίρης, “escaped the edge of the sword” — Elijah, David. Then three power-family words in a row: ἐδυναμώθησαν ἀπὸ ἀσθενείας, “from weakness were made strong” — δυναμόω, added to the family: given δύναμις (Samson, Gideon); ἐγενήθησαν ἰσχυροὶ ἐν πολέμῳ, “grew mighty in war” — ἰσχυρός, also added: strength in action; παρεμβολὰς ἔκλιναν ἀλλοτρίων, “and caused foreign armies to flee.” δύναμις, δυναμόω, ἰσχυρός: God's power given to weak people through faith.","words":[{"strongs":"G4570","hebrew":"σβέννυμι","translit":"sbénnymi","english":"quenched"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"escaped"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"edge"},{"strongs":"G3162","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G1412","hebrew":"δυναμόω","translit":"dynamóō","english":"made strong"},{"strongs":"G769","hebrew":"ἀσθένεια","translit":"asthéneia","english":"weakness"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G3925","hebrew":"παρεμβολή","translit":"parembolḗ","english":"armies"},{"strongs":"G2827","hebrew":"κλίνω","translit":"klínō","english":"flee"},{"strongs":"G245","hebrew":"ἀλλότριος","translit":"allótrios","english":"foreign"}]},{"v":35,"text":"Women received their dead by resurrection. Others were tortured, not accepting their deliverance, that they might obtain a better resurrection.","understanding":"ἔλαβον γυναῖκες ἐξ ἀναστάσεως τοὺς νεκροὺς αὐτῶν, “Women received their dead by resurrection” — the widow of Zarephath and the Shunammite woman, whose sons Elijah and Elisha raised. ἀνάστασις from the rise family. ἄλλοι δὲ ἐτυμπανίσθησαν, “Others were tortured” — stretched on a wheel or beaten to death. οὐ προσδεξάμενοι τὴν ἀπολύτρωσιν, “not accepting their deliverance” — they could have been freed by denying God. ἵνα κρείττονος ἀναστάσεως τύχωσιν, “that they might obtain a better resurrection” — ἀνάστασις again, with κρείττων: not a return to this life, but resurrection to eternal life. Faith does not always rescue from suffering; sometimes it carries through it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"Women"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Others"},{"strongs":"G5178","hebrew":"τυμπανίζω","translit":"tympanízō","english":"tortured"},{"strongs":"G4327","hebrew":"προσδέχομαι","translit":"prosdéchomai","english":"accepting"},{"strongs":"G629","hebrew":"ἀπολύτρωσις","translit":"apolýtrōsis","english":"deliverance"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G5177","hebrew":"τυγχάνω","translit":"tynchánō","english":"obtain"}]},{"v":36,"text":"Others were tried by mocking and scourging, yes, moreover by bonds and imprisonment.","understanding":"ἕτεροι δὲ ἐμπαιγμῶν καὶ μαστίγων πεῖραν ἔλαβον, “Others were tried by mocking and scourging” — ἕτερος, others of a different lot. ἔτι δὲ δεσμῶν καὶ φυλακῆς, “yes, moreover by bonds and imprisonment” — Jeremiah, Micaiah.","words":[{"strongs":"G2087","hebrew":"ἕτερος","translit":"héteros","english":"Others"},{"strongs":"G1701","hebrew":"ἐμπαιγμός","translit":"empaigmós","english":"mocking"},{"strongs":"G3148","hebrew":"μάστιξ","translit":"mástix","english":"scourging"},{"strongs":"G3984","hebrew":"πεῖρα","translit":"peîra","english":"tried"},{"strongs":"G1199","hebrew":"δεσμόν","translit":"desmón","english":"bonds"},{"strongs":"G5438","hebrew":"φυλακή","translit":"phylakḗ","english":"imprisonment"}]},{"v":37,"text":"They were stoned. They were sawn apart. They were tempted. They were slain with the sword. They went around in sheep skins and in goat skins; being destitute, afflicted, ill-treated","understanding":"ἐλιθάσθησαν, “They were stoned” — Zechariah son of Jehoiada; ἐπρίσθησαν, “They were sawn apart” — Jewish tradition says Isaiah died this way; ἐπειράσθησαν, “They were tempted” (some manuscripts lack this word, and some scholars think it is a copying slip for ‘burned’); ἐν φόνῳ μαχαίρης ἀπέθανον, “They were slain with the sword.” περιῆλθον ἐν μηλωταῖς, ἐν αἰγείοις δέρμασιν, “They went around in sheep skins and in goat skins” — like Elijah; ὑστερούμενοι, θλιβόμενοι, κακουχούμενοι, “being destitute, afflicted, ill-treated.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3034","hebrew":"λιθάζω","translit":"litházō","english":"stoned"},{"strongs":"G4249","hebrew":"πρίζω","translit":"prízō","english":"sawn apart"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tempted"},{"strongs":"G5408","hebrew":"φόνος","translit":"phónos","english":"slain"},{"strongs":"G3162","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"slain"},{"strongs":"G4022","hebrew":"περιέρχομαι","translit":"periérchomai","english":"went around"},{"strongs":"G3374","hebrew":"μηλωτή","translit":"mēlōtḗ","english":"sheep skins"},{"strongs":"G122","hebrew":"αἴγειος","translit":"aígeios","english":"goat"},{"strongs":"G1192","hebrew":"δέρμα","translit":"dérma","english":"skins"},{"strongs":"G5302","hebrew":"ὑστερέω","translit":"hysteréō","english":"destitute"},{"strongs":"G2346","hebrew":"θλίβω","translit":"thlíbō","english":"afflicted"},{"strongs":"G2558","hebrew":"κακουχέω","translit":"kakouchéō","english":"ill-treated"}]},{"v":38,"text":"(of whom the world was not worthy), wandering in deserts, mountains, caves, and the holes of the earth.","understanding":"ὧν οὐκ ἦν ἄξιος ὁ κόσμος, “(of whom the world was not worthy)” — κόσμος from the world family. The world rejected them as unworthy; God's verdict is the reverse. ἐπὶ ἐρημίαις πλανώμενοι καὶ ὄρεσιν καὶ σπηλαίοις καὶ ταῖς ὀπαῖς τῆς γῆς, “wandering in deserts, mountains, caves, and the holes of the earth” — like the hundred prophets Obadiah hid in caves.","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"wandering"},{"strongs":"G2047","hebrew":"ἐρημία","translit":"erēmía","english":"deserts"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountains"},{"strongs":"G4693","hebrew":"σπήλαιον","translit":"spḗlaion","english":"caves"},{"strongs":"G3692","hebrew":"ὀπή","translit":"opḗ","english":"holes"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":39,"text":"These all, having had testimony given to them through their faith, didn’t receive the promise,","understanding":"καὶ οὗτοι πάντες μαρτυρηθέντες διὰ τῆς πίστεως, “These all, having had testimony given to them through their faith” — the chapter returns to its start (v. 2). οὐκ ἐκομίσαντο τὴν ἐπαγγελίαν, “didn’t receive the promise” — the great promise of the Messiah and the new covenant.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"}]},{"v":40,"text":"God having provided some better thing concerning us, so that apart from us they should not be made perfect.","understanding":"τοῦ θεοῦ περὶ ἡμῶν κρεῖττόν τι προβλεψαμένου, “God having provided some better thing concerning us” — κρείττων one last time in this chapter; προβλέπω, to see ahead, foresee and provide. ἵνα μὴ χωρὶς ἡμῶν τελειωθῶσιν, “so that apart from us they should not be made perfect” — τελειόω from the perfect family. The saints of old and believers today will be brought to the goal together, as one people, through Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G4265","hebrew":"προβλέπω","translit":"problépō","english":"provided"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"apart from"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"made perfect"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Waving to a Distant City","verseRef":"v. 13-16","image":"Picture a line of travellers on a long road through a dry land, carrying tents on their backs. On the far horizon, faint in the haze, rise the walls and towers of a shining city. They will not reach it in this lifetime. But they lift their hands and wave to it, and greet it like a home they already belong to. They could turn back to the towns behind them, but they keep walking toward the city.","meaning":"“These all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them and embraced them from afar, and having confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth” (v. 13). “But now they desire a better country, that is, a heavenly one. Therefore God is not ashamed of them, to be called their God, for he has prepared a city for them” (v. 16). Faith lives as a pilgrim, greeting God's promises from afar. Ask the Spirit to loosen your grip on this world and fix your heart on the city God has prepared."},{"title":"The Knife and the Ram","verseRef":"v. 17-19","image":"Picture an old man climbing a mountain with his son, carrying fire and a knife. The boy carries the wood. At the top the old man builds an altar, binds the boy, and raises the knife — his hands trembling but steady. In his heart he has reasoned it out: ‘God promised descendants through this son. If he must die, God will raise him.’ Then a voice from heaven stops him, and he sees a ram caught in a thicket.","meaning":"“By faith, Abraham, being tested, offered up Isaac” (v. 17), “concluding that God is able to raise up even from the dead. Figuratively speaking, he also did receive him back from the dead” (v. 19). Faith holds on to God's promise even when His command seems to contradict it, trusting His power over death. And the scene points to the Father who did not spare His own Son."},{"title":"Seeing the Invisible","verseRef":"v. 24-27","image":"Picture a young prince in the royal palace of Egypt, surrounded by gold, servants, scholars and power. Outside, Hebrew slaves toil under the whip. He looks at the treasures around him and then out at the suffering people of God. He chooses the slaves. Years later, facing Pharaoh's fury, he walks out of Egypt without fear, his eyes fixed on Someone no one else can see.","meaning":"“choosing rather to share ill treatment with God’s people, than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a time” (v. 25), “accounting the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of Egypt; for he looked to the reward” (v. 26). “for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible” (v. 27). Faith sees what the eyes cannot. Ask the Spirit to help you weigh the passing pleasures of sin against the lasting reward, and to see the invisible God."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"What Faith Is (v. 1-3)","body":"Faith is the assurance of things hoped for and the conviction of things not seen; by it the ancients were commended, and by it we understand that the universe was formed by God's word, so that the visible came from the invisible."},{"label":"Faith Before the Flood (v. 4-7)","body":"Abel offered a better sacrifice and was declared righteous; Enoch pleased God and was taken without dying, for without faith it is impossible to please God; Noah, warned of unseen things, reverently built the ark, condemned the world and became heir of the righteousness that comes by faith."},{"label":"Faith of the Patriarchs (v. 8-22)","body":"Abraham obeyed God's call without knowing where he was going, lived in tents in the promised land, and looked for God's city; Sarah received power to conceive; they all died in faith, seeking a better, heavenly country; Abraham offered Isaac, reasoning that God could raise the dead; Isaac, Jacob and Joseph looked forward in faith to God's future."},{"label":"Faith of Moses and Israel (v. 23-31)","body":"Moses' parents hid him; Moses refused Egyptian status, chose ill-treatment with God's people over sin's passing pleasures, valued the reproach of Christ above Egypt's treasures, left Egypt seeing the Invisible One, and kept the Passover; Israel crossed the Red Sea; Jericho's walls fell; and Rahab was spared."},{"label":"Faith That Triumphs and Faith That Endures (v. 32-40)","body":"Time would fail to tell of judges, kings and prophets who conquered, did justice, received promises and were made strong out of weakness; others were tortured, mocked, imprisoned, stoned, sawn in two and killed, wandering destitute — people the world was not worthy of; all were commended for their faith, yet did not receive what was promised, because God had planned something better, so that they would be made perfect together with us."}],"sections":[{"heading":"What Faith Is and What It Does","paragraphs":["Chapter 10 ended with the righteous living by faith. Chapter 11 shows what that looks like. “Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, proof of things not seen” (v. 1). The word ‘assurance’ is ὑπόστασις, the same word used of Christ as the exact imprint of God's ‘substance’ (1:3): faith rests on what is solid and real. Faith gives present reality to future promises and conviction about unseen truth. Paul says, “for we walk by faith, not by sight” (2 Corinthians 5:7).","Faith begins with creation: “By faith, we understand that the universe has been framed by the word of God, so that what is seen has not been made out of things which are visible” (v. 3). God spoke (ῥῆμα), and the ages were set in order. And faith is essential to pleasing God: “Without faith it is impossible to be well pleasing to him, for he who comes to God must believe that he exists, and that he is a rewarder of those who seek him” (v. 6). Faith trusts both God's existence and His goodness.","The phrase ‘by faith’ rings out eighteen times in the chapter. Each person heard God's word and acted on it, often without seeing the result. Abel worshipped, Enoch walked with God, Noah built, Abraham went, Sarah conceived, Moses chose, Israel crossed, Rahab welcomed. Faith is never passive. It is trust that obeys."]},{"heading":"Pilgrims Looking for a City","paragraphs":["Abraham stands at the centre. “By faith, Abraham, when he was called, obeyed to go out to the place which he was to receive for an inheritance. He went out, not knowing where he went” (v. 8). Stephen recalls that God told him, “Get out of your land, and from your relatives, and come into a land which I will show you” (Acts 7:3), yet “He gave him no inheritance in it, no, not so much as to set his foot on” (Acts 7:5). Abraham lived in tents in the land of promise, because “he looked for the city which has the foundations, whose builder and maker is God” (v. 10).","The patriarchs died without receiving the fulfilment: “These all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them and embraced them from afar, and having confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth” (v. 13). They could have gone back to Mesopotamia, but they did not. “But now they desire a better country, that is, a heavenly one. Therefore God is not ashamed of them, to be called their God” (v. 16). Peter tells believers to live “as foreigners and pilgrims” (1 Peter 2:11), and Paul says, “For our citizenship is in heaven” (Philippians 3:20).","The supreme test came when God asked Abraham to offer Isaac. Abraham obeyed, “concluding that God is able to raise up even from the dead. Figuratively speaking, he also did receive him back from the dead” (v. 19). Paul describes the same kind of faith: “Without being weakened in faith, he didn’t consider his own body, already having been worn out” (Romans 4:19). Faith reckons with God's resurrection power."]},{"heading":"Faith That Conquers and Faith That Suffers","paragraphs":["Moses shows faith as a choice of values. He “refused to be called the son of Pharaoh’s daughter” (v. 24), “choosing rather to share ill treatment with God’s people, than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a time” (v. 25). The time-family word πρόσκαιρος says sin's pleasures are ‘for a season’. He counted “the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of Egypt; for he looked to the reward” (v. 26). And “he endured, as seeing him who is invisible” (v. 27). Faith sees the unseen God and acts accordingly.","Then the writer runs out of time. He lists judges, kings and prophets who “from weakness were made strong, grew mighty in war, and caused foreign armies to flee” (v. 34). Three power-family words — δύναμις, δυναμόω, ἰσχυρός — describe God's strength given to weak people. Some saw great deliverances: “Women received their dead by resurrection” (v. 35). Even Rahab, a Gentile prostitute, is honoured; she appears in Jesus' genealogy: “Salmon became the father of Boaz by Rahab” (Matthew 1:5).","But faith does not always lead to escape. “Others were tortured, not accepting their deliverance, that they might obtain a better resurrection” (v. 35). They were mocked, imprisoned, stoned, sawn apart, killed with the sword, and wandered destitute — people “of whom the world was not worthy” (v. 38). Their faith was just as real as the faith that conquered. And all of them “didn’t receive the promise” (v. 39), because “God having provided some better thing concerning us, so that apart from us they should not be made perfect” (v. 40). The saints of every age will be brought to completion together in Christ."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Living by Faith, Not by Sight","paragraphs":["The New Testament repeatedly contrasts faith and sight. “while we don’t look at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen. For the things which are seen are temporal, but the things which are not seen are eternal” (2 Corinthians 4:18). “For we were saved in hope, but hope that is seen is not hope” (Romans 8:24). Jesus told Thomas, “Blessed are those who have not seen, and have believed” (John 20:29). Peter writes of Christ, “though now you don’t see him, yet believing, you rejoice greatly with joy unspeakable and full of glory” (1 Peter 1:8).","Several of the heroes of Hebrews 11 appear elsewhere in the New Testament. Jesus spoke of “the blood of righteous Abel” (Matthew 23:35), and John explains that Cain killed him “Because his deeds were evil, and his brother’s righteous” (1 John 3:12). Peter calls Noah “a preacher of righteousness” (2 Peter 2:5). Jesus said, “Your father Abraham rejoiced to see my day. He saw it, and was glad” (John 8:56). James writes that Rahab was justified “in that she received the messengers, and sent them out another way” (James 2:25).","The city the patriarchs sought is the city John saw: “I saw the holy city, New Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared like a bride adorned for her husband” (Revelation 21:2). Jesus promised, “In my Father’s house are many homes. If it weren’t so, I would have told you. I am going to prepare a place for you” (John 14:2). The martyrs of every age wait, as John saw, until the full number is complete (Revelation 6:11) — the same truth as Hebrews 11:40."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Proof / conviction / evidence","hebrew":"ἔλεγχος","translit":"élenchos","strongs":"G1650","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “proof, conviction.” Its only other New Testament use is 2 Timothy 3:16, where Scripture is profitable for ‘reproof’. It means evidence that convinces, the proof that settles a question. Faith is the inner conviction that what God says about unseen realities is true. It is not believing without reason, but trusting the testimony of the God who cannot lie."},{"english":"Rewarder","hebrew":"μισθαποδότης","translit":"misthapodótēs","strongs":"G3406","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “a renumerator” (the dictionary's spelling of ‘remunerator’, one who pays back). It appears only here in the New Testament. Faith believes not only that God exists, but that He is good — that He responds to those who seek Him. The related noun μισθαποδοσία, ‘reward’, appears in 10:35 and 11:26. God is not indifferent to those who seek Him; He rewards them, above all with Himself."},{"english":"Pilgrim / resident foreigner","hebrew":"παρεπίδημος","translit":"parepídēmos","strongs":"G3927","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “an alien alongside, i.e. a resident foreigner.” It describes someone living temporarily in a place that is not his home. Peter uses it twice for believers (1 Peter 1:1; 2:11). The patriarchs confessed they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth. Believers live fully and responsibly in this world, but they are not at home here; their citizenship is in heaven."},{"english":"For a time / temporary","hebrew":"πρόσκαιρος","translit":"próskairos","strongs":"G4340","verses":"v. 25","insight":"Strong's: “for the occasion only, i.e. temporary.” It is built on καιρός, a season, and so this study adds it to the time family. Paul uses it in 2 Corinthians 4:18: ‘the things which are seen are temporal’. Jesus used it of the shallow hearer who ‘endures for a while’. Moses weighed the seasonal pleasures of sin against the eternal reward, and chose the reward."},{"english":"Invisible","hebrew":"ἀόρατος","translit":"aóratos","strongs":"G517","verses":"v. 27","insight":"Strong's: “invisible.” Paul uses it of God, ‘the King eternal, immortal, invisible’ (1 Timothy 1:17), and of the ‘invisible God’ of whom Christ is the image (Colossians 1:15). Hebrews 11:27 joins it with ὁράω, ‘to see’: Moses endured ‘as seeing him who is invisible’. Faith is the eye of the soul that sees the God whom no physical eye can see."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"Through the great chapter of faith, the Holy Spirit invites you to join the long line of those who trusted God.","items":[{"title":"Faith Acts on God's Word","body":"Every hero of this chapter heard God and acted — built, went, offered, chose, crossed. The Spirit asks: what has God told you to do that you have not yet done? Faith is not just agreeing with truth; it is obeying it. Ask Him for courage to take the next step of obedience."},{"title":"You Are a Pilgrim","body":"The patriarchs lived in tents and longed for a better country. The Spirit wants to loosen your grip on this world — its comforts, status and security — and fix your heart on the city God has prepared. Ask Him where you have settled down too comfortably."},{"title":"Faith Endures Even Without Deliverance","body":"Some believers in this chapter saw miracles; others suffered and died without rescue. Both were faithful. The Spirit wants to free you from the idea that faith always produces escape. If you are suffering, He is not absent. Ask Him for faith that endures, looking for a better resurrection."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Obedience Check","body":"Like Abraham (v. 8), is God calling you to step out without knowing every detail? Name the step, and the fear that holds you back."},{"title":"The Pilgrim Check","body":"What in your life shows that you are seeking a heavenly country (v. 14)? What shows that you are too settled here? Make one change this month."},{"title":"The Values Check","body":"Moses weighed sin's temporary pleasures against Christ's reproach (vv. 25–26). Where are you tempted to choose the temporary? Write the choice down and decide in advance."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Your Own ‘By Faith’ List","body":"Write a personal ‘Hebrews 11’ list: times in your life or family's history when you or others acted ‘by faith’ and saw God's faithfulness. Then add a line for the step of faith you are taking now. Read it on discouraging days and add to it over the years."},{"title":"Study One Hero a Week","body":"For the next ten weeks, choose one person mentioned in Hebrews 11 and read their story in the Old Testament. Ask: What did they hear from God? What did faith look like for them? What did it cost? What can I learn? Share what you learn with someone else."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I believe that You exist and that You reward those who seek You. Give me the faith that is the assurance of things hoped for and the conviction of things not seen. By faith I understand that You made the universe by Your word. Give me the faith of Abel to offer You my best, of Enoch to walk with You, of Noah to obey when others mock. Give me the faith of Abraham to go when You call, even when I do not know where I am going; to live as a pilgrim, looking for the city You have built; and to trust that You can raise the dead. Give me the faith of Moses to choose Your people and Christ's reproach over the passing pleasures of sin, and to endure as seeing You who are invisible. Make me strong out of weakness. And if You call me to suffer rather than to escape, give me faith to endure, looking for a better resurrection. Thank You that You have provided something better, so that with all Your people I will be made perfect in Christ. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Abraham Offers Isaac","ref":"Genesis 22:1-19","relatedVerses":"v. 17-19","summary":"God tested Abraham, telling him to take his only son Isaac, whom he loved, and offer him as a burnt offering on a mountain in Moriah. Early the next morning Abraham set out. On the third day he told his servants that he and the boy would worship and come back. When Isaac asked where the lamb was, Abraham said God would provide. At the altar he bound Isaac and raised the knife, but the angel of the LORD stopped him. Abraham saw a ram caught in a thicket and offered it instead, and named the place ‘The LORD Will Provide’. Hebrews 11:19 reveals what was in Abraham's mind: he reasoned that God could raise Isaac from the dead."},{"title":"Moses Chooses God's People","ref":"Exodus 2:1-15; Acts 7:20-29","relatedVerses":"v. 23-27","summary":"Moses was born when Pharaoh had ordered all Hebrew baby boys to be killed. His parents saw that he was beautiful and hid him for three months, then placed him in a basket on the Nile, where Pharaoh's daughter found and adopted him. Stephen says, “Moses was instructed in all the wisdom of the Egyptians. He was mighty in his words and works” (Acts 7:22). At forty, he went out to his own people, defended a Hebrew being beaten, and had to flee. “He supposed that his brothers understood that God, by his hand, was giving them deliverance; but they didn’t understand” (Acts 7:25). Hebrews 11 sees in this the choice of faith: rejecting Egypt's treasures to share the suffering of God's people."},{"title":"Rahab and the Spies","ref":"Joshua 2; 6:22-25","relatedVerses":"v. 31","summary":"Before Israel attacked Jericho, Joshua sent two spies, who stayed at the house of Rahab, a prostitute. When the king of Jericho demanded that she hand them over, she hid them on her roof under stalks of flax and sent the soldiers away. She told the spies that the people of Jericho were terrified, because they had heard what the LORD had done, and she confessed that the LORD is God in heaven above and on earth below. She asked them to spare her family, and they told her to tie a scarlet cord in her window. When Jericho fell, Rahab and her family were saved. She later became an ancestor of King David and of Jesus."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":12,"chapterTitle":"Run the Race, Receive the Discipline, Come to Zion","themeTitle":"“Looking to Jesus, the Author and Perfecter of Faith”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Therefore let us also, seeing we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, lay aside every weight and the sin which so easily entangles us, and let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us,","understanding":"τοιγαροῦν καὶ ἡμεῖς, “Therefore let us also” — after chapter 11's gallery. τοσοῦτον ἔχοντες περικείμενον ἡμῖν νέφος μαρτύρων, “seeing we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses” — μάρτυς: not spectators in the stands so much as witnesses whose lives testify that faith finishes. ὄγκον ἀποθέμενοι πάντα, “lay aside every weight” — ὄγκος, bulk, extra weight a runner strips off; καὶ τὴν εὐπερίστατον ἁμαρτίαν, “and the sin which so easily entangles us” — like a long robe wrapping round the legs; ἁμαρτία from the sin family. δι᾽ ὑπομονῆς τρέχωμεν τὸν προκείμενον ἡμῖν ἀγῶνα, “and let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us” — ἀγών, the contest (Paul's ‘good fight’ in 2 Timothy 4:7).","words":[{"strongs":"G5108","hebrew":"τοιοῦτος","translit":"toioûtos","english":"so great"},{"strongs":"G3509","hebrew":"νέφος","translit":"néphos","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witnesses"},{"strongs":"G4029","hebrew":"περίκειμαι","translit":"períkeimai","english":"surrounded"},{"strongs":"G659","hebrew":"ἀποτίθημι","translit":"apotíthēmi","english":"lay aside"},{"strongs":"G3591","hebrew":"ὄγκος","translit":"ónkos","english":"weight"},{"strongs":"G2139","hebrew":"εὐπερίστατος","translit":"euperístatos","english":"so easily entangles"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G5143","hebrew":"τρέχω","translit":"tréchō","english":"run"},{"strongs":"G4295","hebrew":"πρόκειμαι","translit":"prókeimai","english":"set before"},{"strongs":"G73","hebrew":"ἀγών","translit":"agṓn","english":"race"}]},{"v":2,"text":"looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith, who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising its shame, and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God.","understanding":"ἀφορῶντες εἰς τὸν τῆς πίστεως ἀρχηγὸν καὶ τελειωτὴν Ἰησοῦν, “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith.” ἀφοράω, added to the see family: to look away from everything else and fix the eyes on one. Two word families in one title: ἀρχηγός from the author family — the pioneer who started the race and runs ahead (2:10); τελειωτής from the perfect family — the one who brings faith to its finish, the only use of the word in the Bible. He is the first and the last runner. ὃς ἀντὶ τῆς προκειμένης αὐτῷ χαρᾶς ὑπέμεινεν σταυρόν, “who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross” — the joy of His Father's will done and His people saved. αἰσχύνης καταφρονήσας, “despising its shame” — treating the most shameful death as nothing compared with the joy. ἐν δεξιᾷ τε τοῦ θρόνου τοῦ θεοῦ κεκάθικεν, “and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God.”","words":[{"strongs":"G872","hebrew":"ἀφοράω","translit":"aphoráō","english":"looking"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G747","hebrew":"ἀρχηγός","translit":"archēgós","english":"author"},{"strongs":"G5051","hebrew":"τελειωτής","translit":"teleiōtḗs","english":"perfecter"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G4716","hebrew":"σταυρός","translit":"staurós","english":"cross"},{"strongs":"G2706","hebrew":"καταφρονέω","translit":"kataphronéō","english":"despising"},{"strongs":"G152","hebrew":"αἰσχύνη","translit":"aischýnē","english":"shame"},{"strongs":"G2523","hebrew":"καθίζω","translit":"kathízō","english":"sat down"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For consider him who has endured such contradiction of sinners against himself, that you don’t grow weary, fainting in your souls.","understanding":"ἀναλογίσασθε γὰρ τὸν τοιαύτην ὑπομεμενηκότα ὑπὸ τῶν ἁμαρτωλῶν εἰς ἑαυτὸν ἀντιλογίαν, “For consider him who has endured such contradiction of sinners against himself” — ἀναλογίζομαι, to compare, reckon up carefully. ἵνα μὴ κάμητε ταῖς ψυχαῖς ὑμῶν ἐκλυόμενοι, “that you don’t grow weary, fainting in your souls” — κάμνω, to tire, and ἐκλύω, to come undone, like an exhausted runner collapsing. ψυχή from the life family. The cure for weariness is to compare your race with His.","words":[{"strongs":"G357","hebrew":"ἀναλογίζομαι","translit":"analogízomai","english":"consider"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G485","hebrew":"ἀντιλογία","translit":"antilogía","english":"contradiction"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinners"},{"strongs":"G2577","hebrew":"κάμνω","translit":"kámnō","english":"grow weary"},{"strongs":"G1590","hebrew":"ἐκλύω","translit":"eklýō","english":"fainting"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"}]},{"v":4,"text":"You have not yet resisted to blood, striving against sin;","understanding":"οὔπω μέχρις αἵματος ἀντικατέστητε, “You have not yet resisted to blood” — they had suffered loss and insult (10:32–34), but none had yet been killed. πρὸς τὴν ἁμαρτίαν ἀνταγωνιζόμενοι, “striving against sin” — ἀγών again: a wrestling match or contest against sin, both temptation within and persecution without.","words":[{"strongs":"G3768","hebrew":"οὔπω","translit":"oúpō","english":"not yet"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G478","hebrew":"ἀντικαθίστημι","translit":"antikathístēmi","english":"resisted"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G464","hebrew":"ἀνταγωνίζομαι","translit":"antagōnízomai","english":"striving"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and you have forgotten the exhortation which reasons with you as with children, “My son, don’t take lightly the chastening of the Lord, nor faint when you are reproved by him;","understanding":"καὶ ἐκλέλησθε τῆς παρακλήσεως, “and you have forgotten the exhortation” — Proverbs 3:11–12, which speaks to them ὡς υἱοῖς, ‘as to sons’. “My son, don’t take lightly the chastening of the Lord, nor faint when you are reproved by him.” Two members of the new discipline family: παιδεία, ‘chastening’ — the training of a child; and ἐλέγχω, ‘reproved’ — exposed and corrected. Two wrong reactions: to shrug it off, or to collapse under it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1585","hebrew":"ἐκλανθάνομαι","translit":"eklanthánomai","english":"forgotten"},{"strongs":"G3874","hebrew":"παράκλησις","translit":"paráklēsis","english":"exhortation"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G3643","hebrew":"ὀλιγωρέω","translit":"oligōréō","english":"take lightly"},{"strongs":"G3809","hebrew":"παιδεία","translit":"paideía","english":"chastening"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1590","hebrew":"ἐκλύω","translit":"eklýō","english":"faint"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reproved"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he receives.”","understanding":"“For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he receives.” ἀγαπάω from the love family; παιδεύω and μαστιγόω from the discipline family. Discipline does not mean God has stopped loving you; it is evidence that He does. He trains only those He has received as sons.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loves"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"chastens"},{"strongs":"G3146","hebrew":"μαστιγόω","translit":"mastigóō","english":"scourges"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G3858","hebrew":"παραδέχομαι","translit":"paradéchomai","english":"receives"}]},{"v":7,"text":"It is for discipline that you endure. God deals with you as with children, for what son is there whom his father doesn’t discipline?","understanding":"εἰς παιδείαν ὑπομένετε, “It is for discipline that you endure” — the same word παιδεία, now rendered ‘discipline’: English uses several words for one Greek family. ὡς υἱοῖς ὑμῖν προσφέρεται ὁ θεός, “God deals with you as with children,” τίς γὰρ υἱὸς ὃν οὐ παιδεύει πατήρ; “for what son is there whom his father doesn’t discipline?” Their sufferings were not signs of God's rejection but of His fatherhood.","words":[{"strongs":"G3809","hebrew":"παιδεία","translit":"paideía","english":"discipline"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endure"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G4374","hebrew":"προσφέρω","translit":"prosphérō","english":"deals"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"father"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"discipline"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But if you are without discipline, of which all have been made partakers, then are you illegitimate, and not children.","understanding":"εἰ δὲ χωρίς ἐστε παιδείας, “But if you are without discipline,” ἧς μέτοχοι γεγόνασιν πάντες, “of which all have been made partakers” — every true child shares in it. ἄρα νόθοι καὶ οὐχ υἱοί ἐστε, “then are you illegitimate, and not children” — νόθος, a child born outside marriage, who in that culture often received no father's training or inheritance. Lack of discipline would be the worrying sign.","words":[{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"without"},{"strongs":"G3809","hebrew":"παιδεία","translit":"paideía","english":"discipline"},{"strongs":"G3353","hebrew":"μέτοχος","translit":"métochos","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G3541","hebrew":"νόθος","translit":"nóthos","english":"illegitimate"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"children"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Furthermore, we had the fathers of our flesh to chasten us, and we paid them respect. Shall we not much rather be in subjection to the Father of spirits, and live?","understanding":"εἶτα τοὺς μὲν τῆς σαρκὸς ἡμῶν πατέρας εἴχομεν παιδευτάς, “Furthermore, we had the fathers of our flesh to chasten us” — παιδευτής, trainer. καὶ ἐνετρεπόμεθα, “and we paid them respect.” οὐ πολὺ μᾶλλον ὑποταγησόμεθα τῷ πατρὶ τῶν πνευμάτων, καὶ ζήσομεν; “Shall we not much rather be in subjection to the Father of spirits, and live?” — ζάω from the life family: submitting to His training is the way to life.","words":[{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G3810","hebrew":"παιδευτής","translit":"paideutḗs","english":"chasten"},{"strongs":"G1788","hebrew":"ἐντρέπω","translit":"entrépō","english":"paid them respect"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For they indeed, for a few days, punished us as seemed good to them; but he for our profit, that we may be partakers of his holiness.","understanding":"οἱ μὲν γὰρ πρὸς ὀλίγας ἡμέρας κατὰ τὸ δοκοῦν αὐτοῖς ἐπαίδευον, “For they indeed, for a few days, punished us as seemed good to them” — παιδεύω again, here rendered ‘punished’: human fathers train imperfectly, for a short time, by their best judgement. ὁ δὲ ἐπὶ τὸ συμφέρον, “but he for our profit,” εἰς τὸ μεταλαβεῖν τῆς ἁγιότητος αὐτοῦ, “that we may be partakers of his holiness.” God's discipline is never mistaken and always aims at the highest goal: sharing His own holiness.","words":[{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"few"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"punished"},{"strongs":"G1380","hebrew":"δοκέω","translit":"dokéō","english":"seemed good"},{"strongs":"G4851","hebrew":"συμφέρω","translit":"symphérō","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G3335","hebrew":"μεταλαμβάνω","translit":"metalambánō","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G41","hebrew":"ἁγιότης","translit":"hagiótēs","english":"holiness"}]},{"v":11,"text":"All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous; yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby.","understanding":"πᾶσα δὲ παιδεία πρὸς μὲν τὸ παρὸν οὐ δοκεῖ χαρᾶς εἶναι ἀλλὰ λύπης, “All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous” — honest: discipline hurts. ὕστερον δὲ καρπὸν εἰρηνικὸν τοῖς δι᾽ αὐτῆς γεγυμνασμένοις ἀποδίδωσιν δικαιοσύνης, “yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby.” γυμνάζω from the discipline family: trained like an athlete (as in 5:14). Pain now, fruit afterward — but only for those who let it train them.","words":[{"strongs":"G3809","hebrew":"παιδεία","translit":"paideía","english":"chastening"},{"strongs":"G3918","hebrew":"πάρειμι","translit":"páreimi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G1380","hebrew":"δοκέω","translit":"dokéō","english":"seems"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joyous"},{"strongs":"G3077","hebrew":"λύπη","translit":"lýpē","english":"grievous"},{"strongs":"G5305","hebrew":"ὕστερον","translit":"hýsteron","english":"afterward"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruit"},{"strongs":"G1516","hebrew":"εἰρηνικός","translit":"eirēnikós","english":"peaceful"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G1128","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","english":"exercised"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"yields"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Therefore lift up the hands that hang down and the feeble knees,","understanding":"διὸ τὰς παρειμένας χεῖρας καὶ τὰ παραλελυμένα γόνατα ἀνορθώσατε, “Therefore lift up the hands that hang down and the feeble knees” — words from Isaiah 35 to exhausted runners: straighten up! ἀνορθόω, to set upright again.","words":[{"strongs":"G461","hebrew":"ἀνορθόω","translit":"anorthóō","english":"lift up"},{"strongs":"G3935","hebrew":"παρίημι","translit":"paríēmi","english":"hang down"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G3886","hebrew":"παραλύω","translit":"paralýō","english":"feeble"},{"strongs":"G1119","hebrew":"γόνυ","translit":"góny","english":"knees"}]},{"v":13,"text":"and make straight paths for your feet, so what is lame may not be dislocated, but rather be healed.","understanding":"καὶ τροχιὰς ὀρθὰς ποιήσατε τοῖς ποσὶν ὑμῶν, “and make straight paths for your feet” — from Proverbs 4. ἵνα μὴ τὸ χωλὸν ἐκτραπῇ, ἰαθῇ δὲ μᾶλλον, “so what is lame may not be dislocated, but rather be healed” — ἐκτρέπω, the medical word for a joint wrenched out of place (‘turn away’ in 2 Timothy 4:4). The strong must clear the path so that the weak are healed, not further injured.","words":[{"strongs":"G3717","hebrew":"ὀρθός","translit":"orthós","english":"straight"},{"strongs":"G5163","hebrew":"τροχιά","translit":"trochiá","english":"paths"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G5560","hebrew":"χωλός","translit":"chōlós","english":"lame"},{"strongs":"G1624","hebrew":"ἐκτρέπω","translit":"ektrépō","english":"dislocated"},{"strongs":"G2390","hebrew":"ἰάομαι","translit":"iáomai","english":"healed"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Follow after peace with all men, and the sanctification without which no man will see the Lord,","understanding":"εἰρήνην διώκετε μετὰ πάντων, “Follow after peace with all men” — διώκω, to pursue, chase. καὶ τὸν ἁγιασμόν, “and the sanctification” — holiness of life. οὗ χωρὶς οὐδεὶς ὄψεται τὸν κύριον, “without which no man will see the Lord” — ὁράω from the see family. Holiness is not the price of seeing God but the necessary mark of those who will; Jesus said the pure in heart will see God.","words":[{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"Follow after"},{"strongs":"G38","hebrew":"ἁγιασμός","translit":"hagiasmós","english":"sanctification"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"without"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":15,"text":"looking carefully lest there be any man who falls short of the grace of God; lest any root of bitterness springing up trouble you, and many be defiled by it;","understanding":"ἐπισκοποῦντες μή τις ὑστερῶν ἀπὸ τῆς χάριτος τοῦ θεοῦ, “looking carefully lest there be any man who falls short of the grace of God” — ἐπισκοπέω, to oversee (the root of ‘overseer’): every believer is to watch over the others. μή τις ῥίζα πικρίας ἄνω φύουσα ἐνοχλῇ, “lest any root of bitterness springing up trouble you” — from Deuteronomy 29, where it meant a person drawn into idolatry who poisons the community. καὶ δι᾽ αὐτῆς μιανθῶσιν πολλοί, “and many be defiled by it.” One person's unbelief or bitterness can spread.","words":[{"strongs":"G1983","hebrew":"ἐπισκοπέω","translit":"episkopéō","english":"looking carefully"},{"strongs":"G5302","hebrew":"ὑστερέω","translit":"hysteréō","english":"falls short"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4491","hebrew":"ῥίζα","translit":"rhíza","english":"root"},{"strongs":"G4088","hebrew":"πικρία","translit":"pikría","english":"bitterness"},{"strongs":"G5453","hebrew":"φύω","translit":"phýō","english":"springing up"},{"strongs":"G1776","hebrew":"ἐνοχλέω","translit":"enochléō","english":"trouble"},{"strongs":"G3392","hebrew":"μιαίνω","translit":"miaínō","english":"defiled"}]},{"v":16,"text":"lest there be any sexually immoral person, or profane person, like Esau, who sold his birthright for one meal.","understanding":"μή τις πόρνος ἢ βέβηλος ὡς Ἠσαῦ, “lest there be any sexually immoral person, or profane person, like Esau” — βέβηλος, godless, treating sacred things as common. ὃς ἀντὶ βρώσεως μιᾶς ἀπέδοτο τὰ πρωτοτόκια ἑαυτοῦ, “who sold his birthright for one meal.” Esau traded a future blessing for an immediate appetite — exactly the danger facing readers tempted to give up Christ to escape present hardship.","words":[{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexually immoral"},{"strongs":"G952","hebrew":"βέβηλος","translit":"bébēlos","english":"profane"},{"strongs":"G2269","hebrew":"Ἠσαῦ","translit":"Ēsaû","english":"Esau"},{"strongs":"G1035","hebrew":"βρῶσις","translit":"brōsis","english":"meal"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"sold"},{"strongs":"G4415","hebrew":"πρωτοτόκια","translit":"prōtotókia","english":"birthright"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For you know that even when he afterward desired to inherit the blessing, he was rejected, for he found no place for a change of mind though he sought it diligently with tears.","understanding":"ἴστε γὰρ ὅτι καὶ μετέπειτα θέλων κληρονομῆσαι τὴν εὐλογίαν ἀπεδοκιμάσθη, “For you know that even when he afterward desired to inherit the blessing, he was rejected” — ἀποδοκιμάζω, rejected after testing. μετανοίας γὰρ τόπον οὐχ εὗρεν, “for he found no place for a change of mind” — μετάνοια; whose mind is unclear: Isaac could not reverse the blessing, or Esau could not find genuine repentance. καίπερ μετὰ δακρύων ἐκζητήσας αὐτήν, “though he sought it diligently with tears” — he wanted the blessing, not God. Some choices have consequences that tears cannot undo.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3347","hebrew":"μετέπειτα","translit":"metépeita","english":"afterward"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desired"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherit"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"},{"strongs":"G593","hebrew":"ἀποδοκιμάζω","translit":"apodokimázō","english":"rejected"},{"strongs":"G3341","hebrew":"μετάνοια","translit":"metánoia","english":"change of mind"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G1567","hebrew":"ἐκζητέω","translit":"ekzētéō","english":"sought it diligently"},{"strongs":"G1144","hebrew":"δάκρυ","translit":"dákry","english":"tears"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For you have not come to a mountain that might be touched, and that burned with fire, and to blackness, darkness, storm,","understanding":"Now a contrast between two mountains. οὐ γὰρ προσεληλύθατε ψηλαφωμένῳ ὄρει, “For you have not come to a mountain that might be touched” — Sinai, physical and earthly. καὶ κεκαυμένῳ πυρί, “and that burned with fire,” καὶ γνόφῳ καὶ ζόφῳ καὶ θυέλλῃ, “and to blackness, darkness, storm” — the terrifying scene of Exodus 19.","words":[{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G5584","hebrew":"ψηλαφάω","translit":"psēlapháō","english":"touched"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"},{"strongs":"G2545","hebrew":"καίω","translit":"kaíō","english":"burned"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G1105","hebrew":"γνόφος","translit":"gnóphos","english":"blackness"},{"strongs":"G2217","hebrew":"ζόφος","translit":"zóphos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G2366","hebrew":"θύελλα","translit":"thýella","english":"storm"}]},{"v":19,"text":"the sound of a trumpet, and the voice of words; which those who heard it begged that not one more word should be spoken to them,","understanding":"καὶ σάλπιγγος ἤχῳ καὶ φωνῇ ῥημάτων, “the sound of a trumpet, and the voice of words” — ῥῆμα from the word family: God's spoken words from the fire. ἧς οἱ ἀκούσαντες παρῃτήσαντο μὴ προστεθῆναι αὐτοῖς λόγον, “which those who heard it begged that not one more word should be spoken to them” — λόγος, the same family. The people were too terrified to keep listening.","words":[{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpet"},{"strongs":"G2279","hebrew":"ἦχος","translit":"ēchos","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3868","hebrew":"παραιτέομαι","translit":"paraitéomai","english":"begged"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"}]},{"v":20,"text":"for they could not stand that which was commanded, “If even an animal touches the mountain, it shall be stoned”;","understanding":"οὐκ ἔφερον γὰρ τὸ διαστελλόμενον, “for they could not stand that which was commanded,” “If even an animal touches the mountain, it shall be stoned.” Sinai's holiness kept everyone at a distance.","words":[{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"stand"},{"strongs":"G1291","hebrew":"διαστέλλομαι","translit":"diastéllomai","english":"commanded"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"animal"},{"strongs":"G2345","hebrew":"θιγγάνω","translit":"thingánō","english":"touches"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"},{"strongs":"G3036","hebrew":"λιθοβολέω","translit":"lithoboléō","english":"stoned"}]},{"v":21,"text":"and so fearful was the appearance that Moses said, “I am terrified and trembling.”","understanding":"καί, οὕτω φοβερὸν ἦν τὸ φανταζόμενον, “and so fearful was the appearance” — φαντάζω, the spectacle; from the root of φαίνω in the appear family. Even Moses, the mediator, said: “I am terrified and trembling.” If Moses trembled, who could approach?","words":[{"strongs":"G5398","hebrew":"φοβερός","translit":"phoberós","english":"fearful"},{"strongs":"G5324","hebrew":"φαντάζω","translit":"phantázō","english":"appearance"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G1630","hebrew":"ἔκφοβος","translit":"ékphobos","english":"terrified"},{"strongs":"G1790","hebrew":"ἔντρομος","translit":"éntromos","english":"trembling"}]},{"v":22,"text":"But you have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to innumerable multitudes of angels,","understanding":"ἀλλὰ προσεληλύθατε Σιὼν ὄρει, “But you have come to Mount Zion” — perfect tense: you have already arrived. καὶ πόλει θεοῦ ζῶντος, Ἰερουσαλὴμ ἐπουρανίῳ, “and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem” — the city Abraham looked for (11:10). καὶ μυριάσιν ἀγγέλων, “and to innumerable multitudes of angels” — tens of thousands.","words":[{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G4622","hebrew":"Σιών","translit":"Siṓn","english":"Zion"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"Mount"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G2419","hebrew":"Ἱερουσαλήμ","translit":"Hierousalḗm","english":"Jerusalem"},{"strongs":"G3461","hebrew":"μυριάς","translit":"myriás","english":"innumerable multitudes"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"}]},{"v":23,"text":"to the festal gathering and assembly of the firstborn who are enrolled in heaven, to God the Judge of all, to the spirits of just men made perfect,","understanding":"πανηγύρει, “to the festal gathering” — a joyful festival assembly. καὶ ἐκκλησίᾳ πρωτοτόκων ἀπογεγραμμένων ἐν οὐρανοῖς, “and assembly of the firstborn who are enrolled in heaven” — the church, whose names are registered in heaven. καὶ κριτῇ θεῷ πάντων, “to God the Judge of all.” καὶ πνεύμασι δικαίων τετελειωμένων, “to the spirits of just men made perfect” — τελειόω from the perfect family: the Old Testament saints of chapter 11, now brought to the goal through Christ (fulfilling 11:40).","words":[{"strongs":"G3831","hebrew":"πανήγυρις","translit":"panḗgyris","english":"festal gathering"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G4416","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","english":"firstborn"},{"strongs":"G583","hebrew":"ἀπογράφω","translit":"apográphō","english":"enrolled"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2923","hebrew":"κριτής","translit":"kritḗs","english":"Judge"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"just"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"made perfect"}]},{"v":24,"text":"to Jesus, the mediator of a new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better than that of Abel.","understanding":"καὶ διαθήκης νέας μεσίτῃ Ἰησοῦ, “to Jesus, the mediator of a new covenant” — a word-family point: here ‘new’ is νέος, new in time, freshly given; in 8:8 and 9:15 it was καινός, new in kind. The covenant is both. καὶ αἵματι ῥαντισμοῦ κρεῖττον λαλοῦντι παρὰ τὸν Ἄβελ, “and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better than that of Abel” — κρείττων, ‘better’, one of its last uses. Abel's blood cried from the ground for justice; Jesus' blood speaks forgiveness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G3316","hebrew":"μεσίτης","translit":"mesítēs","english":"mediator"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G4473","hebrew":"ῥαντισμός","translit":"rhantismós","english":"sprinkling"},{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaks"},{"strongs":"G6","hebrew":"Ἄβελ","translit":"Ábel","english":"Abel"}]},{"v":25,"text":"See that you don’t refuse him who speaks. For if they didn’t escape when they refused him who warned on the earth, how much more will we not escape who turn away from him who warns from heaven,","understanding":"βλέπετε μὴ παραιτήσησθε τὸν λαλοῦντα, “See that you don’t refuse him who speaks” — βλέπω from the see family, as ‘watch out’ (3:12). The last warning of the letter: the same verb as ‘begged’ in v. 19. εἰ γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι οὐκ ἐξέφυγον ἐπὶ γῆς παραιτησάμενοι τὸν χρηματίζοντα, “For if they didn’t escape when they refused him who warned on the earth,” πολὺ μᾶλλον ἡμεῖς οἱ τὸν ἀπ᾽ οὐρανῶν ἀποστρεφόμενοι, “how much more will we not escape who turn away from him who warns from heaven.”","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"See"},{"strongs":"G3868","hebrew":"παραιτέομαι","translit":"paraitéomai","english":"refuse"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaks"},{"strongs":"G1628","hebrew":"ἐκφεύγω","translit":"ekpheúgō","english":"escape"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5537","hebrew":"χρηματίζω","translit":"chrēmatízō","english":"warned"},{"strongs":"G654","hebrew":"ἀποστρέφω","translit":"apostréphō","english":"turn away"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"}]},{"v":26,"text":"whose voice shook the earth then, but now he has promised, saying, “Yet once more I will shake not only the earth, but also the heavens.”","understanding":"οὗ ἡ φωνὴ τὴν γῆν ἐσάλευσεν τότε, “whose voice shook the earth then” — at Sinai. νῦν δὲ ἐπήγγελται λέγων, “but now he has promised, saying,” “Yet once more I will shake not only the earth, but also the heavens.” The quotation is from Haggai 2, which promised a final shaking of all creation.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4531","hebrew":"σαλεύω","translit":"saleúō","english":"shook"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promised"},{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G4579","hebrew":"σείω","translit":"seíō","english":"shake"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"}]},{"v":27,"text":"This phrase, “Yet once more”, signifies the removing of those things that are shaken, as of things that have been made, that those things which are not shaken may remain.","understanding":"τὸ δέ, ‘Ἔτι ἅπαξ,’ δηλοῖ, “This phrase, ‘Yet once more’, signifies” — ἅπαξ, once: one final time. τὴν τῶν σαλευομένων μετάθεσιν ὡς πεποιημένων, “the removing of those things that are shaken, as of things that have been made” — created things that can be removed. ἵνα μείνῃ τὰ μὴ σαλευόμενα, “that those things which are not shaken may remain.” Only what is eternal will survive the final shaking.","words":[{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G1213","hebrew":"δηλόω","translit":"dēlóō","english":"signifies"},{"strongs":"G3331","hebrew":"μετάθεσις","translit":"metáthesis","english":"removing"},{"strongs":"G4531","hebrew":"σαλεύω","translit":"saleúō","english":"shaken"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"made"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"}]},{"v":28,"text":"Therefore, receiving a Kingdom that can’t be shaken, let us have grace, through which we serve God acceptably, with reverence and awe,","understanding":"διὸ βασιλείαν ἀσάλευτον παραλαμβάνοντες, “Therefore, receiving a Kingdom that can’t be shaken” — present tense: we are receiving it now. ἔχωμεν χάριν, “let us have grace” — χάρις can also mean thanks: ‘let us be grateful’. δι᾽ ἧς λατρεύωμεν εὐαρέστως τῷ θεῷ, “through which we serve God acceptably” — λατρεύω from the servant family: worship-service. μετὰ αἰδοῦς καὶ εὐλαβείας, “with reverence and awe” — in the manuscripts the WEB follows: αἰδώς, reverent modesty, and εὐλάβεια from the godliness family (as in 5:7); many older manuscripts read εὐλαβείας καὶ δέους, ‘reverence and fear’. Grace does not make worship casual; it makes it grateful and reverent.","words":[{"strongs":"G761","hebrew":"ἀσάλευτος","translit":"asáleutos","english":"can’t be shaken"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G3880","hebrew":"παραλαμβάνω","translit":"paralambánō","english":"receiving"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G2102","hebrew":"εὐαρέστως","translit":"euaréstōs","english":"acceptably"},{"strongs":"G127","hebrew":"αἰδώς","translit":"aidṓs","english":"reverence"},{"strongs":"G2124","hebrew":"εὐλάβεια","translit":"eulábeia","english":"awe"}]},{"v":29,"text":"for our God is a consuming fire.","understanding":"καὶ γὰρ ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶν πῦρ καταναλίσκον, “for our God is a consuming fire” — from Deuteronomy 4:24. ‘Our’ God: the same God who is gracious in Christ is holy fire. The God of Sinai has not changed; in Christ we come to Him safely, but never lightly.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2654","hebrew":"καταναλίσκω","translit":"katanalískō","english":"consuming"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Runner Fixing His Eyes","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a long-distance runner in a vast stadium, the stands packed with former champions who once ran the same course. He has thrown off his heavy cloak and the robe that kept wrapping round his legs. His muscles burn; he is tempted to slow down. Then he lifts his eyes to the finish line, where One stands who has already run the whole course — through a cross — and now sits in honour, waiting. The runner fixes his gaze there and keeps going.","meaning":"“Therefore let us also, seeing we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, lay aside every weight and the sin which so easily entangles us, and let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us” (v. 1), “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith” (v. 2). Throw off what slows you down. Keep your eyes on Jesus, who began your faith and will finish it."},{"title":"A Father's Training","verseRef":"v. 5-11","image":"Picture a father teaching his son to ride a bicycle. He corrects, holds, lets go, watches the boy fall and scrape his knee, picks him up, and sends him off again. The boy cries and thinks his father is hard. Years later, riding freely down a hill, he understands: every fall and correction was love making him strong.","meaning":"“For whom the Lord loves, he chastens” (v. 6). “All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous; yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby” (v. 11). Your hardships may be God's training. Ask the Spirit not only to remove the pain but to teach you through it, so that you share His holiness."},{"title":"Two Mountains","verseRef":"v. 18-24","image":"Picture a dark mountain wrapped in smoke and fire, thunder rolling and a trumpet blasting louder and louder. People cower at its foot, begging for the voice to stop. A fence keeps them back. Then picture another mountain bathed in light, where a great city shines. Angels by the thousand are celebrating. A joyful crowd of ordinary believers stands near the throne, and at the centre is Jesus, whose blood speaks mercy.","meaning":"“For you have not come to a mountain that might be touched, and that burned with fire” (v. 18). “But you have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem” (v. 22), “to Jesus, the mediator of a new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better than that of Abel” (v. 24). In Christ you have come to the mountain of grace. Come with joy — and with reverence, for “our God is a consuming fire” (v. 29)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Run Looking to Jesus (v. 1-3)","body":"Surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, believers should throw off every weight and entangling sin and run with endurance the race set before them, fixing their eyes on Jesus, the pioneer and perfecter of faith, who endured the cross for the joy set before Him and is seated at God's right hand; considering Him will keep them from growing weary."},{"label":"God's Fatherly Discipline (v. 4-13)","body":"The readers had not yet resisted to the point of blood, and had forgotten that God disciplines those He loves as sons; all true children receive discipline, and if earthly fathers disciplined for a short time as they saw fit, God disciplines for our good, that we may share His holiness; discipline is painful now but later yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness, so they should strengthen weak hands and knees and make straight paths."},{"label":"Pursue Peace and Holiness; Beware Esau's Choice (v. 14-17)","body":"Pursue peace with everyone and the holiness without which no one will see the Lord, watching that no one falls short of God's grace, that no bitter root causes trouble, and that no one is sexually immoral or godless like Esau, who sold his birthright for a single meal and later could not undo it, though he sought it with tears."},{"label":"Sinai and Zion (v. 18-24)","body":"Believers have not come to the terrifying, touchable mountain of Sinai with its fire, darkness, storm, trumpet and voice, which even Moses feared, but to Mount Zion, the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, to myriads of angels in festal gathering, to the church of the firstborn enrolled in heaven, to God the Judge of all, to the spirits of the righteous made perfect, and to Jesus, mediator of a new covenant, whose sprinkled blood speaks better than Abel's."},{"label":"The Final Warning and the Unshakable Kingdom (v. 25-29)","body":"Do not refuse Him who speaks: if those who refused the earthly warning did not escape, much less will those who reject the One who warns from heaven; God will shake heaven and earth once more, removing what can be shaken, so that what cannot be shaken remains; therefore, receiving an unshakable kingdom, let us be grateful and worship God acceptably with reverence and awe, for our God is a consuming fire."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Looking to Jesus","paragraphs":["Chapter 12 turns from the heroes of faith to the race we must run. “Therefore let us also, seeing we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, lay aside every weight and the sin which so easily entangles us, and let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us” (v. 1). The witnesses of chapter 11 testify that faith can finish. Runners strip off anything that slows them down: not only sins, but ‘weights’ — things that may not be wrong in themselves but hold us back. Paul writes, “Don’t you know that those who run in a race all run, but one receives the prize? Run like that, that you may win” (1 Corinthians 9:24).","The key is where we look: “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith, who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising its shame, and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God” (v. 2). Two word families describe Him: ἀρχηγός (author family) — the pioneer who began the race of faith and ran it first; and τελειωτής (perfect family) — the one who completes it. He is not only our example; He is the source and finisher of our faith.","When we are tired, we are to compare our struggle with His: “For consider him who has endured such contradiction of sinners against himself, that you don’t grow weary, fainting in your souls” (v. 3). “You have not yet resisted to blood, striving against sin” (v. 4). Jesus resisted to the point of blood. Remembering His endurance renews ours. Paul says, “Let us not be weary in doing good, for we will reap in due season, if we don’t give up” (Galatians 6:9)."]},{"heading":"Discipline as Proof of Sonship","paragraphs":["The readers had forgotten something important: “My son, don’t take lightly the chastening of the Lord, nor faint when you are reproved by him” (v. 5), “For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he receives” (v. 6). The discipline family appears throughout this passage. One Greek word, παιδεία, is rendered ‘chastening’, ‘discipline’ and, as a verb, ‘punished’. It means the whole training of a child — teaching, correction and shaping. Jesus says to the church, “As many as I love, I reprove and chasten” (Revelation 3:19).","Hardship is not proof that God has abandoned us; it can be proof that He is treating us as children. “It is for discipline that you endure. God deals with you as with children” (v. 7). Earthly fathers trained us imperfectly and briefly, “but he for our profit, that we may be partakers of his holiness” (v. 10). The goal of God's discipline is not punishment but participation in His own holiness.","The writer is honest: “All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous; yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby” (v. 11). The word ‘exercised’ (γυμνάζω) is athletic training. James says, “knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance” (James 1:3). So, “Therefore lift up the hands that hang down and the feeble knees” (v. 12). Pursue peace and holiness, watch over one another, and do not be like Esau, who traded his future for one meal."]},{"heading":"Not Sinai, but Zion","paragraphs":["The letter's climax contrasts two mountains. Believers have not come to Sinai, “a mountain that might be touched, and that burned with fire, and to blackness, darkness, storm” (v. 18), where the voice was so terrifying that the people begged it to stop and even Moses said, “I am terrified and trembling” (v. 21). “But you have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to innumerable multitudes of angels” (v. 22). Paul calls it “the Jerusalem that is above” (Galatians 4:26).","At Zion we meet a joyful assembly: angels in festival, “the festal gathering and assembly of the firstborn who are enrolled in heaven, to God the Judge of all, to the spirits of just men made perfect” (v. 23). Jesus told His disciples to “rejoice that your names are written in heaven” (Luke 10:20). The Old Testament believers of chapter 11 are now ‘made perfect’ — the perfect-family word τελειόω fulfilling 11:40. And at the centre is “Jesus, the mediator of a new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better than that of Abel” (v. 24). Abel's blood cried for justice; Jesus' blood speaks mercy.","Yet grace does not remove awe. “See that you don’t refuse him who speaks” (v. 25). God promised through Haggai to shake heaven and earth once more, removing everything that can be shaken. “Therefore, receiving a Kingdom that can’t be shaken, let us have grace, through which we serve God acceptably, with reverence and awe” (v. 28), “for our God is a consuming fire” (v. 29). Peter asks, “what kind of people ought you to be in holy living and godliness” (2 Peter 3:11)?"]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Race, the Father's Love and the Final Shaking","paragraphs":["Paul often uses the image of a race. “I therefore run like that, not aimlessly” (1 Corinthians 9:26). “Forgetting the things which are behind, and stretching forward to the things which are before” (Philippians 3:13), “I press on toward the goal for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus” (Philippians 3:14). Jesus Himself “intently set his face to go to Jerusalem” (Luke 9:51), enduring the cross for the joy set before Him.","The writer quotes Haggai's promise of a final shaking. Haggai's own words are, “Yet once, it is a little while, and I will shake the heavens, the earth, the sea, and the dry land” (Haggai 2:6), and “and I will shake all nations” (Haggai 2:7). Jesus said, “Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away” (Matthew 24:35). Peter describes the day when “the heavens will pass away with a great noise, and the elements will be dissolved with fervent heat” (2 Peter 3:10).","The joyful assembly on Mount Zion appears in Revelation: “I saw, and behold, the Lamb standing on Mount Zion” (Revelation 14:1). Those who are holy will see God: “Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God” (Matthew 5:8), and in the end, “They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads” (Revelation 22:4). That is why the writer calls us to pursue holiness, run with endurance, and serve God with reverence and awe."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Perfecter / finisher","hebrew":"τελειωτής","translit":"teleiōtḗs","strongs":"G5051","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “a completer, i.e. consummater.” It appears only here in the entire Bible and may have been coined by the writer. It belongs to the perfect family: the one who brings faith to its τέλος, its goal. Paired with ἀρχηγός, the pioneer, it means Jesus is both the beginning and the end of faith — the first to run the course and the One who carries His people across the finish line."},{"english":"Easily entangling / clinging","hebrew":"εὐπερίστατος","translit":"euperístatos","strongs":"G2139","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “well standing around, i.e. (a competitor) thwarting (a racer) in every direction (figuratively, of sin in genitive case).” It appears only here. It may describe sin that ‘stands around’ us on every side, or that clings closely like a long garment wrapped round a runner's legs. Either way, the point is the same: sin trips believers in their race and must be deliberately laid aside."},{"english":"Discipline / chastening / training","hebrew":"παιδεία","translit":"paideía","strongs":"G3809","verses":"v. 5, 7, 8, 11","insight":"Strong's: “tutorage, i.e. education or training; by implication, disciplinary correction.” It comes from παῖς, a child, and describes the whole process of raising a child. The WEB renders it ‘chastening’ in 12:5 and 12:11 and ‘discipline’ in 12:7–8, and Paul uses it of Scripture's ‘instruction in righteousness’ (2 Timothy 3:16). This study tags it in the new discipline family."},{"english":"Profane / godless","hebrew":"βέβηλος","translit":"bébēlos","strongs":"G952","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “accessible (as by crossing the door-way), i.e. (by implication, of Jewish notions) heathenish, wicked.” It describes someone who treats holy things as common — whose life has no room for the sacred. Esau is the example: he valued an immediate meal more than the birthright and blessing God had attached to it. Paul warns Timothy to avoid ‘profane babblings’ with the same word."},{"english":"Unshakable / immovable","hebrew":"ἀσάλευτος","translit":"asáleutos","strongs":"G761","verses":"v. 28","insight":"Strong's: “unshaken, i.e. (by implication) immovable (figuratively).” Luke uses it of the bow of Paul's ship, ‘stuck fast and remained immovable’ when it ran aground (Acts 27:41). Believers are receiving a Kingdom that cannot be shaken. When God shakes everything created, His Kingdom — and those who belong to it — will remain."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit calls you to keep running, to receive discipline as love, and to worship with joy and awe.","items":[{"title":"What Is Weighing You Down?","body":"The Spirit wants you to run freely. Ask Him to show you any ‘weight’ — a habit, relationship, distraction or attachment — and any sin that keeps tripping you. Lay it aside today, and fix your eyes again on Jesus."},{"title":"Discipline Is Not Rejection","body":"Perhaps you are going through a painful season and wondering whether God has turned against you. The Spirit reminds you: the Lord disciplines those He loves. Ask Him what He is teaching you and how He is making you share His holiness."},{"title":"You Have Come to Zion","body":"You are not standing at the foot of Sinai in terror. You have come to the joyful city of God, to the church of the firstborn, and to Jesus, whose blood speaks mercy. The Spirit invites you to worship with joy — and with the reverence due to a holy God."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Weight Check","body":"List three things that slow your spiritual race (v. 1). Choose one to lay aside this week, even if it is not sinful in itself."},{"title":"The Discipline Check","body":"How are you responding to hardship — taking it lightly, collapsing under it, or letting it train you (vv. 5, 11)? Ask God what fruit He wants to grow."},{"title":"The Esau Check","body":"Is there any ‘one meal’ — an immediate pleasure or relief — that you are tempted to trade for your spiritual inheritance (v. 16)? Name it and refuse it."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Look to Jesus Daily","body":"Each morning for a month, before checking your phone or starting work, spend five minutes ‘looking to Jesus’ (v. 2): read a short Gospel passage about Him, and thank Him that He is the author and perfecter of your faith. Notice how this affects your endurance through the day."},{"title":"Make Straight Paths for the Weak","body":"Verse 13 calls the strong to make the path easier for the lame. Identify someone in your church who is struggling — weary, wounded or new. This month, walk alongside them: pray with them, help practically, and remove obstacles that could cause them to stumble."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You for the great cloud of witnesses who show that faith can finish. Help me lay aside every weight and the sin that so easily entangles me, and run with endurance the race You have set before me. I fix my eyes on Jesus, the author and perfecter of my faith, who for the joy set before Him endured the cross, despised its shame, and sat down at Your right hand. When I grow weary, help me consider Him. Thank You that You discipline me as Your child, because You love me. Help me not to take Your discipline lightly or to faint under it, but to be trained by it, so that I share Your holiness and bear the peaceful fruit of righteousness. Make me pursue peace with everyone and the holiness without which no one will see You. Keep me from bitterness and from selling my inheritance for a moment's relief. Thank You that I have not come to the terrors of Sinai, but to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God, to the joyful assembly of the firstborn, and to Jesus, whose blood speaks better than Abel's. I am receiving a kingdom that cannot be shaken. Make me grateful, and let me worship You acceptably, with reverence and awe, for You are a consuming fire. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Esau Sells His Birthright","ref":"Genesis 25:29-34; 27:30-40","relatedVerses":"v. 16-17","summary":"One day Esau came in from the field exhausted and hungry, and found his brother Jacob cooking red lentil stew. He begged for some. Jacob asked for his birthright in exchange, and Esau, thinking only of his hunger, swore it over and ate. Genesis says he despised his birthright. Years later, when Isaac gave the firstborn's blessing to Jacob, Esau cried out with a loud and bitter cry and begged for a blessing, but the blessing could not be reversed. Hebrews 12:16–17 uses Esau as a warning against trading eternal things for immediate desires."},{"title":"The Terror of Sinai","ref":"Exodus 19:10-25; 20:18-21","relatedVerses":"v. 18-21","summary":"On the third day after Israel arrived at Sinai, there was thunder and lightning, a thick cloud on the mountain and a very loud trumpet blast. The whole mountain was wrapped in smoke because the LORD descended on it in fire, and it trembled violently. Boundaries were set around it; any person or animal that touched it was to be put to death. When the people heard God's voice and saw the fire, they trembled and stood far off, begging Moses to speak to them instead of God. Hebrews 12 contrasts this terrifying scene with the joyful approach believers now have to God through Jesus."},{"title":"Jesus Endures the Cross","ref":"Luke 22:39-23:46","relatedVerses":"v. 2-3","summary":"In Gethsemane Jesus prayed, “Father, if you are willing, remove this cup from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but yours, be done” (Luke 22:42). He was arrested, mocked, beaten and condemned. He carried His cross to the place called The Skull, where He was crucified between two criminals, mocked by rulers and soldiers, and yet prayed for His executioners. Darkness covered the land, and He cried out, ‘Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!’ and breathed His last. Hebrews 12:2 says He endured the cross and despised its shame for the joy set before Him — and now He is seated at the right hand of God's throne."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Hebrews","chapter":13,"chapterTitle":"Life Outside the Camp with the Unchanging Christ","themeTitle":"“Jesus Christ Is the Same Yesterday, Today, and Forever”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Let brotherly love continue.","understanding":"ἡ φιλαδελφία μενέτω, “Let brotherly love continue” — φιλαδελφία from the love family: the warm affection of brothers and sisters. μένω, to remain: they already had it (6:10; 10:33–34); it must not fade under pressure. The great doctrine of the letter now becomes ordinary love.","words":[{"strongs":"G5360","hebrew":"φιλαδελφία","translit":"philadelphía","english":"brotherly love"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"continue"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Don’t forget to show hospitality to strangers, for in doing so, some have entertained angels without knowing it.","understanding":"τῆς φιλοξενίας μὴ ἐπιλανθάνεσθε, “Don’t forget to show hospitality to strangers” — φιλοξενία, added to the love family: love of strangers. Travelling Christians depended on believers' homes; inns were often unsafe and immoral. διὰ ταύτης γὰρ ἔλαθόν τινες ξενίσαντες ἀγγέλους, “for in doing so, some have entertained angels without knowing it” — Abraham and Sarah at Mamre (Genesis 18), and Lot in Sodom.","words":[{"strongs":"G1950","hebrew":"ἐπιλανθάνομαι","translit":"epilanthánomai","english":"forget"},{"strongs":"G5381","hebrew":"φιλονεξία","translit":"philonexía","english":"hospitality"},{"strongs":"G2990","hebrew":"λανθάνω","translit":"lanthánō","english":"without knowing"},{"strongs":"G3579","hebrew":"ξενίζω","translit":"xenízō","english":"entertained"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Remember those who are in bonds, as bound with them; and those who are ill-treated, since you are also in the body.","understanding":"μιμνῄσκεσθε τῶν δεσμίων ὡς συνδεδεμένοι, “Remember those who are in bonds, as bound with them” — feel their chains as your own. τῶν κακουχουμένων ὡς καὶ αὐτοὶ ὄντες ἐν σώματι, “and those who are ill-treated, since you are also in the body” — you too are physically vulnerable; it could be you. Jesus said that visiting the prisoner is visiting Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G3403","hebrew":"μιμνήσκω","translit":"mimnḗskō","english":"Remember"},{"strongs":"G1198","hebrew":"δέσμιος","translit":"désmios","english":"bonds"},{"strongs":"G4887","hebrew":"συνδέω","translit":"syndéō","english":"bound with them"},{"strongs":"G2558","hebrew":"κακουχέω","translit":"kakouchéō","english":"ill-treated"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Let marriage be held in honor among all, and let the bed be undefiled: but God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers.","understanding":"τίμιος ὁ γάμος ἐν πᾶσιν, “Let marriage be held in honor among all” — τίμιος, precious, valued. καὶ ἡ κοίτη ἀμίαντος, “and let the bed be undefiled” — ἀμίαντος, the word used of Jesus as undefiled High Priest (7:26). πόρνους δὲ καὶ μοιχοὺς κρινεῖ ὁ θεός, “but God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers.” Some in that world despised marriage (as ascetic false teachers did, 1 Timothy 4:3); others despised faithfulness. The writer honours both marriage and purity.","words":[{"strongs":"G1062","hebrew":"γάμος","translit":"gámos","english":"marriage"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"held in honor"},{"strongs":"G2845","hebrew":"κοίτη","translit":"koítē","english":"bed"},{"strongs":"G283","hebrew":"ἀμίαντος","translit":"amíantos","english":"undefiled"},{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexually immoral"},{"strongs":"G3432","hebrew":"μοιχός","translit":"moichós","english":"adulterers"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Be free from the love of money, content with such things as you have, for he has said, “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you.”","understanding":"ἀφιλάργυρος ὁ τρόπος, “Be free from the love of money” — ἀφιλάργυρος, added to the love family: not a lover of money, the opposite of the φιλάργυροι of 2 Timothy 3:2. ἀρκούμενοι τοῖς παροῦσιν, “content with such things as you have” — after losing their property, as 10:34 recalls, contentment was a real test. Why? αὐτὸς γὰρ εἴρηκεν, “for he has said,” “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you.” In Greek the promise piles up five negatives — ‘I will never, never, never leave you’. God's presence is better than money.","words":[{"strongs":"G866","hebrew":"ἀφιλάργυρος","translit":"aphilárgyros","english":"love of money"},{"strongs":"G5158","hebrew":"τρόπος","translit":"trópos","english":"Be"},{"strongs":"G714","hebrew":"ἀρκέω","translit":"arkéō","english":"content"},{"strongs":"G447","hebrew":"ἀνίημι","translit":"aníēmi","english":"leave"},{"strongs":"G1459","hebrew":"ἐγκαταλείπω","translit":"enkataleípō","english":"forsake"}]},{"v":6,"text":"So that with good courage we say, “The Lord is my helper. I will not fear. What can man do to me?”","understanding":"ὥστε θαρροῦντας ἡμᾶς λέγειν, “So that with good courage we say” — from Psalm 118: “The Lord is my helper. I will not fear. What can man do to me?” βοηθός, helper (the word of 4:16's ‘help’). Those who had faced confiscation and prison could sing this with meaning.","words":[{"strongs":"G2292","hebrew":"θαῤῥέω","translit":"tharrhéō","english":"good courage"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G998","hebrew":"βοηθός","translit":"boēthós","english":"helper"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Remember your leaders, men who spoke to you the word of God, and considering the results of their conduct, imitate their faith.","understanding":"μνημονεύετε τῶν ἡγουμένων ὑμῶν, “Remember your leaders” — probably former leaders who had died, perhaps as martyrs. οἵτινες ἐλάλησαν ὑμῖν τὸν λόγον τοῦ θεοῦ, “men who spoke to you the word of God” — λόγος from the word family. ὧν ἀναθεωροῦντες τὴν ἔκβασιν τῆς ἀναστροφῆς, “and considering the results of their conduct” — ἀναθεωρέω, to look carefully again (θεωρέω from the see family); ἔκβασις, the outcome, the way their lives ended. μιμεῖσθε τὴν πίστιν, “imitate their faith.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"Remember"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"leaders"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G333","hebrew":"ἀναθεωρέω","translit":"anatheōréō","english":"considering"},{"strongs":"G1545","hebrew":"ἔκβασις","translit":"ékbasis","english":"results"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"conduct"},{"strongs":"G3401","hebrew":"μιμέομαι","translit":"miméomai","english":"imitate"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today, and forever.","understanding":"Ἰησοῦς Χριστὸς ἐχθὲς καὶ σήμερον ὁ αὐτὸς καὶ εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας, “Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today, and forever.” Two time-family words: σήμερον, ‘today’ (the word of chapters 3–4), and αἰών, the ages. Leaders change and die (v. 7); Jesus does not. He is the same Son of 1:12 (‘you are the same’), the same High Priest who lives forever. The God of Malachi said the same: He does not change.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G5504","hebrew":"χθές","translit":"chthés","english":"yesterday"},{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"today"},{"strongs":"G846","hebrew":"αὐτός","translit":"autós","english":"same"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Don’t be carried away by various and strange teachings, for it is good that the heart be established by grace, not by food, through which those who were so occupied were not benefited.","understanding":"διδαχαῖς ποικίλαις καὶ ξέναις μὴ παραφέρεσθε, “Don’t be carried away by various and strange teachings” — ξένος, foreign: the same root as ‘strangers’ in v. 2. Welcome strangers; do not welcome strange doctrines. καλὸν γὰρ χάριτι βεβαιοῦσθαι τὴν καρδίαν, “for it is good that the heart be established by grace” — καλός from the good family. οὐ βρώμασιν, “not by food” — ritual food laws and sacrificial meals. ἐν οἷς οὐκ ὠφελήθησαν οἱ περιπατοῦντες, “through which those who were so occupied were not benefited.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4064","hebrew":"περιφέρω","translit":"periphérō","english":"carried away"},{"strongs":"G4164","hebrew":"ποικίλος","translit":"poikílos","english":"various"},{"strongs":"G3581","hebrew":"ξένος","translit":"xénos","english":"strange"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teachings"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G950","hebrew":"βεβαιόω","translit":"bebaióō","english":"established"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G1033","hebrew":"βρῶμα","translit":"brōma","english":"food"},{"strongs":"G5623","hebrew":"ὠφελέω","translit":"ōpheléō","english":"benefited"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"occupied"}]},{"v":10,"text":"We have an altar from which those who serve the holy tabernacle have no right to eat.","understanding":"ἔχομεν θυσιαστήριον, “We have an altar” — the cross of Christ, His sacrifice. ἐξ οὗ φαγεῖν οὐκ ἔχουσιν ἐξουσίαν οἱ τῇ σκηνῇ λατρεύοντες, “from which those who serve the holy tabernacle have no right to eat.” ἐξουσία from the power family: here ‘right’, authorisation. λατρεύω from the servant family. Those who cling to the old system as the way to God do not share in Christ's sacrifice.","words":[{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G5315","hebrew":"φάγω","translit":"phágō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For the bodies of those animals, whose blood is brought into the holy place by the high priest as an offering for sin, are burned outside of the camp.","understanding":"ὧν γὰρ εἰσφέρεται ζῴων τὸ αἷμα περὶ ἁμαρτίας εἰς τὰ ἅγια διὰ τοῦ ἀρχιερέως, “For the bodies of those animals, whose blood is brought into the holy place by the high priest as an offering for sin,” τούτων τὰ σώματα κατακαίεται ἔξω τῆς παρεμβολῆς, “are burned outside of the camp” — on the Day of Atonement, the sin-offering carcasses were not eaten but burned outside the camp (Leviticus 16).","words":[{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"animals"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G1533","hebrew":"εἰσφέρω","translit":"eisphérō","english":"brought"},{"strongs":"G39","hebrew":"ἅγιον","translit":"hágion","english":"holy place"},{"strongs":"G749","hebrew":"ἀρχιερεύς","translit":"archiereús","english":"high priest"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"bodies"},{"strongs":"G2618","hebrew":"κατακαίω","translit":"katakaíō","english":"burned"},{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G3925","hebrew":"παρεμβολή","translit":"parembolḗ","english":"camp"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Therefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people through his own blood, suffered outside of the gate.","understanding":"διὸ καὶ Ἰησοῦς, ἵνα ἁγιάσῃ διὰ τοῦ ἰδίου αἵματος τὸν λαόν, “Therefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people through his own blood,” ἔξω τῆς πύλης ἔπαθεν, “suffered outside of the gate” — Golgotha was outside Jerusalem's walls. He fulfilled the sin offering in the place of rejection.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctify"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2398","hebrew":"ἴδιος","translit":"ídios","english":"own"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G4439","hebrew":"πύλη","translit":"pýlē","english":"gate"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Let us therefore go out to him outside of the camp, bearing his reproach.","understanding":"τοίνυν ἐξερχώμεθα πρὸς αὐτὸν ἔξω τῆς παρεμβολῆς, “Let us therefore go out to him outside of the camp” — leave the security of the old religious system and its approval. τὸν ὀνειδισμὸν αὐτοῦ φέροντες, “bearing his reproach” — the very reproach Moses chose in 11:26. This was the readers' real decision: stay inside the camp of respectable religion, or go out to Jesus and share His rejection.","words":[{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"go out"},{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"outside"},{"strongs":"G3925","hebrew":"παρεμβολή","translit":"parembolḗ","english":"camp"},{"strongs":"G3680","hebrew":"ὀνειδισμός","translit":"oneidismós","english":"reproach"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"bearing"}]},{"v":14,"text":"For we don’t have here an enduring city, but we seek that which is to come.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ ἔχομεν ὧδε μένουσαν πόλιν, “For we don’t have here an enduring city” — not even Jerusalem, which would soon be destroyed. ἀλλὰ τὴν μέλλουσαν ἐπιζητοῦμεν, “but we seek that which is to come” — the city with foundations that Abraham sought (11:10, 16).","words":[{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"enduring"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G1934","hebrew":"ἐπιζητέω","translit":"epizētéō","english":"seek"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Through him, then, let us offer up a sacrifice of praise to God continually, that is, the fruit of lips which proclaim allegiance to his name.","understanding":"δι᾽ αὐτοῦ οὖν ἀναφέρωμεν θυσίαν αἰνέσεως διὰ παντὸς τῷ θεῷ, “Through him, then, let us offer up a sacrifice of praise to God continually” — two offering-family words: ἀναφέρω and θυσία. The animal sacrifices have ended, but believers still offer sacrifices. τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν καρπὸν χειλέων ὁμολογούντων τῷ ὀνόματι αὐτοῦ, “that is, the fruit of lips which proclaim allegiance to his name” — ὁμολογέω, to confess, acknowledge openly.","words":[{"strongs":"G399","hebrew":"ἀναφέρω","translit":"anaphérō","english":"offer up"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G133","hebrew":"αἴνεσις","translit":"aínesis","english":"praise"},{"strongs":"G1275","hebrew":"διαπαντός","translit":"diapantós","english":"continually"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruit"},{"strongs":"G5491","hebrew":"χεῖλος","translit":"cheîlos","english":"lips"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"proclaim allegiance"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"}]},{"v":16,"text":"But don’t forget to be doing good and sharing, for with such sacrifices God is well pleased.","understanding":"τῆς δὲ εὐποιΐας καὶ κοινωνίας μὴ ἐπιλανθάνεσθε, “But don’t forget to be doing good and sharing” — εὐποιΐα, added to the good family: doing good, practical kindness; κοινωνία, sharing what you have. τοιαύταις γὰρ θυσίαις εὐαρεστεῖται ὁ θεός, “for with such sacrifices God is well pleased.” θυσία again: praise (v. 15) and generosity (v. 16) are the sacrifices of the new covenant.","words":[{"strongs":"G2140","hebrew":"εὐποιΐα","translit":"eupoiḯa","english":"doing good"},{"strongs":"G2842","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","english":"sharing"},{"strongs":"G1950","hebrew":"ἐπιλανθάνομαι","translit":"epilanthánomai","english":"forget"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G2100","hebrew":"εὐαρεστέω","translit":"euarestéō","english":"well pleased"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Obey your leaders and submit to them, for they watch on behalf of your souls, as those who will give account, that they may do this with joy, and not with groaning, for that would be unprofitable for you.","understanding":"πείθεσθε τοῖς ἡγουμένοις ὑμῶν καὶ ὑπείκετε, “Obey your leaders and submit to them” — present leaders (compare v. 7). αὐτοὶ γὰρ ἀγρυπνοῦσιν ὑπὲρ τῶν ψυχῶν ὑμῶν, “for they watch on behalf of your souls” — ἀγρυπνέω, added to the sleep family: to go without sleep, to keep watch like a shepherd at night. ψυχή from the life family. ὡς λόγον ἀποδώσοντες, “as those who will give account” — λόγος again, as in 4:13. ἵνα μετὰ χαρᾶς τοῦτο ποιῶσιν καὶ μὴ στενάζοντες, “that they may do this with joy, and not with groaning,” ἀλυσιτελὲς γὰρ ὑμῖν τοῦτο, “for that would be unprofitable for you.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"Obey"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"leaders"},{"strongs":"G5226","hebrew":"ὑπείκω","translit":"hypeíkō","english":"submit"},{"strongs":"G69","hebrew":"ἀγρυπνέω","translit":"agrypnéō","english":"watch"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"account"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G4727","hebrew":"στενάζω","translit":"stenázō","english":"groaning"},{"strongs":"G255","hebrew":"ἀλυσιτελής","translit":"alysitelḗs","english":"unprofitable"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Pray for us, for we are persuaded that we have a good conscience, desiring to live honorably in all things.","understanding":"προσεύχεσθε περὶ ἡμῶν, “Pray for us” — προσεύχομαι from the pray family. πειθόμεθα γὰρ ὅτι καλὴν συνείδησιν ἔχομεν, “for we are persuaded that we have a good conscience” — καλός from the good family. ἐν πᾶσιν καλῶς θέλοντες ἀναστρέφεσθαι, “desiring to live honorably in all things” — καλῶς, beautifully, honourably.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"Pray"},{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"persuaded"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"honorably"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desiring"},{"strongs":"G390","hebrew":"ἀναστρέφω","translit":"anastréphō","english":"live"}]},{"v":19,"text":"I strongly urge you to do this, that I may be restored to you sooner.","understanding":"περισσοτέρως δὲ παρακαλῶ τοῦτο ποιῆσαι, “I strongly urge you to do this,” ἵνα τάχιον ἀποκατασταθῶ ὑμῖν, “that I may be restored to you sooner.” The writer was kept away — perhaps by imprisonment or other duties — and believed their prayers could make a difference.","words":[{"strongs":"G4056","hebrew":"περισσοτέρως","translit":"perissotérōs","english":"strongly"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"urge"},{"strongs":"G600","hebrew":"ἀποκαθίστημι","translit":"apokathístēmi","english":"restored"},{"strongs":"G5032","hebrew":"τάχιον","translit":"táchion","english":"sooner"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Now may the God of peace, who brought again from the dead the great shepherd of the sheep with the blood of an eternal covenant, our Lord Jesus,","understanding":"A great benediction. ὁ δὲ θεὸς τῆς εἰρήνης, “Now may the God of peace” — after so many warnings, God is the God of peace. ὁ ἀναγαγὼν ἐκ νεκρῶν, “who brought again from the dead” — the only direct mention of the resurrection in Hebrews, using ἀνάγω, ‘led up’. τὸν ποιμένα τῶν προβάτων τὸν μέγαν, “the great shepherd of the sheep” — as Moses brought Israel up from the sea, God brought up the great Shepherd from death. ἐν αἵματι διαθήκης αἰωνίου, “with the blood of an eternal covenant, our Lord Jesus.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G321","hebrew":"ἀνάγω","translit":"anágō","english":"brought again"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4166","hebrew":"ποιμήν","translit":"poimḗn","english":"shepherd"},{"strongs":"G4263","hebrew":"πρόβατον","translit":"próbaton","english":"sheep"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"}]},{"v":21,"text":"make you complete in every good work to do his will, working in you that which is well pleasing in his sight, through Jesus Christ, to whom be the glory forever and ever. Amen.","understanding":"καταρτίσαι ὑμᾶς ἐν παντὶ ἔργῳ ἀγαθῷ εἰς τὸ ποιῆσαι τὸ θέλημα αὐτοῦ, “make you complete in every good work to do his will” — καταρτίζω from the perfect family: fit you out, equip, mend; ἔργον and ἀγαθός from the work and good families. ποιῶν ἐν ὑμῖν τὸ εὐάρεστον ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ, “working in you that which is well pleasing in his sight” — God works in us what He asks of us. διὰ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, ᾧ ἡ δόξα εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “through Jesus Christ, to whom be the glory forever and ever. Amen.” αἰών from the time family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2675","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","english":"make you complete"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2101","hebrew":"εὐάρεστος","translit":"euárestos","english":"well pleasing"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in his sight"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":22,"text":"But I exhort you, brothers, endure the word of exhortation; for I have written to you in few words.","understanding":"παρακαλῶ δὲ ὑμᾶς, ἀδελφοί, ἀνέχεσθε τοῦ λόγου τῆς παρακλήσεως, “But I exhort you, brothers, endure the word of exhortation” — λόγος from the word family. The writer calls his letter a sermon and asks them to bear with it, including its hard warnings. καὶ γὰρ διὰ βραχέων ἐπέστειλα ὑμῖν, “for I have written to you in few words” — Hebrews takes under an hour to read aloud; he could have said much more (9:5; 11:32).","words":[{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G430","hebrew":"ἀνέχομαι","translit":"anéchomai","english":"endure"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G3874","hebrew":"παράκλησις","translit":"paráklēsis","english":"exhortation"},{"strongs":"G1024","hebrew":"βραχύς","translit":"brachýs","english":"few"},{"strongs":"G1989","hebrew":"ἐπιστέλλω","translit":"epistéllō","english":"written"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Know that our brother Timothy has been freed, with whom, if he comes shortly, I will see you.","understanding":"γινώσκετε τὸν ἀδελφὸν Τιμόθεον ἀπολελυμένον, “Know that our brother Timothy has been freed” — γινώσκω from the know family. This is the only mention of Timothy being imprisoned; we do not know when or where it happened. The writer was clearly part of Paul's circle, but he does not give his name. μεθ᾽ οὗ, ἐὰν τάχιον ἔρχηται, ὄψομαι ὑμᾶς, “with whom, if he comes shortly, I will see you” — ὁράω from the see family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"Know"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G5095","hebrew":"Τιμόθεος","translit":"Timótheos","english":"Timothy"},{"strongs":"G630","hebrew":"ἀπολύω","translit":"apolýō","english":"freed"},{"strongs":"G5032","hebrew":"τάχιον","translit":"táchion","english":"shortly"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Greet all of your leaders and all the saints. The Italians greet you.","understanding":"ἀσπάσασθε πάντας τοὺς ἡγουμένους ὑμῶν καὶ πάντας τοὺς ἁγίους, “Greet all of your leaders and all the saints.” ἀσπάζονται ὑμᾶς οἱ ἀπὸ τῆς Ἰταλίας, “The Italians greet you.” This may mean believers in Italy sending greetings (if the writer was there), or Italians living elsewhere sending greetings home (if the readers were in Rome). Many think the letter was sent to believers in or near Rome, but it cannot be proved.","words":[{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"Greet"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"leaders"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G2482","hebrew":"Ἰταλία","translit":"Italía","english":"Italians"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Grace be with you all. Amen.","understanding":"ἡ χάρις μετὰ πάντων ὑμῶν. ἀμήν, “Grace be with you all. Amen.” The letter that showed the throne of grace (4:16) and warned against insulting the Spirit of grace (10:29) ends as all the apostolic letters do: with grace, for everyone.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Unexpected Guests","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture an old man sitting at the entrance of his tent in the heat of the day. He looks up and sees three travellers standing nearby. He runs to meet them, bows low and begs them to rest under his tree. He and his wife hurry to bake bread and prepare a calf. He serves them with his own hands. Only later does he realise that the guests were messengers of God — and that the Lord Himself had visited him.","meaning":"“Let brotherly love continue” (v. 1). “Don’t forget to show hospitality to strangers, for in doing so, some have entertained angels without knowing it” (v. 2). “Remember those who are in bonds, as bound with them” (v. 3). Love of believers, love of strangers and love of the suffering are the shape of a church that holds fast to Christ. Ask the Spirit whom you should welcome or remember this week."},{"title":"The Same Yesterday, Today and Forever","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture a church noticeboard covered with photographs of pastors and leaders from years gone by — some young, some grey-haired, many now gone. Each served, preached, prayed and died. Below the photographs, carved into the wall, is a single unchanging inscription. Generations come and go; leaders rise and fall; the words on the wall remain.","meaning":"“Remember your leaders, men who spoke to you the word of God, and considering the results of their conduct, imitate their faith” (v. 7). “Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today, and forever” (v. 8). Faithful leaders are gifts, but they pass. Christ does not. His grace, His priesthood, His promises are the same now as when your faith began, and they will be the same forever."},{"title":"Outside the Camp","verseRef":"v. 11-14","image":"Picture the great camp of Israel, with the tabernacle at its centre, the priests busy, the people secure. Outside the boundary, beyond the tents, is a smouldering heap where the bodies of the sin offerings are burned. No one wants to go there; it is the place of rejection. Then picture a hill outside the walls of Jerusalem, where Jesus hangs on a cross. And picture a small company of believers walking out through the city gate, leaving the comfortable centre, to stand with Him.","meaning":"“Therefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people through his own blood, suffered outside of the gate” (v. 12). “Let us therefore go out to him outside of the camp, bearing his reproach” (v. 13). “For we don’t have here an enduring city, but we seek that which is to come” (v. 14). Following Jesus may mean leaving places of approval and comfort. Ask the Spirit for courage to go to Him, wherever He is."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Love in Practice (v. 1-6)","body":"Let brotherly love continue; show hospitality to strangers; remember prisoners and the ill-treated; honour marriage and keep it pure; be free from the love of money and content, because God has promised never to leave or forsake His people, so they can say with confidence that the Lord is their helper."},{"label":"Leaders, the Unchanging Christ and Strange Teaching (v. 7-9)","body":"Remember former leaders who spoke God's word and imitate their faith; Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today and forever; do not be carried away by various strange teachings, for the heart is strengthened by grace, not by food rules."},{"label":"Outside the Camp (v. 10-16)","body":"Believers have an altar from which those who serve the tabernacle have no right to eat; as sin-offering bodies were burned outside the camp, Jesus suffered outside the gate to sanctify His people by His blood, so we go out to Him bearing His reproach, seeking the city to come; through Him we offer continual sacrifices of praise and the sacrifices of doing good and sharing, which please God."},{"label":"Leaders, Prayer and Benediction (v. 17-25)","body":"Obey present leaders who keep watch over souls and will give account; pray for the writer, who has a clear conscience and wants to return soon; may the God of peace, who brought back from the dead the great Shepherd of the sheep by the blood of the eternal covenant, equip them with every good thing to do His will; bear with this brief word of exhortation; Timothy has been released; greetings from those of Italy; grace be with all."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Love That Holds Fast","paragraphs":["After twelve chapters of soaring doctrine and solemn warnings, Hebrews ends with ordinary Christian living. “Let brotherly love continue” (v. 1). Two love-family words open the chapter: φιλαδελφία, brotherly love, and φιλοξενία, love of strangers. “Don’t forget to show hospitality to strangers, for in doing so, some have entertained angels without knowing it” (v. 2). Paul urges the same: “In love of the brothers be tenderly affectionate to one another” (Romans 12:10), and Peter says, “Be hospitable to one another without grumbling” (1 Peter 4:9).","Love reaches those who suffer: “Remember those who are in bonds, as bound with them; and those who are ill-treated, since you are also in the body” (v. 3). Jesus said, “I was in prison, and you came to me” (Matthew 25:36). Love honours marriage: “Let marriage be held in honor among all, and let the bed be undefiled” (v. 4). And love is free from greed: “Be free from the love of money, content with such things as you have” (v. 5).","The reason for contentment is God's presence. He has said, “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you” (v. 5). Paul learned the same secret: “for I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content in it” (Philippians 4:11). And so believers can say, “The Lord is my helper. I will not fear. What can man do to me?” (v. 6). Paul asks, “If God is for us, who can be against us?” (Romans 8:31)."]},{"heading":"The Same Yesterday, Today and Forever","paragraphs":["The readers are to “Remember your leaders, men who spoke to you the word of God, and considering the results of their conduct, imitate their faith” (v. 7). Their first leaders had probably died, some perhaps as martyrs. Leaders come and go, but “Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today, and forever” (v. 8). Two time-family words — σήμερον (‘today’) and αἰών (‘forever’) — sum up the letter's message: the Christ of the past is the Christ of now and of every age to come. God said through Malachi, “For I, Yahweh, don’t change” (Malachi 3:6).","Because Christ does not change, believers should not be “carried away by various and strange teachings, for it is good that the heart be established by grace, not by food” (v. 9). The Greek for ‘strange’ (ξένος) shares its root with ‘strangers’ (v. 2): believers should welcome strangers but not strange doctrines. The heart is strengthened by God's grace in Christ, not by religious rules about food and ritual.","So the readers face a choice. “Therefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people through his own blood, suffered outside of the gate” (v. 12). “Let us therefore go out to him outside of the camp, bearing his reproach” (v. 13). John records that Jesus “went out, bearing his cross, to the place called ‘The Place of a Skull’” (John 19:17). To follow Him may mean leaving the security of accepted religion. But “we don’t have here an enduring city, but we seek that which is to come” (v. 14)."]},{"heading":"New-Covenant Sacrifices and the God of Peace","paragraphs":["The animal sacrifices are finished, but believers still offer sacrifices. “Through him, then, let us offer up a sacrifice of praise to God continually, that is, the fruit of lips which proclaim allegiance to his name” (v. 15). “But don’t forget to be doing good and sharing, for with such sacrifices God is well pleased” (v. 16). Two offering-family words describe praise and generosity as the worship of the new covenant. Peter calls believers “a holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God through Jesus Christ” (1 Peter 2:5).","Leaders are to be followed, because “they watch on behalf of your souls, as those who will give account” (v. 17). The word ‘watch’ (ἀγρυπνέω) means to go without sleep, like a shepherd guarding a flock at night. And the writer asks for prayer: “Pray for us, for we are persuaded that we have a good conscience, desiring to live honorably in all things” (v. 18). Paul asked the same: “Brothers, pray for us” (1 Thessalonians 5:25).","The letter closes with one of the most beautiful benedictions in Scripture: “Now may the God of peace, who brought again from the dead the great shepherd of the sheep with the blood of an eternal covenant, our Lord Jesus” (v. 20), “make you complete in every good work to do his will, working in you that which is well pleasing in his sight, through Jesus Christ, to whom be the glory forever and ever. Amen” (v. 21). Jesus is “the good shepherd” who “lays down his life for the sheep” (John 10:11). The perfect-family word καταρτίζω promises that God will equip us for everything He asks, because He Himself is working in us. As Paul writes, “For it is God who works in you both to will and to work, for his good pleasure” (Philippians 2:13)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Hospitality, Contentment and the Great Shepherd","paragraphs":["Hospitality was a mark of the early church. Paul lists it among the marks of love: “contributing to the needs of the saints; given to hospitality” (Romans 12:13). Jesus said, “I was a stranger, and you took me in” (Matthew 25:35). Contentment is a recurring theme: “But godliness with contentment is great gain” (1 Timothy 6:6), and “But having food and clothing, we will be content with that” (1 Timothy 6:8). Jesus taught, “But seek first God’s Kingdom, and his righteousness; and all these things will be given to you as well” (Matthew 6:33).","The sacrifices of the new covenant include generosity. Paul called the Philippians' gift “a sweet-smelling fragrance, an acceptable and well-pleasing sacrifice to God” (Philippians 4:18). Paul also urged believers to honour their leaders: “to respect and honor them in love for their work’s sake” (1 Thessalonians 5:13). Leaders are under-shepherds of the flock which God “purchased with his own blood” (Acts 20:28).","Jesus is the Shepherd of His people in every age. Peter writes, “For you were going astray like sheep; but now have returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls” (1 Peter 2:25), and promises, “When the chief Shepherd is revealed, you will receive the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away” (1 Peter 5:4). And the unchanging Christ is the Lord “who is and who was and who is to come, the Almighty” (Revelation 1:8)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Hospitality / love of strangers","hebrew":"φιλονεξία","translit":"philonexía","strongs":"G5381","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “hospitableness.” The dictionary spells the word φιλονεξία, but it is φιλοξενία, from φίλος (friend, lover) and ξένος (stranger). Paul lists it among the marks of genuine love (Romans 12:13), and it is a qualification for church leaders (φιλόξενος, Titus 1:8). This study adds it to the love family. Hospitality is love that opens the door to those who are not yet friends."},{"english":"Free from the love of money","hebrew":"ἀφιλάργυρος","translit":"aphilárgyros","strongs":"G866","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “unavaricious.” It is the negative of φιλάργυρος, ‘lover of money’ (2 Timothy 3:2). Paul requires it of overseers (1 Timothy 3:3). It describes a person whose heart is not owned by money. The writer grounds it not in willpower but in God's promise never to leave or forsake His people: when God is your security, money loses its grip."},{"english":"Yesterday / in time past","hebrew":"χθές","translit":"chthés","strongs":"G5504","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “\"yesterday\"; by extension, in time past or hitherto.” Here, in the letter's great confession about Christ, ‘yesterday’ refers to the whole past — the Christ who created the worlds, spoke through the prophets, lived, died, rose and ascended. He is the same ‘today’ (σήμερον, the word of Psalm 95 in chapters 3–4) and ‘forever’ (εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας). His character, power and promises do not change."},{"english":"Praise / thank-offering","hebrew":"αἴνεσις","translit":"aínesis","strongs":"G133","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “a praising (the act), i.e. (specially) a thank(-offering).” It appears only here in the New Testament. In the Greek Old Testament it translated the ‘thanksgiving offering’, a peace offering brought to give thanks to God. Now, with animal sacrifices ended, believers offer praise itself as their sacrifice, continually, through Jesus."},{"english":"Watch / keep awake","hebrew":"ἀγρυπνέω","translit":"agrypnéō","strongs":"G69","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “to be sleepless, i.e. keep awake.” Jesus used it when He told His disciples to stay alert for His coming, and Paul used it of watching in prayer. In Hebrews 13:17 it pictures leaders losing sleep over the souls in their care, like shepherds watching their flock by night. This study adds it to the sleep family beside γρηγορέω."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"As Hebrews ends, the Holy Spirit turns doctrine into daily faithfulness and points you to the unchanging Christ.","items":[{"title":"Love Made Practical","body":"The Spirit wants your faith to show in brotherly love, hospitality, remembering the suffering, faithfulness in marriage and freedom from the love of money. Ask Him which of these He is highlighting for you, and take one concrete step this week."},{"title":"He Has Not Changed","body":"Whatever has changed in your life — leaders, circumstances, health, relationships — Jesus Christ is the same. The Spirit invites you to rest in His unchanging faithfulness. Bring Him your uncertainties and anchor yourself again in the One who is the same yesterday, today and forever."},{"title":"Go Out to Him","body":"Following Jesus may mean leaving places of comfort, approval or familiarity. The Spirit is calling you to go to Jesus outside the camp, bearing His reproach. Ask Him where He wants you to stand with Him, even if it costs you."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Hospitality Check","body":"When did you last welcome someone outside your circle into your home or life (v. 2)? Plan one act of hospitality this month."},{"title":"The Contentment Check","body":"Are you content with what you have (v. 5)? Name one thing you are tempted to crave, and set verse 5's promise against it."},{"title":"The Praise Check","body":"Is your life marked by a continual sacrifice of praise and of doing good (vv. 15–16)? Choose one way to make each a daily habit."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Remember Those in Bonds","body":"This month, learn about Christians who are imprisoned or persecuted for their faith somewhere in the world. Pray for them by name if you can, write a letter of encouragement through a trusted ministry, or support their families. Remember them ‘as bound with them’ (v. 3)."},{"title":"Honour and Pray for Your Leaders","body":"Write a note of thanks to a leader in your church who watches over your soul (v. 17). Tell them specifically how their ministry has helped you. Then commit to praying for them weekly for the next three months, as the writer asked his readers to pray for him (v. 18)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are the same yesterday, today and forever. Thank You that You do not change, even when everything around me does. Let brotherly love continue among Your people. Make me hospitable to strangers, mindful of those in chains and those who suffer, faithful in marriage and pure in heart. Free me from the love of money, and make me content, because You have said, ‘I will never leave you nor forsake you.’ The Lord is my helper; I will not fear. Thank You for leaders who spoke Your word to me; help me imitate their faith. Keep me from being carried away by strange teachings, and strengthen my heart by grace. You suffered outside the gate to make me holy through Your own blood. Give me courage to go out to You, bearing Your reproach, seeking the city that is to come. Through You I offer a continual sacrifice of praise, and I will not forget to do good and to share. God of peace, who brought back from the dead the great Shepherd of the sheep by the blood of the eternal covenant, equip me with everything good to do Your will, and work in me what is pleasing in Your sight, through Jesus Christ, to whom be glory forever and ever. Amen. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Abraham Entertains Angels","ref":"Genesis 18:1-15","relatedVerses":"v. 2","summary":"Abraham was sitting at the entrance of his tent by the oaks of Mamre in the heat of the day when he saw three men standing nearby. He ran to meet them, bowed down, and urged them to rest and eat. He and Sarah hurried to bake bread and prepare a tender calf, and Abraham served them under the tree. One of the visitors promised that Sarah would have a son within a year. Sarah laughed, but the LORD asked, ‘Is anything too hard for the LORD?’ Abraham discovered that he had welcomed the Lord and His angels. Hebrews 13:2 points to scenes like this: some have entertained angels without knowing it."},{"title":"Jesus Crucified Outside the Gate","ref":"John 19:16-30","relatedVerses":"v. 11-13","summary":"Pilate handed Jesus over to be crucified. “He went out, bearing his cross, to the place called ‘The Place of a Skull’, which is called in Hebrew, ‘Golgotha’” (John 19:17). There He was crucified between two others. John notes that the place was near the city, outside its walls. Jesus suffered in the place of rejection, like the sin-offering burned outside the camp. Hebrews 13 calls believers to go out to Him there, sharing His reproach, rather than staying in the security of religious approval."},{"title":"The Good Shepherd","ref":"John 10:1-18","relatedVerses":"v. 20","summary":"Jesus described Himself as the shepherd whose sheep know his voice, who calls them by name and leads them out. He contrasted Himself with thieves who come to steal and hired hands who run away when the wolf comes. “I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep” (John 10:11). He said that He would lay down His life and take it up again. Hebrews 13:20 celebrates the fulfilment: the God of peace has brought back from the dead the great Shepherd of the sheep by the blood of the eternal covenant."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"Hebrews","titleEmojis":"👑⚓","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Jesus Is Better — So Hold Fast and Run","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of Hebrews in one overarching thought, it is this: because God has spoken His final word in His Son — who is greater than the angels, greater than Moses, a better High Priest than Aaron, the mediator of a better covenant, and the once-for-all sacrifice who sat down at God's right hand — believers must not drift back to shadows, but must draw near to God with confidence, hold fast their confession without wavering, encourage one another, and run with endurance, looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith, who is the same yesterday, today and forever.","paragraphs":["Hebrews was written to believers who were tired. They had begun well, suffering insults and the loss of their property with joy. But years of pressure had worn them down. Some had stopped meeting together; some were dull of hearing; some were drifting back toward the familiar world of the temple, priests and sacrifices that seemed safer than following Jesus outside the camp. The writer, whose name we do not know, wrote a ‘word of exhortation’ (13:22) to bring them back to Jesus.","His method is to show, step by step, that Jesus is better. God “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son” (1:2), who is “the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance” (1:3). He is greater than the angels, who worship Him. He is greater than Moses, who was a faithful servant in God's house, while Jesus is the Son over it. He gives a better rest than Joshua gave. He is a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek, greater than Aaron. He mediates a better covenant, ministers in the true tabernacle, and offered Himself once for all.","Woven through the teaching are five warnings: do not drift (2:1–4); do not harden your hearts (3:7–4:13); grow up, and do not fall away (5:11–6:12); do not keep on sinning wilfully after knowing the truth (10:26–31); do not refuse Him who speaks from heaven (12:25–29). They are some of the most solemn words in Scripture. But each is followed by encouragement: God is faithful, Jesus is our sympathetic High Priest, the hope is an anchor, the writer is persuaded of better things.","And the letter ends with a call to run. After the great gallery of faith in chapter 11, the readers are told, “let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us” (12:1), “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith” (12:2). They are not at Sinai but at Mount Zion. They are receiving a kingdom that cannot be shaken. And whatever changes, “Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today, and forever” (13:8)."]},"map":{"intro":"Hebrews is the nineteenth book of the New Testament. It stands after Paul's letters and before the letters of James, Peter, John and Jude. It was probably written before AD 70, while the temple sacrifices were still being offered (8:4; 10:1–2). Its author is unknown; early Christians suggested Paul, Barnabas, Apollos and others, but the letter does not say. The recipients were probably Jewish Christians, perhaps in or near Rome (13:24).","sections":[{"title":"Teaching Woven With Exhortation","body":"Hebrews alternates between showing who Jesus is and calling readers to respond. Christ is better than the angels (1–2), Moses and Joshua (3–4), and Aaron (5–7); He brings a better covenant, sanctuary and sacrifice (8–10). Then come the great responses: draw near and hold fast (10), live by faith like the heroes of old (11), run with endurance and receive God's discipline (12), and live out love outside the camp (13). The doctrine is never merely academic; it is always aimed at endurance."},{"title":"Words That Need Care","body":"Several passages in Hebrews require careful reading. The authorship cannot be settled. In 2:9 a few manuscripts read ‘apart from God’ instead of ‘by the grace of God’. The warnings of 6:4–6 and 10:26–31 are understood differently by sincere Christians — as describing genuine believers who can fall away, people who experienced much without being truly born again, or real warnings God uses to keep His people — and this study has said so plainly while giving the text's own force. In 9:16–17 the writer plays on διαθήκη as both ‘covenant’ and ‘will’. Several Old Testament quotations follow the Greek translation (for example 10:5 and 11:21)."},{"title":"A Feast of Word Families","body":"Hebrews added more word families to this study than any other book: image (εἰκών, χαρακτήρ), pattern (τύπος, ὑπόδειγμα, σκιά), rest (κατάπαυσις, σαββατισμός), perfect (τελειόω, τελειωτής), hold fast (κατέχω, κρατέω), offering (θυσία, προσφορά, ὁλοκαύτωμα), author (ἀρχηγός, αἴτιος) and discipline (παιδεία, γυμνάζω). Its key contrasts — shadow and substance, standing and sitting, Sinai and Zion, χρόνος and καιρός and σήμερον — all serve one message: Jesus is the reality, and He is better."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: Hebrews ended with a call to endurance, faith that works, and a kingdom that cannot be shaken. The next two books are James, a practical letter about faith that is proved by works, and 1 Peter, a letter of hope for believers suffering as pilgrims in a hostile world.","books":[{"book":"James","connection":"James writes, “James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are in the Dispersion” (James 1:1). Like Hebrews, it is written to Jewish believers under pressure, and like Hebrews it prizes endurance: “knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance” (James 1:3), “Let endurance have its perfect work, that you may be perfect and complete” (James 1:4) — the perfect family again. Hebrews 11 showed faith that acts; James insists on it: “Even so faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself” (James 2:17). Hebrews 13 called for doing good and sharing; James says, “But be doers of the word, and not only hearers” (James 1:22). Watch for how James tests whether faith is real."},{"book":"1 Peter","connection":"Peter writes “to the chosen ones who are living as foreigners in the Dispersion” (1 Peter 1:1) — pilgrims like the heroes of Hebrews 11. He begins with the living hope that Hebrews also celebrated: God “caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (1 Peter 1:3). He calls believers “a royal priesthood” (1 Peter 2:9) who offer spiritual sacrifices, echoing Hebrews 13:15. And he prepares them for suffering: “Beloved, don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you” (1 Peter 4:12). Watch for how Peter, like Hebrews, points suffering believers to Christ's example and to the glory to come."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"God's Final Word in His Son","anchorRef":"Hebrews 1:1-3","body":"God, who spoke through the prophets in many ways, “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son” (1:2). The Son is heir of all things, Creator of the worlds, “the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance” (1:3), who upholds all things by His word and purified us from our sins before sitting down at God's right hand. He is greater than the angels. There is no fuller word to wait for and nothing better to go back to."},{"title":"A Great High Priest Who Sympathises","anchorRef":"Hebrews 4:14-16; 7:25","body":"“For we don’t have a high priest who can’t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin” (4:15). Jesus is a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek, appointed by God's oath. “Therefore he is also able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through him, seeing that he lives forever to make intercession for them” (7:25). So we draw near to the throne of grace with boldness."},{"title":"One Sacrifice, Once for All","anchorRef":"Hebrews 9:12; 10:12-14","body":"The old priests stood daily offering sacrifices that could never take away sins. Christ entered the true holy place “through his own blood” and obtained “eternal redemption” (9:12). He “offered one sacrifice for sins forever” and “sat down on the right hand of God” (10:12). “For by one offering he has perfected forever those who are being sanctified” (10:14). The shadows of the old covenant have given way to the reality of the new."},{"title":"Faith That Sees the Invisible","anchorRef":"Hebrews 11:1, 6","body":"“Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, proof of things not seen” (11:1). Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Moses, Rahab and many others lived by faith, acting on God's word, often without seeing the fulfilment. Some conquered; others suffered and died. All were commended. “Without faith it is impossible to be well pleasing to him” (11:6). Faith treats God's promises as real and His unseen presence as true."},{"title":"Hold Fast and Run With Endurance","anchorRef":"Hebrews 10:23; 12:1-2","body":"“let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering; for he who promised is faithful” (10:23). The warnings of Hebrews are real, but the call is clear: draw near, hold fast, encourage one another, and “run with perseverance the race that is set before us” (12:1), “looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith” (12:2). God disciplines His children for their good, and we are receiving a kingdom that cannot be shaken."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of Hebrews practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":1,"title":"Draw Near Daily Through Your High Priest","lesson":"The Lesson: “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need” (4:16). Jesus has opened the way, and He lives to intercede for you.","practice":"The Practice: For the next month, begin each day by consciously drawing near to God through Jesus. Read one verse about His priesthood (4:14–16; 7:25; 9:24; 10:19–22), then bring two lists to God: ‘mercy’ for your failures and ‘grace’ for your needs. Close by thanking Jesus that He is interceding for you. Notice how this changes the way you face the day."},{"step":2,"title":"Refuse to Drift — Gather and Encourage","lesson":"The Lesson: “Let us consider how to provoke one another to love and good works, not forsaking our own assembling together” (10:24–25). Drift happens alone; endurance grows together.","practice":"The Practice: Commit to regular, faithful attendance with your church for the next three months. Each week, deliberately encourage at least one person there — a word of hope, a prayer, an invitation to serve. If you notice someone missing, contact them. Keep a simple record of whom you have encouraged."},{"step":3,"title":"Run Your Race Looking to Jesus","lesson":"The Lesson: “let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us, looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of faith” (12:1–2). Lay aside weights and sin, and fix your eyes on Him.","practice":"The Practice: Write down one ‘weight’ and one entangling sin that slow your race, and take a concrete step this week to lay each aside. Then choose a Hebrews 11 hero and study their story, asking what faith looked like for them. Finally, memorise Hebrews 12:1–2 and 13:8, and recite them whenever you are tempted to give up."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father, I close the letter to the Hebrews, and I declare: You have spoken Your final word in Your Son. Jesus is the heir of all things, the Creator of the worlds, the radiance of Your glory and the exact imprint of Your nature. He upholds all things by His powerful word, and He purified me of my sins and sat down at Your right hand. He is better than the angels, better than Moses, better than every priest and every sacrifice. I will not drift away from so great a salvation. Today I hear Your voice, and I will not harden my heart. I enter Your rest by faith, resting from my own works as You rested from Yours. I have a great High Priest who sympathises with my weakness, who was tempted in every way yet without sin, and who lives forever to intercede for me; so I draw near to the throne of grace with boldness. He is a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek, the guarantor of a better covenant. He entered the true holy place once for all with His own blood and obtained eternal redemption. By one offering He has perfected forever those who are being sanctified, and You remember my sins no more. I hold fast the confession of my hope without wavering, because You who promised are faithful. I will not forsake gathering with Your people, and I will stir others up to love and good works. Faith is the assurance of things hoped for and the conviction of things not seen; by faith I please You. Surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, I lay aside every weight and sin and run with endurance, looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of my faith. I receive Your discipline as a child You love. I have come not to Sinai but to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God and to Jesus, whose blood speaks better than Abel's. I am receiving a kingdom that cannot be shaken, so I serve You with reverence and awe. I go out to Him outside the camp, bearing His reproach, offering the sacrifice of praise and doing good. Jesus Christ is the same yesterday, today and forever. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
